window.__labSearchIndex={"built":"2026-11-26","fields":["feature","page","kind","heading","url","text"],"e":[["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","direct-debit/docs/product.html","v2.7 18-Nov-2026 Source: Direct Debit - Product Document v2.7.docx Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference Owner Ghulam Rabbani, Product (Payment Gateway) Version 2.7 draft — 18 November 2026 Replaces “Direct Debit _ MP Scope” (Merchant Portal team) and its section 5, “Direct Debit Functional Scope” (07 September 2026) Audience Business, Risk, Compliance, Finance, Operations, Support, Merchant Portal, Merchant Account, DD Backend and APEX teams How this document is organised Part What it covers Who it is for Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea What Direct Debit is, who does what, how money and data flow, how a merchant is enabled, key rules, scope, open items Everyone Part B — Functional requirements What the product must do. Changes since the 07-Sep-2026 version are in blue ; superseded text is in red strikethrough Product, Business, QA, all build teams Part C — Technical requirements Delivery plan, frontend and Backend story alignment, and the Backend stories ( S1 to S14 ) Developers, QA, Delivery What changed in version 2.7 (18 November 2026) Topic Change Where Cancellation — customer messages Decided (Product, 18-Nov): every customer message about a cancellation is sent by email only (no SMS): cancellation request (and each resend), cancellation accepted, cancellation rejected by the bank. Exact wording added. The request email and the cancellation page tell the customer that the cancellation does not take effect until they sign it (DDS go-live checklist) S8 sections 3 and 5 , A14 , B18 Cancellation link No expiry: the link works while the request awaits the customer’s signature. The merchant can resend it once per day S8 sections 2 and 3 Cancellation accepted When DDS reports the cancellation request accepted ( ACCP ), Geidea cancels the mandate on its side at once; no separate mandate status inquiry S8 section 5 After a cancellation is confirmed Payments already in a generated payment file run to their final status; every other follow-up (retry, representment, rollover, Amend, scheduled collections) stops and is Cancelled S8 rule 8 , section 4 Contract references A DDA reference is never reused, including after the contract is cancelled B3 , S8 rule 7 What changed in version 2.6 (18 October 2026) Topic Change Where TBFC moved to Phase 2 Decided (Product, 18-Oct): “To Be Filled By Customer” is not built in this phase; the merchant always enters the bank account or card. DDS does not require it for go-live (it is not in DDS’s go-live checklist, and DDS’s API has no TBFC mode). Everything in this document marked TBFC — S2 section 4.1 , S3 section 5 , the TBFC bulk flow in S9 , the Awaiting Customer Details status — is kept for Phase 2. Create Contract rejects bankInfoFillByCustomer = true in this phase A11 , B3 , B5 , B14 , C2 , C3 , S2 , S3 , S9 Frequencies offered Decided (Product, 18-Oct): this phase offers Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Quarterly, Half-yearly, Annually. Every two months, Every four months and One Time Only are DDS frequencies Geidea does not offer yet; Create Contract rejects them (V16) A4 , B3 , S1 section 5.2 , S2 Re-setting a collection in Error Decided (Product, 18-Oct): a collection in Error can be moved by Amend to any later date inside the contract period, including a date after later collections . It is exempt from the order rule ( A9 ); the minimum gap is checked against the collections just before and after its new date ( A10 , A11 ). No rollover is added for this case B8 , S7 section 2 What changed in version 2.5 (15 October 2026) Items marked Asked DDS are not yet confirmed; they are also listed in A13. Topic Change Where DDS timings confirmed DDS confirmed (08-Oct): pay / no-pay is final at the Central Bank 18:00 cut-off (banks confirm between 11:00 and 18:00; a collection a bank has not settled by 18:00 is failed by the Central Bank as “Technical issues at Paying Bank”). The FT reference is generated between 01:00 and 05:00 the next day. Same timings every day; an empty f"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Scope sign-off","direct-debit/docs/product.html#scope-sign-off","Function SPOC Sign-off Business owner Business Risk Risk & Compliance Cybersecurity Cybersecurity FinOps FinOps Finance Finance Operations Operations UAT UAT team Sign-off given on the earlier version does not carry over; this version needs a fresh sign-off."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Reference documents","direct-debit/docs/product.html#reference-documents","DDS REST API document v2.0 (includes the “SLA Timing / Batch Process” table) UAE PASS Signature API document v1.3 DDMP User Manual v1.0 and Payer’s Journey v2.1 DDMP Operation Rule Book v1 FAQ — Direct Debit v1.2 (Payer); UAE PASS FAQ DDMP Onboarding Documents; DDMP Customer Support Document APEX Merchant API UAE Specifications v1.27 Direct Debit Portal Walkthrough (live test, 11 to 15 September 2026) Direct Debit MP Backlog (Merchant Portal frontend stories US-01 to US-32 ), and the Backend stories created in Jira for its Epics 2 and 4 (Part C section C3 )"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Glossary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#glossary","Term Meaning Direct Debit (DD) A customer’s signed authority for a merchant to pull money from the customer’s bank account or card on agreed dates DDS / DDMP Direct Debit System FZ LLC, also called Direct Debit Marketplace. The licensed provider Geidea integrates with UAEDDS The UAE Central Bank’s Direct Debit System, the scheme that DDS submits to OIC Originator Identification Code. The Central Bank ID of the company collecting the money. Geidea’s is 560000001 DDA / mandate Direct Debit Authority. The signed, bank-approved authority itself, with its amount limits, frequency and validity dates Contract Geidea’s term for the mandate plus its subscription and collection schedule Subscription The collection schedule Geidea runs under a mandate. DDS does not hold it Collection (occurrence) One scheduled debit: a date and an amount Payment One attempt at DDS for a collection. Retries re-use the same payment FT reference Funds-transfer reference issued by DDS for a paid collection. It appears on Geidea’s bank statement and is the key Finance uses to reconcile the credit TBFC “To Be Filled By Customer”: the merchant leaves the bank or card details for the customer to enter MA Merchant Account: Geidea’s onboarding and merchant configuration system MP Merchant Portal: what the merchant’s users work in DD Backend (DD BE) Geidea’s Direct Debit service: order model, rules, daily jobs and the DDS integration APEX Geidea’s settlement platform: pricing, grouping and merchant payout PTLF The bulk transaction file the DD Backend sends to APEX MID Merchant ID. In Merchant Portal each MID is a “store”"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","direct-debit/docs/product.html#part-a-direct-debit-at-geidea",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A1. What Direct Debit is","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a1.-what-direct-debit-is","Direct Debit is the digital replacement for the post-dated cheque. Instead of handing over a book of cheques, the customer signs one electronic authority (the mandate) with UAE PASS. The mandate is registered with the UAE Central Bank’s Direct Debit System and approved by the customer’s own bank. From then on the merchant can collect each instalment on its due date without the customer doing anything. Key characteristics: Regulated by the UAE Central Bank. Every mandate and every collection passes through the Central Bank’s scheme (UAEDDS). Geidea reaches it through the licensed provider DDS. A pull payment. The merchant initiates each collection, within the limits the customer signed: a minimum and maximum amount, a frequency, and a start and end date. Not real-time. There is no instant authorisation as with a card. Collections are sent in a daily file the evening before the due date, debited the next day, and confirmed later that day. Built for payments over a period of time. It suits a subscription or instalment plan: rent over a year, school fees over terms. A one-off collection is possible but is not the main use. Local only. UAE-issued bank accounts and cards, in AED, for customers with an Emirates ID and UAE PASS. Legally strong. A collection made in line with the signed mandate cannot be disputed on commercial grounds, and the bank issues a formal proof of failure (bounce memo) when a collection is refused. Direct Debit is not a payment method inside the payment gateway. It does not touch MPGS or CyberSource. It is a separate integration with its own Backend."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A2. Market use case and target segments","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a2.-market-use-case-and-target-segments","The problem it solves. Merchants who collect large amounts on a schedule rely today on post-dated cheques or on chasing customers for transfers. Cheques are slow to collect, costly to handle and store, easy to lose, and painful when one bounces. Direct Debit keeps the legal weight of a cheque and removes the paper: collection is automatic, status is visible the same day, and a failed collection can be retried or carried forward. Post-dated cheques Direct Debit Customer effort Writes and hands over a cheque per instalment Signs once with UAE PASS Collection Merchant deposits each cheque Automatic on the due date Visibility Bank statement, days later Paid or rejected the same day Failure Bounced cheque, manual follow-up Up to 3 retries, automatic carry-forward, bounce memo Change of amount or date New cheque Merchant amends the collection within the signed limits Target segments in scope now Real Estate: rent, service charges, instalment plans. Education: school and university fees. Other segments with scheduled receivables (insurance, financing, memberships) can follow; nothing in the build is segment-specific. Positioning. Any company can go to DDS directly. Geidea’s value is what sits on top: the subscription engine, schedule amendments, retry and rollover handling, and one consolidated payout with the merchant’s other payment methods. In Phase 2 the same engine is opened to partners through an API ( A11 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A3. Payment instruments","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a3.-payment-instruments","Instrument In scope Notes UAE bank account (IBAN) Yes The main instrument. The bank is chosen from a list Geidea maintains to match DDS’s bank table UAE-issued credit card Yes, with conditions Allowed by default. Can be switched off per merchant (see A9 ) Non-UAE accounts or cards No Not supported by the scheme Note on credit cards (open item). DDS currently accepts the card number only in clear text. Handling a full card number brings PCI-DSS obligations, and the Merchant Portal is outside the PCI zone today. Until this is settled: Credit Card can be disabled for any merchant through its Direct Debit settings. The card number is always shown masked (last 4 digits). Decided (14-Oct-2026): the full number is held in Geidea’s token vault; the DD Backend keeps only the token and turns it back into the number when it has to be sent to DDS. The IBAN is not treated this way: it is shown in full on every screen and stored in full by the DD Backend (encrypted at rest, never written to logs). APEX does not need the card number: a static value is sent for every Direct Debit record (agreed with APEX, 06-Oct-2026). DDS has said it will support an encrypted card number; no date has been given. The customer’s bank account or card details are checked against Central Bank records as part of UAE PASS signing, not before."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A4. Key scheme rules and timings","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a4.-key-scheme-rules-and-timings",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A4. Key scheme rules and timings","Document","Rules at a glance","direct-debit/docs/product.html#rules-at-a-glance","Rule Value Maximum amount per collection AED 100,000,000 (scheme limit). Geidea can set a lower limit per merchant Amount type Fixed (every collection identical) or Variable (within the signed minimum and maximum) Frequency The mandate carries a frequency ceiling. The actual schedule can be less frequent, never more Minimum gap between two debits Set by DDS per frequency, counted from the actual debit date (table below) Customer signature UAE PASS. An unsigned mandate expires after 7 working days Bank approval of a signed mandate Up to 3 working days Payment file cut-off 20:00 on the day before the due date Retries of a rejected collection 3 after the original attempt (4 attempts in total), the same rule as a bounced cheque Changing a mandate’s terms Not possible. Cancel and create a new one, with a new signature Disputes A collection made in line with the signed mandate cannot be disputed or reversed on commercial grounds. A narrow route exists for processing errors only (wrong amount, unauthorised instruction), within 30 days. There is no chargeback as with cards Customer cancellation The customer may cancel through the merchant or directly with their own bank. When the customer cancels at the bank, the next collection is refused with a “mandate cancelled” reason and Geidea closes the contract Statement narration The customer’s bank statement shows Geidea (the OIC owner), not the merchant. Banks control this wording"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A4. Key scheme rules and timings","Document","Minimum gap between debits (confirmed by DDS, 24-Sep-2026)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#minimum-gap-between-debits-confirmed-by-dds-24-sep-2026","Frequency DDS period (days) Minimum gap (days) Daily 1 0 Weekly 7 4 Monthly 30 23 Every two months 60 50 Quarterly 90 80 Every four months 120 100 Half-yearly 180 160 Annually 360 330 Example: a Monthly mandate debited on 15 September cannot be debited again before 8 October. Offered by Geidea in this phase (18-Oct-2026): Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Quarterly, Half-yearly, Annually. Every two months, Every four months and One Time Only are DDS frequencies Geidea does not offer yet."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A4. Key scheme rules and timings","Document","DDS processing times (DDS REST API document v2.0, “SLA Timing / Batch Process”)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#dds-processing-times-dds-rest-api-document-v2.0-sla-timing-batch-process","Action What DDS does Response time What Geidea does Mandate Submits to the Central Bank every 30 minutes between 07:00 and 19:00 The customer’s bank has up to 3 working days to approve or reject Checks mandate status once a day at 18:15 Payment file Sends the batch to the Central Bank at 01:00 on the due date Same-day settlement between 20:00 and midnight Uploads the file by 20:00 the day before; checks paid / not paid at 18:30 on the due date, after the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off; checks the FT reference at 06:30 the next day (generated 01:00 to 05:00) Cancellation Submits to the Central Bank every 30 minutes between 07:00 and 19:00 The customer’s bank has up to 1 working day MVP ( S8 ) Retry of a failed payment Processed the next working day Retries must be sent before a per-collection retry deadline"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A4. Key scheme rules and timings","Document","Deduction and settlement timings for one collection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#deduction-and-settlement-timings-for-one-collection","D is the due date. If D is a weekend or public holiday, the debit moves to the next working day. When What happens D-1, by 20:00 Geidea uploads the payment file D, 01:00 DDS sends the batch to the Central Bank D Customer’s account is debited D, 11:00 to 18:00 Banks confirm collections. At the 18:00 Central Bank cut-off pay / no-pay is final; a collection not settled by then is failed as “Technical issues at Paying Bank” D, 18:30 Geidea checks DDS: the collection is Paid or Rejected D, after the 18:30 check Geidea builds the APEX file (PTLF) with the collections that turned Paid. The send time is configurable (see A8 ) D, 20:00 to midnight Full amount is credited to Geidea’s bank account D+1 Merchant payout (T+1, see A8 ) D+1, 01:00 to 05:00 DDS places the FT reference (DDS recommends checking from 06:00; month-end delays of about 30 minutes seen) D+1, 06:30 Geidea checks DDS for the FT reference. It is used by FinOps for reconciliation, not for payout DDS confirmed these timings (08-Oct-2026). They are the Central Bank’s settlement timings under the UAE DDS platform rules and apply every day; on a settlement holiday the file is empty. DDS syncs every status with the Central Bank every 10 minutes. What the September live test established from records also holds: a collection due on Sunday 13-Sep was debited on Monday 14-Sep, and the Paid status comes before the FT reference. DDS’s internal API SLA and escalation path are still to be agreed ( A13 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A5. Commercial and funds model","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a5.-commercial-and-funds-model","One originator. Geidea is the entity registered with the Central Bank, under OIC 560000001. Merchants are not registered individually; Geidea identifies them internally by MID. A merchant using its own OIC is deferred. Funds flow. The Central Bank credits the full collected amount to Geidea’s bank account linked to the OIC. DDS never holds the money and deducts nothing. DDS charges. DDS invoices Geidea monthly for its commission. Finance checks the invoice against the Direct Debit transaction counts in APEX. Merchant pricing. Geidea’s Direct Debit fee is set per merchant in MA at onboarding and applied by APEX, where Direct Debit is a payment scheme of its own. The fee is a flat fee per collection for now , separate from the merchant’s existing per-transaction fee. MA will provision both a flat fee and a percentage for Direct Debit so that percentage pricing can be offered later without further work. The commercial price points are a Business decision. Payout. APEX pays the merchant the collected amount less Geidea’s fee and VAT, together with the merchant’s other payment methods. No chargeback reserve. Because a correctly processed collection cannot be reversed on commercial grounds, payout does not need a chargeback-style hold. The narrow processing-error dispute route still exists."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A6. Who does what","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a6.-who-does-what",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A6. Who does what","Document","Parties outside Geidea","direct-debit/docs/product.html#parties-outside-geidea","Party Role Merchant Creates contracts, manages schedules, retries failed collections, receives payouts Customer (payer) Reviews the contract, enters bank or card details when asked, signs with UAE PASS DDS / DDMP Registers mandates, runs UAE PASS signing, submits payment files to the Central Bank, reports status and FT references UAE PASS The customer’s digital identity and signature Central Bank (UAEDDS) and the customer’s bank Approve or reject mandates; debit the customer; credit Geidea Geidea’s bank Holds the OIC-linked account that receives the collected funds"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A6. Who does what","Document","Inside Geidea","direct-debit/docs/product.html#inside-geidea","System or team Role in Direct Debit MA (Merchant Account) Direct Debit is assigned to a MID as a product during onboarding. Pricing and payout frequency are set here and sent to APEX. Holds the merchant’s Direct Debit settings (where the current PGW Config sits for Tabby, Tamara, VIS, etc.) MP (Merchant Portal) The merchant’s screens: portfolio, create contract, contract detail, collections, actions, bulk upload. Also hosts user management, where Direct Debit permissions are assigned Customer review page Geidea-hosted page the customer opens from the link: customer verification via OTP, contract review, bank or card entry, hand-off to UAE PASS DD Backend Holds the order model (mandate, subscription, collection, payment) and manages customer notifications. Enforces every rule. Runs the daily jobs. The only system that talks to DDS. Sends paid collections to APEX and fetches payout status from it. Serves all data to the Merchant Portal. Holds the bank list and the working-day calendar in its configuration APEX Receives paid collections in a PTLF file. Treats Direct Debit as a new scheme. Prices each matched record, groups it and pays the merchant under the MID’s normal payout frequency. Exposes payout status through its existing inquiry API. Shows Direct Debit as a new scheme in internal and merchant-facing reports Finance / Operations Reconcile the credits on Geidea’s bank statement against Direct Debit collections using the FT reference. This is an offline Finance/FinOps activity, outside the Direct Debit build. Reconcile the DDS monthly invoice for payment. Handle exceptions raised by the daily jobs, through the operations console ( S14 )"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A6. Who does what","Document","High-level solution","direct-debit/docs/product.html#high-level-solution","# Step 1 MA sends the MID, the Direct Debit product, pricing and payout frequency to APEX (existing process) 2 MA provides the merchant’s Direct Debit settings to the DD Backend 3 The merchant’s users create and manage contracts in Merchant Portal 4 Merchant Portal reads and writes everything through the DD Backend’s APIs 5 The customer opens the review link 6 The review page verifies the customer, shows the contract and takes the bank or card details through the DD Backend 7 The customer signs with UAE PASS 8 The DD Backend registers mandates with DDS, uploads the daily payment file, and fetches statuses and FT references 9 DDS submits mandates and debit instructions to the Central Bank and the customer’s bank 10 The Central Bank credits the full amount to Geidea’s bank account 11 The DD Backend sends paid collections to APEX in a PTLF file 12 APEX marks the records in the file as matched, then prices and groups them for payout 13 APEX pays the merchant 14 DDS invoices Geidea monthly; Finance checks it against APEX"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A7. End-to-end flows","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a7.-end-to-end-flows",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A7. End-to-end flows","Document","Flow 1 — Contract creation and signing","direct-debit/docs/product.html#flow-1-contract-creation-and-signing","Merchant Portal captures the customer, the contract terms and the collection schedule in one guided flow, and shows every planned collection before sending. The DD Backend validates everything, saves the contract, and registers the mandate with DDS. If the merchant chose “To Be Filled By Customer”, the Backend holds the contract and registers it only when the customer has entered the bank or card details, because DDS cannot create a mandate without them. The customer gets a link, proves who they are with a one-time password, reviews the contract and schedule, and signs with UAE PASS. The bank approves within 3 working days. The Backend picks this up in its daily 18:15 check and activates the subscription."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A7. End-to-end flows","Document","Flow 2 — Subscription and daily collection cycle","direct-debit/docs/product.html#flow-2-subscription-and-daily-collection-cycle","Every evening the Backend picks the collections due the next day, checks each one (contract still active, subscription not paused, amount within limits, minimum gap respected) and uploads one file per OIC to DDS before the 20:00 cut-off. DDS accepts a file all or nothing. If DDS rejects it, the Backend removes the rows DDS complained about, uploads the rest once more and raises an alert. If the file still does not go, it can be re-run through the operations console ( S14 ). DDS accepts one file per OIC per day; uploads it rejected don’t count. On the due date the Backend fetches the result; paid collections go to APEX the same day ( Flow 3 ). The next morning it fetches the FT reference and the actual debit date. The FT reference is for Finance’s reconciliation; payout does not wait for it."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A7. End-to-end flows","Document","Flow 3 — APEX, settlement and payout","direct-debit/docs/product.html#flow-3-apex-settlement-and-payout","Only paid collections go to APEX, as soon as DDS reports them Paid on the due date. They do not wait for the FT reference (decision of 06-Oct-2026, see A8 ). Mandates, subscriptions, and rejected or failed collections never go to APEX. They go in a PTLF file only . There is no real-time API call to APEX for Direct Debit (agreed with APEX, 06-Oct-2026): payment confirmation arrives at cut-off times, so there is nothing to show in real time, and a record sent by API could be picked up early by same-day settlement. Every record that arrives in the file is treated by APEX as matched , which makes it ready for pricing. APEX prices it, groups it and includes it in the merchant’s normal payout. Bank statement reconciliation is not part of this flow. Matching the credit on Geidea’s bank statement to a collection by FT reference is an offline Finance/FinOps activity (open item, A13 ). FT reference in APEX. The FT reference arrives the day after the record is already in APEX. Adding it to that record needs a change request with APEX’s vendor (Emcrey), which APEX will raise once Product documents the requirement. If that is delayed, the DD Backend provides FinOps with an extract of paid collections and their FT references. Merchant Portal shows payout status for collected amounts, taken from APEX’s existing inquiry API. No new APEX work is needed for this. To agree with the MP and Backend teams: whether the Portal calls APEX directly in real time, or the DD Backend reads APEX on a schedule and serves the Portal. Merchant Portal “Transactions” tab. APEX will add a filter to exclude Direct Debit records (FR.03 of APEX’s requirements), and the Portal must apply it, so that Direct Debit records do not appear among card transactions. Direct Debit records are shown in the Direct Debit screens. Once a month Finance reconciles DDS’s invoice against the Direct Debit transactions in APEX."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A7. End-to-end flows","Document","Flow 4 — Failed collections","direct-debit/docs/product.html#flow-4-failed-collections","Retry. The merchant can retry a rejected collection up to 3 times, but only before that collection’s retry deadline. The deadline exists because a late successful retry would sit too close to the next collection and DDS would refuse it. Terminal refusals. Some reasons (account closed, card expired, mandate cancelled, account blocked) mean the mandate can never be collected again. The collection fails at once and Geidea closes the contract. Automatic rollover. If the contract allows it, a finally failed amount is added to the next scheduled collection, within the contract’s maximum and a limit on consecutive rollovers. Amend. The merchant can change the date or amount of any future collection within the signed limits. This is also how a merchant recovers an amount that could not be collected or rolled over. Proof of failure. The bank’s bounce memo can be downloaded for a rejected collection."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A8. Settlement and merchant payout timing","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a8.-settlement-and-merchant-payout-timing","Decided (Business, 06-Oct-2026): the merchant is settled on T+1, even if Geidea receives the Direct Debit funds later. What follows from the decision: A collection is sent to APEX when DDS reports it Paid on the due date. It does not wait for the FT reference, which comes the next day. The FT reference is not needed for pricing or payout . It is used by FinOps to reconcile the credits Geidea receives in its settlement account. The credit normally reaches Geidea’s bank account the same evening (20:00 to midnight on the debit day). The payout does not depend on it. To carry the FT reference on the APEX record, APEX must be able to update a record that already exists. This is a change request with APEX’s vendor (Emcrey); APEX will raise it once Product documents the requirement. If it is delayed, the DD Backend gives FinOps an extract of paid collections with their FT references. How APEX decides the payout day Each MID has one payout frequency (T+X) . It applies to every record on the MID, Direct Debit included. It cannot be set per payment method today. X is counted from the match date : the day the PTLF file is loaded in APEX, not the day of the debit. APEX loads PTLF files by about 06:30 and runs pricing and grouping until about 07:15. A file loaded by then is in that day’s normal run. What this means for the APEX file When the Direct Debit file reaches APEX Result for a merchant on daily payout After the 18:30 status check on D and before APEX’s 06:30 load on D+1 Paid in the normal payout on D+1. This is the target Later on D+1 Misses the normal run. Paid that day only with a top-up pricing and a second payout, otherwise on D+2. To be avoided A collection that DDS reports as Paid only at a later check goes into the next file. Direct Debit is paid together with the merchant’s other payment methods. Holding the file a day, which was considered for paying Direct Debit a day later than eCommerce, is no longer needed. For a MID that is not on daily payout, APEX applies the MID’s T+X to Direct Debit as well. How T+1 is then achieved for Direct Debit (for example a payout frequency at scheme level, if APEX confirms it exists) is to be confirmed with APEX ( A13 item 2). Still to settle with APEX: the exact send time of the file, and how APEX counts the payout day for a file loaded on the evening of D compared with one loaded early on D+1 ( A13 item 1). For the build: the APEX file send time must be a configurable Backend setting ( S1 section 5.4 , S12 ). It falls after the 18:30 status check on the due date and before APEX’s 06:30 load the next day."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a9.-merchant-enablement-and-configuration",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","Document","Step 1 — Onboarding in MA","direct-debit/docs/product.html#step-1-onboarding-in-ma","Direct Debit is selected as a product for the MID in MA. It sits on the merchant’s CNP (eCommerce) MID ; there is no separate Direct Debit MID type. Compliance review. Any MID that is assigned the Direct Debit product goes to Compliance for review, whatever its MCC. There is no MCC restriction in the system; Education and Real Estate are the commercial focus. Direct Debit pricing and the payout frequency are set up. Pricing is a flat fee per collection for now; MA provisions both a flat fee and a percentage for Direct Debit. The payout frequency is the MID’s own; no separate Direct Debit settlement setting is needed in MA. MA sends the MID, its products, pricing and payout frequency to APEX. This is the existing process; Direct Debit is one more product in it."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","Document","Step 2 — Direct Debit settings in PGW Config","direct-debit/docs/product.html#step-2-direct-debit-settings-in-pgw-config","Once the Direct Debit product is assigned to the MID, these settings become available in the merchant’s PGW Config in MA. They are set by Geidea, not by the merchant. Setting Default What it does Enable Direct Debit Off The master switch. On: the Direct Debit menu appears in the merchant’s portal and the Direct Debit APIs work for this MID. Off: the menu is hidden and every Direct Debit action is blocked. Switching it off also pauses every active subscription for the merchant; collections already sent to DDS still complete. Switching it back on does not resume them; the merchant resumes each one OIC — Which originator code the merchant’s contracts are registered under. Today always Geidea’s. Each contract keeps the OIC it was created with Disable Credit Card Instrument Off (card allowed) On: neither the merchant nor the customer can choose Credit Card; only Bank Account is offered. Used while the card-number question in A3 is open. Affects new contracts only Maximum Contract Amount AED 100,000,000 The highest “maximum amount per collection” a contract may carry for this merchant. Set by Risk. It is checked per contract; there is no cap across all of a merchant’s contracts Minimum first-collection lead time 4 working days The first collection must be at least this far after the contract is created. It leaves time for the customer to sign and the bank to approve, so the first collection is not missed Contract review expiry 7 days How long the customer’s review link stays valid. The link expires at the end of its last day; after that it shows “expired” at once, and the unsigned contract is closed by a daily job Suppress Geidea customer notifications Off Phase 2 (partner model). The switch exists in the data model now and stays Off; it is not shown in PGW Config in the MVP. When On, Geidea does not send the signing-request or activation messages, because an API partner sends the review link itself. The review and signing page is still Geidea’s, and Geidea still sends the one-time password for it Enable Bulk Contract Upload Off On: the bulk upload option appears in the merchant’s portal Controlling who may do what is a per-user matter and is not part of PGW Config, which is per MID. See step 3."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","Document","Backend configuration (Geidea only)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#backend-configuration-geidea-only","Separate from the merchant settings, the DD Backend holds configuration that applies to all merchants: Item Why it is needed DDS login per OIC Every call to DDS is made with the login of the OIC a contract belongs to. Kept in the secrets vault Card encryption key For sending a card number to DDS in encrypted form, once DDS supports it (open item) Bank list The banks a merchant or customer can pick. DDS matches the bank by its exact name and offers no API for the list, so Geidea keeps its own copy. If a name is missing or spelled differently, DDS rejects the contract. It must be updated whenever DDS adds or removes a bank UAE working-day calendar Weekends and UAE public holidays. Several rules count in working days, and the Backend cannot apply them without it: the first collection must be at least 4 working days out; the bank has up to 3 working days to approve; an unsigned mandate expires after 7 working days; a retry is processed the next working day, which sets each collection’s retry deadline; and a debit due on a non-working day moves to the next working day. A wrong or out-of-date calendar leads to missed first collections and retries refused by DDS. Public holidays must be added as they are announced Frequency and minimum-gap table DDS’s rule for how close two debits may be. Held as configuration because DDS can change the numbers Job schedule The times of the daily jobs, set within DDS’s own cut-offs Terminal refusal reasons The bank reasons that end a contract (account closed, card expired and so on) Who maintains the bank list and the calendar day to day is to be agreed; the operations console ( S14 ) is the natural place to edit them."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","Document","Step 3 — User permissions in Merchant Portal","direct-debit/docs/product.html#step-3-user-permissions-in-merchant-portal","Merchant Portal has a role framework: the business administrator (the first user of the business, covering all its stores) creates roles, ticks permissions per module, and assigns roles to users. Permissions there are per action; for example Export Transactions is separate from View Transactions. Direct Debit adds its own permission group to this framework, visible only when Direct Debit is enabled for the business. Proposed permissions (07-Oct-2026). The final list is to be agreed with the MP team. Permission Allows View Direct Debit See the menu, KPIs, contracts, collections and history; download the mandate document and proof of failure Export Contracts Export the contract list Create Contract Create a single contract Manage Unsigned Contract Resend the signing link, discard an unsigned contract, edit notes Pause / Resume Subscription Pause and resume Amend Collection Change the date or amount of future collections Retry Collection Retry a rejected collection Cancel Contract Cancel an active contract and resend the customer’s cancellation link Bulk upload — maker Upload a bulk file and submit it for approval Bulk upload — checker Approve or reject a bulk file uploaded by someone else Every action permission requires View. The checks are made on every request, so hiding a button is never the only control. The functional requirement is in B15 and the technical story is S13."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","Document","Store selection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#store-selection","In Merchant Portal each MID is a store, and a merchant with several branches has several. Store selection is an existing Portal feature: by default a screen covers all the stores the user can access, and the user can narrow it to one or several with the store selector. Direct Debit works the same way: the contract list, KPIs, search and export cover the stores selected, added together. A contract belongs to exactly one MID, so creating a contract and bulk upload are for one store, and an action on a contract is checked against that contract’s store. See B16."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A9. Merchant enablement and configuration","Document","Geidea support view","direct-debit/docs/product.html#geidea-support-view","Support and call-centre staff today search a MID in MA and view the merchant’s portal as the merchant sees it. Direct Debit needs the same so that staff can answer merchant queries about contracts, collections and payouts. The design (view-only or with actions, which roles, audit) is an open item. See B17."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A10. User journeys","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a10.-user-journeys","These replace the nine journeys in the earlier document. The earlier ones were checked against the current design and differ in these ways: they treated one-time and recurring mandates as separate journeys (it is one guided flow); the customer journey had no identity verification step; the recovery journey offered “combine billing” as a merchant choice (rollover is automatic; the merchant’s tool is Amend); and settlement was stated as T+1 (still open)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 1 — Enable a merchant","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-1-enable-a-merchant","# Who What happens 1 Sales / Onboarding Selects Direct Debit as a product for the merchant’s CNP MID in MA 2 Compliance Reviews the MID 3 Onboarding Sets Direct Debit pricing and payout frequency 4 MA Sends MID, product and pricing to APEX 5 Geidea admin Switches on Enable Direct Debit in PGW Config and sets the other settings 6 Merchant admin Assigns Direct Debit permissions to users in Merchant Portal"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 2 — Create a contract","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-2-create-a-contract","# Who What happens 1 Merchant user Selects the store, opens Direct Debit, starts a new contract 2 Merchant user Enters the customer’s name, Emirates ID, mobile, email, a merchant reference, a customer-facing description and optional internal notes 3 Merchant user Enters the bank account or card, or ticks “To Be Filled By Customer” 4 Merchant user Sets validity dates, Fixed or Variable amount, minimum and maximum, frequency ceiling 5 Merchant user Sets the first collection date, the collection frequency and amount, and rollover rules 6 Portal Shows every planned collection; the merchant can adjust individual dates and amounts within the rules 7 Merchant user Reviews and sends 8 DD Backend Validates, saves, registers the mandate with DDS (unless TBFC), sends the customer the review link 9 Portal Shows the contract as Pending, or Awaiting Customer Details for TBFC"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 3 — Customer reviews and signs","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-3-customer-reviews-and-signs","# Who What happens 1 Customer Receives the link by SMS, email and WhatsApp 2 Customer Opens the link. Sees the verification screen: Geidea and DDS logos, merchant name, contract description, own name, masked Emirates ID, email and mobile 3 Customer Chooses to receive a one-time password by SMS or email, and enters it 4 Customer Reviews the contract terms and the full collection schedule 5 Customer (TBFC only) Chooses bank account or card and enters the details. The mandate is registered with DDS at this point 6 Customer Signs with UAE PASS. The bank details are checked against Central Bank records during signing 7 Customer Sees the result and can download the signed mandate 8 Bank Approves or rejects within 3 working days 9 DD Backend Activates the subscription when the mandate is Active; the customer gets a confirmation message If the link expires (default 7 days), the contract is closed and the merchant creates a new one. The customer has no decline button in the MVP: a customer who does not want to sign simply ignores the link."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 4 — Collections","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-4-collections","# Who What happens 1 DD Backend The evening before each due date, sends the collection to DDS 2 Bank Debits the customer on the due date 3 DD Backend Fetches the result; the Portal shows Paid or Rejected, with the reason 4 DD Backend Sends the paid collection to APEX the same day. Next morning, records the FT reference and the actual debit date 5 APEX Prices, groups and pays the merchant 6 Merchant user Sees payout status on the contract detail page"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 5 — Recover a failed collection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-5-recover-a-failed-collection","# Who What happens 1 Merchant user Sees a Rejected collection, its reason and its retry deadline 2 Merchant user Retries (up to 3 times, before the deadline) 3 DD Backend If retries run out or the deadline passes, marks the collection Failed 4 DD Backend If the contract allows rollover, adds the amount to the next collection; otherwise the reason is shown 5 Merchant user May amend a later collection to recover the amount; may download the bounce memo 6 Merchant user A collection in Error (payment validation error) is not retried: the merchant re-sets it with Amend schedule (new date, and amount if Variable); it is sent again as a fresh payment with a new payment reference"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 6 — Manage a contract","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-6-manage-a-contract","# Who What happens 1 Merchant user Pauses a subscription; collections that fall due while paused are skipped. Resumes it later 2 Merchant user Amends the date or amount of future collections, within the signed limits 3 Merchant user Resends the signing link, or discards a contract the customer has not signed 4 Merchant user Edits internal notes 5 Merchant user Cancels an active contract with a reason (Cancel Contract permission); optionally pauses collections at the same time. The customer signs the cancellation with UAE PASS; once the bank accepts, the contract is DD Cancelled Collections continue until the customer signs the cancellation; to stop them at once the merchant pauses the subscription. A cancellation request cannot be withdrawn."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 7 — Bulk upload","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-7-bulk-upload","# Who What happens 1 Maker Chooses the Standard or TBFC template, uploads the file 2 DD Backend Checks the file as it is read; a file that fails cannot be submitted 3 Maker Reviews the rows and submits the file for approval 4 Checker Reviews the file and approves or rejects it with a reason 5 DD Backend Creates a contract per row and reports the outcome of each row"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A10. User journeys","Document","Journey 8 — Support","direct-debit/docs/product.html#journey-8-support","# Who What happens 1 Support agent Searches the MID in MA and opens the merchant’s Direct Debit view (design open) 2 Support agent Sees contracts, collections, failure reasons and payout status, and answers the merchant"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A11. Scope and phasing","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a11.-scope-and-phasing","Phase Included MVP Merchants using Geidea’s Merchant Portal. Enablement and settings; user permissions; create contract (bank account and card); customer review and signing; activation; daily collection cycle; retry, automatic rollover, bounce memo; pause/resume, amend, discard, notes, cancellation; portfolio, contract detail, export, activity history; bulk upload (Standard and TBFC); in-portal notifications and the event store; APEX file and payout status; operations console (minimum scope to be agreed, S14 ). Individual customers only. Launch with a small number of MIDs Post-MVP Contract cancellation (contract cancellation moved into the MVP, 15-Oct-2026) ; Geidea support view if not delivered with MVP Phase 2 To Be Filled By Customer (TBFC) (moved 18-Oct-2026): the customer enters the instrument on the review page, and TBFC bulk upload. Partner (API) model: partners creating and managing contracts from their own systems through Geidea’s API; review link delivered to the partner; webhooks; the Suppress Geidea customer notifications setting; partner access to the operations console for its own OIC; co-branded review page. Also: joint accounts; non-individual (company) customers; merchant-owned or partner-owned OIC Removed Manual rollover of skipped collections and its undo. Replaced by Amend"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A11. Scope and phasing","Document","Provisions for the Phase 2 partner model","direct-debit/docs/product.html#provisions-for-the-phase-2-partner-model","The partner model is not built in the MVP. In it, a partner runs its own merchant-facing system and uses Geidea’s Direct Debit engine through an API; the customer still reviews and signs on Geidea’s page. The current build must follow the principles below, so that Phase 2 can be added on top without changing the foundations. # What the current build must do Why it matters for Phase 2 1 Every business rule and permission is enforced in the DD Backend, never only in Merchant Portal A partner’s system will call the same APIs without the Portal in front 2 The Merchant Portal uses the DD Backend only through its documented, versioned APIs ( /direct-debit/v1/... ), with the merchant identified by MID. No Portal-only shortcuts or direct database access The same APIs become the partner API without being redesigned 3 Schedule generation, validation and status logic live in the Backend; the Portal only displays what the Backend returns A partner must get the same results without re-implementing the logic 4 Every event is written to the event store from day one, including contract created (with the review link and its expiry) and link resent Webhooks are then only a delivery mechanism added on top 5 Customer messages (signing request, resend, activation) are sent by one Backend component, behind a per-merchant switch that exists in the data model from the start (default: Geidea sends) Phase 2 turns the switch on for a partner who sends its own SMS and email; nothing else changes 6 The customer review and signing page is a standalone Geidea page reached by link, not tied to a Merchant Portal session, and takes the merchant name and contract description from data. The one-time password is always sent by Geidea The page stays Geidea’s for partners; the link is simply delivered by someone else. Co-branding can be added later 7 The review link is returned in the create-contract response, whoever sends it to the customer The partner needs it from the API 8 Every contract carries its OIC; DDS logins, payment files, job runs and batches are all kept per OIC Allows a partner or merchant with its own OIC, and operations-console access limited to that OIC 9 The permission check accepts a caller that is not a Portal user ( S13 section 3 ) A partner authenticates with API credentials for a MID 10 Errors are returned as machine-readable codes with field-level detail A partner’s system must act on them without a screen These are repeated for the build teams in Part C, section C5."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A12. Operations and support","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a12.-operations-and-support","DDS’s own support runs Monday to Friday, 09:00 to 17:00, with escalation after 24 and 48 hours. Geidea does not pass this on to merchants; Geidea’s support is the merchant’s single contact. Area Requirement Merchant support front line Geidea Support owns merchant communication and tickets, including incidents that need DDS Operations console Direct Debit runs on scheduled jobs against fixed DDS cut-offs. An internal console is proposed (story S14 , for discussion) that shows every job run, its batch and its records, flags a job that failed or did not run, emails a group mailbox, and lets an authorised user re-run a job or upload a payment file by hand. In Phase 2, partners integrated by API may be given access limited to their own OIC, so every view and action is kept per OIC from the start Operational alerts Payment file not accepted by 19:45, whole-file rejection, a job that did not run, missed submissions, a contract-creation call with an unknown outcome, a payment with no FT reference after 7 days, reconciliation mismatches. Each alert must lead to an action in the console Reference data The bank list and the UAE working-day calendar are held in Backend configuration ( A9 ) and must be kept current Playbooks Mandate rejection, failed collection, retry, terminal refusal, reconciliation mismatch, payout delay, customer who does not recognise “Geidea” on their statement Business continuity Manual procedure for payment-file upload and status checks through DDS’s portal if the integration is down If the integration is down: re-run through the API once restored; DDS’s portal is read-only for an API client (monitoring only), so there is no manual upload — escalate to DDS Messaging Merchant-facing material must not promise guaranteed payment; collections depend on funds and the bank"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A13. Risks and open items","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a13.-risks-and-open-items",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A13. Risks and open items","Document","Open items","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-items","# Item Owner 1 Payout day decided (06-Oct-2026): T+1 ( A8 ). Remaining: the exact send time of the APEX file, and how APEX counts the payout day for a file loaded on the evening of the due date compared with early the next morning APEX team, DD Backend team 2 Payout frequency is per MID in APEX (confirmed). For a MID that is not on daily payout, how T+1 is achieved for Direct Debit: whether a scheme-level T+X setting exists and works ( A8 ) APEX team 3 Store selection: Direct Debit follows the Portal’s existing multi-store selection (all stores by default, one or several selectable). Built that way for the summary screen; to be applied to every Direct Debit screen and action ( A9 , B16 ) MP team, DD Backend 4 Geidea support view of a merchant’s Direct Debit activity through MA ( A9 , B17 ) MA, MP, Support 5 Credit card number handling: DDS clear card number and encryption key. Token vault decided ( A3 ); its integration to be designed IT Security, DDS, Product 6 APEX field values still needed: terminalId, posEntryMode, destinationBin, processingCode / transaction type, static pan; and the PTLF file specification ( S12 ). PTLF-only is agreed APEX team 7 Payout status in Merchant Portal, from APEX’s existing inquiry API: the Portal calls APEX directly in real time, or the DD Backend reads APEX on a schedule and serves the Portal ( A7 , S10 ) MP team, DD Backend team 8 Operations console and job control ( S14 ): scope for go-live, where it lives, who builds and operates it; what happens to a collection whose payment file missed its date; keeping every view per OIC so that partners can be given access in Phase 2 Product, DD Backend team, Operations 9 Bulk upload: design discussion needed before development starts Product, MP, DD Backend 10 Frontend backlog corrected as “Direct Debit _ MP Backlog” v1.0 (15-Oct): missing stories added (customer review page US-33 , discard US-34 , notes US-35 , Transactions-tab exclusion US-36 , TBFC bulk US-37 , cancellation US-39 and US-40 ), built stories get change stories. To be taken through refinement and into Jira ( C3 ) MP team, DD Backend team, Delivery 11 Who keeps the bank list and the working-day calendar up to date in Backend configuration ( A9 ) Product, Operations 12 Direct Debit price points for merchants. Structure is settled: flat fee per collection for now, with flat and percentage both provisioned in MA Business, Finance 13 Answered by DDS (15-Oct-2026): no lookup by merchant reference after a timeout (confirms S2 : no automatic retry, Ops review); SUSP and DENY not supported by banks; a second payment file only in edge cases (see item 28) DDS 14 Bounce memo: DDS confirmed (15-Oct) it is available after every rejection; prove it in the first decline test DDS, QA 15 WhatsApp availability for customer messages; activation message wording; any further customer notifications — see item 25 for rejection and failure notices Tech, Business 16 Statement narration shows Geidea, not the merchant: customer communication to reduce confusion Business, Support 17 Support model, severities and playbooks ( A12 ) Operations, Support 18 Direct Debit settlement report: format and columns to be defined with FinOps and Operations. This decides what is passed to APEX in userData , which stays open until then ( S12 ). APEX will show Direct Debit as a new scheme in Report 7 and in DTR/CDTR, VAT and MSR Product, FinOps, Operations, APEX 19 Bank statement reconciliation: matching credits on Geidea’s bank statement to collections by FT reference. Offline Finance/FinOps activity, outside the Direct Debit build; Direct Debit to be added to Finance’s reconciliation reports requirement Product to raise with Finance / FinOps 20 Merchant Portal “Transactions” tab must exclude Direct Debit records, using the exclusion filter APEX is adding ( A7 ) APEX, MP team 21 DDS timings answered (08-Oct, A4 ). Still open: availability time of the Day-End Reconciliation report; whether the credit to Geidea’s account is still "],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › A13. Risks and open items","Document","Status of the open points in the earlier document","direct-debit/docs/product.html#status-of-the-open-points-in-the-earlier-document","Earlier open point Status now Regulatory / OIC model Resolved. Geidea’s OIC is the single originator. Merchant-owned OIC deferred Funds flow Resolved. Merchant payout is T+1 ( A8 ); bank statement reconciliation is with Finance (item 19). Full amount credited to Geidea’s account the evening of the debit API coverage Resolved against DDS REST API v2.0 and UAE PASS Signature API v1.4. Bounce memo untested (item 14). Cancellation in the MVP ( S8 ) Scheduling ownership Resolved. Geidea builds and owns the subscription engine and daily payment file Cut-offs / SLAs Resolved. See A4 Retry rules Resolved. 3 retries, retry deadline, terminal reasons Data model Resolved. Order model agreed; APEX mapping agreed except item 6 and userData (item 18) APEX / transaction view Mostly resolved. PTLF file only; field mapping agreed with APEX; items 6, 18 and 20 pending Fees / VAT Structure settled (flat fee, percentage provisioned); price points open (item 12) Cancellations / disputes Dispute position documented ( A4 ). Cancellation in the MVP ( S8 , item 24) . A customer cancelling at their bank is handled by design: the next collection is refused and the contract is closed Security / privacy Partly open (item 5). Card held as a token-vault token, never in full; IBAN stored and shown in full; logs masked Support Open (item 17)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part A — Direct Debit at Geidea","Document","A14. DDS go-live checklist","direct-debit/docs/product.html#a14.-dds-go-live-checklist","DDS shared its “Checklist Before Client Go-Live” (October 2026). Each item is mapped below to this document. Migration items do not apply: Geidea has no dashboard merchants to migrate. Checklist item Covered by Status Mandate creation journey S2 , S3 Covered (TBFC is Phase 2) Discard journey (contract in draft) S7 section 3 (merchant discard; DDS Discard for Pending and Request Expired) Covered: merchant discard; no customer-side discard in the MVP — DDS leaves it to Geidea ( A13 item 26) IBAN and signing; hint for resetting the UAE PASS signing password S3 section 7.4 (IBAN failure); B5 (hint) Covered Cancellation journey; the customer signs the cancellation form; tell the customer the cancellation is not effective until signed S8 Covered: in the MVP; customer signs on Geidea’s page with UAE PASS. The request email and the cancellation page say the cancellation does not take effect until the customer signs ( S8 sections 3.3 , 3.5 and 3.7 ) Mandate request expiry S3 section 10 (link expiry, DDS Discard); S4 ( RQXP ) Covered Payment journey; payment status responses S5 Covered Representment and bounce memo S6 Covered; bounce memo after every rejection (DDS); to prove in the first decline test ( A13 item 14) UAE PASS errors; retry signing without a new mandate S3 section 7 Covered: “Try again” on the same mandate while the link is valid; IBAN mismatch needs a new contract All due payments in one file; status by file ID S5 Covered: one accepted file per OIC per day (DDS-confirmed) File sent before 20:00 on the day before; no amend API for payments S5 ; S7 section 2 (Amend locked 18:45 the day before) Covered Payment status after 18:00 on the due date and after 06:00 the next day S5 Covered: 18:30 and 06:30 Mandate status once a day after 18:00 until Active or Rejected S4 Covered: 18:15 daily Statuses and rejection reasons for payments and mandates, to merchant and customer S10 , S11 Merchant covered; customer notices are Geidea’s design, solution pending ( A13 item 25) Scheduling on time and in bulk; only Active mandates in the file S5 Covered File accepted only if every row is valid; remove or correct and re-upload S5 Covered Mandate status once a day for up to 3 days after signing S4 Covered: checked daily until final — DDS confirmed (15-Oct) some banks take 3 to 4 days Prevent duplicate mandates; use Discard for unsigned ones S2 (reference unique across the OIC, with the Geidea reference code), S3 section 10 , S7 section 3 Covered Retry or recreate after an IBAN error S3 section 7.4 , S7 section 3 Covered: discard and create a new contract Clear message for a signing failure; IBAN validation error returned in the redirect S3 section 7 Covered: messages mapped from UAE PASS Signature API v1.4 Representment up to 3 times; check status again afterwards S6 Covered (Representment Pending tracked) A payment in Error (frequency mismatch) cannot be represented until corrected S5 , S7 , B8 Covered: no representment; merchant re-sets the collection with Amend and it is sent as a fresh payment with a new reference (DDS-confirmed 15-Oct)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Part B — Functional Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#part-b-functional-requirements","This part states what Geidea’s Direct Debit implementation must do at the functional level: screens, business rules, data captured, validations, statuses and timing. It is the updated version of “Direct Debit Functional Scope” (section 5), first published on 07 September 2026. Section numbers B1 to B14 match the earlier 5.1 to 5.14; B15 to B18 are new. Endpoint, parameter and error-code detail is in Part C. How to read the changes Blue text is new or changed since the 07-Sep-2026 version. Red strikethrough is superseded by a later decision. It is kept only so the team can see what changed, and is removed at the next clean publish. Black text is unchanged. Summary of changes since 07-Sep-2026 Section What changed B2 Onboarding in MA, Compliance review for every MID with Direct Debit, product and pricing sent to APEX. Flat fee now, percentage provisioned. Merchant settings defined. Action controls are user permissions ( B15 ), not PGW Config. Credit Card allowed by default with a per-merchant disable switch. B3 Bank list maintained by Geidea. Merchant reference unique across the OIC. Minimum-gap table. First collection at least 4 working days out. Amend replaces “edit after activation”. Discard. What happens when registration with DDS fails. Card-number handling open. B4 Error status added. Suspended moved to Phase 2. Geidea-only statuses. Daily status check. One status list for the Portal, with DDS statuses tagged DD. B5 TBFC resolved. Verification screen and one-time password by SMS or email. Link expiry (end of day, daily job) and resend. One mandate document for the merchant, unsigned then signed. B6 Completed status. Skip reasons. Manual rollover no longer the recovery route. B7 Daily timeline aligned to DDS’s recommended check times: status check 18:00, FT reference 06:30 next day. APEX file built from the Paid status on the due date. Whole-file rejection and manual fallback. Job control. Minimum-gap safety net. B8 Retry deadline. Terminal refusals close the contract. Five rollover states. Manual rollover removed from MVP. Bounce memo. B9 Cancellation is post-MVP. B10 PTLF file only to APEX; field values agreed with APEX, a few still to come; merchant payout is T+1 and the file is sent on the Paid status without waiting for the FT reference; payout status from APEX’s existing inquiry API; bank statement reconciliation outside the build; Transactions tab must exclude Direct Debit. B11 to B13 Notification suppression, event list, no full card number or IBAN stored, MID as the only merchant identifier. Partner (API) model is Phase 2: B13 lists what the current build must provision; the review and signing page is always Geidea’s. B14 Standard and TBFC bulk flows, validation at upload, maker/checker with reject. Design discussion needed before development. B15 to B18 New: user permissions (per action, in the Portal’s role framework), multi-store selection, Geidea support view, open items. Contract status lifecycle (see B4 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B1. Scope & Actors","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b1.-scope-actors","Geidea owns the merchant-facing product: enablement, mandate/subscription management, payer signing hand-off, collection orchestration, transaction visibility, reconciliation and merchant payout. Direct Debit System FZ LLC (branded Direct Debit / DDS / UAEDDS / “DDMP”) performs the scheme-facing processing: mandate submission to the Central Bank and payer bank, UAE PASS signing, payment execution, and settlement crediting. Direct Debit is not a payment method inside Geidea’s payment gateway — it never touches MPGS or CyberSource. It is a separate integration, built and owned by Backend independently of the PGW rails. Terminology. “Contract” = the DDS mandate plus its Geidea subscription and collection schedule. “Collection” = one scheduled debit (Occurrence in the Order Model). “Payment” = one attempt at DDS for a collection; retries re-use the same Payment."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B2. Merchant Enablement","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b2.-merchant-enablement","OIC Model : Geidea itself is the Central Bank–onboarded entity (OIC 560000001). Individual sub-merchants are not separately onboarded with the Central Bank — Geidea manages sub-merchant identification and settlement splitting entirely internally Every merchant is assigned an OIC in its DD configuration. In Phase 1 only Geidea-owned OICs can be assigned; merchant-owned OICs can be recorded but not assigned until their settlement/invoicing flow is designed. Each contract keeps the OIC it was created under. Changing a merchant’s OIC affects new contracts only. DDS login credentials are held per OIC (test and production), in the secrets vault. Admin can test them without creating anything. Merchant Account : Direct Debit will be positioned as a payment method within the Payment Gateway product Feature Toggle : Used to enable/disable Direct Debit as an option in merchant portal. Further user level control to allow/restrict specific features i.e. create contract, cancel contract, pause subscription, retry occurrence & rollover occurrence contract actions (create, resend signing link, discard, edit notes), subscription actions (pause/resume, amend collection, retry collection) and bulk upload (maker and checker as separate permissions) . Encryption key input for handling card data transmission. These action controls are user-level permissions managed in Merchant Portal user management (owned by the MP team), not merchant-level (MID) settings in PGW Config. See B15. “Rollover occurrence” is removed because manual rollover is out of MVP ( B8 ); “cancel contract” is post-MVP ( B9 ). Every Backend endpoint enforces the permission, even where the portal already hides the button, so the control holds for any caller (in Phase 2, partners call the same API). Onboarding in MA Direct Debit is selected as a product for the MID during onboarding in MA (Merchant Account). DD uses the merchant’s CNP (eCommerce) MID ; there is no separate DD MID type. Assigning the DD product sends the MID for Compliance review , whatever its MCC. There is no MCC restriction in the system. DD pricing is set up in MA. MA sends the MID, its enabled products, pricing and payout frequency to APEX through the existing process. Only when the DD product is assigned do the DD settings below become available in the merchant’s PGW Config. Payout frequency is held per MID in APEX and applies to every record on the MID, Direct Debit included (confirmed with APEX, 06-Oct-2026). Merchant payout for Direct Debit is T+1 (Business decision, 06-Oct-2026; see B10 ). For a MID that is not on daily payout, how APEX applies T+1 to Direct Debit is to be confirmed with APEX. No separate Direct Debit settlement setting is needed in MA. Direct Debit pricing is a flat fee per collection for now, separate from the existing per-transaction fee. MA provisions both a flat fee and a percentage for Direct Debit."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › B2. Merchant Enablement","Document","Merchant Direct Debit configuration (per MID)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-direct-debit-configuration-per-mid","Setting Default Rule Enable Direct Debit No Master switch. No = DD menu hidden and every DD API blocked for the merchant. Switching to No also pauses every Active subscription ; collections already sent to DDS carry on. Switching back to Yes does not auto-resume — the merchant resumes each one. OIC — Required when DD is enabled. Must be an Active, Geidea-owned OIC. Disable Credit Card Instrument No (card allowed) Yes = Credit Card cannot be chosen by the merchant or the customer (TBFC). Used per merchant while card-number encryption with DDS is open (DDS today only accepts a clear card number — a PCI issue). Affects new contracts only. Launch is planned with a small number of MIDs. Maximum Contract Amount AED 100,000,000 Set by Risk, one flat number per merchant, capped at the DDS scheme limit of AED 100M. Checked per contract (max amount per collection); no aggregate cap across a merchant’s contracts. Lowering it does not affect existing contracts. Minimum first-collection lead time 4 working days 0–30. The first collection must be at least this many working days after contract creation, to allow for bank approval (up to 3 working days) and the 20:00 file cut-off. Contract review expiry 7 days 1–30. How long the customer’s review link stays valid. Suppress Geidea customer notifications No Phase 2 (partner model, B13 ). Exists in the data model now, stays No, and is not shown in PGW Config in the MVP. Yes = Geidea does not send the signing-request or activation messages; the partner sends the review link itself. The review page stays Geidea’s, and Geidea still sends its one-time password. Enable Bulk Contract Upload No Shows/blocks bulk upload ( B14 ). Every configuration change is audited with old and new values."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › B2. Merchant Enablement","Document","Reference data held by Geidea","direct-debit/docs/product.html#reference-data-held-by-geidea","Banks list — held in DD Backend configuration (DDS has no banks API). Bank names must match DDS’s spelling exactly, or DDS rejects the contract. It is updated whenever DDS publishes a new spec version. Frequency / minimum-gap table — held as configuration (see B3 ), since DDS may change the numbers. UAE working-day calendar — weekends and public holidays, held in DD Backend configuration. The Backend needs it for every rule that counts working days: the first-collection lead time, the bank’s 3-working-day approval, the 7-working-day signing limit, the retry deadline, and debits that move off a non-working day. Public holidays must be added as they are announced. Scheduler times — held as configuration (see B7 ). Terminal refusal reason codes — held as configuration (see B8 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B3. Mandate Creation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b3.-mandate-creation","Contract creation and the subscription schedule must be captured in a single guided flow Data Captured : Customer identity and contact: full name, email, UAE mobile number, customer type (Individual only for phase-1), and identity document (Emirates ID for individuals; trade licence detail for non-individuals, once that journey is in scope). Formats: mobile 05XXXXXXXX; Emirates ID 15 digits starting 784; name max 100 characters / 7 words. Merchant reference number: Unique per merchant. Mandatory, 3–26 characters. Checked by Geidea before DDS is called, across all merchants under the same OIC (DDS enforces the reference per OIC). A reference stays “used” even after its contract is discarded or cancelled — a new contract needs a new reference. The merchant reference is sent to DDS as the DDA reference number: it is the contract’s only business reference, and there is no separate Geidea contract reference. It is printed on the signed mandate form (“Consumer/Premises/etc”) and is the base of every payment reference ( B7 ). Geidea reference code (15-Oct-2026). So that references stay unique across all merchants under Geidea’s OIC while the merchant keeps most of the field, the DD Backend gives each merchant a 5-digit code when Direct Debit is enabled for it, and puts it in front of the reference the merchant types (e.g. 48213-UNIT304 ). The full value is what goes to DDS, the signed form, the payment reference and APEX; uniqueness then only has to be checked within the merchant. The merchant’s part is capped so the total stays within 26 (the input field stops accepting characters at the cap). Pending DD Backend team: one code per merchant or per MID; whether a separator is used (cap 20 or 21); how the code is kept unique when it is generated. Notes - a free-text field visible to the merchant only, never shown to the customer and never sent to DDS. Max 1,000 characters; belongs to the contract (mandate) and can be updated at any time, in any status. Contract Description - a short, plain-language description of what the contract is for (e.g. “Monthly rent collection - Building 12, Unit 304”). This is customer-facing: it is shown on the review-and-sign page, because otherwise the only context the customer has for what they are signing is the merchant’s name and a reference number that means nothing to them. Distinct from Notes above. Max 500 characters; also used in the signing notification. Never sent to DDS. Payment instrument: Bank Account (bank selected from a live-fetched bank list - this list changes over time and must not be hard-coded or cached Geidea’s maintained banks list — see B2 ; DDS offers no banks API ) or UAE-issued Credit Card (unless the merchant’s Disable Credit Card Instrument setting is on) . Non-UAE instruments are out of scope. Card data encryption for maintaining within Geidea as well as while sending to DD. Card-number handling is an open item: DDS accepts only a clear card number today, and using Geidea’s token vault to hold the card number securely is to be explored ( B12 , B18 ). Validity window (commencement/expiry), amount type (fixed/variable), min/max amount, and frequency — both the mandate’s frequency ceiling and the subscription’s actual collection frequency Phase 2 (decided 18-Oct-2026): TBFC is not built in this phase; the merchant always enters the instrument. Merchant can choose to have the instrument data entered by the customer when opening the contract weblink, reviewing contract detail, inputting instrument detail and then proceed towards contract creation. Under this “To Be Filled By Customer” (TBFC) option the merchant makes no instrument choice at all — the customer chooses Bank Account or Credit Card as well as entering the details (see B5 ). Retries are not an input — the cap is fixed at 3 re-presentations by regulation ( B8 ). Amount Types & Limits : Fixed - every installment is identical (within Min & Max); changing the amount requires a new contract. Rule: amount = min = max, every c"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B4. Mandate Status Lifecycle","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b4.-mandate-status-lifecycle","Ten Eleven statuses exist at DDS. The business-facing label and the raw status code must both be tracked - support and reconciliation need the raw code while merchants should only ever see the business label. Business label Code Meaning Geidea handling Pending PNDG Created, awaiting the payer’s review/signature. View/remind/ cancel discard only. No collections/occurrences created at this point Schedule exists locally (collections Scheduled); nothing is sent for collection until Active. Denied DENY Customer actively declined to sign. Terminal. Show reason; merchant needs to create a new mandate. Subscription and pending collections Cancelled. Not used: DDS confirmed (15-Oct-2026) that banks do not support DENY. Request Expired RQXP No customer action within 7 working days. Terminal. Merchant needs to create a new mandate. Geidea discards it at DDS. Subscription and pending collections Cancelled. Pending Bank System Submission SUBP Signed via UAE PASS; moving into Central Bank/payer-bank submission. In progress. No collections/occurrences created at this point Nothing sent for collection yet. Pending Bank Approval APRP Awaiting the payer bank’s decision (up to 3 working days) — most banks within 1 working day, some 3 to 4, and one or two wait for a confirmation call with the customer (DDS, 15-Oct-2026). Geidea keeps checking until a final status (ACCP, RJCT, ERR); there is no time limit. In progress ; show ageing . No collections/occurrences created at this point Nothing sent for collection yet. Rejected RJCT Rejected by validation, the Central Bank, or the payer bank, with a reason code. Show reason to merchant in plain language; allow correction via a new mandate. Plain labels held for each RR reason code (e.g. RR05 “Account name doesn’t match”). Subscription and pending collections Cancelled. Active ACCP Mandate fully approved and operational. Enables subscription activation, payment-file inclusion, and collection. Central Bank reference stored. Customer activation message sent ( B11 ). Suspended SUSP Payments cannot be collected against this mandate. Contract suspended by the Issuer Bank Not used: DDS confirmed (15-Oct-2026) that banks do not support SUSP. If ever seen, log and alert Ops. Cancelled CANC Cancelled by the merchant, possibly at the customer’s request. Terminal. Disable future collections; preserve history. Payments already sent to DDS continue to be tracked. Expired EXPR Validity window has passed. Terminal. Disable future collections; preserve history. Subscription Completed. Error ERR Error at DDS / bank. Final — the contract’s life ends. Merchant and Ops see the status and error; merchant creates a new contract. Alert Ops. Geidea-only statuses (exist before or instead of a DDS status): Label When Awaiting Customer Details TBFC contract created; customer has not yet entered the instrument. Nothing exists at DDS. Shown on the contract list as well as detail. Link Expired Review link expired before the customer finished. The link stops working at once; a daily job closes the contract. If the mandate was already at DDS, Geidea discards it there. Discarded Merchant discarded an unsigned contract ( B3 ). Cancelled — <reason> Closed by Geidea after a terminal refusal, e.g. “Cancelled — Account closed” ( B8 ). Not cancelled at DDS. Cancellation Pending A cancellation request is open for an Active mandate: awaiting the customer’s signature, or with the bank ( B9 ). The DDS status is still Active. How status is learned. DDS does not push mandate status changes to Geidea. Geidea checks every mandate still on its way to activation (PNDG / SUBP / APRP) once a day at 18:15 (after the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off) — timed so that approvals seen today go into tonight’s 19:00 payment file — plus Active mandates the day after their end date, to record EXPR. Every status change is kept in a status history. One status for the Portal (07-Oct-2026). A contract has exactly one status, taken from one list: the DDS statuses above ca"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B5. Contract Signing via UAE Pass","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b5.-contract-signing-via-uae-pass","Geidea sends its own signing notification by SMS, Whatsapp & email only. DDS notifications will be supressed. Messages are in Arabic and English and carry the merchant name, contract description, review link and link expiry date. If WhatsApp is not technically available, Tech must confirm that explicitly before it is dropped. In Phase 2, a partner with Suppress Geidea customer notifications on sends the review link itself ( B13 ). The customer opens a Geidea-hosted link, reviews the mandate and the collection schedule together, accepts T&Cs and signs using UAE PASS. The digital signature certificate is valid 3 years; an expired or invalid certificate requires re-verification before signing can proceed. Verification before any contract data is shown (25-Sep-2026). Anyone holding the link (a forwarded SMS, a shared phone) must not be able to see contract or bank details. The link first opens a verification screen showing the Geidea and DDS logos, the merchant name (needed because DDS always shows Geidea, via its OIC), the contract description, the customer name, and masked Emirates ID, email and mobile. The customer chooses to receive a one-time password by SMS or email , enters it, and only then sees the contract. Applies to every review link. OTP length, validity, resend and lock-out limits are configurable (proposed: 6 digits, 5 minutes, lock after repeated failures). Link states. The link shows one of: no longer available (discarded, cancelled, rejected, error), already signed, expired, verification required, or the contract. Link expiry and resend. The link expires after the merchant’s review expiry (default 7 days). The merchant can resend it (max 5 times); a resend extends the expiry, but for a mandate already at DDS never beyond DDS’s own 7-working-day expiry (RQXP). The link expires at the end of its last day and stops working at once; a daily job then closes the contract (status Link Expired). If the merchant selected “To Be Filled By Customer” for the bank/card fields, the customer completes those before signing — this data must be masked/ encrypted in transit and not exposed to the merchant beyond an approved masked view . The merchant then sees the IBAN in full, or the card number masked (v2.5). Note : Support in API missing. Query taken up with DDS team TBFC — resolved (DDS-confirmed 08-Sep-2026). DDS has no deferred-instrument mode: a mandate cannot be created without instrument details. So Geidea holds the whole contract, subscription and schedule locally (status Awaiting Customer Details) and does not call DDS at creation. After verification the customer chooses Bank Account or Credit Card (card hidden if the merchant has disabled it) and enters the details; Geidea then registers the mandate with DDS in real time and the customer continues straight to signing in the same session — no new link. If DDS rejects the details, the customer corrects and resubmits. The customer cannot change anything else on the contract. Outcome: a completed signature moves the mandate to Pending Bank System Submission; the customer failing to complete signing produces Denied or Request Expired (see status table above). Geidea always confirms the result with DDS rather than trusting the browser redirect alone. DDS’s callback returns finished, failed (with the reason), canceled (the customer left) or ddsError (UAE PASS Signature API v1.4). On failure the customer sees a plain-language reason and can try again while the link is valid; an IBAN validation failure cannot be retried (merchant discards and recreates — B3 ). No decline option for the customer (15-Oct-2026). DDS leaves it to Geidea whether the customer, the merchant or both may discard an unsigned mandate. In the MVP only the merchant can ( B3 ). A customer who does not want to sign ignores the link; DDS marks the mandate Request Expired after 7 days. Forgotten signing password. Before the customer signs, the page tells them how to reset a forgotten UAE PASS signing password: U"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B6. Subscription & Collection Schedule","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b6.-subscription-collection-schedule","Geidea’s Backend owns the collection schedule end to end (the “Subscription Engine”) — DDS holds no schedule of its own beyond the mandate’s amount/frequency ceiling. A subscription’s actual collection frequency must be no more frequent than its mandate’s frequency ceiling — it can be sparser, never denser. First Collection Date is a distinct value from the mandate’s own commencement date — a mandate signed on the 25th of a month can have its first real collection fall on the 5th of the following month. The recurring day-of-month is anchored off this date, not set independently. Subscription status: Pending Activation (until the linked mandate is Active) → Active (automatic on mandate activation) → Paused → Completed (every collection has reached a final state) or Cancelled (contract closed — including before activation: rejected, denied, expired link, discarded, error) . These statuses are entirely internal to Geidea — it is never sent to or recognised by DDS. Pause Subcription: Pausing a subscription must suspend collection activity only — the mandate itself stays Active throughout. Pause is forward-only: any occurrence already due, submitted, or settled before the pause takes effect is unaffected; only occurrences due after that point are excluded from generation and payment-file submission. Allowed only on an Active subscription with an Active mandate; an optional reason can be recorded. A mirrored Resume action must exist to reactivate a paused subscription. Collections skipped during the pause stay Skipped and are not collected. Occurrences that come due while paused are set to a terminal Skipped status at file-build time — no payment is ever created or submitted for them. Skipped is distinct from Failed: Failed means a payment was submitted and rejected or errored; Skipped means no payment was ever attempted. There is no retry path for a Skipped occurrence — the only way to recover its amount is the manual rollover mechanism in Section B7. — manual rollover is out of MVP. A merchant who wants to recover the amount does so by amending an upcoming collection ( B3 ). Why a collection can be Skipped (recorded as a reason): paused; mandate not yet Active in time for the file; minimum gap would be broken (amount rolls over automatically — B7 ); or missed submission (e.g. yesterday’s job failed — alert Ops)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B7. Payment Execution - Daily Processing Chain","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b7.-payment-execution---daily-processing-chain","Each day, the system must identify every occurrence due the following day, build a single bulk payment submission, and submit it to DDS ahead of DDS’s hard cutoff of 8:00 PM the day before the due date (T-1). Each line carries the mandate reference, the payment reference, the amount, and the due date. One file per OIC per day. The payment reference is the merchant reference plus the collection’s 6-digit sequence number (e.g. SAND-1114-000003) and stays the same through retries. A collection re-set after an Error gets a new payment reference: the next unused 6-digit number on the contract (e.g. 000013 on a 12-collection contract) — B8. Once a collection is in a file it can no longer be amended. Before a collection is added to the file it is checked in this order: contract closed → Cancelled; mandate not yet Active → Skipped; subscription paused → Skipped; due date after the mandate end date → Cancelled; amount outside min/max → Error + Ops alert; minimum-gap safety net fails → Skipped and its amount rolls over automatically. Minimum-gap safety net. Just before sending, Geidea checks the collection against the latest actual (or expected) debit date on the mandate. If it would be too close — typically because a holiday pushed the previous debit later — it is Skipped and its amount rolls onto the next collection ( B8 ), rather than moving the date, which would ripple through the rest of the schedule. DDS accepts a payment file all or nothing : one bad row rejects the whole file. Geidea then removes the rows DDS complained about (those collections go to Error), re-uploads the rest once, and raises an alert. If the file is still not accepted by 19:45, an alert is raised for manual action. One accepted file per OIC per day (DDS, 15-Oct-2026). DDS counts files it has accepted, not upload attempts: a file rejected for bad rows does not count, so the corrected file is uploaded again the same day. If an upload times out, uploading the same file again returns a duplicate-payment error for rows already received — which confirms the first upload worked ( S5 ). Once a file is accepted there is no second one that day: a mandate that becomes Active after the file was uploaded misses the next day’s collection, which is Skipped (mandate not yet active) and rolls over. Manual fallback. An authorised Geidea user must be able to re-run the payment file job for the day through the API (operations console, S14 ). Download the generated file, upload it on DDS’s portal by hand and mark it as uploaded. An API client cannot upload files on DDS’s portal: DDS offers API upload only, plus a read-only monitoring dashboard (DDS, 15-Oct-2026). If a collection misses its date because no file went out, DDS will not accept it later with the same due date. How it is recovered (sent the next day with a new date, or skipped and rolled over) is open and is part of the S14 discussion. Until decided, it is marked Skipped (“missed submission”) with an alert. Every scheduled job must keep a record of each run, be safe to run again for the same date, and be triggerable by hand. A job that fails, or does not start when expected, raises an alert by email ( S14 ). DDS confirms pay/no-pay status per line by 6:00 PM on the due date itself : banks confirm between 11:00 and 18:00 and the result is final at the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off; a collection a bank has not settled by then is failed as “Technical issues at Paying Bank” (DDS, 08-Oct-2026). Geidea checks at 18:30. Same timings every day; on a settlement holiday the file is empty . This is a status signal only, not a cash event - it must not be treated as confirmation of an actual credit. Payment statuses from DDS: Pending (PNDG), Under Process (APRP), Accepted-Paid (ACCP), Rejected-Not Paid (RJCT), Error (ERR), Representment Pending (RPND — a retry in progress). Each refusal carries a reason code with a plain label (e.g. I “Insufficient funds”, S “Payment stopped by customer”). FT reference and actual debit date. The FT "],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B8. Failure Handling, Retry & Rollover","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b8.-failure-handling-retry-rollover","Retry : A rejected payment can be re-submitted for retry, capped at 3 attempts (original attempt + 3 retries = 4 total). Each retry reuses the original payment reference rather than creating a new one. Once the cap is reached, no further retry is possible on that occurrence. Retry is a merchant action (permission-controlled), allowed only while the mandate is Active and the subscription is not paused. Retry deadline. A successful retry is a debit, so the minimum gap counts from it; a late retry would make DDS refuse the next collection. Each collection therefore has a last day a retry may be sent: retry deadline = next collection’s due date − minimum gap days − 1 working day (−1 working day because DDS processes a retry on the next working day; for the last collection the mandate end date is used.) Examples: Monthly, due 1 Sep, next 1 Oct → deadline 7 Sep. Monthly, due 1 Feb, next 1 Mar → 5 Feb. Weekly, Mon 7 Sep → Wed 9 Sep. If the rejection arrives after the deadline there is no retry window. The deadline is recalculated when a date is amended. Terminal refusals. Some reason codes mean the mandate can never be collected again: A, V, W (account closed), D (account holder deceased), E, F, G (card blocked / expired / cancelled), O (mandate cancelled), X, Y, Z (account blocked by court / law enforcement / Central Bank). The collection goes straight to Failed — no retry, no rollover — and the contract is closed by Geidea as “Cancelled — <reason>”, with its subscription and future collections Cancelled. It is not cancelled at DDS (that needs the customer’s signature, B9 ); nothing more is sent against it. Collections in Error (v2.5). A collection DDS returns in Error (ERR) is a validation failure at DDS or the Central Bank, for example a debit that breaks the frequency rule. DDS confirmed (15-Oct-2026) that it cannot be represented : it must be corrected and submitted as a fresh payment. So: the payment record keeps its Error status for history; the collection shows “Payment validation error”, with no Retry and no bounce memo ; the merchant re-sets the collection through Amend schedule ( B3 ) — a new future date that keeps the minimum gap and the contract period, and for Variable contracts a new amount if wanted. The new date may fall after later collections (decided 18-Oct-2026): the order of collections does not bind an Error collection, but the minimum gap to the collections just before and after its new date always applies. The collection is then Scheduled again and goes out in a later payment file as a fresh payment with a new payment reference ( B7 ). It does not use any of the 3 retries and is not rolled over automatically. A mandate in Error stays final ( B4 ). “Technical issues at Paying Bank.” A collection the bank did not settle by the 18:00 cut-off is failed by the Central Bank with this reason. It is not caused by the customer and is not terminal: treated as a rejection that can be retried. Its reason code is T (“Technical Issues at Paying Bank – Auto NoPay Response from UAEDDS”, DDS REST API v2.0; confirmed by DDS 15-Oct-2026). When a rejection becomes final (Failed): the 3rd retry is rejected; or the retry deadline has passed with no retry in progress (checked daily at 07:00); or a terminal reason code; or the mandate is closed. Automatic Rollover : Applies to a Failed occurrence once its retries are exhausted or the next occurrence becomes due while the previous occurrence is in Failed status as soon as it becomes Failed (retry cap reached or retry deadline passed — not terminal refusals), and to a collection Skipped by the minimum-gap safety net ( B7 ), and only if rollover is enabled on the contract. The failed amount folds onto a future occurrence the next collection that is still Scheduled and not yet sent , provided doing so stays within the contract’s max amount and the rollover allowance below has not been used up. If either check fails, the occurrence is marked terminally Failed and a proof-of-failure docum"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B9. Cancellation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b9.-cancellation","There is no in-place amendment of a mandate’s core terms. Any change to those terms - amount, frequency, validity window, instrument - requires cancelling the existing mandate and creating a new one, with a fresh customer digital signature; a cancellation on its own does not carry over to a new mandate automatically. (Amending individual collection dates/amounts within the signed terms is allowed — B3.) Every cancellation must capture a reason, drawn from a defined set of reason codes (e.g. account closed, card replaced/expired/lost, unreliable billing, dispute resolution, moving providers, or other) : DDS’s cancellation reason codes P01–P99 (for example P50 account closed or closing, P30–P33 card cancelled, expired, replaced or lost, P20 moving service providers, P99 other with comments) — S8 . Cancellation requires customer consent via UAE Pass signing In the MVP scope (Product, 15-Oct-2026). DDS’s go-live checklist lists the cancellation journey as core; DDS allows it to follow go-live but strongly recommends it, because a customer cannot close a bank account while an active direct debit is on it. How it works (DDS-confirmed, 15-Oct-2026): the merchant starts a cancellation on a DD Active contract, with a reason; Geidea creates the cancellation request at DDS and sends the customer a link; the customer verifies and signs the cancellation form with UAE PASS on Geidea’s page; the Central Bank and the customer’s bank accept it (up to 1 working day) and the contract becomes DD Cancelled : future collections are cancelled and history is kept. While the request is open the contract shows Cancellation Pending (awaiting customer signature, or with the bank). Collections while pending. Collections continue until the customer signs : the mandate stays valid and DDS keeps accepting payments. A merchant who wants them to stop at once pauses the subscription; the cancel dialog says this in a note and offers “Also pause collections now”. Once the customer has signed, collections stop ; if the bank then rejects the cancellation, they resume. No withdraw. A cancellation request cannot be withdrawn (DDS has no API) and never expires. If the customer does not sign it stays pending and collections continue unless paused; the merchant is told this before confirming. One pending request per contract. Permission. Only users with the Cancel Contract permission see the action ( B15 ). Discard ≠ Cancel. An unsigned contract is discarded ( B3 ), not cancelled. Contracts closed by a terminal bank refusal are closed on Geidea’s side only ( B8 ). A customer may also cancel directly with their own bank ( B4 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B10. Reconciliation & Settlement - To be Finalized with the APEX team","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b10.-reconciliation-settlement---to-be-finalized-with-talha","Agreed with APEX team on 09-Sep, 30-Sep, 01-Oct and 06-Oct-2026. A few field values and the PTLF file specification are still to come from APEX team; userData stays open until the settlement report format is defined ( B18 ). Full field specification: story S12 in Part C. Mandates and Subscriptions carry no money movement and are not posted to APEX at all. A Payment (one collection attempt) is the only unit that federates to APEX, and only once it reaches Accepted/Paid status with a funds-transfer reference present . It does not wait for the funds-transfer (FT) reference (decision of 06-Oct-2026). Federation runs on a schedule (a Backend batch job), not per payment since payment confirmation is received at a cut-off time and not realtime. APEX API for DD federation is yet to be finalized Format: a PTLF bulk file only , laid out like APEX’s Create Transaction structure. No API call to APEX is made for Direct Debit (agreed 06-Oct-2026). APEX treats every record in the file as matched and ready for pricing. Timing. The file is built after the 18:30 status check on the due date and carries the collections that turned Paid. Its send time is a Backend setting, to be set with APEX so that the file is in APEX’s normal run for a T+1 payout: APEX counts the MID’s T+X from the day the file is loaded, and loads files by about 06:30 for that day’s run. A collection seen as Paid only at a later check goes in the next file. One APEX record per collection; retries never create a second record. Key values sent to APEX (full table in S12 ): APEX field Value for Direct Debit interSystemID DD:<OIC>:<DDS payment reference>, e.g. DD:560000001:SAND-1114-000003 referenceNumber (12 digits) Last digit of the year + day of the year of the debit date + DDS’s payment ID padded to 8 digits orderId and subscriptionNumber The merchant reference of the contract (same value in both), so every payment under one contract can be found together arn DDS’s FT reference. Not yet available when the file is built; filled later only once APEX can update an existing record (see below) localDate / localTime Date and time the payment turned Paid (the debit date), not the due date amount / settlementAmount, currency Amount collected; 784 (AED) messageType / processingCode / responseCode 200 / Payment series 5x0000 / 00 cardProduct, tranDestination, scheme DD (new APEX value) pan Bank account: fixed placeholder. Card: the full card number (see B12 ) authorizationCode Time of the transaction (HHMMSS) terminalName / terminalLocation “DDebit <MID>” terminalId, posEntryMode, destinationBin Open - values awaited from the APEX team Fee, VAT, payout and clearing fields Not sent; APEX fills them APEX owns fee/MDR calculation, VAT treatment, settlement grouping , reconcilation with central bank settlement to Geidea and merchant payout computation. Direct Debit is a new scheme in APEX. Reconciling the credits on Geidea’s bank statement against collections by FT reference is an offline Finance/FinOps activity and is outside the Direct Debit build ( B18 ). DDS never touches the funds and charges no per-transaction fee; it invoices Geidea monthly, manually, for its own commission. DDS will not provide settlement files or reports of any kind - only its documented APIs may be used to retrieve transaction and reconciliation data. Geidea pulls DDS’s Day-End Reconciliation report daily and logs any mismatch (status, amount, FT reference) for Ops. Finance reconciles DDS’s monthly invoice against the DD transaction counts in APEX. Merchant Portal must read contract/subscription/occurrence data directly from Geidea’s Direct Debit backend, and read payout/settlement-status data from APEX. The scope can be a narrow, targeted lookup for payout details rather than bulk-federating the full dataset into APEX just to keep it as a single inquiry source only - final design still pending payout status, payout ID and payout date come from APEX’s existing inquiry API (Transaction Inquiry by subscriptionNumbe"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B11. Notifications","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b11.-notifications","Customer-facing notifications will be kept minimal: signature request, confirmation of contract activation. Need to confirm from business on additional customer side notifications consisting of upcoming occurrence, collection status (pass/failed), etc . Also the verification OTP ( B5 ). Activation message wording to be finalised near build. The one-time password is always sent by Geidea. Rejection reasons for the customer (v2.5, solution pending). DDS’s go-live checklist expects the customer, as well as the merchant, to see the reasons when a mandate or a collection is rejected or fails. Geidea suppresses DDS’s own customer messages, so Geidea must send these. DDS confirmed (15-Oct-2026) that full-integration clients have DDS’s payer notifications disabled, and that DDS sets no minimum set of events or wording: it is Geidea’s decision. Which events, channels and wording is to be decided ( A13 item 25). Geidea asks DDS to suppress its payer notifications when requesting production credentials. Merchant-facing notifications cover contract status changes and per-occurrence processing events, delivered both in-portal and via webhook in-portal in the MVP; delivery by webhook comes with the partner model in Phase 2 . Events: contract — notification sent, instrument provided, signed, signing failed, link expired, activated, rejected, error, cancelled, expired, discarded; collection — submitted, paid, rejected, error, skipped, retry submitted, failed, rolled over, rollover blocked; subscription — paused, resumed, schedule amended, completed. Every event is stored from day one, so webhook delivery can be added later without changing how events are raised."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B12. Security & Data Handling","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b12.-security-data-handling","Instrument data (IBAN, card number) must be transmitted to DDS as required by the mandate-creation and signing flow with proper encryption - must be stored on Geidea’s side in encrypted form onlythe full IBAN or card number is never stored by Geidea and never written to logs; only the last 4 digits are kept - masked display on FE v2.5: the IBAN is stored in full (encrypted at rest) and shown in full on every merchant and customer screen; the card number is never stored by the DD Backend — it is held in Geidea’s token vault, the DD Backend keeps the token, and the card is always shown masked. Neither is ever written to logs. Card numbers (open item). DDS currently accepts only a clear card number, which would break PCI-DSS compliance; DDS has said it will support an encrypted card number (no date). Credit Card can be disabled per merchant until this is settled ( B2 ). To be explored: Decided (v2.5): holding the card number in Geidea’s token vault, with the DD Backend keeping only a token and fetching the number when it must be sent to DDS. APEX does not need the card number; a static value is sent for every Direct Debit record (agreed 06-Oct-2026). The Card Encryption Public Key mechanics are also open with IT Security and DDS. Emirates ID and mobile number are masked in logs and on customer-facing screens. DDS credentials are held in the secrets vault and never returned by any API. The customer review page shows no contract data until verification ( B5 ). Every create, status change, amend, pause, retry, rollover, discard and configuration change is audited (who, when, old and new value). Mandate and payment status changes each keep a history."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B13. API Support","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b13.-api-support","Solution design should be such that a merchant/partner desiring to setup its own Direct Debit service through its frontend while having Geidea rails at its back should be able to do so. Phase 2. The partner model is not built in the MVP. The MVP serves merchants through Geidea’s Merchant Portal. The current build must provision for it so that no foundational change is needed later. Named driver: Noon Payments. How the partner model will work: the partner creates and manages contracts from its own system through the same Geidea DD API. The contract review and signing page always belongs to Geidea. The DD Backend generates the review link and gives it to the partner in the create-contract response and by webhook; the partner sends it to the customer through its own SMS and email. The customer verifies, reviews, enters the instrument where needed and signs on Geidea’s page, and Geidea sends the one-time password for it. Co-branding of the page can be looked at later. What the current build must do to provision for it: Enforce every rule and permission in the DD Backend, never only in the Portal. Have the Merchant Portal use the Backend only through its documented, versioned APIs, with MID as the merchant identifier in every API and all API documentation. The Backend maps MID to its internal store ID; that mapping never appears in the API. Keep schedule generation, validation and status logic in the Backend. Store every event from day one, including contract created (with the review link) and link resent. Send customer messages from one Backend component behind a per-merchant switch (Suppress Geidea customer notifications, default No). Keep the customer review page standalone (reached by link, no Portal session), with the one-time password always sent by Geidea, and return the review link in the create-contract response. Keep OIC on every contract, payment file, job run and batch. Let the permission check accept a caller that is not a Portal user. Return machine-readable error codes."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B14. Bulk Contract Upload","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b14.-bulk-contract-upload","Option to create contracts in bulk based on csv file upload. File format to capture complete contract and subscription data points Bulk file validation to be in place to ensure that the contents are complete and adequate for proper contract and subscription setup Maker/checker for bulk file upload and its review & approval DDS has no bulk-mandate API (DDS-confirmed); Geidea registers each row with DDS one at a time, exactly like a single contract. In Phase 1 (driven by a real-estate partner’s portfolio onboarding). Enabled per merchant ( B2 ). Two separate flows: Standard (every row has full instrument details) and TBFC (every row is To Be Filled By Customer — no instrument data expected). No mixed files. In this phase only the Standard flow is built; the TBFC flow is Phase 2. Validation at upload: everything that can be checked when the file is read is checked immediately (file type and columns, mandatory fields, formats, duplicates inside the file). A file that fails these checks cannot be submitted; the maker corrects it and uploads again. The remaining business rules are applied when each row is processed after approval, and any row that fails is reported with its reason. Maker/checker is on the same side: a merchant upload is approved by a merchant checker; a Geidea Ops upload (on a merchant’s behalf) by a Geidea checker. Maker and checker are separate user permissions ( B15 ), and a maker cannot approve their own file. The checker sees a list of bulk files with status, maker and checker names and timestamps, opens a Pending Review file to see every record, and can approve or reject with a reason . Processing: Standard files are picked up by a job every 15 minutes after approval and each row is registered with DDS; the file status reflects queued / processing / completed / partially completed. TBFC files are processed immediately on approval — each row becomes a contract in Awaiting Customer Details and the customer is sent the review link; no DDS call at that point. Note: a design discussion is required before bulk upload is taken up for development."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B15. Direct Debit User Permissions","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b15.-direct-debit-user-permissions","Merchant Portal already has a role framework: the business administrator creates roles, ticks permissions per module and assigns roles to users. Permissions are per action (for example Export Transactions is separate from View Transactions). Direct Debit adds its own permission group to it, shown only when DD is enabled for the business. Proposed permissions (07-Oct-2026); the final list is to be agreed with the MP team: Permission Allows View Direct Debit See the DD menu, KPIs, contract list and detail, collections, activity history; download the mandate document and proof of failure Export Contracts Export the contract list Create Contract Create a single contract Manage Unsigned Contract Resend signing link, discard unsigned contract, edit notes Pause / Resume Subscription Pause and resume Amend Collection Change the date or amount of future collections Retry Collection Retry a rejected collection Cancel Contract Start a cancellation of an Active contract; resend the customer’s cancellation link Bulk upload - maker Upload a bulk file and submit it for approval Bulk upload - checker Approve or reject a bulk file (never the user’s own upload) A user can have View without any action permission. Every action permission requires View: selecting an action permission selects View with it, so no permission can be granted on its own and lead to an empty page. Permissions apply within the stores (MIDs) the user is allowed to work in ( B16 ). An action the user has no permission for is hidden. An action the user has permission for, but which the status of the contract or collection does not allow, is shown disabled. The permission is checked on every request, so a screen left open after a permission was removed, or a direct call, cannot bypass it. The status rules of each action (for example, retry only on a Rejected collection) still apply on top of the permission. Every permission change and every permitted action is audited. Cancel Contract is its own permission ( B9 ). Manual rollover is not in the MVP and has no permission."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B16. Store Selection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b16.-store-selection","In Merchant Portal each MID is a store. Store selection is an existing Portal feature: by default a screen covers all the stores the user can access, and the user can narrow it to one or several with the store selector. Direct Debit follows the same model: the contract list, KPIs, search and export cover the stores selected, added together. With several stores selected, each contract shows its store. A contract belongs to exactly one MID. Creating a contract and bulk upload are for one store; an action on a contract is checked against that contract’s store. Users only see and act on contracts of stores they are permitted for."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B17. Geidea Support View","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b17.-geidea-support-view","Geidea support and call-centre staff can today search a MID in MA and impersonate it to see the merchant’s portal, Pay by Link and payouts. The same is needed for Direct Debit, so staff can see a merchant’s contracts, collections, failures and payout status when handling queries. To be designed: view-only or with actions, which Geidea roles, and audit of access ( B18 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part B — Functional Requirements","Document","B18. Open Items","direct-debit/docs/product.html#b18.-open-items","The full register, with owners, is in Part A section A13. Items that affect these requirements directly: Merchant payout is decided: T+1. Open: the APEX file send time (APEX, DD Backend); the change request for APEX to take the FT reference on an existing record, and the interim extract for FinOps (Product, APEX, FinOps). For a MID that is not on daily payout: how APEX applies T+1 to Direct Debit, for example a payout frequency at scheme level if it exists (APEX team). Final list of Direct Debit permissions in the Portal’s role framework ( B15 ) (MP, Product). Multi-store selection ( B16 ) to be applied to every Direct Debit screen and action. Geidea support view of a merchant’s Direct Debit activity ( B17 ). Payout status: Portal calls APEX directly, or the DD Backend reads APEX on a schedule and serves the Portal ( B10 ). Operations console and job control, including what happens to a collection whose payment file missed its date, ( B7 , S14 ). Card number handling: DDS clear card number, encryption key (IT Security, DDS). Token vault decided (v2.5); integration to be designed. APEX values for terminalId, posEntryMode, destinationBin, processingCode / transaction type and static pan; PTLF file specification (APEX team). Direct Debit settlement report format and the resulting userData content (Product, FinOps, Operations). Bank statement reconciliation by FT reference: offline, with Finance/FinOps; outside the build. Merchant Portal Transactions tab to exclude Direct Debit records (APEX, MP). Bulk upload design discussion before development. WhatsApp availability; activation message wording; further customer notifications (Tech, Business). Bounce memo: available after every rejection (DDS, 15-Oct-2026); to prove in the first decline test. Contract cancellation: DDS’s questions answered (15-Oct); in MVP scope; customer emails decided (18-Nov). Open: Arabic email wording and loan reason codes (Business); asking DDS for a way to withdraw an unsigned cancellation request (Product) — S8. Customer notices on rejections — Geidea’s design ( A13 item 25). Cancellation emails (request, cancelled, rejected) are part of S8 section 3.7 . Pending DD Backend: reference code details ( A13 item 29)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference","Document","Part C — Technical Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#part-c-technical-requirements","This part is for the build teams. It contains the delivery plan, the alignment between the Merchant Portal frontend stories and the Backend stories, and the Backend stories themselves. Backend stories ( S1 to S14 ) are written by Product as complete features. Each gives endpoints, parameter tables, field mappings to the order model and to DDS, rules and worked examples. Frontend stories ( US-01 to US-40 ) are the Merchant Portal backlog, kept in the “Direct Debit MP Backlog” page , corrected as “Direct Debit _ MP Backlog” v1.0 (15-Oct-2026) . They are not repeated here; this part shows how they line up with the Backend stories and what has to change in them. S1 to S7 were written on 24-Sep-2026 and are updated here to the decisions made since. S12 was updated on 06-Oct-2026 after the meeting with APEX. S9 to S14 are new drafts for review ; S8 was written on 15-Oct-2026. Version 2.4 (07-Oct-2026) updates S1 (section 5.4), S3 , S4 , S5 , S10 , S12 and S13 for the decisions listed under “What changed in version 2.4” at the top of this document. Version 2.5 (15-Oct-2026) updates S1 (section 5.4, reference code), S2 (merchant reference with the reference code), S3 (signing outcomes from UAE PASS Signature API v1.4, password hint), S4 (no DENY or SUSP), S5 and S6 (check times after the 18:00 cut-off; collections in Error re-set through Amend, not retried), S7 (Amend for Error collections), S8 (cancellation written; in the MVP), S9 and S10 (IBAN in full, card in the token vault, Central Bank reference, KPI cards over every contract in the selected stores), C4 rule 4, and rewrites C2 and C3 against the corrected frontend backlog. Points still with DDS are marked Asked DDS and listed in A13."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","C1. Story map","direct-debit/docs/product.html#c1.-story-map","# Story What it delivers Status S1 DD Merchant Enablement & Configuration OIC and DDS credentials, merchant Direct Debit settings, banks list, frequency table, scheduler times, shared DDS client Written, updated 07-Oct (scheduler times) S2 Create DDS Contract One contract created, validated and registered with DDS; TBFC held locally Written, updated 02-Oct S3 Contract Sending & Signing Customer journey: link, verification and OTP, contract view, TBFC instrument entry, UAE PASS, mandate document, link expiry Written, updated 14-Oct S4 Mandate Approval & Activation Daily status sync, status handling, subscription activation Written, updated 14-Oct (sync at 18:15) S5 Daily Collection Cycle Payment file, status tracking, FT reference, debit date, subscription completion Written, updated 14-Oct (status check 18:30) S6 Failed Collection Handling Retry, final failure, automatic rollover, bounce memo Written, updated 15-Oct (Error collections re-set through Amend, not retried) S7 Contract & Subscription Management Pause/resume, amend a collection, discard, notes Written, updated 02-Oct ; 15-Oct (Amend re-sets a collection in Error) S8 Contract Cancellation Cancel with reason, customer UAE PASS signature Post-MVP , not written Written 15-Oct-2026; in the MVP S9 Bulk Contract Upload Standard and TBFC bulk files, maker/checker, processing New draft. Design discussion needed before development S10 Merchant Portal Views & Reporting Contract list, search, KPIs, detail, collections with payout status, activity history, export New draft, updated 07-Oct S11 Merchant Notifications & Webhooks Event store and in-portal notifications (MVP); webhooks (Phase 2) New draft S12 APEX Federation Daily PTLF file to APEX, field specification, payout read-back Draft, updated 07-Oct. Sent on the Paid status. A few field values and the PTLF file specification awaited from APEX team; userData open S13 Direct Debit User Permissions Permissions in the Merchant Portal role framework, checked on every request New draft, updated 07-Oct. Final permission list to be agreed with MP S14 Operations Console & Job Control Job run log, missed-run detection, email alerts, manual re-run, payment file fallback, exceptions queue New draft, for discussion"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","C2. Delivery slices","direct-debit/docs/product.html#c2.-delivery-slices","Why. The frontend and Backend teams each work from their own backlog, split differently. The result has been Backend work the frontend does not yet use, and frontend work blocked on Backend work not yet done. Neither backlog is rewritten. Instead, every frontend story and every Backend story belongs to one delivery slice : an end-to-end capability that can be demonstrated. Sprints are planned by slice, so both teams take their part of the same slice together. Working rules API contract first. For each slice the endpoints and fields in the Backend story are agreed before either team builds, so the frontend can build against a mock. Shared references. Each frontend ticket names the Backend story section it depends on; each Backend story names the frontend stories it serves. Done means it can be demonstrated end to end at the fortnightly demo. The contract list and detail screens come early (slice 3). The order model is built in slices 1 and 2; once the Backend exposes the inquiry APIs and the Portal shows contracts, collections and detail mapped to the order model, every later slice can be viewed and tested through the Portal. Slice Capability Backend Frontend stories (backlog v1.0) 1 Enablement, access and user permissions S1 , S13 US-01 , US-02 2 Create contract (including TBFC) (TBFC is Phase 2) S2 US-07 to US-13 (built; change stories) 3 Contract list, detail and collections (inquiry) S10 sections 1 to 4 US-04 , US-17 , US-18 4 Customer review and signing S3 US-33 5 Activation and status tracking S4 ; S3 resend and PDFs US-14 , US-15 , US-16 6 Daily collection cycle S5 Feeds the statuses in US-18 7 Failure recovery S6 US-22 , US-23 , US-26 8 Contract management and cancellation S7 , S8 US-19 , US-20 , US-34 , US-35 , US-21 , US-39 , US-40 9 Payout and APEX S12 US-27 , US-36 10 Portfolio KPIs, search, export, activity history S10 sections 5 to 8 US-03 , US-05 , US-06 , US-32 11 Notifications and error handling (webhooks are Phase 2) S11 sections 1 to 3 and 5 US-30 , US-31 ; US-38 customer notices (solution pending) 12 Bulk upload S9 US-28 , US-29 , US-37 13 Operations console and job control S14 Internal console screens (owner to be agreed; not Merchant Portal) Removed Manual rollover — US-24 , US-25 The job run log and manual re-run in S14 should be built alongside the first scheduled job (slice 5), not left to the end. Slice 6 has no screen of its own; the frontend tests it through slice 3’s screens, using seeded data or DDS’s test status-change APIs."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","C3. Frontend story alignment","direct-debit/docs/product.html#c3.-frontend-story-alignment","The frontend backlog was reviewed on 14-Oct-2026: each of the 32 stories was read on its Jira page and compared with this document, the DDS go-live checklist ( A14 ) and the build on the test portal. The corrected backlog is the document “Direct Debit _ MP Backlog” v1.0 (15-Oct-2026); it holds every story in full, with corrections highlighted and a build status. Rule (Product): this document is the reference; a frontend story that differs from it is corrected. A story already built is not reopened: its correction is raised as a new story linked to it. Verdict per story Verdict Stories Keep US-01 , US-06 , US-11 , US-16 , US-27 , US-29 , US-30 Built — changes in a change story (US-nn.1, linked to the original) US-03.1 (Total Collections counts Paid only; Declined = Rejected + Error; KPIs always cover every contract in the selected stores — no filters, no date range), US-04.1 (store column, row menu, actual debit date; no default date range; IBAN in full), US-05.1 (status and frequency options; no DD Denied), US-07.1 (reference typed by the merchant behind the Geidea reference code, up to 20 characters, unique per merchant — code details pending DD Backend ; 784; 7 words; description 500), US-08.1 (TBFC in Phase 1; IBAN and card formats; IBAN in full, card masked), US-09.1 (all DDS frequencies; cap from the merchant setting; sum-of-collections rule removed), US-10.1 (4 working days from today), US-12.1 (dates editable on Fixed), US-13.1 (Not Submitted outcomes), US-17.1 (description, Central Bank reference once active, Outstanding, actions; Cancel only through the cancellation flow) Updated (not built yet) US-14 (remove ageing, Suspended and Denied), US-15 (resend extends the link; refused once expired), US-20 (no manual rollover), US-23 (fifth rollover state), US-26 (bounce memo for any rejection), US-28 (two flows), US-31 (no silent failures), US-32 (event list) Rewritten US-02 (per-action permissions), US-18 (collection statuses incl. Rejected, Error shown as “Payment validation error”, Cancelled, Representment Pending; reasons; retry deadline; five rollover states), US-19 (now “Amend Collection Schedule”, S7 section 2 ; also re-sets a collection in Error with a new payment reference), US-22 (retry a Rejected collection only; Error is re-set through Amend; Failed is final), US-21 (cancel an active contract, S8 ) Removed US-24 , US-25 (manual rollover) Placeholder US-38 customer notices on rejections (solution pending; Geidea’s design) New US-33 customer review and signing page (new Epic 11), US-34 discard, US-35 edit notes, US-36 keep Direct Debit out of the Transactions tab, US-37 TBFC bulk upload, US-39 track a cancellation, US-40 customer signs the cancellation (Epic 11) Backend stories per frontend epic One Backend feature per frontend epic, holding the Backend stories that serve its frontend stories, written in the frontend format (user story and acceptance criteria) with a pointer to the section of this document. Epics 2 and 4 are in Jira; the other codes are reserved. Frontend epic Backend story Serves Section of this document Jira Epic 1 — Access & Permissions BE-E1.1 Enablement and module access (switching Direct Debit off pauses active subscriptions) US-01 S1 section 3 To create BE-E1.2 Permission enforcement US-02 S13 To create Epic 2 — Dashboard & Portfolio BE-E2.1 Contract List US-04 S10 section 1 MEU-692 BE-E2.2 Contract Search & Filters US-05 S10 section 6 MEU-693 BE-E2.3 Portfolio KPIs US-03 S10 section 5 MEU-694 BE-E2.4 Contract Portfolio Export US-06 S10 section 7 MEU-695 Epic 3 — Create Contract BE-E3.1 Create contract: validation and outcomes US-07 to US-13 S2 To create (change story) Epic 4 — Status & Authorization BE-E4.1 Mandate Status Sync & Status Handling US-14 S4 sections 1 , 3 and 4 ; B4 MEU-697 BE-E4.2 Contract Status Model US-14 S10 section 3 ; B4 MEU-698 BE-E4.3 Resend Signing Link & Link Expiry US-15 S3 sections 1 and 10 MEU-699 BE-E4.4 Mandate Document US-16 S3 section 9 MEU-700 Epic 5 — Contr"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","C4. Rules for every story","direct-debit/docs/product.html#c4.-rules-for-every-story","These apply to every story without being repeated in each one. Field names. snake_case names are fields in the agreed Direct Debit - Order Model . camelCase names are API request/response fields or DDS fields; each table says which. Every story lists the Order Model fields it adds, under “Order Model changes” — add them to the Order Model and the schema as part of that story. Where the rules live. The Backend enforces every rule, even when the Merchant Portal already hides or blocks the option. In Phase 2, partners call the same API directly, so nothing may depend on the Portal doing the check ( C5 ). Audit trail. Every create, status change, edit, pause, retry, rollover and discard writes an audit record: entity, entity ID, action, old value, new value, who (user ID or system ), when. Sensitive data. (v2.5) The IBAN is stored in full, encrypted at rest, and may be returned in full to the Portal and the customer page. The card number is never stored by the DD Backend: it is held in Geidea’s token vault, the Mandate keeps the token and the last 4 digits. Neither is ever written to logs. Emirates ID and mobile number are masked in logs. Status history. Every status change on Mandate and Payment writes a history row (status, reason, time, source). DDS calls go through the shared DDS client defined in S1 section 6 (credentials by OIC, formats, error handling, retry policy). Merchant events. Each story lists the events it raises (e.g. contract.signed ). S11 stores them and shows them to merchants as in-portal notifications. Delivery by webhook is Phase 2. Until S11 is built, events are stored in an event table. Times are UAE time (UTC+4). All scheduler times come from configuration ( S1 section 5 ), never hard-coded. API response envelope is: code , success (boolean, never a string), message , timestamp , data . Errors return a machine-readable error code plus a list of field errors. Merchant identifier. Every API and all API documentation identify the merchant by MID . The Backend maps MID to its internal store ID GUID; that mapping never appears in the API. These stories are the requirement. The Backend team implements what is written here. Where the DD Backend team’s Integration Document v2 differs, each story lists it under “Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team”; anything not built must be agreed with Product as excluded or deferred."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","C5. Provisions for the Phase 2 partner model","direct-debit/docs/product.html#c5.-provisions-for-the-phase-2-partner-model","The partner (API) model is Phase 2 and is not built now. In it, a partner runs its own merchant-facing system on Geidea’s Direct Debit engine through the API, while the customer still reviews and signs on Geidea’s page. Every story in this part must be built so that Phase 2 can be added without changing the foundations. Where a story mentions partners, it is describing a provision, not MVP scope. # What the current build must do Why it matters for Phase 2 1 Every business rule and permission is enforced in the DD Backend, never only in Merchant Portal A partner’s system will call the same APIs without the Portal in front 2 The Merchant Portal uses the DD Backend only through its documented, versioned APIs ( /direct-debit/v1/... ), with the merchant identified by MID. No Portal-only shortcuts or direct database access The same APIs become the partner API without being redesigned 3 Schedule generation, validation and status logic live in the Backend; the Portal only displays what the Backend returns A partner must get the same results without re-implementing the logic 4 Every event is written to the event store from day one, including contract created (with the review link and its expiry) and link resent Webhooks are then only a delivery mechanism added on top 5 Customer messages (signing request, resend, activation) are sent by one Backend component, behind a per-merchant switch that exists in the data model from the start (default: Geidea sends) Phase 2 turns the switch on for a partner who sends its own SMS and email; nothing else changes 6 The customer review and signing page is a standalone Geidea page reached by link, not tied to a Merchant Portal session, and takes the merchant name and contract description from data. The one-time password is always sent by Geidea The page stays Geidea’s for partners; the link is simply delivered by someone else. Co-branding can be added later 7 The review link is returned in the create-contract response, whoever sends it to the customer The partner needs it from the API 8 Every contract carries its OIC; DDS logins, payment files, job runs and batches are all kept per OIC Allows a partner or merchant with its own OIC, and operations-console access limited to that OIC 9 The permission check accepts a caller that is not a Portal user ( S13 section 3 ) A partner authenticates with API credentials for a MID 10 Errors are returned as machine-readable codes with field-level detail A partner’s system must act on them without a screen Phase 2 scope, not to be built now: partner API credentials and onboarding; webhook registration and delivery ( S11 section 4 ); exposing the Suppress Geidea customer notifications setting ( S1 section 3 ); partner access to the operations console ( S14 section 7 ); co-branding of the review page; partner-owned OIC."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","C6. Backend stories","direct-debit/docs/product.html#c6.-backend-stories","S1 to S7 follow, then S8 to S14."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S1 — DD Merchant Enablement & Configuration","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s1-dd-merchant-enablement-configuration","Delivery slice: 1 · Serves frontend stories: US-01 (and the settings behind every other story)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S1 — DD Merchant Enablement & Configuration","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary","Before any merchant can create a Direct Debit contract, Geidea must be able to switch DD on for that merchant, tell the Backend which OIC and DDS login to use, and hold the settings every later story reads. This story builds that foundation. Item Value Users Geidea admin / Back Office (sets config); Merchant Portal (reads config) Delivers OIC registry, DDS credentials, merchant DD configuration, banks list, frequency table, scheduler times, shared DDS client Read by Every other story Existing UI reference Merchant Portal Replica: PGW Config in MA modal (merchant settings) and DD BE Config modal (credentials, card encryption key) What an OIC is (plain terms). The OIC (Originator Identification Code) is the Central Bank ID of the company collecting the money. Geidea has one: 560000001 . Every DDS call is made with the DDS username/password that belongs to an OIC, and all money collected under that OIC lands in the bank account linked to it. Today every merchant uses Geidea’s OIC."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S1 — DD Merchant Enablement & Configuration","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. OIC registry","direct-debit/docs/product.html#oic-registry","Create admin endpoints (Geidea back-office only): POST /direct-debit/v1/admin/oics , GET /direct-debit/v1/admin/oics , GET /direct-debit/v1/admin/oics/{oicId} , PUT /direct-debit/v1/admin/oics/{oicId} . Store one record per OIC: Field M/O Validation / meaning oic_code M 9 digits, unique (e.g. 560000001 ) owner_type M Geidea | Merchant . Only Geidea can be assigned to merchants in Phase 1 (see 3.3) display_name M Max 100 chars, e.g. “Geidea — Settlement Bank” settlement_bank_name M Bank of the OIC-linked account settlement_account_masked M Last 4 digits only status M Active | Inactive . An Inactive OIC cannot be assigned, and new contracts cannot be created under it"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. DDS credentials per OIC","direct-debit/docs/product.html#dds-credentials-per-oic","Each OIC holds the DDS username and password for each environment (test, production). These are the credentials used for every DDS call made for mandates under that OIC. Store them in the secrets vault, not in the database. API responses never return them; they show •••• and a lastUpdatedAt . Provide POST /direct-debit/v1/admin/oics/{oicId}/test-credentials . It proves the login works without creating anything: call DDS Day-End Reconciliation Report GET /v1/merchant/payments/day-end?date={today} . Result: HTTP 200 or 404 = credentials valid (404 only means “no payments that day”); HTTP 401 = credentials wrong. Also store here the Card Encryption Public Key (from the DD BE Config modal): used to encrypt a card number before sending it to DDS. Mechanics still open (see Open point a)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Merchant Direct Debit configuration","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-direct-debit-configuration","Create GET /direct-debit/v1/admin/merchants/{merchantId}/config and PUT /direct-debit/v1/admin/merchants/{merchantId}/config for Geidea admin (the PGW Config in MA screen), and a read-only GET /direct-debit/v1/config for the Merchant Portal, which returns the calling merchant’s settings (no secrets). Store one configuration record per merchant: Setting (config field) Type Default Rule Used by Enable Direct Debit ( enable_direct_debit ) Yes/No No Master switch. No hides the DD menu and blocks every DD API for this merchant. All other settings below sit under this switch All OIC ( oic_id ) OIC reference — Required when DD is enabled. Must be an Active OIC with owner_type = Geidea S2 , S5 Reference code ( dd_reference_code ) 5 digits Generated Generated by the DD Backend when Direct Debit is first enabled; never changes and never reused for another merchant. Put in front of every merchant reference ( S2 rule 2 .5, field merchantReference ) so references stay unique across merchants under the OIC. Shown read-only in PGW Config. Pending DD Backend team: per merchant or per MID; separator; how uniqueness is guaranteed at generation (5 random digits give 100,000 codes, so each new code must be checked against existing ones) S1 , S2 Disable Credit Card Instrument ( disable_credit_card_instrument ) Yes/No No (card allowed) Yes = Credit Card cannot be chosen by merchant or customer. Switch to Yes for a merchant while card-number encryption with DDS is still open (today DDS only accepts a clear card number, which would break PCI compliance) S2 , S3 Maximum Contract Amount ( max_contract_amount ) AED, 2 dp 100,000,000 1 ≤ value ≤ 100,000,000 (DDS scheme cap). One flat number per merchant, set by Risk. Checked per contract only; no cap across all of a merchant’s contracts S2 Minimum first-collection lead time ( min_first_collection_lead_working_days ) Integer (working days) 4 0–30. The first collection must be at least this many working days after contract creation, to leave time for bank approval (up to 3 working days) and the 20:00 file cut-off S2 Contract review expiry (days) ( contract_review_expiry_days ) Integer 7 1–30. How long the customer’s review link stays valid S2 , S3 Suppress Geidea customer notifications ( suppress_customer_notifications ) Yes/No No Phase 2 (partner model, C5 ). Add the field now, default No; do not show it in PGW Config in the MVP. Yes = Geidea does not send the signing-request or activation messages; an API partner sends the review link through its own SMS and email. The review and signing page is still Geidea’s, and Geidea still sends the one-time password for it ( S3 section 3 ) S2 , S3 , S4 Enable Bulk Contract Upload ( enable_bulk_contract_upload ) Yes/No No Shows/blocks bulk upload S9 Rules when settings change: Changing the OIC affects new contracts only. Existing mandates keep the OIC copied onto them when they were created. Switching Enable Direct Debit to No : block new contracts and all merchant actions immediately, and pause every Active subscription of that merchant (set Subscription.status = Paused , pause_reason = dd_disabled ). Collections already sent to DDS carry on. Switching back to Yes does not resume them automatically; the merchant resumes each one ( S7 ). Switching Disable Credit Card Instrument to Yes blocks card for new contracts and new TBFC submissions only. Existing card mandates are not affected. Lowering Maximum Contract Amount does not affect existing contracts. Every change writes an audit record with old and new values."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Action permissions (moved to S13)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#action-permissions-moved-to-s13","The per-merchant user permissions that were listed here ( allow_create_contract , allow_resend_signing_link , and so on) are removed (decision 25-Sep-2026). Allowing or blocking individual Direct Debit actions is a user-level permission in Merchant Portal user management, not a MID-level setting in PGW Config. The requirement is now story S13 — Direct Debit User Permissions . What stays true for every story: an endpoint that performs a permission-controlled action returns 403 ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED when the calling user does not hold the permission ( S13 section 3 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Reference data and scheduler times","direct-debit/docs/product.html#reference-data-and-scheduler-times","5.1 Banks list Store DDS’s Banks Master Table as a Geidea-maintained reference table: bank_id (DDS number), bank_name (spelled exactly as DDS spells it — DDS matches on the name), active (Yes/No). GET /direct-debit/v1/banks returns active banks for the portal dropdown. Admin can add, rename or deactivate banks. DDS has no API for this list; it changes when DDS publishes a new spec version (e.g. v1.8 added Zand, v1.9 removed several). The list is held in DD Backend configuration and must be updated when that happens; a wrong or missing name makes DDS reject the contract. 5.2 Frequency table Store as configuration (DDS may change these numbers). GET /direct-debit/v1/frequencies returns it for the portal. Frequency (DDS value) Rank (1 = most frequent) DDS period (days) Minimum gap between two successful debits (days) Daily 1 1 0 Weekly 2 7 4 Monthly 3 30 23 Every Two Months (not offered in this phase) 4 60 50 Quarterly 5 90 80 Every Four Months (not offered in this phase) 6 120 100 Half-yearly 7 180 160 Annually 8 360 330 One Time Only (not offered in this phase) 9 — Not applicable (one collection only) How to read it: a Monthly mandate debited on 15 Sep cannot be debited again before 8 Oct (15 Sep + 23 days). The gap is always counted from the actual debit date , not the due date (DDS-confirmed). “Rank” lets code compare frequencies: a subscription’s frequency must have a rank ≥ the mandate’s frequency rank. 5.3 UAE working-day calendar A table of UAE non-working days (weekends + public holidays), held in DD Backend configuration. The Backend cannot apply these rules without it: the first-collection lead time ( S2 rule V15), the bank’s 3-working-day approval, the 7-working-day cap on a resent link ( S3 section 1 ), the retry deadline ( S6 section 1.2 ), and the shift of a debit due on a non-working day ( S5 ). Public holidays must be added as they are announced. Who keeps it current is an open point; the operations console ( S14 ) is the natural place to edit it. 5.4 Scheduler times (global configuration) Job Default time (UAE) Story Mandate status sync Daily 18:15 S4 Payment file build and upload Daily 19:00; must finish by 19:50 (DDS hard cut-off 20:00) S5 Payment status check on due date 18:30 (after the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off) ; optional earlier check at 14:00 S5 FT reference check 06:30 on the day after the debit; retry 12:00; then daily S5 Day-End Reconciliation pull Daily 06:30, for the previous day S5 APEX file (PTLF) Configurable. After the 18:30 payment status check on the due date and before APEX’s 06:30 load the next day; exact default to be set with APEX ( S12 section 4 ). The file is built from the Paid status and does not wait for the FT reference S12 Retry deadline job (closes retry windows, triggers rollover) Daily 07:00 S6 Review link expiry job (closes contracts whose link expired the day before) Daily, shortly after midnight S3 Standard bulk file processing Every 15 minutes S9 These follow DDS’s timing table (DDS REST API document v2.0, “SLA Timing / Batch Process”): mandates go to the Central Bank every 30 minutes between 07:00 and 19:00; the payment batch goes at 01:00 on the due date; the payment file must be uploaded by 20:00 the day before the due date. DDS confirmed on 08-Oct-2026 that pay / no-pay is final at the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off and that the FT reference is generated between 01:00 and 05:00 the next day, every day (an empty file on settlement holidays). The mandate sync runs at 18:15 and the payment status check at 18:30, after the cut-off, as DDS’s go-live checklist asks. All times are configuration. Geidea sets them within DDS’s own time windows and adjusts them based on production experience. 5.5 Terminal payment reason codes Some bank refusal reasons mean the mandate can never be collected again (for example, the account is closed). A retry cannot help. Store this list as configuration; S6 section 2.1 uses it. Code Meaning A Account closed D Account holder deceased E Card blocked"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Shared DDS client","direct-debit/docs/product.html#shared-dds-client","One internal component makes every call to DDS, so all stories behave the same way. Base URL per environment: test https://test.directdebit.ae/api , production https://directdebit.ae/api . All paths start /v1/merchant/... . Login: HTTP Basic auth with the username/password of the OIC passed in by the caller (the mandate’s own oic , never the merchant’s current OIC). Formats: dates dd/MM/yyyy ; text trimmed, no double spaces; only these special characters: ( ) { } ! & @ $ : / - _ . + space . Errors: DDS returns validation errors as {\"errors\": {\"<field>\": [\"<reason>\"]}} . The client turns this into a list of field + reason for the caller. Retries — the simple rule: Calls that only read (get status, get PDF, day-end report) may be retried automatically up to 3 times (after 5s, 30s, 2 min). Calls that create or change something at DDS (Create DDA, upload payment file, represent payment, discard, cancel) are never retried automatically. If DDS doesn’t answer, we can’t know whether it acted, so the calling story decides what to do (each story says how). Timeouts: 30 seconds per call (configurable). Logging: log method, path, HTTP status and duration for every call. Mask IBAN, card number, Emirates ID and mobile in logged bodies. Never log the password."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S1 — DD Merchant Enablement & Configuration","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points","# Point Status a Card Encryption Public Key: what it encrypts, who issues and rotates it, and whether it depends on DDS’s own encrypted-card-number change Open — needs IT Security + DDS b Merchant-owned OIC ( owner_type = Merchant ): stored but not assignable until its settlement/invoicing flow is designed Deferred"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S2 — Create DDS Contract","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s2-create-dds-contract","Delivery slice: 2 · Serves frontend stories: US-07 to US-13"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S2 — Create DDS Contract","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-1","The Backend exposes one endpoint that creates a Direct Debit contract (Mandate + Subscription + Occurrence schedule) and, unless the customer will fill in their own instrument (TBFC), registers the mandate with DDS and sends the customer a review-and-sign link. Item Value Story ID S2 Consumers Geidea Merchant Portal (MVP); partner systems through the same API (Phase 2, C5 ) DDS API used Create DDA — POST /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities Order Model entities written Mandate, Subscription, Occurrence Depends on S1 (DDS client, OIC and credentials, merchant DD configuration, action permissions, banks list, frequency table) Followed by S3 Contract Sending & Signing, then S4 Mandate Approval & Activation Out of scope Joint Account, Non-Individual customers, Bulk upload ( S9 ), sending and signing ( S3 ) Field names in snake_case are Order Model fields (see Direct Debit - Order Model ). Field names in camelCase are API request fields or DDS fields — the tables say which."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S2 — Create DDS Contract","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-1",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Endpoint","direct-debit/docs/product.html#endpoint","Create POST /direct-debit/v1/contracts for contract creation, used by the Merchant Portal in the MVP and, in Phase 2, by partners calling the same API. The merchant is identified by MID ( merchantId ), taken from the authenticated session, never trusted from the request body. All DD APIs and API documentation use MID. Internally the Backend maps MID to its store ID GUID; that mapping stays inside the Backend and is not exposed in the API."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Pre-checks (before any processing)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#pre-checks-before-any-processing","Run these checks in order, reading the merchant’s Direct Debit configuration ( S1 section 3 ). The first failure stops the request and nothing is created. # Check Config element read On failure 2.1 Direct Debit is enabled for the merchant Direct Debit Config → Enable Direct Debit (Yes/No) 403 DD_NOT_ENABLED 2.2 Contract creation is allowed for the merchant User permission Contract actions ( S13 ) 403 ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED 2.3 An OIC is assigned to the merchant Direct Debit Config → OIC 422 OIC_NOT_CONFIGURED 2.4 If paymentMethodType = Credit Card , card is allowed for this merchant Direct Debit Config → Disable Credit Card Instrument (Yes/No). If Yes , reject any Credit Card request 422 INSTRUMENT_NOT_ENABLED 2.5 merchantReference is not already used on any mandate under the same OIC . The value checked is the full DDA reference, {dd_reference_code}-{merchantReference} ; because the code is unique per merchant, this is in effect a check within the merchant Local lookup on Mandate.dda_reference_number + Mandate.oic 409 DUPLICATE_MERCHANT_REFERENCE — DDS is not called Why 2.4 exists. Credit Card is allowed by default. DDS currently only accepts the card number in clear text, which would break Geidea’s PCI-DSS compliance, so while that encryption point stays open Geidea can switch Disable Credit Card Instrument to Yes for any merchant. The Backend enforces the flag even though the portal also hides the option, because partners call this API directly. Why 2.5 exists. DDS rejects duplicate references, but a timeout can leave a mandate created at DDS without Geidea knowing. Checking locally first means a DDS duplicate error can only ever mean “an earlier attempt of ours went through”, which makes that case detectable. The check is OIC-wide (decision 01-Oct-2026) because DDS itself enforces the reference per OIC: two merchants under Geidea’s OIC cannot use the same reference."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Request parameters and validation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#request-parameters-and-validation","All validation runs server-side and returns every failed rule in one response, not only the first. All text is trimmed, double spaces are rejected, and special characters are limited to the DDS set ( ) { } ! & @ $ : / - _ . + space . 3a. Field-level rules M = mandatory, O = optional, C = conditional. API field M/O/C Validation merchantReference M 3–26 chars 3 to 20 characters (26 minus the 5-digit reference code and a hyphen; pending DD Backend : 21 if no separator) , letters/digits/allowed specials; unique per merchant (check 2.5). The Portal stops input at the cap. contractDescription O Max 500 chars; shown to the customer on the review page notes O Max 1,000 chars; merchant-only, never shown to customer or sent to DDS customerName M Max 100 chars, max 7 words customerEmail M Valid email format, max 100 chars customerMobile M 05XXXXXXXX (10 digits) emiratesId M 15 digits, starts with 784 bankInfoFillByCustomer M true / false . true = TBFC (see step 4.1). In this phase only false is accepted; true returns TBFC_NOT_AVAILABLE (TBFC is Phase 2) paymentMethodType C — M if TBFC = false; must be absent if TBFC = true Bank Account | Credit Card ; Credit Card subject to check 2.4 bankName C — M if TBFC = false Must match an entry in the banks list ( S1 section 5.1 ) accountHolderTitle C — M if Bank Account Max 100 chars, letters only, max 7 words bankAccountType C — M if Bank Account Current | Savings iban C — M if Bank Account AE + 21 digits (23 chars) cardNumber C — M if Credit Card 16 digits, Luhn check cardHolderName C — M if Credit Card Max 100 chars, max 7 words startDate M YYYY-MM-DD ; contract validity start (DDS commencesOn ) endDate M YYYY-MM-DD ; contract validity end (DDS expiresOn ) amountType M Fixed | Variable amount C — M if Fixed Decimal, 2 dp; the regular installment minAmount M Decimal, 2 dp, > 0 maxAmount M Decimal, 2 dp frequencyCeiling M DDS values: Daily , Weekly , Monthly , Every Two Months , Quarterly , Every Four Months , Half-yearly , Annually , One Time Only frequency M Same value set as frequencyCeiling — the actual collection cadence firstCollectionDate M YYYY-MM-DD rollover.enabled M true / false rollover.maxConsecutive C — M if rollover enabled Integer ≥ 1 collections[] M Array of { dueDate: YYYY-MM-DD, amount: decimal } — the schedule shown to the merchant in the occurrence preview Retries are not an input. The cap is fixed at 3 re-presentations (4 attempts total) by regulation. 3b. Contract × collections rules # Rule Error code V1 startDate ≥ today, and endDate > startDate INVALID_CONTRACT_PERIOD V2 startDate ≤ firstCollectionDate ≤ endDate FIRST_COLLECTION_OUT_OF_PERIOD V3 At least 1 collection. If frequencyCeiling = One Time Only , exactly 1 INVALID_COLLECTION_COUNT V4 The first collection’s dueDate = firstCollectionDate FIRST_COLLECTION_MISMATCH V5 Every dueDate is ≥ firstCollectionDate and ≤ endDate COLLECTION_OUT_OF_PERIOD V6 dueDate s are strictly ascending, no duplicates COLLECTION_DATE_ORDER V7 frequency is not more frequent than frequencyCeiling . Order, most to least frequent: Daily > Weekly > Monthly > Every Two Months > Quarterly > Every Four Months > Half-yearly > Annually > One Time Only FREQUENCY_EXCEEDS_CEILING V8 The gap between any two consecutive collection dueDate s is ≥ the minimum gap (days) for the contract’s frequencyCeiling , per the DDS frequency table below (confirmed by DDS team, 24-Sep-2026). E.g. Monthly: ≥ 23 days COLLECTION_GAP_TOO_SHORT V9 The number of collections equals the count the Backend generates from firstCollectionDate + frequency up to endDate . Individual dates may differ from the generated dates (merchant edits in the preview) only if V5, V6 and V8 still hold SCHEDULE_COUNT_MISMATCH V10 Every collection amount is ≥ minAmount and ≤ maxAmount COLLECTION_AMOUNT_OUT_OF_RANGE V11 If amountType = Fixed : amount = minAmount = maxAmount , every collection amount = amount , and rollover.enabled = false FIXED_AMOUNT_VIOLATION V12 If amountType = Variable : collectio"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Create local records, then call DDS","direct-debit/docs/product.html#create-local-records-then-call-dds","4a. Order Model updates required before build The fields below are not yet in the agreed Order Model . Add them to the Order Model (and the Backend schema) as part of this story: Entity.field Type / values Purpose Mandate.customer_type enum: Individual | Non-Individual Sent to DDS as customerType . Always Individual in Phase 1 Mandate.credit_card_holder_name string, max 100 Sent to DDS for Credit Card instrument Mandate.uaepass_signing_url string, server-side only Built in step 6; released to the customer only after the OTP gate ( S3 section 3 ) Mandate.created_by user ID Audit trail Mandate.signing_notification_sent_at timestamp Step 7 audit Mandate.signing_notification_channels list: SMS , Email Step 7 audit Occurrence.original_amount decimal The amount as first scheduled; never changes after creation. Keeps the original plan visible for history and reporting after edits ( S7 ) or rollovers ( S6 ) change amount Mandate.mandate_creation_stage — extend values add submitting_to_dds , dds_rejected , dds_outcome_unknown to the existing awaiting_customer_instrument | submitted_to_dds Tracks the outcome of the DDS call (step 5; Open point b) 4b. Create Mandate, Subscription and Occurrences (merchant-provided instrument) In one database transaction, create the records below. “API” = the request field. Order Model field Set to Mandate merchant_id From session oic Snapshot of the merchant’s OIC from Direct Debit Config at this moment (a later OIC change must not alter this mandate) dda_reference_number {dd_reference_code}-{merchantReference} , e.g. 48213-UNIT304 (≤ 26 characters). This full value goes to DDS as ddaReferenceNumber , is printed on the signed form and is the base of every payment reference contract_description API contractDescription notes API notes customer_full_name API customerName customer_email API customerEmail customer_mobile_number API customerMobile customer_type Individual customer_id_type UAE Emirates Identity Card customer_id_number API emiratesId cust_nid API emiratesId instrument_provided_by merchant instrument_type API paymentMethodType bank_name API bankName account_title API accountHolderTitle (Bank Account only) account_type API bankAccountType (Bank Account only) credit_card_holder_name API cardHolderName (Credit Card only) instrument_reference (v2.5) Bank Account: the full IBAN, encrypted at rest. Credit Card: last 4 digits only; the full card number goes to Geidea’s token vault and the token is stored in card_token . Never logged commences_on API startDate expires_on API endDate min_amount API minAmount max_amount API maxAmount amount_type API amountType payment_frequency API frequencyCeiling mandate_creation_stage submitting_to_dds status_code , status_label null until DDS responds created_at , created_by System, session user Subscription mandate_id The new Mandate status Pending Activation frequency API frequency subscription_start_date API firstCollectionDate subscription_end_date = Mandate.expires_on day_of_month_anchor Day-of-month of firstCollectionDate rollover_enabled API rollover.enabled rollovers_allowed API rollover.maxConsecutive (0 if rollover disabled) schedule_version 1 Occurrence (one row per collections[] item) subscription_id The new Subscription sequence_number 1..n in dueDate order due_date Item dueDate amount Item amount original_amount Item amount (same as amount at creation) amount_source scheduled for every occurrence at creation. merchant_edited is set only by later edits ( S7 section 2 ); rollover_adjusted only by rollovers ( S6 section 3 ) status Scheduled rolled_over none payment_created false 4c. Call DDS Create DDA Fetch the DDS credentials linked to Mandate.oic ( S1 section 2 ) and call Create DDA POST /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities with this mapping: Order Model field DDS request field Format / note customer_full_name customerFullName customer_email customerEmail customer_mobile_number customerMobileNumber 05XXXXXXXX dda_reference_number ddaReferenceNumber custo"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Handle the DDS response","direct-debit/docs/product.html#handle-the-dds-response","Update the Mandate from the Create DDA response: DDS outcome Order Model updates Returned to caller 201 Created { ddaId, ddarId } dda_id ← ddaId ; ddar_id ← ddarId ; status_code = PNDG ; status_label = Pending ; mandate_creation_stage = submitted_to_dds Continue to step 6 400 { errors: { field: [reason] } } mandate_creation_stage = dds_rejected 422 with each DDS error mapped back to the API field name. Merchant may retry with the same merchantReference — nothing was created at DDS Timeout / 5xx / no response mandate_creation_stage = dds_outcome_unknown ; raise an Ops alert 502 DDS_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN . No automatic retry — DDS may have created the mandate, and DDS offers no lookup by ddaReferenceNumber to check DDS’s Create DDA response carries no status; PNDG is the documented initial state of every new DDA. Status changes after this come from the signing callback ( S3 section 7 ) and the daily status sync ( S4 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Generate the contract review & signing link","direct-debit/docs/product.html#generate-the-contract-review-signing-link","Applies to both paths (merchant-provided and TBFC). Review URL = https://<portal-host>/contracts/review/{mandateId} — the Geidea-hosted review page. Set Mandate.review_link_expires_at = now + Direct Debit Config Contract review expiry (days) (default 7). The link expires at the end of that day: the time part is always 23:59:59 UAE time. Merchant-provided path only: build the UAE PASS URL from ddar_id — {DDS baseURL}/appApi/v1/customer/uaepass-signer-process?mode=web&ddarId={ddar_id}&redirectUrl={Geidea signing-callback URL} — and store it in Mandate.uaepass_signing_url . It is not returned to the merchant and is shown to the customer only after the OTP check ( S3 section 3 ). Under TBFC it is built in S3 section 5 ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","7. Send the signing notification to the customer","direct-debit/docs/product.html#send-the-signing-notification-to-the-customer","Read the merchant’s Suppress Geidea customer notifications setting ( S1 section 3 , default No). If No (default): read customer_email and customer_mobile_number from the Mandate and send the signing request by SMS, email and WhatsApp using Geidea’s templates ( S3 section 1 ): merchant display name, contract_description , review URL, link expiry date. If Yes (Phase 2, partner sends its own messages): send nothing. The partner sends the customer the reviewUrl returned in step 8 (also delivered by webhook, event contract.created ) through its own SMS and email. The page behind that link is Geidea’s. Set signing_notification_sent_at and signing_notification_channels (empty when suppressed). A failed send does not roll back the contract. Log it; the merchant can resend ( S3 section 1 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","8. API response","direct-debit/docs/product.html#api-response","Return 201 with: Field Source mandateId Mandate.mandate_id merchantReference Mandate.dda_reference_number ddaId Mandate.dda_id (null under TBFC) status , statusLabel Mandate.status_code / status_label ; under TBFC null / Awaiting Customer Details reviewUrl , reviewLinkExpiresAt Step 6 subscription subscriptionId , status , frequency , startDate , endDate schedule[] Per Occurrence: sequenceNumber , dueDate , amount , status Phase 2 — when notifications are suppressed (partner integration), the response is the same. The partner takes reviewUrl and reviewLinkExpiresAt from it and sends the link to the customer itself. The contract review and signing page always belongs to Geidea: the customer verifies, reviews, enters the instrument under TBFC and signs there. The response never contains the full card number or the UAE PASS signing URL. (v2.5) The IBAN may be returned in full."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S2 — Create DDS Contract","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-1","# Point Status a Credit Card instrument: DDS currently accepts only a clear card number, and the DD Backend must never store it. Decided (v2.5): Explore holding hold it in Geidea’s token vault and keep only a token on the Mandate ( Mandate.card_token ) for whenever the number has to be sent to DDS. Open: the token vault integration and DDS encryption. APEX no longer needs the card number: a static pan is sent for every record ( S12 , agreed 06-Oct-2026). Open — IT Security, Product"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S2 — Create DDS Contract","Document","Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team (Integration Document v2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#gaps-to-flag-to-george-integration-document-v2","These requirements are not yet reflected in the DD Backend team’s Integration Document v2 and need to be added: # Requirement in this story What v2 has today G1 TBFC: hold the contract locally, do not call DDS until the customer enters the instrument (section 4.1 ) Calls DDS immediately and returns a ddaId even when bankInfoFillByCustomer = true G2 Merchant identified by MID in all APIs and documentation; MID maps internally to the store ID GUID Uses storeId everywhere, no MID G3 merchantReference mandatory, 3–26 chars, unique per merchant Optional, max 50 G4 No maxRetries input; cap fixed at 3 Takes maxRetries G5 Geidea sends the customer notification unless the merchant’s suppress toggle is on (section 7 ) States Geidea never sends customer notifications G6 Validation rules V1–V15, incl. minimum gap (V8) and lead time (V15) Not specified G7 Pre-checks 2.1–2.5 incl. Disable Credit Card flag and user permission Not specified G8 Order Model fields in section 4a Not present"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s3-contract-sending-signing","Delivery slices: 4 (customer page) and 5 (resend, documents) · Serves frontend stories: US-15 , US-16 , US-33 (customer review and signing page)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-2","This story covers the customer’s whole journey after a contract is created: receiving the link, opening Geidea’s review page, proving who they are (verification screen and one-time password), reviewing the contract, entering their bank details when the merchant chose TBFC, signing with UAE PASS, seeing the result, and the page closing. It ends when the mandate is signed ( SUBP ) or the link expires. Item Value Users Customer (review page, no login); merchant (resend link) DDS APIs used Create DDA (TBFC only), UAE PASS signer process (redirect), Get Unsigned PDF, Get Signed PDF, Get Status of a DDA, Discard DDA Starts from S2 step 6–7 (review link created and first notification sent) Hands over to S4 (bank approval) The customer never logs in. The link plus the verification step is what protects the contract details."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-2",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Signing notification (first send and resend)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#signing-notification-first-send-and-resend","S2 sends the first message. This story owns the templates and the resend. Channels: SMS, email and WhatsApp, Arabic + English. If WhatsApp is not technically available, Tech must confirm that explicitly before it is dropped. Template content: merchant display name, contract_description , review link, link expiry date. Suppression (Phase 2): if the merchant’s Suppress Geidea customer notifications = Yes ( S1 section 3 ), Geidea sends neither the first message nor a resend; the partner sends the review link through its own channels. The one-time password in section 3 is always sent by Geidea, because the review page is Geidea’s. Resend: POST /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/resend-signing-link . The user must hold the Contract actions permission ( S13 ). Allowed only while the customer hasn’t finished: mandate_creation_stage = awaiting_customer_instrument , or status_code = PNDG . Not allowed once review_link_expires_at has passed (section 10 ). Resend extends the link: new review_link_expires_at = end of day (23:59:59 UAE time) on today + contract_review_expiry_days . Limit for contracts already at DDS ( PNDG ): DDS itself expires an unsigned mandate 7 working days after it was created ( RQXP ). The extension cannot go past that date — cap review_link_expires_at at created_at + 7 working days. TBFC contracts not yet sent to DDS have no such cap. Maximum 5 resends per contract (configurable), to prevent message spam. Update signing_notification_sent_at , signing_notification_channels ; increment signing_notification_count . Phase 2: if notifications are suppressed, Geidea sends nothing: the call extends the link, returns reviewUrl and the new expiry, and raises contract.link_resent for the partner’s webhook."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Customer opens the review page","direct-debit/docs/product.html#customer-opens-the-review-page","The review page ( https://<portal-host>/contracts/review/{mandateId} ) first calls GET /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/access . The Backend decides which screen the customer sees: Condition (checked in this order) pageState returned Customer sees Mandate not found, discarded, cancelled, rejected or denied or error NOT_AVAILABLE “This contract is no longer available. Contact <merchant>.” Already signed ( status_code is SUBP , APRP or ACCP ) ALREADY_SIGNED “You have already signed this contract.” review_link_expires_at ≤ now EXPIRED “This link has expired. Contact <merchant> for a new one.” No valid review session yet (verification is always required) OTP_REQUIRED Verification screen (step 3) Otherwise READY The contract (step 4) The response for every state includes only pageState and the merchant display name. For OTP_REQUIRED it also includes the verification-screen data in step 3a. No contract terms, schedule or instrument details are returned until the state is READY ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Verification and OTP access check","direct-debit/docs/product.html#verification-and-otp-access-check","Why: the review page shows the customer’s contract and bank details. Anyone who gets hold of the link (a forwarded SMS, a shared phone) should not be able to see them. So before any contract data is shown, the customer confirms who they are with a one-time password sent to a channel already on the contract. (Design decision 25-Sep-2026; replaces the earlier “type your full mobile number, SMS only” design.) Step 3a — verification screen. Returned with pageState = OTP_REQUIRED : Shown Source Geidea logo and DDS logo Static Merchant name Merchant profile. Needed because DDS always shows Geidea (the OIC owner), so the customer must see which merchant this contract is with What the contract is for Mandate.contract_description Customer name customer_full_name Emirates ID, masked customer_id_number , last 4 digits only Email, masked customer_email , e.g. a•••••@gmail.com Mobile, masked customer_mobile_number , e.g. 05•••••64 Step 3b — choose the channel and send the OTP. POST /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/send-otp with { \"channel\": \"SMS\" | \"EMAIL\" } . Generate a 6-digit OTP, valid 5 minutes. Send it to the contract’s mobile (SMS) or email, as chosen. Store only a hash of it and the channel used. The customer can switch channel and ask again. A new OTP cancels the previous one. Allowed 60 seconds after the last send, maximum 3 sends per 30 minutes across both channels. Step 3c — check the OTP. POST /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/verify-otp with { \"otp\": \"123456\" } : Wrong or expired: OTP_INVALID / OTP_EXPIRED . After 5 wrong attempts, lock verification for this contract for 30 minutes ( OTP_LOCKED ). Right: create a review session — a token valid 30 minutes, tied to this one mandate. Return it to the page. Every later customer call in this story (contract view, PDF, instrument entry, start signing) must carry a valid review session token, otherwise return 401 REVIEW_SESSION_REQUIRED . All numbers above (6 digits / 5 min / 60 s / 3 sends / 5 attempts / 30 min) are proposed defaults, held in configuration. The OTP is sent by Geidea for every merchant, including those with Suppress Geidea customer notifications on."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Contract view","direct-debit/docs/product.html#contract-view","GET /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/getCustomerContractDetails (review session required). Returns: Shown to customer Source (Order Model) Merchant name Merchant profile What the contract is for Mandate.contract_description Customer name customer_full_name Emirates ID (masked, last 4) customer_id_number Mobile (masked) customer_mobile_number Payment instrument instrument_type + bank_name + instrument_reference (IBAN in full; card last 4 — v2.5) . Under TBFC: instrumentRequired: true and no instrument Validity commences_on – expires_on Amount type, min and max per collection amount_type , min_amount , max_amount Frequency Subscription.frequency Schedule Every Occurrence: sequence_number , due_date , amount ; plus the total Link valid until review_link_expires_at Never returned: notes (merchant-only), full card number, uaepass_signing_url . Unsigned mandate PDF. GET /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/unsigned-pdf streams DDS Get Unsigned Mandate PDF GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddaId}/unsigned-pdf . Only available once dda_id exists (so under TBFC, only after step 5)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. TBFC: customer enters their payment instrument","direct-debit/docs/product.html#tbfc-customer-enters-their-payment-instrument","Phase 2 — not built in this phase (decided 18-Oct-2026). Kept here for Phase 2. Only when instrument_provided_by = customer and mandate_creation_stage = awaiting_customer_instrument . Otherwise return 409 INSTRUMENT_ALREADY_PROVIDED . The page shows the Bank Account / Credit Card choice. Credit Card is hidden, and rejected by the Backend, if the merchant’s Disable Credit Card Instrument = Yes (read the setting now, at submission, not at contract creation). POST /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/instrument with the same instrument fields and validation as S2 section 3a : paymentMethodType , bankName , accountHolderTitle , bankAccountType , iban (or cardNumber , cardHolderName ). The customer cannot change anything else on the contract. Save to the Mandate: instrument_type , bank_name , account_title , account_type (or credit_card_holder_name ), instrument_reference (full IBAN, or card last 4 with the card number sent to the token vault and card_token saved — v2.5) ; set mandate_creation_stage = submitting_to_dds . Call DDS Create DDA with exactly the S2 section 4c mapping, using the mandate’s oic credentials. Handle the answer exactly as S2 section 5 : 201 → save dda_id , ddar_id , status_code = PNDG , stage submitted_to_dds , build uaepass_signing_url ( S2 section 6.3 ). 400 → stage back to awaiting_customer_instrument and show the customer the field errors so they can correct and resubmit. Timeout → stage dds_outcome_unknown , show “We could not complete this step, please try again later or contact <merchant>”, alert Ops. On success the page continues straight to the contract view with the instrument filled in, then signing (step 6). One session, no new link. Partners (Phase 2). There is no partner-side instrument entry. The review and signing page is always Geidea’s, so a partner’s customer enters the instrument on the same page, through the same steps."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Start signing with UAE PASS","direct-debit/docs/product.html#start-signing-with-uae-pass","Before the Sign button, the page shows: “Forgot your UAE PASS signing password? Reset it in UAE PASS › Profile › Reset signing password.” (DDS go-live checklist) Customer clicks Sign . Page calls POST /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/start-signing (review session required). Allowed only when status_code = PNDG and the link has not expired. Return uaepass_signing_url ; the page redirects the browser there. The URL (built in S2 section 6.3 / S3 section 5 ) is: {DDS baseURL}/appApi/v1/customer/uaepass-signer-process?mode=web&ddarId={ddar_id}&redirectUrl={Geidea signing-callback URL} . DDS then walks the customer through UAE PASS (mobile number, app confirmation, signing password). At signing, DDS also checks the IBAN and Emirates ID against Central Bank records."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","7. Signing callback","direct-debit/docs/product.html#signing-callback","When the customer finishes (or fails), DDS sends the customer’s browser to Geidea’s callback: GET /direct-debit/v1/webhooks/direct-debit/signing-callback?action=sign&signerProcessId=...&status=...&statusMsg=...&ddId=...&signerProcessMode=web . Per DDS UAE PASS Signature API v1.4: status is finished , failed , canceled (the customer left UAE PASS) or ddsError (unexpected DDS error); statusMsg carries the failure message, URL-encoded; signerProcessId and signerProcessMode may be 0 when the failure happened before UAE PASS opened (DDS checks run first). Use status , statusMsg and ddId for the outcome. Find the Mandate by dda_id = ddId . If none, show the NOT_AVAILABLE page and alert Ops. Don’t trust the URL alone. These parameters arrive through the customer’s browser, so they could be edited. Always confirm by calling DDS Get Status of a DDA GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddaId}/status . Then act on DDS’s answer: DDS status after signing Backend updates Result page shown SUBP (signed, pending bank submission) or APRP status_code = DDS value; status_label = Pending System Submission / Pending Bank Approval; signed_at = now; signer_process_id = signerProcessId Success : “Contract signed. Your bank will review it within up to 3 working days.” Download signed PDF (step 9) Still PNDG and callback status=failed or ddsError last_signing_error = statusMsg / ddsError ; signing_attempts + 1 Failed : plain-language reason + “Try again” (back to step 6) while the link is valid Still PNDG , callback said finished Nothing yet; re-check once after 30 s “We’re confirming your signature…”; if still PNDG , treat as failed Still PNDG and callback status=canceled signing_attempts + 1 Not completed : “You left before signing. You can sign whenever you’re ready while the link is valid.” Anything else ( DENY , ERR , RJCT ) Set status as returned ( S4 rules apply) Not completed : “Please contact <merchant>” IBAN validation failure ( statusMsg contains iban validation failed ): DDS checks the Emirates ID (or trade licence) against the IBAN and bank name before UAE PASS opens; the account-holder name is not used. The bank details don’t match Central Bank records, so retrying won’t help. Show “Your bank details could not be verified. Please contact <merchant>.” Show the message for the exact statusMsg (v1.4), then “Please contact <merchant>.”: and raise merchant event contract.signing_failed with reason IBAN_VALIDATION_FAILED and the statusMsg . The merchant must discard ( S7 ) and create a new contract. statusMsg (decoded) Customer message iban validation failed : Invalid format Your bank account details are not in a valid format. iban validation failed : DDS could'nt find bank Your bank is not available for direct debit. iban validation failed : DDS could'nt find details Your bank account details could not be found. iban validation failed : DDS could'nt match the details when idType = EI Your Emirates ID does not match this bank account. iban validation failed : DDS could'nt match the details when idType = TL The trade licence does not match this bank account (non-individual customers, Phase 2). Other DDS messages before UAE PASS (examples, v1.4): Signing process has already been completed for id : {ID} → show ALREADY_SIGNED ; Only pending mandate is allowed to sign → re-check the status and show the matching page (usually NOT_AVAILABLE ); Resource not found with id : {ID} → NOT_AVAILABLE and alert Ops. Any other failed message → Failed with “Try again”."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","8. Closing the page","direct-debit/docs/product.html#closing-the-page","After success, end the review session. Any later visit to the link shows ALREADY_SIGNED (step 2). The customer receives no further messages from Geidea until activation ( S4 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","9. Mandate document (signed and unsigned PDF)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#mandate-document-signed-and-unsigned-pdf","Changed 07-Oct-2026: the signed PDF is fetched once after signing and otherwise on request (the 15-minute retry is removed), and the merchant has one document endpoint instead of separate signed and unsigned ones. Right after a successful signing (step 7), try once to fetch DDS Get Signed Mandate PDF GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddaId}/signed-pdf and store it in Geidea’s document store; save the reference in Mandate.signed_pdf_ref . If the attempt fails, do nothing more: no retry job and no alert. The next request for the document fetches it (rule 3). Customer: the success page offers the signed PDF ( GET /direct-debit/v1/sign/{mandateId}/signed-pdf , review session required). If it is not stored yet, fetch it from DDS, store it and return it. Merchant: one endpoint , GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/document (path indicative). The Backend chooses the document from the contract’s status; the Portal never asks for “signed” or “unsigned”. The response states documentType = unsigned or signed . Contract status Document Where it comes from Awaiting Customer Details, Not Submitted None Nothing exists at DDS Pending ( PNDG ) Unsigned DDS Get Unsigned Mandate PDF on each request; not stored Pending System Submission ( SUBP ), Pending Bank Approval ( APRP ) Signed Stored copy; if none, fetch DDS Get Signed Mandate PDF , store it, return it Active ( ACCP ) and every later status (Expired, Cancelled, Cancelled — reason) Signed Stored copy; if none, fetch from DDS, store it, return it. The unsigned document is never used Rejected ( RJCT ), Error ( ERR ) Signed if the customer had signed ( signed_at filled); otherwise none As above Denied, Request Expired, Link Expired, Discarded None The customer never signed Controls. Only the signed document is ever stored, so an unsigned copy can never be returned after the customer signs. The caller needs the View Direct Debit permission and access to the contract’s store; a contract of another merchant returns not found. If DDS does not answer, return a clear “not available right now, try again” result, not a server error. A status with no document returns “not available”. Documents are read-only. Test environments only: provide POST /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/signed-pdf for QA without UAE PASS. It must be disabled in production."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","10. Link expiry job","direct-debit/docs/product.html#link-expiry-job","Runs once a day, shortly after midnight ( S1 section 5.4 ). It closes contracts the customer never finished, so they don’t sit open forever. It is not what stops an expired link from working: the review page (section 2 ) and the resend endpoint (section 1 ) compare review_link_expires_at with the current time on every request, so an expired link is refused at once. (Changed 07-Oct-2026: the job was hourly, and the link expired at an exact time rather than at the end of the day.) Pick every mandate where review_link_expires_at < now and the customer hasn’t signed: Contract situation What the job does TBFC, customer never entered details ( mandate_creation_stage = awaiting_customer_instrument ) Nothing exists at DDS, so no DDS call. Set stage link_expired , status_label = Link Expired , expired_at = now Registered at DDS but not signed ( status_code = PNDG ) Call DDS Discard DDA POST /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddaId}/discard so it can’t be signed later. Then status_label = Link Expired , expired_at = now. If Discard fails, retry on the next run; after 3 failures alert Ops In both cases: Subscription status = Cancelled ; every Occurrence status = Cancelled ; raise event contract.link_expired . To proceed, the merchant creates a new contract. Note: DDS also expires unsigned mandates itself after 7 working days ( RQXP ). Geidea’s own expiry (default 7 calendar days) usually comes first; if DDS’s comes first, S4 handles it."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes","Add to the Order Model: Entity.field Type / values Purpose Mandate.signing_notification_count integer Resend limit (step 1) Mandate.signed_at timestamp When DDS confirmed the signature Mandate.signer_process_id string UAE PASS signing reference from the callback Mandate.signing_attempts integer Failed signing attempts Mandate.last_signing_error string Last failure reason from DDS Mandate.signed_pdf_ref string Stored signed PDF Mandate.expired_at timestamp When the link expiry job closed the contract Mandate.mandate_creation_stage — extend values add link_expired TBFC link expired before details were entered Subscription.status — extend values add Cancelled if not present Contract closed before activation Occurrence.status — extend values add Cancelled Collection will never happen because the contract closed Technical tables (not Order Model): review_otp_challenge (mandate_id, otp_hash, expires_at, attempts, resends, locked_until) and review_session (token, mandate_id, expires_at)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","Document","Merchant events raised","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-events-raised","contract.notification_sent , contract.instrument_provided (TBFC), contract.signed , contract.signing_failed , contract.link_expired ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-2","# Point Status a Exact names and casing of the DDS signing-callback parameters ( signerProcessId , ddId , status , statusMsg , ddsError ) to be confirmed against a sandbox run Dev to verify"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S3 — Contract Sending & Signing","Document","Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team (Integration Document v2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#gaps-to-flag-to-george-integration-document-v2-1","# Requirement in this story What v2 has today G1 Verification screen and OTP (SMS or email) before any contract data is shown, on every review link Contract details returned on a plain GET by contract ID G2 Page states ( NOT_AVAILABLE , ALREADY_SIGNED , EXPIRED , OTP_REQUIRED , READY ) Not specified G3 TBFC instrument entry by the customer (section 5 ) Missing G4 Callback confirmed with Get Status of a DDA before updating the mandate (section 7.2 ) Updates status from callback parameters G5 Resend endpoint extending link expiry, SMS/email/WhatsApp, suppression toggle Only records that the partner sent a notification G6 Signed PDF fetched and stored after signing; link expiry job with DDS Discard (sections 9 –10) Missing"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s4-mandate-approval-activation","Delivery slice: 5 · Serves frontend stories: US-14"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-3","After the customer signs, DDS sends the mandate to the Central Bank and the customer’s bank, which approve or reject it within up to 3 working days — most banks within 1 working day, some in 3 to 4, and one or two only after a confirmation call with the customer (DDS, 15-Oct-2026); there is no time limit, so the check runs until a final status . DDS does not notify Geidea when this happens — the Backend has to ask. This story is the daily check that asks DDS for every open mandate’s status and applies the result: activating the subscription when approved, closing the contract when rejected, expired or cancelled. Item Value Trigger Daily job (default 18:15 ) DDS APIs used Get Status of a DDA; Discard DDA Starts from S3 (mandate signed, SUBP ) — but the job checks every mandate that isn’t final Hands over to S5 (collections run for Active mandates) The normal path:"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-3",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Daily mandate status sync job","direct-debit/docs/product.html#daily-mandate-status-sync-job","Runs daily at the configured time (default 18:15 , S1 section 5.4 ). 18:15 is after the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off (DDS go-live checklist: check once a day after 18:00) and early enough that approvals seen today go into tonight’s 19:00 payment file ( S5 ). Selects every mandate still on its way to activation: status_code = PNDG , SUBP or APRP . Also selects Active ( ACCP ) mandates whose expires_on was yesterday, once, to record EXPR . Other Active mandates are not checked daily in MVP (see note below). For each mandate, call DDS Get Status of a DDA GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{dda_id}/status with the credentials of the mandate’s own oic . If the status is the same as stored, only update status_checked_at . If it changed, apply section 3 and write a Mandate Status History row (old status, new status, reason, source = daily_sync ). One mandate failing (timeout, 5xx) must not stop the others. Failed ones are retried by the DDS client ( S1 section 6.5 ) and then picked up the next day. The job must finish before the 19:00 payment file job starts. Alert Ops if it is still running at 18:50. Note — customer cancelling directly with their bank. DDS documents no webhook or push notification for this; the only way to learn it is to ask DDS. MVP does not check Active mandates for it. There is no financial risk: any collection sent afterwards is refused by DDS or the bank (reason code O , mandate cancelled), and S6 section 2.1 then closes the contract. DDS responses (examples from DDS spec): Pending/Signed/With bank/Cancelled/Expired: {\"id\": 975, \"status\": \"SUBP\", \"ddaRefNo\": \"SAND-1113\"} Rejected: adds \"reasonCode\": \"RR05\" Error: adds \"errors\": {\"accountNumber\": [\"INVALID PAYER ACCOUNT...\"]} Active: adds \"cbDdaRefNo\": \"29000003662025000000007\" (Central Bank reference)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Refresh status on demand — removed from the MVP","direct-debit/docs/product.html#refresh-status-on-demand-removed-from-the-mvp","Removed on 07-Oct-2026; no merchant “Refresh status” action is built. Signing is known at once through the signing callback ( S3 section 7 ), and bank approval is picked up by the daily check. The first collection is at least 4 working days out ( S2 rule V15), so waiting for the 18:15 check is rarely material. Operations can re-run the status sync job from the console ( S14 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. What to do for each DDS status","direct-debit/docs/product.html#what-to-do-for-each-dds-status","When a mandate’s status changes, update it as below. “Not-yet-submitted occurrences” means Occurrences with status = Scheduled and payment_created = false . DDS status Plain meaning Mandate updates ( status_code , status_label , others) Subscription Occurrences Also PNDG Waiting for customer to sign Pending No change No change — SUBP Signed, DDS about to send to bank Pending System Submission No change No change — APRP With Central Bank / customer’s bank Pending Bank Approval No change No change — ACCP Approved — Active Active; cb_dda_ref_no = cbDdaRefNo ; activated_at = now Pending Activation → Active No change ( S5 collects them) Unless notifications are suppressed: customer SMS/email/WhatsApp “Your direct debit with <merchant> is active. Track it in the DD app.” Event contract.activated RJCT Bank rejected it Rejected; reject_reason_code = reasonCode ; reject_reason_label = plain label (section 3.1 ) Cancelled Not-yet-submitted → Cancelled Event contract.rejected DENY Customer declined in UAE PASS Not used: banks do not support DENY (DDS, 15-Oct-2026). If ever returned, log and alert Ops RQXP Customer didn’t sign within 7 working days Request Expired. Call DDS Discard DDA to close it Cancelled Not-yet-submitted → Cancelled Event contract.link_expired ERR Error at DDS/bank Error; last_dds_error = the errors object as text. Final: the contract’s life ends here. Merchant and Ops can see the status and error; the merchant must create a new contract Cancelled Not-yet-submitted → Cancelled Alert Ops; event contract.error CANC Cancelled (by merchant via S8 ; confirmed by the cancellation status check, S8 section 5 ) Cancelled; cancelled_at = now Cancelled Not-yet-submitted → Cancelled . Payments already sent to DDS keep being tracked by S5 Event contract.cancelled EXPR End date passed Expired Completed Not-yet-submitted → Cancelled Event contract.expired SUSP Suspended (Phase 2) Not used: banks do not support SUSP (DDS, 15-Oct-2026) No automatic change; log and alert Ops — — — 3.1 Bank reject reasons ( RJCT ) — plain labels to store in reject_reason_label Code Label Code Label RR01 Signature incorrect RR13 Account not in AED RR02 Signature missing / more signatures needed RR14 Signatory not authorised for this amount RR04 Invalid account RR15 Account blocked by court order RR05 Account name doesn’t match RR16 Account blocked by law enforcement RR06 Account holder deceased RR17 Account blocked by Central Bank RR07 Account closed RR18 Not accepted by customer’s bank RR08 Card cancelled RR50 Invalid consumer number RR09 Card expired RR54 Rejected by originator RR10 Card blocked RR55 Originator confirmation not obtained RR11 Invalid card RR90 Compliance issue RR12 Dormant account RR91 / RR92 Scanned mandate unusable / details mismatch"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Activation and the first collection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#activation-and-the-first-collection","A collection can only go into a payment file if the mandate is already Active when the file is built (19:00 the day before the due date). The Backend only learns a mandate is Active at the 18:15 check. So whether the first collection makes it depends on when the approval is seen . Example — first collection due 5 Oct: Bank approves Backend sees it 5 Oct collection 2 Oct, 11:00 2 Oct 18:15 check Goes in the 4 Oct 19:00 file → collected on 5 Oct 4 Oct, 15:00 4 Oct 18:15 check Just in time → 4 Oct 19:00 file 4 Oct, 19:30 5 Oct 18:15 check Missed — the 4 Oct file was already built Rule for this story: when a mandate becomes Active, do not change any occurrence. S5 handles the missed case: at file-build time, an occurrence whose mandate is not yet Active is marked Skipped with skip_reason = not_active_in_time . (Moving a skipped collection’s amount onto a later one — manual rollover — is out of MVP scope.) Prevention (agreed): S2 rule V15 requires the first collection date to be at least 4 working days (configurable) after contract creation, so normal approval times don’t cause a missed first collection."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-1","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Mandate Status History (new entity) history_id , mandate_id , old_status , new_status , reason_code , changed_at , source ( create_dda , signing_callback , daily_sync , link_expiry_job ) Audit of every mandate status change, same pattern as Payment Status History Mandate.status_checked_at timestamp Last time DDS was asked Mandate.last_dds_error string ERR details Subscription.status — extend values add Completed Contract ran to its end date Subscription.activated_at , Subscription.closed_at timestamps Activation and cancellation/completion time"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","Document","Merchant events raised","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-events-raised-1","contract.activated , contract.rejected , contract.link_expired , contract.error , contract.cancelled , contract.expired ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-3","# Point Status a Customer activation message wording and channels Open — finalise near implementation"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation","Document","Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team (Integration Document v2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#gaps-to-flag-to-george-integration-document-v2-2","# Requirement in this story What v2 has today G1 Daily status sync job and full status handling table (section 1 , section 3 ), incl. ERR as final and RQXP → Discard Background jobs declared out of scope G2 Mandate status table must include APRP , ERR , RQXP (and SUSP as Phase 2) consistently Status reference table lists 7 codes; lifecycle table lists different ones G3 Mandate Status History entity Missing"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s5-daily-collection-cycle","Delivery slice: 6 · Serves frontend stories: the collection statuses shown in US-18"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-4","Every evening the Backend sends DDS one file listing tomorrow’s collections; over the next day it checks what the bank did with each one. This story covers that whole cycle: choosing which collections to send, building and uploading the file before DDS’s 20:00 cut-off, tracking each payment until it is paid or rejected, recording the actual debit date and FT reference, and closing the subscription when nothing is left to collect. Item Value Trigger Scheduled jobs (times in S1 section 5.4 ) DDS APIs used Upload Bulk Direct Debit Payment Requests; Bulk Payment Requests Status Report; Day-End Reconciliation Report Order Model entities written Occurrence, Payment, Payment Status History, Payment File (new), Subscription Hands over to S6 (rejected payments), S12 (paid payments to APEX)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Story summary","Document","The daily timeline for one collection due on day D","direct-debit/docs/product.html#the-daily-timeline-for-one-collection-due-on-day-d","When (UAE) What happens Who D-1, 18:15 Mandate status check ( S4 ) — so newly approved mandates count Geidea D-1, 19:00 Select collections due on D, build the file, upload to DDS (sections 1 –2) Geidea D-1, 20:00 DDS hard cut-off. Anything not uploaded misses day D DDS D, ~01:00 DDS sends the batch to the Central Bank; customer’s bank debits the account DDS / banks D, 11:00 – 18:00 Banks confirm; at the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off pay / no-pay is final (“Technical issues at Paying Bank” for a bank that did not settle) Banks / Central Bank D, 18:30 Payment status check: paid / not paid (section 3 ) Geidea D, after the 18:30 check (configurable) Collections that turned Paid go to APEX ( S12 ). They do not wait for the FT reference Geidea D, 20:00 – 24:00 Central Bank credits the money to Geidea’s OIC account Central Bank D+1, 06:30 FT reference check and Day-End report (actual debit date) (section 3 ). For FinOps reconciliation; payout does not wait for it Geidea If D is a weekend or holiday, the file is still sent on D-1, but the bank debits on the next working day. That’s why the actual debit date must be recorded (section 3.4 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-4",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Choose tomorrow’s collections (payment file job, part 1)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#choose-tomorrows-collections-payment-file-job-part-1","The job starts at 19:00 (configurable) and works one OIC at a time, because each OIC has its own DDS login and its own file. Candidates: Occurrences with status = Scheduled , payment_created = false and due_date = tomorrow (D). Left-behind check: any Occurrence still Scheduled with due_date ≤ today was missed (e.g. the job failed yesterday). DDS rejects past dates, so mark it Skipped with skip_reason = missed_submission and alert Ops. For each candidate, run these checks in order . The first one that applies decides; if none applies, the collection is sent. # Check If true Occurrence result C1 Contract is closed (Mandate final status, or Subscription Cancelled / Completed ) Don’t send Cancelled C2 Mandate is not yet Active ( status_code ≠ ACCP ) Don’t send Skipped , skip_reason = not_active_in_time C3 Subscription is Paused Don’t send Skipped , skip_reason = paused C3a The customer has signed a cancellation of the contract ( S8 section 4 ) Don’t send Skipped , skip_reason = cancellation_signed ; no rollover C4 due_date is after Mandate.expires_on Don’t send Cancelled C5 amount is outside the mandate’s min_amount – max_amount (should never happen — S2 / S6 / S7 prevent it) Don’t send; alert Ops Error C6 Minimum gap safety net fails (section 1.1 ) Don’t send Skipped , skip_reason = min_gap , then automatic rollover ( S6 section 3 )"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1.1 The minimum gap rule — what it is, what can break it, how we stay inside it","direct-debit/docs/product.html#the-minimum-gap-rule-what-it-is-what-can-break-it-how-we-stay-inside-it","What DDS enforces. On one mandate, two successful debits must be at least a minimum number of days apart. The number depends on the mandate’s frequency ( S1 section 5.2 ): Monthly 23, Weekly 4, Quarterly 80, etc. It is counted from the date the money was actually debited , not from the due date. DDS confirmed: a Monthly mandate debited on 15 Sep cannot be debited again before 8 Oct (15 Sep + 23). If we send a collection earlier than that, DDS rejects it. Why a normal schedule is safe. Collections generated on the same day each month are 28–31 days apart, comfortably above 23. Weekly (7 vs 4) and Quarterly (~90 vs 80) are the same. So the rule only bites when something moves an actual debit date closer to the next collection. The three things that can push us inside the gap, and what prevents each: Cause Example (Monthly, collections on the 1st) Prevention A retry succeeds late 1 Sep rejected, retried 20 Sep, debited 21 Sep → next collection 1 Oct is only 10 days later Retry deadline ( S6 section 1.2 ): retries stop early enough that any successful retry still leaves the gap before the next collection Merchant edits a date Merchant moves the October collection to 10 Sep Edit checks ( S7 section 2 ): an edit that breaks the gap is rejected Bank holiday pushes the debit late 1 Feb collection debited 7 Feb after a long holiday → 1 Mar is only 22 days later Can’t be prevented in advance → safety net check C6 below Safety net — check C6 , run on every collection just before it is sent: Find the reference date — the latest of: the actual debit date ( Payment.debit_date , or the date it turned ACCP if the Day-End report hasn’t arrived yet) of the most recent paid Payment on this mandate; for any Payment on this mandate still in progress ( PNDG , APRP , RPND ), the date it is expected to be debited: its due date, or for a retry the next working day after it was sent — moved to the next working day if that is a holiday. Earliest allowed date = reference date + minimum gap days. If tomorrow (D) is before the earliest allowed date: don’t send. Mark the Occurrence Skipped with skip_reason = min_gap , then run automatic rollover ( S6 section 3 ) so its amount is added to the next collection. Raise occurrence.skipped with the earliest allowed date. If the mandate has no earlier payments, the check passes. Examples (Monthly, gap 23): Situation Reference date Earliest allowed Collection Result August debited on 1 Aug 1 Aug 24 Aug 1 Sep Sent September retried and debited 7 Sep (within the retry deadline) 7 Sep 30 Sep 1 Oct Sent February debited 7 Feb after a holiday 7 Feb 2 Mar 1 Mar Skipped (min_gap); amount rolls onto 1 Apr Why skip + rollover, not move the date: moving one collection’s date would push the gap problem onto the following collection and could ripple through the rest of the schedule. Rollover keeps every date as signed and still recovers the money on the next collection (subject to the contract maximum, S6 section 3 ). If rollover is off or blocked, the merchant can recover the amount by editing an upcoming collection ( S7 section 2 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Build and upload the payment file (payment file job, part 2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#build-and-upload-the-payment-file-payment-file-job-part-2","2.1 Create a Payment for each collection being sent (same transaction as the file record): Order Model field Set to Payment.occurrence_id The Occurrence Payment.payment_ref_no {dda_reference_number}-{6-digit sequence} , where the sequence is the Occurrence sequence_number padded to 6 digits. E.g. SAND-1114-000003 . Stays the same through retries. A collection re-set after an Error ( S7 section 2 ) gets a new Payment with the next unused number on the contract — one more than the highest sequence or payment number used so far (e.g. SAND-1114-000013 on a 12-collection contract); a number is never reused. Saved in Payment.payment_seq . Payment.claimed_amount Occurrence amount Payment.payment_file_ref The new Payment File record Payment.retry_count 0 Occurrence.payment_created true — from now on the Occurrence can’t be edited 2.2 Build the CSV file. One file per OIC per day. File name: dd-payments-{oic}-{ddMMyyyy} using today’s date, e.g. dd-payments-560000001-12092026 . Columns, in this order: ddaRefNo, paymentRefNo, claimedAmount, dueDate, remarks . CSV column Source Example ddaRefNo Mandate.dda_reference_number SAND-1114 paymentRefNo Payment.payment_ref_no SAND-1114-000003 claimedAmount Payment.claimed_amount 5000 dueDate Occurrence.due_date as dd/MM/yyyy 05/10/2026 remarks Occurrence.occurrence_id (optional, for our own tracing) If there are no collections for an OIC, send no file. 2.3 Upload. DDS Upload Bulk Direct Debit Payment Requests POST /v1/merchant/payment-files (multipart, the CSV file). Important: DDS accepts the file all or nothing . If a single row is wrong, DDS rejects the entire file and nothing is collected. DDS answer What to do 201 {\"paymentFileID\": \"54234\", \"records\": [{\"id\": 3351, \"customerDdsRefNo\": \"SAND-1112\", \"paymentRefNo\": \"000007\", \"status\": \"PNDG\"}]} Save paymentFileID on the Payment File. Match each record to our Payment by customerDdsRefNo + the 6-digit paymentRefNo ; set Payment.dds_payment_id = id , current_status = PNDG ; Occurrence status = Submitted ; history row. Event occurrence.submitted 400 , whole file rejected, with per-row errors Take out the rows DDS complained about: their Occurrences become Error (store DDS’s reason), their Payments are marked not submitted, and payment_created goes back to false . Upload the remaining rows once more as a new file. Alert Ops and raise occurrence.error for each removed row 400 again on the second upload Stop. Leave everything unsubmitted, alert Ops urgently (there are still minutes before 20:00 for manual action) No answer / timeout We don’t know if DDS got it. Upload the same file once more: if DDS replies “duplicate payment reference” for every row, the first upload worked — mark all as Submitted and fill in dds_payment_id later from the Day-End report (section 3.4 ). Otherwise handle the answer as above 2.4 Deadline. Alert Ops at 19:45 if any OIC’s file is not yet accepted. After 20:00, collections not accepted will be caught by the left-behind check (section 1 , item 2) tomorrow. Point for the DD Backend team to consider: the whole chain (select → build → upload → handle a rejection → re-upload) has to finish inside 19:00–20:00. The DD Backend team must confirm the job completes comfortably inside this window at expected volumes, or propose a different window (e.g. an earlier start) if it isn’t practical. Scheduler times are configuration ( S1 section 5.4 ), set by Geidea within DDS’s windows and tuned from production experience."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Track each payment until it is finished","direct-debit/docs/product.html#track-each-payment-until-it-is-finished","3.1 Status checks. For every Payment File that still has unfinished payments, call DDS Bulk Payment Requests Status Report GET /v1/merchant/payment-files/{paymentFileID}/status at the configured times (default 18:30 on the due date, after the Central Bank’s 18:00 cut-off , with an optional earlier check at 14:00; then 06:30 and 12:00 each following day for the FT reference). A payment is finished when it is ACCP with an FT reference, or RJCT / ERR with no retry running. Stop checking a file when all its payments are finished, or after 7 days (then alert Ops). The response has one record per payment with status , reasonCode and, once paid, ftRefNo . 3.2 What each payment status means for the Occurrence. On every change: update Payment.current_status , current_status_label , reason_code ; write a Payment Status History row; update the Occurrence as below. DDS payment status Plain meaning Payment label Occurrence status Event PNDG Received by DDS, being validated Pending Submitted — APRP Due date reached, with the bank Under Process Submitted — ACCP Paid Accepted-Paid Paid ; collected_at = now occurrence.paid RJCT Customer’s bank refused (reason code tells why) Rejected-Not Paid Rejected ( S6 takes over) occurrence.rejected ERR Technical error at DDS/Central Bank Error Error — “Payment validation error”. The Payment keeps ERR for history. No retry and no bounce memo (DDS, 15-Oct-2026: representment is for RJCT only). The merchant re-sets the collection with Amend ( S7 section 2 ); the next payment file sends a fresh Payment with a new payment reference (2.1). Not rolled over automatically occurrence.error RPND A retry is in progress ( S6 ) Representment Pending Submitted — 3.3 Paid / not-paid reason codes (store the code in reason_code , show the label): Code Label Code Label 0 Paid in full N Dormant account 1 Partly paid O Mandate cancelled A Account closed P Not honoured — customer to contact bank C Compliance issue R Details don’t match mandate D Account holder deceased S Payment stopped by customer E Card blocked T Technical issue at customer’s bank F Card expired U Amount not yet due G Card cancelled V / W Account closed (authority / bank compliance) I Insufficient funds X / Y / Z Account blocked (court / law enforcement / Central Bank) L Card limit exceeded M Refused at originator’s request 3.4 FT reference and actual debit date. The FT reference ( ftRefNo ) is the bank transfer reference; it ties the payment to Geidea’s bank statement. It does not arrive with “paid”: DDS places it between 01:00 and 05:00 the next day and recommends checking from 06:00; Geidea checks at 06:30. Keep checking (section 3.1 ) until it is there, then set Payment.ft_ref_no . It is used by FinOps for reconciliation; pricing and payout do not need it. Set Payment.federation_flag = true as soon as current_status = ACCP . The FT reference is not a condition (changed 07-Oct-2026, following the Business decision of 06-Oct-2026 that merchant payout is T+1). S12 picks these up for the next APEX file. Actual debit date: every day at 06:30, call DDS Day-End Reconciliation Report GET /v1/merchant/payments/day-end?date={yesterday, yyyy-mm-dd} . For each ACCP record, match to our Payment by customerDdsRefNo + paymentRefNo and set Payment.debit_date = that date. Also fill dds_payment_id and ft_ref_no if still missing. HTTP 404 = no payments that day (not an error). Until debit_date is known, other stories use the date the payment turned ACCP as a stand-in. This includes the APEX file ( S12 ), which is now built before the Day-End report arrives. If the report disagrees with what we hold (status, amount, FT reference), log an exception for Ops."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Close the subscription when nothing is left","direct-debit/docs/product.html#close-the-subscription-when-nothing-is-left","After every Occurrence update, check the Subscription. When every Occurrence is in a final state — Paid , Failed , Cancelled , or Skipped (once no Scheduled collection is left, a Skipped one has nowhere to roll to, so it counts as final) — set Subscription.status = Completed and closed_at = now. Raise subscription.completed . The Mandate stays Active at DDS until its end date (then S4 records EXPR )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-2","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Payment File (new entity) payment_file_ref (PK), oic , file_name , business_date , row_count , dds_payment_file_id , status ( Uploaded / Rejected / Resubmitted ), uploaded_at , dds_response One row per uploaded CSV Payment.payment_file_ref FK → Payment File Replaces storing only DDS’s payment_file_id Payment.claimed_amount decimal Amount actually sent to DDS Payment.debit_date date Actual date the customer was debited (Day-End report) — used for the minimum gap rule and by APEX Occurrence.skip_reason paused | not_active_in_time | min_gap | missed_submission Why a collection was skipped Occurrence.collected_at timestamp When it turned Paid Occurrence.error_reason string Why it is in Error"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","Document","Merchant events raised","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-events-raised-2","occurrence.submitted , occurrence.skipped , occurrence.paid , occurrence.rejected , occurrence.error , subscription.completed ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-4","# Point Status a Whether DDS accepts a second file on the same day with the same file name (needed for the re-upload after a rejection) Open — ask DDS"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S5 — Daily Collection Cycle","Document","Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team (Integration Document v2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#gaps-to-flag-to-george-integration-document-v2-3","# Requirement in this story What v2 has today G1 Payment file job, status tracking, FT Ref and Day-End jobs (sections 1 –3) Background jobs declared out of scope G2 Minimum gap check from the actual debit date (section 1.1 ) Missing G3 RPND (Representment Pending) in payment and collection statuses Missing from both status tables G4 Whole-file rejection handling and single re-upload (section 2.3 ) Missing G5 Payment File entity, Payment.debit_date , Occurrence.skip_reason Missing"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S6 — Failed Collection Handling","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s6-failed-collection-handling","Delivery slice: 7 · Serves frontend stories: US-22 , US-23 , US-26"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S6 — Failed Collection Handling","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-5","When the customer’s bank refuses a collection, the merchant can try again up to 3 times — the same rule as a bounced cheque (1 original + 3 re-presentations = 4 attempts). If it still fails, the collection is final Failed , and if the contract allows rollover, its amount is added to a later collection automatically. The bank’s bounce memo is available as proof. Item Value Users Merchant (retry); system (final failure, automatic rollover) DDS APIs used Payment Representment; Payment Bounce Memo Starts from S5 marks an Occurrence Rejected Related S7 (pause, edit). Manual rollover of Skipped collections is out of MVP scope Important: a retry re-uses the same DDS payment (same dds_payment_id , same payment_ref_no ). DDS moves its status RJCT → RPND → ACCP or RJCT . A collection failed by the Central Bank as “Technical issues at Paying Bank” (bank did not settle by 18:00) is a normal, retryable rejection; its reason code is T (DDS REST API v2.0; DDS-confirmed 15-Oct-2026). No new Payment row is created for a retry."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S6 — Failed Collection Handling","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-5",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Merchant retry","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-retry","POST /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{contractId}/collections/{collectionId}/retry — no request body. 1.1 Allowed only when all are true (otherwise 422 with the matching code): Check Error code User holds the Subscription actions permission ( S13 ) ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED (403) Occurrence status = Rejected and its Payment current_status = RJCT . A collection in Error is never retried: DDS represents RJCT only (15-Oct-2026); it is re-set with Amend ( S7 section 2 ) NOT_RETRYABLE Reason code is not a terminal code (section 2.1 ) NOT_RETRYABLE Payment.retry_count < 3 RETRIES_EXHAUSTED Today is on or before the collection’s retry_deadline (section 1.2 ) RETRY_WINDOW_CLOSED Mandate status_code = ACCP CONTRACT_NOT_ACTIVE Subscription is not Paused SUBSCRIPTION_PAUSED 1.2 Retry deadline — why retries must stop early. A successful retry is a debit, so DDS’s minimum gap ( S5 section 1.1 ) is counted from it. If a retry succeeds late in the cycle, too few days are left before the next collection and DDS rejects that next collection. So every collection gets a retry deadline : the last day a retry may be sent such that, even if it succeeds, the next collection still respects the gap. retry_deadline = next collection's due date − minimum gap days − 1 working day Next collection = the next Occurrence in the same subscription (any status except Cancelled ). For the last collection, use Mandate.expires_on instead. Minimum gap days = from the frequency table ( S1 section 5.2 ), using the mandate’s payment_frequency . −1 working day because DDS processes a retry on the next working day, so the money moves one working day after we send it. If the result lands on a non-working day, move it back to the previous working day. Examples: Contract Collection due Next collection Minimum gap Retry deadline Check Monthly 1 Sep 1 Oct 23 7 Sep Retry sent 7 Sep → debited 8 Sep → 1 Oct is 23 days later ✓ Monthly 1 Feb 1 Mar 23 5 Feb Debited 6 Feb → 23 days to 1 Mar ✓ (a fixed 7-day window would fail here) Weekly Mon 7 Sep Mon 14 Sep 4 Wed 9 Sep Debited Thu 10 Sep → 4 days ✓ Quarterly 1 Jan 1 Apr 80 ~9 Jan Debited ~10 Jan → 80 days ✓ Rules: Store Occurrence.retry_deadline . Calculate it when the Occurrence is created ( S2 ) and recalculate it whenever its own date or the next collection’s date changes ( S7 ). A retry request after the deadline is refused with RETRY_WINDOW_CLOSED . The day after the deadline, the collection becomes final Failed if it is still Rejected — see section 2 , trigger F2. If the rejection itself only arrives after the deadline (possible on Weekly contracts), there is no retry window: the collection becomes Failed straight away. 1.3 Call DDS. Payment Representment POST /v1/merchant/payments/{dds_payment_id}/represent . DDS answer What to do 200 {\"message\": \"Updated Successfully\"} Payment.retry_count + 1; current_status = RPND (label Representment Pending); Occurrence status = Submitted ; last_retry_at = now; history row; event occurrence.retry_submitted . S5 status checks then track the result on the same Payment File 400 “You can represent max 3 times” Set retry_count = 3 ; apply the final-failure rule (section 2 ) 404 (not found / not rejected at DDS) Don’t change anything; refresh the payment’s status from DDS and return NOT_RETRYABLE No answer / timeout Don’t change the status. Return RETRY_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN ; the next S5 status check shows whether DDS moved it to RPND"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. When a rejection becomes a final failure","direct-debit/docs/product.html#when-a-rejection-becomes-a-final-failure","A Rejected collection can still be retried, so it isn’t final yet. It becomes Failed (final, failed_at = now) at the first of these moments: # Trigger Why F1 The 3rd retry comes back RJCT ( retry_count = 3 ) No retries left F2 The retry deadline (section 1.2 ) has passed and the collection is still Rejected with no retry in progress. If a retry was in progress at the deadline, wait for its result; if it comes back RJCT , apply F2 then A retry after this point could break the minimum gap for the next collection F3 The payment is refused with a terminal reason code (section 2.1 ) Retrying cannot help F4 The Mandate is closed ( CANC , EXPR ) Nothing more can be collected How F2 runs: a daily job at 07:00 ( S1 section 5.4 ) finds collections whose retry_deadline was yesterday or earlier, still Rejected , with no retry in progress. It marks them Failed and runs automatic rollover (section 3 ) straight away — well before the next collection’s payment file is built. Example (Monthly, collections on the 1st): the 1 Sep collection is rejected. Retry deadline = 7 Sep. The merchant retries on 3 Sep; it is rejected again, and no more retries are sent. On 8 Sep at 07:00 the job marks September Failed and rolls its amount onto the 1 Oct collection. Had the 3 Sep retry succeeded, September would be Paid and October would go through normally. On Failed : raise occurrence.failed ; the bounce memo (section 4 ) is the proof of failure. 2.1 Refusals that end the contract Some refusal reasons mean this mandate can never be collected again — e.g. the account is closed or the card expired. These are the terminal reason codes in S1 section 5.5 (A, D, E, F, G, O, V, W, X, Y, Z). When a payment comes back RJCT with a terminal reason code: The Occurrence goes straight to Failed — no retry allowed (retry returns NOT_RETRYABLE ), no rollover. The contract is closed: Mandate.status_label = Cancelled — <reason label> (e.g. “Cancelled — Account closed”); closure_reason_code = the reason code; closed_at = now. Subscription status = Cancelled ; every not-yet-submitted Occurrence → Cancelled . Raise contract.cancelled with the reason. This is a Geidea-side closure. The mandate is not cancelled at DDS (that would need the customer’s UAE PASS signature, S8 ); nothing more will be sent against it."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Automatic rollover","direct-debit/docs/product.html#automatic-rollover","In plain terms: if a collection finally fails, its amount is added onto the next collection, so the merchant doesn’t lose it. Two limits protect the customer: the new amount can never exceed the contract’s maximum per collection, and only a limited number of failures in a row can be carried forward. Runs immediately when an Occurrence becomes Failed , or is Skipped by the minimum-gap safety net ( S5 check C6 ). Only if Subscription.rollover_enabled = true (always false for Fixed-amount contracts). If rollover is off or blocked, the merchant can recover the amount by editing an upcoming collection ( S7 section 2 ). 3.1 The rules, in order Find the destination: the next Occurrence in the schedule (after the failed one) with status = Scheduled and payment_created = false . Skipped and Cancelled ones are passed over. If there is none → set rolled_over = no_destination on the failed one. Stop. Check the streak limit. Count the rollover streak : the number of Occurrences marked rolled_over since the most recent Paid one on this subscription (anywhere in the schedule). If streak ≥ rollovers_allowed → set rolled_over = exhausted . Stop. Check the ceiling: new amount = destination amount + failed Occurrence’s amount . If new amount > Mandate.max_amount → set rolled_over = blocked_by_ceiling . Stop. (Don’t count this against the streak.) Roll over: Destination: amount = new amount; amount_source = rollover_adjusted . Failed Occurrence: rolled_over = rolled_over ; rolled_over_to_occurrence_id = destination. Its status stays Failed . Raise occurrence.rolled_over (or occurrence.rollover_blocked for the stop cases). The amount moved is the failed collection’s current amount, which may already include an earlier rollover. 3.2 Worked example. Variable contract: installment AED 5,000, max_amount AED 15,000, rollovers_allowed = 2. Month What happens Streak before Result Jan Paid — Streak resets to 0 Feb Fails (5,000) 0 Rolled onto Mar: Mar = 5,000 + 5,000 = 10,000 . Streak now 1 Mar Fails (10,000) 1 Rolled onto Apr: Apr = 5,000 + 10,000 = 15,000 (= max, allowed). Streak now 2 Apr Fails (15,000) 2 Streak = limit → exhausted . Apr stays Failed; May stays 5,000 May Paid — Streak resets to 0 Same contract, but the merchant had edited April up to 7,000 ( S7 ): Mar fails (10,000) → Apr would be 7,000 + 10,000 = 17,000 > 15,000 → blocked_by_ceiling . Mar stays Failed at 10,000; Apr stays 7,000. ( S2 rule V13 makes this impossible with the original schedule; it can only happen after edits.) Why the streak resets on Paid: a successful collection shows the customer is paying again, so earlier carried-forward amounts are no longer stacking up."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Bounce memo (proof of failure)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#bounce-memo-proof-of-failure","GET /direct-debit/v1/payments/{collectionId}/bounce-memo returns the PDF. Backend calls DDS Payment Bounce Memo GET /v1/merchant/payments/{dds_payment_id}/bounce-memo , stores the PDF on first fetch ( Payment.bounce_memo_ref ) and serves the stored copy afterwards. Available for any Payment that has been RJCT at least once. Otherwise return 404 BOUNCE_MEMO_NOT_AVAILABLE . Not yet tested with DDS: no rejected payment has been run through DDS yet, so exactly when the memo becomes available is unconfirmed. Build as above and verify in the first decline test."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S6 — Failed Collection Handling","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-3","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Occurrence.rolled_over — extend values add no_destination to none / rolled_over / blocked_by_ceiling / exhausted No later collection to roll onto Occurrence.rolled_over_to_occurrence_id FK → Occurrence, on the source Replaces rolled_over_from_occurrence_id on the destination (one destination can receive from several sources; find them with a reverse lookup) Occurrence.failed_at timestamp When it became final Failed Payment.last_retry_at timestamp Last representment Payment.bounce_memo_ref string Stored bounce memo PDF Mandate.closure_reason_code , Mandate.closed_at string, timestamp Contract closed by a terminal refusal (section 2.1 ) Mandate.status_label — extend values add Cancelled — <reason label> Shown to merchant and Ops Occurrence.retry_deadline date Last day a retry may be sent (section 1.2 ); recalculated when this or the next collection’s date changes"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S6 — Failed Collection Handling","Document","Merchant events raised","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-events-raised-3","occurrence.retry_submitted , occurrence.failed , occurrence.rolled_over , occurrence.rollover_blocked ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S6 — Failed Collection Handling","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-5","# Point Status a Bounce memo behaviour to be confirmed in the first DDS decline test Open — pending test"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S6 — Failed Collection Handling","Document","Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team (Integration Document v2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#gaps-to-flag-to-george-integration-document-v2-4","# Requirement in this story What v2 has today G1 Retry deadline per collection; retries refused after it; still-rejected collections become Failed the next day and roll over (section 1.2 , section 2 F2) Retries allowed until exhausted; rollover waits “until next collection becomes effective” G2 Terminal reason codes end the contract (section 2.1 ) Missing G3 Automatic rollover also for collections Skipped by the minimum-gap safety net Missing G4 Rollover states none / rolled_over / blocked_by_ceiling / exhausted / no_destination Uses None / Auto / Manual / Blocked / Exhausted G5 Manual rollover is not in MVP v2 routes manual rollover through the retry endpoint — remove"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s7-contract-subscription-management","Delivery slice: 8 · Serves frontend stories: US-19 , US-20 , US-34 (discard), US-35 (edit notes)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-6","Everything a merchant can change on a contract after creating it, without cancelling it: pause and resume collections, amend the date or amount of any future collection (like DDS’s Amend Payment), discard a contract the customer never signed, and update internal notes. None of these touch DDS except discard — DDS never sees the schedule. Manual rollover of skipped collections (and its undo) is out of MVP scope. Action Endpoint User permission ( S13 ) Pause POST /direct-debit/v1/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/pause Subscription actions Resume POST /direct-debit/v1/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resume Subscription actions Amend a collection PATCH /direct-debit/v1/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/collections Subscription actions Discard POST /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/discard Contract actions Update notes PUT /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/notes Contract actions If the user does not hold the permission, return 403 ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED . The lock rule used throughout: a collection can be changed only while status = Scheduled and payment_created = false , and not after 18:45 on the day before its due date (the payment file job starts at 19:00 and may already be picking it up). After that, it belongs to S5."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-6",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Pause and resume","direct-debit/docs/product.html#pause-and-resume","Pause stops future collections for a while; the mandate stays Active at DDS and at the bank (Geidea-only, DDS is not told). Allowed when: Subscription status = Active and Mandate status_code = ACCP . Else 422 CANNOT_PAUSE . Request: { \"reason\": \"Customer travelling\" } (optional, max 500). Set Subscription.status = Paused , paused_at = now, pause_reason . What it affects (forward only): collections already sent to DDS ( payment_created = true ) carry on normally. Any collection whose file is built while paused is marked Skipped with skip_reason = paused ( S5 check C3 ). Event subscription.paused . Resume Allowed when Subscription status = Paused and Mandate status_code = ACCP . Else 422 CANNOT_RESUME . Set status = Active , resumed_at = now. Return the next Scheduled collection date. Collections skipped during the pause stay Skipped . Their amounts are not collected (manual rollover is out of MVP scope). Event subscription.resumed . Example: paused 20 Jan, resumed 10 Mar. February (due 5 Feb) and March (due 5 Mar) are Skipped. April collects normally."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Amend a collection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#amend-a-collection","What it is. The schedule is generated when the contract is created, but the merchant can change the due date and/or amount of any future collection at any time before it is sent to DDS — the same as the per-row Amend Payment action on DDS’s own portal (seen in the live DDS test: current due date and amount shown, new due date and new amount entered, Confirm). It also gives the merchant an escape route: if a corner case stops a collection (e.g. it was skipped and rollover couldn’t help), the merchant can raise a later collection’s amount, within the contract’s limits, to recover the money. DDS never sees the schedule, so nothing is sent to DDS; the change only affects which date and amount go into a future payment file. 2.1 One API, at subscription level — validates and saves in the same call. PATCH /direct-debit/v1/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/collections Why subscription level, not one collection at a time: the gap rules compare each collection with its neighbours. Some changes are only valid together — e.g. moving October later is only allowed if November also moves later. Checked one at a time, whichever edit goes first is refused because its neighbour is still in the old place. Sending all changes in one call lets the Backend check the final schedule. A single change is simply a list with one item. { \"scheduleVersion\": 3, \"reason\": \"Recover skipped September amount\", \"changes\": [ { \"collectionId\": \"c-oct\", \"newDueDate\": \"2026-10-15\" }, { \"collectionId\": \"c-nov\", \"newDueDate\": \"2026-11-10\", \"newAmount\": 10000.00 } ] } Field M/O Rule scheduleVersion M Must equal the subscription’s current schedule_version . Protects against two users editing at the same time reason O Max 250 chars; stored on every changed collection changes[] M 1 or more items. Send only the collections being changed, never the whole schedule — so a stale screen can’t overwrite collections the merchant didn’t touch changes[].collectionId M A collection of this subscription; each ID at most once changes[].newDueDate , changes[].newAmount O At least one per item How the Backend processes the call: Load the subscription’s current schedule and apply all changes to a copy of it in memory. Run every rule below against that resulting schedule — gap and order checks use the new neighbours. If any rule fails: save nothing; return all failures, each tagged with its collectionId . If all pass: save every change in one transaction (all or nothing). 2.2 Validation rules. “Gap” = the minimum gap days for the mandate’s frequency ( S1 section 5.2 ), e.g. Monthly 23. # Rule Error code Whole request A0 scheduleVersion equals the current Subscription.schedule_version SCHEDULE_CHANGED (409) — portal reloads the schedule A1 User holds the Subscription actions permission ( S13 ) ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED (403) A2 Mandate is Active ( ACCP ); Subscription is Active or Paused CONTRACT_NOT_ACTIVE A13 Every collectionId belongs to this subscription and appears only once INVALID_COLLECTION Each changed collection A3 Collection status = Scheduled and not yet sent ( payment_created = false ) , or status = Error (its Payment ended in ERR , a payment-validation error — B8 ). An Error collection must be given a new due date COLLECTION_NOT_EDITABLE A4 Not after 18:45 on the day before the collection’s current due date (the 19:00 file job may already be picking it up) COLLECTION_LOCKED A5 At least one of newDueDate / newAmount sent and different from the current value NOTHING_TO_CHANGE A6 Fixed-amount contract: amount cannot change (date can) FIXED_AMOUNT_NOT_EDITABLE A7 Mandate.min_amount ≤ newAmount ≤ Mandate.max_amount AMOUNT_OUT_OF_RANGE A8 Inside the contract period: commences_on ≤ newDueDate ≤ expires_on , and at least 2 days from today DATE_OUTSIDE_CONTRACT Resulting schedule (checked after all changes are applied) A9 Order kept: every collection’s due date is after the previous one’s and before the next one’s . Exception: a collection being re-set from Error ( A3 ) may move to a date after"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Discard an unsigned contract","direct-debit/docs/product.html#discard-an-unsigned-contract","For contracts the customer hasn’t signed yet — e.g. created with a mistake, or signing failed IBAN validation ( S3 section 7.4 ). Contract situation Allowed? What the Backend does TBFC, customer hasn’t entered details ( awaiting_customer_instrument ) Yes Local only, no DDS call DDS call was rejected ( dds_rejected ) Yes Local only, nothing exists at DDS At DDS, unsigned ( status_code = PNDG or RQXP ) Yes Call DDS Discard DDA POST /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddaId}/discard . 200 = done. 404 or error = stop, return DISCARD_FAILED , change nothing DDS outcome unknown ( dds_outcome_unknown ) No — Ops only Return CONTACT_SUPPORT Signed or later ( SUBP , APRP , ACCP , …) No Return CANNOT_DISCARD_SIGNED — use Cancel ( S8 ) On success: status_label = Discarded ; discarded_at , discarded_by ; Subscription status = Cancelled ; every Occurrence status = Cancelled ; the review link now shows NOT_AVAILABLE ( S3 section 2 ). Event contract.discarded . The merchantReference stays used ( S2 check 2.5); a new contract needs a new reference."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Update internal notes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#update-internal-notes","PUT /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/notes with { \"notes\": \"...\" } (max 1,000). Updates Mandate.notes (merchant-only, never shown to the customer or sent to DDS). Allowed in any status. Notes belong to the Mandate, not the Subscription."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-4","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Subscription.paused_at , resumed_at , pause_reason timestamps, string Pause history Mandate.discarded_at , discarded_by timestamp, user ID Discard audit Mandate.status_label — extend values add Discarded , Link Expired ( S3 ) Local-only end states Occurrence.amend_reason string, max 250 Why the merchant amended the collection (section 2 ) Already in the Order Model and used here: Occurrence.amount_source , last_edited_on , edited_by , Subscription.schedule_version ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Merchant events raised","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-events-raised-4","subscription.paused , subscription.resumed , subscription.schedule_amended , contract.discarded ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-6","No open decisions remain on this story. Agreed: the lock time is 18:45 on the day before the due date; both date and amount can be amended."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Out of MVP scope","direct-debit/docs/product.html#out-of-mvp-scope","Manual rollover of Skipped collections (merchant picks a later collection to add the amount to) and Undo rollover. Automatic rollover of Failed collections ( S6 section 3 ) stays in scope."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S7 — Contract & Subscription Management","Document","Gaps to flag to the DD Backend team (Integration Document v2)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#gaps-to-flag-to-george-integration-document-v2-5","# Requirement in this story What v2 has today G1 Amend one or more future collections’ dates and/or amounts in one subscription-level validate-and-save call, rules A0– A13 (section 2 ) Missing (Update Subscription changes notes only) G2 Notes live on the Mandate: PUT /contracts/{mandateId}/notes Notes on the Subscription G3 Discard rules per contract situation, incl. TBFC and RQXP (section 4 ) Discard allowed only at PNDG G4 Remove manual rollover from the retry endpoint Present"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S8 — Contract Cancellation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s8-contract-cancellation","Written 15-Oct-2026. In the MVP scope (Product decision, 15-Oct-2026). Updated 18-Nov-2026: customer emails, link, closing and follow-up rules (v2.7). DDS’s go-live checklist lists the cancellation journey as core. DDS confirmed (15-Oct-2026) that it may follow go-live but strongly recommends it: a customer cannot close a bank account while an active direct debit is on it, so customers will ask merchants to cancel."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S8 — Contract Cancellation","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-7","Item Value Users Merchant Portal user with the Cancel Contract permission ( S13 ); the customer, who signs on Geidea’s page DDS APIs used DDA Cancellation POST /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddaId}/cancellation ; Get Unsigned / Signed Cancellation Form GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddcaid}/cancellation/unsigned-pdf and .../signed-pdf ; UAE PASS signing URL with ddarId={ddcarId} ; cancellation status GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddcaid}/status ; DDA status ( S4 ) Delivery slice 8 (with S7 ) Serves frontend stories US-21 (cancel a contract), US-39 (track a cancellation), US-40 (customer signs the cancellation)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S8 — Contract Cancellation","Document","Rules (Product, 15-Oct-2026)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#rules-product-15-oct-2026","Cancellation is for a DD Active contract only, with the subscription Active or Paused. A contract the customer has not signed is discarded ( S7 section 3 ), not cancelled. Collections continue until the customer signs the cancellation. The mandate stays valid at DDS until the bank cancels it, and DDS keeps accepting payments (DDS, 15-Oct-2026). A merchant who wants collections to stop at once pauses the subscription; the cancel dialog offers this in the same step and says so in a note. Collections stop once the customer has signed. From the signature until the bank’s decision (up to 1 working day) no collection is sent. If the bank rejects the cancellation, collections resume. A cancellation request cannot be withdrawn (DDS has no API for it). If the customer never signs, the request stays pending: collections continue (unless paused) and the customer can still sign later. The merchant confirms this before starting. One pending cancellation per contract. DDS refuses a second request (HTTP 400, “A cancellation request has already been submitted for this record”). The customer’s signature is required in every case (DDS). A contract closed after a terminal bank refusal stays closed on Geidea’s side only (“Cancelled — reason”, S6 section 2 ). 7. Contract references are never reused. A DDA reference stays used after its contract is cancelled; a replacement contract needs a new reference ( S2 check 2.5 covers mandates in every status). 8. Once the cancellation is confirmed (DDS accepts it), payments already in a generated payment file run to their final status, because they cannot be withdrawn; every other follow-up action stops (section 4 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S8 — Contract Cancellation","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-7",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Start a cancellation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#start-a-cancellation","POST /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/cancellation Field M/O Rule reasonCode M One of DDS’s cancellation reason codes (table below) comments C Mandatory when reasonCode = P99 ; up to 1,000 characters. Sent to DDS as originatorComments pauseCollections O true = also pause the subscription ( S7 section 1 ) once DDS has accepted the request The cancel dialog (Merchant Portal, US-21 ). The Cancel Contract button is shown only to users with the Cancel Contract permission, on a DD Active contract with no pending cancellation. The dialog has: Reason (required): DDS reason codes (table below); Comments required for “Other”. Note (always shown): “Until the customer accepts the cancellation, payment collection will continue. If you want collections to stop now, pause the subscription.” “Also pause collections now” checkbox (sends pauseCollections = true ); shown only if the user also holds Pause / Resume and the subscription is Active. Warning : “A cancellation request can’t be withdrawn. The customer must sign it with UAE PASS before the bank cancels the direct debit.” Buttons: Keep contract · Send cancellation request. Checks (otherwise the error code shown): Check Error code User holds Cancel Contract ( S13 ) ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED (403) Mandate status_code = ACCP CONTRACT_NOT_ACTIVE No pending cancellation on the contract CANCELLATION_ALREADY_PENDING reasonCode valid; comments present for P99 INVALID_REASON Then: Call DDS DDA Cancellation with the mandate’s dda_id and its OIC’s credentials: { \"reasonCode\": \"P50\", \"originatorComments\": \"Customer closing the account\" } . 201 { \"ddcarid\": 173221, \"message\": \"Updated Successfully\", \"ddcaid\": 185612 } → continue. 400 “already been submitted” → CANCELLATION_ALREADY_PENDING ; if Geidea has no record of that request, alert Ops. 404, other error or timeout → DDS_ERROR ; nothing changes; no automatic retry. Create the Cancellation Request record (below) with stage = awaiting_customer . If pauseCollections : pause the subscription ( S7 section 1 ) with reason “Cancellation requested”. The contract status becomes Cancellation Pending ( S10 section 3 ); Mandate.status_code stays ACCP . Send the customer the cancellation link by email (section 3 .7 ). Event contract.cancellation_requested ; audit record. DDS cancellation reason codes (DDS REST API v2.0): Code Reason P01 Loan settled P02 Loan topped up P03 Alternate DDA set up – loan buyout P04 Alternate DDA set up – loan rescheduled P10 Unreliable billing from the originator P11 Unreliable dispute resolution P12 Unfair dispute resolution P20 Moving service providers P30 Card cancelled P31 Card expired P32 Card replaced P33 Card lost, destroyed or damaged P50 Account closed or closing P99 Other (comments required) Whether the loan codes (P01–P04) are shown to every merchant is to be agreed with Business. Order model — new entity Cancellation Request (one per cancellation; at most one pending per mandate): Field Meaning cancellation_id PK mandate_id FK → Mandate ddca_id DDS cancellation request ID ( ddcaid ) — used for the forms and the status ddcar_id DDS cancellation signing request ID ( ddcarid ) — starts the customer’s UAE PASS signing reason_code , comments As sent to DDS requested_by , requested_at Merchant user and time stage awaiting_customer → with_bank → accepted or rejected paused_by_cancellation true when the cancel step paused the subscription review_link_expires_at , notification_count , notification_sent_at The customer’s cancellation link (section 3 ) Cancellation link emails sent (section 2.2 ): kept for the audit trail and the once-a-day resend check. The link has no expiry signed_at , signer_process_id From the signing callback dds_status , decided_at , reject_reason From the status check (section 5 ) signed_pdf_ref The stored signed cancellation form"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Merchant view and actions while pending","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-view-and-actions-while-pending","The contract shows Cancellation Pending with its stage: “awaiting customer signature” or “with the bank”, the reason, who started it and when. Resend the cancellation link (Cancel Contract permission): extends the link to the end of day, today + contract_review_expiry_days ( S1 section 3 ); at most 5 resends. Unlike the contract link, it can be resent after the link has expired, because the DDS request never expires. allowed while stage = awaiting_customer , once per day (the first email counts for the day the request is created). The link has no expiry: it works for as long as the request awaits the customer’s signature, because the DDS request never expires. Error if already sent today: RESEND_LIMIT_REACHED . Download the cancellation form : unsigned while awaiting the customer; signed after. While awaiting the customer, the other subscription actions stay available (pause/resume, amend, retry). Once the customer has signed, they are disabled. There is no withdraw action (rule 4)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. The customer signs the cancellation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#the-customer-signs-the-cancellation","Message (SMS and email, Arabic and English): merchant name, contract description, merchant reference, the cancellation link and its expiry. Wording to be finalised with Business. (email only, Arabic and English): the cancellation request email in section 3.7 . No SMS for any cancellation message. Link https://<portal-host>/contracts/cancel/{cancellationId} : the same verification and one-time password as the contract review page ( S3 sections 2 and 3 ). The link has no expiry: the page works while the request awaits the customer’s signature ( stage = awaiting_customer ) and otherwise shows the matching state (item 6). The page shows the merchant, contract description, merchant reference, the reason, what happens next (“Once your bank accepts the cancellation, no further payments will be collected”), the unsigned cancellation form, and the hint for a forgotten UAE PASS signing password. Above the Sign button: “Your direct debit stays active, and payments can still be collected, until you sign this cancellation.” Sign opens {baseURL}/appApi/v1/customer/uaepass-signer-process?mode=web&ddarId={ddcar_id}&redirectUrl={encoded callback URL with action=cancel} (UAE PASS Signature API v1.4 section 5). Callback : same parameters as contract signing; ddId carries ddcaid . Confirm with DDS ( GET .../{ddcaid}/status ); never rely on the redirect alone. finished (DDS shows the request signed) → stage = with_bank , signed_at ; event contract.cancellation_signed ; the customer sees “Cancellation signed. Your bank will process it within 1 working day.” and can download the signed form. failed or ddsError → plain-language reason and “Try again” while the link is valid. canceled → “You left before signing. You can sign whenever you’re ready while the link is valid.” “You left before signing. Your direct debit stays active, and payments can still be collected, until you sign. You can sign whenever you’re ready.” Page states : no longer available (no pending request, or already decided) · already signed · link expired (ask the merchant to resend) · verification · the cancellation. Customer emails (Arabic and English; Arabic wording to be finalised with Business). Sent from Geidea on behalf of the merchant. {…} are filled from the contract. a. Cancellation request (on start and on each resend) Subject: Action needed: sign to cancel your direct debit with {Merchant} Dear {Customer name}, {Merchant} has requested to cancel your direct debit. Contract: {Contract description} Merchant reference: {Merchant reference} Reason: {Reason} This cancellation will not take effect until you sign it. Until then, your direct debit stays active and payments can still be collected. Review and sign: {link} You will confirm your identity with a one-time password and sign with UAE PASS. Forgot your UAE PASS signing password? Open UAE PASS > Profile > Reset signing password. Once you sign, your bank will process the cancellation within 1 working day. This is an automated message from Geidea on behalf of {Merchant}. b. Cancellation accepted (section 5 , ACCP ) Subject: Your direct debit with {Merchant} is cancelled Dear {Customer name}, Your bank has accepted the cancellation of your direct debit with {Merchant}. Contract: {Contract description} Merchant reference: {Merchant reference} No further payments will be collected under this direct debit. A payment already sent to your bank before the cancellation was accepted may still be processed. This is an automated message from Geidea on behalf of {Merchant}. c. Cancellation rejected by the bank (section 5 , RJCT ) Subject: Your request to cancel your direct debit with {Merchant} was not accepted Dear {Customer name}, Your bank has rejected the request to cancel your direct debit with {Merchant}. Contract: {Contract description} Merchant reference: {Merchant reference} Your direct debit remains active. Please contact your bank for more information. If you still wish to cancel, please contact {Merchant}. This is an automate"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Collections during a cancellation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#collections-during-a-cancellation","Rule (18-Nov-2026): what is already in a generated payment file runs to the end, because it cannot be withdrawn; everything else follows the stage of the cancellation. Before the customer signs ( stage = awaiting_customer ): collections are sent as normal unless the subscription is paused (rule 2). After the customer signs ( stage = with_bank ): nothing new for this contract goes into a payment file. Scheduled collections become Skipped with skip reason cancellation_signed and are not rolled over; retries and representments are held. Anything already in a generated file is processed by DDS as normal. Cancellation confirmed ( ACCP , section 5 ): payments already in a generated payment file are tracked to their final status ( S5 ). If one fails, that result is final: no retry, representment, rollover or Amend. Every other open item becomes Cancelled : scheduled, Skipped, in Error awaiting Amend, and any retry or representment not yet in a file. Bank rejects ( RJCT ): collections resume, and held retries and representments are released if still within their deadline. Collections skipped in the meantime stay Skipped; the merchant can recover them with Amend. Superseded text: Before the customer signs : collections are sent as normal unless the subscription is paused (rule 2). After the customer signs ( stage = with_bank ): the payment file job ( S5 ) does not send the contract’s collections; they become Skipped with skip reason cancellation_signed and are not rolled over. A collection already in a file is processed by DDS as normal. Bank accepts → section 5 . Bank rejects → collections resume; any skipped in the meantime stay Skipped (the merchant can recover them with Amend)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Status check and closing the contract","direct-debit/docs/product.html#status-check-and-closing-the-contract","Daily with the mandate status sync (18:15, S4 ), for every request with stage awaiting_customer or with_bank , call GET /v1/merchant/direct-debit-authorities/{ddcaid}/status : DDS status of the cancellation Action PNDG Still awaiting the customer; no change SUBP , APRP stage = with_bank (covers a missed callback) ACCP Cancellation accepted: stage = accepted , decided_at . Confirm the mandate with Get Status of a DDA — CANC → contract DD Cancelled , subscription Cancelled, every future collection Cancelled, history kept. Fetch the signed form once. Event contract.cancelled ; customer message “Your direct debit with <merchant> is cancelled” Cancellation accepted: stage = accepted , decided_at . Geidea cancels the mandate on its side at once, with no separate mandate status inquiry: Mandate.status_code = CANC , contract DD Cancelled , subscription Cancelled; open items as section 4.3 ; history kept. Fetch the signed cancellation form once. Event contract.cancelled ; customer email 3.7 b RJCT stage = rejected , reject_reason ; contract back to DD Active ; collections resume unless paused. Event contract.cancellation_rejected ; Ops alert Customer email 3.7 c ERR As RJCT , with an Ops alert (DDS processing error, not a bank decision: no customer email) A cancellation request never expires at DDS. The operations console ( S14 ) lists requests still awaiting the customer after 30 days."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Events, permission and audit","direct-debit/docs/product.html#events-permission-and-audit","Customer emails: cancellation request (and each resend), cancellation accepted, cancellation rejected (section 3.7 ). No SMS. Events: contract.cancellation_requested , contract.cancellation_link_resent , contract.cancellation_signed , contract.cancelled , contract.cancellation_rejected ( S11 ). Permission: Cancel Contract ( dd.contract.cancel ) — start a cancellation and resend its link ( S13 ). Every step is written to the audit trail and the contract’s activity history ( S10 section 8 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S8 — Contract Cancellation","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-7","# Point Status a Wording of the customer messages (request, signed, cancelled) Arabic wording of the customer emails (section 3.7 ); English wording decided 18-Nov Business b Whether loan reason codes (P01–P04) are shown to every merchant Business c Whether to ask DDS for a way to withdraw a cancellation request the customer has not signed (DDS offered to look at it) Product"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S9 — Bulk Contract Upload","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s9-bulk-contract-upload","Draft for review. A design discussion is required before this story is taken up for development."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S9 — Bulk Contract Upload","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-8","A merchant (or Geidea Operations on a merchant’s behalf) creates many contracts from one CSV file. One user uploads (the maker), another approves (the checker), and the Backend then creates one contract per row using exactly the same rules as a single contract ( S2 ). DDS has no bulk-mandate API, so each row is registered with DDS one at a time. Item Value Users Maker and checker (Merchant Portal users, or Geidea Operations) DDS API used Create DDA, once per row (Standard flow only) Order Model entities written Bulk File (new), Bulk File Record (new), Mandate, Subscription, Occurrence Depends on S2 , S3 , S13 ; merchant setting Enable Bulk Contract Upload ( S1 section 3 ) Delivery slice 12 Serves frontend stories US-28 , US-29"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S9 — Bulk Contract Upload","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-8",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Two flows, never mixed","direct-debit/docs/product.html#two-flows-never-mixed","In this phase only the Standard flow is built; the TBFC flow is Phase 2 (decided 18-Oct-2026). Flow ( flowType ) Every row What “processed” means standard Carries full instrument details The mandate is registered with DDS and the signing link is sent tbfc Carries no instrument details The contract is held locally as Awaiting Customer Details and the review link is sent. DDS is not called A file belongs to one flow. The merchant chooses the flow before uploading."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Endpoints","direct-debit/docs/product.html#endpoints","Action Endpoint Permission ( S13 ) Download template GET /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files/template?flowType=standard Bulk upload — maker Upload and check a file POST /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files (multipart: file , flowType ) Bulk upload — maker List files GET /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files Maker or checker File detail and rows GET /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files/{bulkFileId} and .../records Maker or checker Submit for approval POST /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files/{bulkFileId}/submit Bulk upload — maker Approve POST /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files/{bulkFileId}/approve Bulk upload — checker Reject POST /direct-debit/v1/bulk-files/{bulkFileId}/reject with { \"reason\": \"...\" } Bulk upload — checker All endpoints work within one MID (store). If Enable Bulk Contract Upload is off for the MID, return 403 BULK_UPLOAD_NOT_ENABLED ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. File format","direct-debit/docs/product.html#file-format","CSV, UTF-8, one header row, one contract per row. Maximum 1,000 rows per file (configurable). Column Standard TBFC Same rule as S2 field merchantReference M M merchantReference contractDescription O O contractDescription notes O O notes customerName , customerEmail , customerMobile , emiratesId M M Same names paymentMethodType M Must be empty paymentMethodType bankName , accountHolderTitle , bankAccountType , iban M if Bank Account Must be empty Same names cardNumber , cardHolderName M if Credit Card Must be empty Same names startDate , endDate M M Same names amountType , amount , minAmount , maxAmount M ( amount if Fixed) Same Same names frequencyCeiling , frequency , firstCollectionDate M M Same names collectionAmount M M The amount of each generated collection rolloverEnabled , rolloverMaxConsecutive M / C Same rollover.enabled , rollover.maxConsecutive The schedule is generated by the Backend from firstCollectionDate , frequency , endDate and collectionAmount . Individual collections cannot be customised in a bulk file; the merchant amends them afterwards ( S7 section 2 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Checks when the file is uploaded (blocking)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#checks-when-the-file-is-uploaded-blocking","Everything that can be checked while reading the file is checked immediately, and the result is returned in the upload response: File type, encoding, header columns present and in the template’s form, row count within the limit. Per row: mandatory columns filled for the flow; formats (dates, amounts, email, mobile, Emirates ID, IBAN, card number); allowed values (amount type, frequencies, instrument type); instrument columns empty for a TBFC file. merchantReference not repeated inside the file. Result: validRecordCount , invalidRecordCount , and for each failed row its row number, column and reason. Any failed row: file status Validation Failed . It cannot be submitted. The maker corrects the file and uploads again. No failed row: file status Validated . Card numbers and IBANs from the file are handled as in S2 (v2.5) : the IBAN is stored in full, the card number goes to the token vault, neither is ever logged."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Maker submits, checker decides","direct-debit/docs/product.html#maker-submits-checker-decides","Maker reviews the rows and submits: status Pending Review . The checker must be a different user from the maker, on the same side (a merchant’s file is approved by a merchant user; a Geidea Operations file by a Geidea user). Otherwise 403 MAKER_CANNOT_APPROVE . Approve: status Approved . Reject: status Rejected , reason mandatory (max 250 characters). A rejected file is final; the maker uploads a new one. The file list shows, for each file: file name, flow, row counts, status, maker and upload time, checker and decision time."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Processing after approval","direct-debit/docs/product.html#processing-after-approval","Flow When What happens per row Standard A job picks up Approved files every 15 minutes Run the full S2 flow for the row: pre-checks, rules V1 to V15, create local records, call DDS Create DDA, generate the link, send the notification TBFC Immediately on approval Run the S2 TBFC branch (section 4.1 ): create local records at Awaiting Customer Details, generate the link, send the notification The business rules that depend on live data are applied here, per row: merchant reference not already used, Maximum Contract Amount, first-collection lead time, minimum gap, Disable Credit Card. A row that fails is marked Failed with the S2 error code and reason; the other rows continue. Each row ends as Created (with its mandateId ) or Failed (with reason). A DDS timeout marks the row Outcome Unknown and raises the same Ops alert as S2 section 5 . File status while running: Processing . At the end: Completed (all rows created), Partially Completed (some failed), or Failed (none created). Processing a file twice must be impossible: a file is picked up only once, and a row already Created is never re-run. Contracts created from a bulk file are ordinary contracts from then on. Mandate.bulk_file_id records where they came from."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","7. Statuses","direct-debit/docs/product.html#statuses","Validation Failed · Validated → Pending Review → Rejected | Approved → Processing → Completed | Partially Completed | Failed"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S9 — Bulk Contract Upload","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-5","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Bulk File (new entity) bulk_file_id , merchant_id , flow_type , originating_side (merchant / geidea), file_name , file_ref , record_count , valid_record_count , invalid_record_count , status , uploaded_by , uploaded_at , reviewed_by , reviewed_at , reject_reason , processed_at One row per uploaded file Bulk File Record (new entity) bulk_file_id , row_number , parsed row data (instrument values masked), validation_result , validation_errors , outcome (Created / Failed / Outcome Unknown), outcome_reason , mandate_id One row per file row Mandate.bulk_file_id FK, nullable Origin of a bulk-created contract"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S9 — Bulk Contract Upload","Document","Merchant events raised","direct-debit/docs/product.html#merchant-events-raised-5","bulk_file.submitted , bulk_file.approved , bulk_file.rejected , bulk_file.completed ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S9 — Bulk Contract Upload","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-8","# Point Status a Design discussion before development (agreed with Product) Open b How Geidea Operations upload on a merchant’s behalf: through MA or an internal tool Open c Row limit per file and DDS call rate during Standard processing Open — Backend to propose d Whether a custom per-row schedule is needed in the file Open — current answer: no"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S10 — Merchant Portal Views & Reporting","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s10-merchant-portal-views-reporting","Draft for review."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S10 — Merchant Portal Views & Reporting","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-9","The read side of Direct Debit: everything the Merchant Portal needs to show a merchant’s contracts and collections. Sections 1 to 4 are built early (delivery slice 3) so that the Portal can display the order model and every later slice can be tested through it. Sections 5 to 8 follow in slice 10. Item Value Users Merchant Portal users with the View Direct Debit permission (MVP); partners through the same API (Phase 2) DDS APIs used None. All data comes from the order model Other systems APEX Transaction Inquiry and Payout Inquiry, for payout status (section 4 ) Delivery slices 3 (sections 1 to 4 ) and 10 (sections 5 to 8 ) Serves frontend stories US-03 , US-04 , US-05 , US-06 , US-14 , US-17 , US-18 , US-27 , US-32 Rules for every endpoint here: the caller needs the View Direct Debit permission ( S13 ); results cover the stores (MIDs) named in the request — all of the user’s stores by default, or the one or several chosen in the Portal’s store selector — and never a store the user is not permitted for; full IBAN, card number and Emirates ID are never returned, only masked values."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S10 — Merchant Portal Views & Reporting","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-9",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Contract list","direct-debit/docs/product.html#contract-list","GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts?page=&pageSize=&sort= Returns one row per contract: Field Source (Order Model) mandateId Mandate.mandate_id merchantReference dda_reference_number mandateReference centralBankReference dda_id (empty until the mandate exists at DDS) cb_dda_ref_no (empty until DD Active). The merchant reference is the contract’s only business reference — it is the DDS DDA reference number; DDS’s internal IDs ( dda_id , ddar_id ) are not shown to the merchant (v2.5) customerName customer_full_name instrument instrument_type + instrument_reference (IBAN in full; card last 4 — v2.5) + bank_name ; “Awaiting customer” under TBFC validFrom , validTo commences_on , expires_on frequency Subscription.frequency previousDeduction Latest Paid collection: actual debit_date and amount nextDue Next Scheduled collection: due_date and amount; empty if none successfulCollections , failedCollections Count and total of Paid; count and total of Failed status , statusLabel Section 3 subscriptionStatus Subscription.status Default sort: newest first. Page size default 25, maximum 100. Each row also returns the store (MID) of the contract, since one list can span several stores, and the actions this user may take on the contract from the list (for example resendLink ), combining permission and status rules. Note (07-Oct-2026). The team has already built the summary screen; its list is served at /api/v1/direct-debit/contracts/summary with the stores, filters, paging and sorting in the request. Paths, parameter names and field names in this story are indicative and are to be aligned with what is built."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Contract detail and collections","direct-debit/docs/product.html#contract-detail-and-collections","GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId} returns everything in section 1 plus: contractDescription , notes , createdAt , createdBy , amount type, minimum and maximum, rollover settings (enabled, allowed, used in the current streak), reviewLinkExpiresAt , resend count, reject reason label, which mandate document is available (none, unsigned or signed — S3 section 9 ), and the list of actions currently allowed for this contract and this user (for example resendLink , discard , pause , resume ), so the Portal does not have to work the rules out itself. Summary cards: successfulCollections , failedCollections , outstandingAmount (Scheduled + Failed, not rolled over; Skipped excluded), nextCollection . GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/collections returns every collection: Field Source collectionId , sequenceNumber Occurrence.occurrence_id , sequence_number paymentReference Payment.payment_ref_no of the latest Payment (e.g. 48213-UNIT304-000003 ); empty until the collection is first sent. A collection re-set after an Error shows its new reference dueDate , amount , originalAmount due_date , amount , original_amount status Scheduled, Submitted, Paid, Rejected, Failed, Skipped, Error, Cancelled statusDetail Reason label for Rejected/Failed ( Payment.reason_code ); skip reason for Skipped; “Retry in progress” when the payment is RPND ; “Payment validation error” and DDS’s message for Error retryCount , retryDeadline Payment.retry_count , Occurrence.retry_deadline rolledOver , rolledOverTo , rolledOverFrom[] rolled_over , rolled_over_to_occurrence_id , reverse lookup collectedOn Payment.debit_date (or collected_at until known) payout Section 4 allowedActions retry , amend , downloadBounceMemo , as the rules and permissions allow (Error: amend only — no retry, no bounce memo) scheduleVersion On the response header object, for Amend ( S7 section 2 )"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Contract status","direct-debit/docs/product.html#contract-status","One status per contract, from one list (07-Oct-2026). The Portal displays this status and filters on it. Statuses that mirror the DDS mandate status carry the tag DD ; statuses that exist only at Geidea carry no tag. The Portal never has to combine two fields or interpret a DDS code. Status Origin Meaning Awaiting Customer Details Geidea TBFC contract; the customer has not yet entered the instrument ( mandate_creation_stage = awaiting_customer_instrument ). Nothing exists at DDS Not Submitted Geidea DDS refused the create request ( dds_rejected : correct and resend) or its outcome is unknown ( dds_outcome_unknown : contact support). The response says which Link Expired Geidea The review link expired before the customer finished ( S3 section 10 ) Discarded Geidea The merchant discarded the unsigned contract ( S7 section 3 ) Cancellation Pending Geidea A cancellation request exists for an Active mandate ( S8 ). The detail shows its stage: awaiting customer signature, or with the bank. status_code stays ACCP Cancelled — reason Geidea Closed by Geidea after a terminal refusal of a collection ( S6 section 2 ), for example “Cancelled — Account closed” DD Pending DDS PNDG Waiting for the customer to sign DD Pending System Submission DDS SUBP Signed; on its way to the bank DD Pending Bank Approval DDS APRP With the Central Bank and the customer’s bank DD Active DDS ACCP Approved; collections can run DD Rejected DDS RJCT Rejected by the bank; the plain-language reason is returned with it DD Denied DDS DENY Customer declined to sign Removed: banks do not support DENY (DDS, 15-Oct-2026) DD Request Expired DDS RQXP Customer did not sign within 7 working days DD Error DDS ERR Error at DDS or the bank; final DD Cancelled DDS CANC Cancelled at DDS DD Expired DDS EXPR End date passed Rules: A Geidea status takes precedence once it applies, in the order of the table. Example: a contract at DD Pending whose link expires becomes Link Expired and stays so, even though status_code still holds PNDG . The other stories ( S2 to S7 ) name the label without the tag, for example “Pending” or “Active”. The value returned to the Portal is the tagged one in this table. Mandate.status_code keeps the raw DDS code for support and reconciliation. It is not needed by the Portal to show or filter the status. Suspended ( SUSP ) is Phase 2 and and Denied ( DENY ) are not supported by the banks (DDS, 15-Oct-2026) and are never returned. Not in the MVP: ageing on DD Pending Bank Approval, and a merchant “Refresh status” action. When the mandate is Active and the subscription is Paused, the response also carries subscriptionStatus = Paused so the Portal can show “Active — collections paused”."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Payout status","direct-debit/docs/product.html#payout-status","Payout applies only to Paid collections that have been sent to APEX ( federation_flag = true and sent). Payout data comes from APEX’s existing inquiry API ; no new APEX work is needed. To agree with the MP and Backend teams (open): (a) the Portal calls APEX directly in real time, or (b) the Backend reads APEX on a schedule, stores the result and serves the Portal. The steps below describe the lookup itself, whichever side makes it. The caller uses APEX Transaction Inquiry once for the contract, filtered by subscriptionNumber = the merchant reference and the MID, and matches each returned transaction to a collection by interSystemID . For each payoutId it then reads status and date from APEX Payout Inquiry . Returned per collection: payoutStatus , payoutId , payoutDate . Not sent, not paid or not yet at APEX: all three empty. Settled payouts do not change; cache them. Do not cache pending ones for more than 15 minutes. If APEX does not answer, return the collections without payout data and payoutAvailable = false . The page must still load."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Portfolio summary (KPIs)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#portfolio-summary-kpis","GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts/summary with the same filters as section 6 for the selected stores only. The cards always show the totals for every contract in those stores: the summary does not take search, filters or a date range, and there is no default date range on the list either (v2.5) . KPI Definition Active Contracts Count with status_code = ACCP (includes Cancellation Pending — the mandate is still active) Pending Customer Sign Count of Awaiting Customer Details + Pending Pending Bank Approval Count of Pending System Submission + Pending Bank Approval Total Collections Count and amount of Paid collections Failed Collections Count and amount of Failed collections, and the number of contracts affected Declined Contracts Count of Rejected + Denied + Error (proposed; Request Expired and Link Expired are not counted) Empty results return 0, never an error. With several stores selected, each KPI is the sum across those stores, and the KPI counts equal what the unfiltered list returns for the same stores and filters ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Search and filters","direct-debit/docs/product.html#search-and-filters","On GET /contracts and /contracts/summary (the summary ignores them — section 5 ) : search with searchBy = merchantReference | mandateReference | customerName | emiratesId . Emirates ID is matched in full, never partially. Filters: status (one or more labels), subscriptionStatus , createdFrom / createdTo (optional, empty by default — v2.5) , hasFailedCollections . Stores: the request names the stores (MIDs), as in the rule at the top of this story. Clearing the other filters never widens access beyond the stores the user is permitted for. While a search is active the Portal hides the KPI cards, so search has to work on the list and the export; the summary does not use it (section 5 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","7. Export","direct-debit/docs/product.html#export","GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts/export?format=csv with the same stores, search and filters. It needs the export permission ( S13 ). Columns as section 1 , including the store of each contract, with the instrument (IBAN in full, card masked — v2.5) and merchant-facing labels. No DDS-only or internal fields. Up to 10,000 rows synchronously; above that return 422 EXPORT_TOO_LARGE and ask the user to narrow the filter."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","8. Activity history","direct-debit/docs/product.html#activity-history","GET /direct-debit/v1/contracts/{mandateId}/activity returns, newest first, the merchant-relevant events for the contract, built from the audit trail and the status history tables (Rules 3 and 5): contract created, signing link sent or resent, customer verified, instrument provided, signed, signing failed, status changes (old and new label), link expired, discarded, paused, resumed, collection amended (old and new date and amount, reason), collection submitted, paid, rejected (reason), retry sent, failed, rolled over or rollover blocked, skipped (reason), notes edited. Each entry: time, actor (user name, “Customer” or “System”), event, detail. Read-only. No instrument values."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S10 — Merchant Portal Views & Reporting","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-6","None. This story only reads."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S10 — Merchant Portal Views & Reporting","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-9","# Point Status a Definition of “Declined Contracts” Proposed above — Product to confirm b When payout status is fetched from APEX: every time a Portal page listing records is loaded, or only when the user opens a contract’s detail screen (this draft: detail screen only). And one APEX call per contract or a batched call Open — MP team, DD Backend team, APEX c Geidea support view (impersonation from MA): same endpoints with a Geidea role, read-only Open — see B17"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S11 — Merchant Notifications & Webhooks","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s11-merchant-notifications-webhooks","Draft for review."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S11 — Merchant Notifications & Webhooks","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-10","Every story raises events (for example contract.signed , occurrence.rejected ). This story stores them and shows the relevant ones to merchant users in the Portal. Delivering them to partners by webhook is Phase 2 ; it is specified here so that the event store is built to support it. Item Value Users Merchant Portal users (in-portal notifications, MVP); partner systems (webhooks, Phase 2) Delivery slice 11 (sections 1 to 3 and 5 ). Section 4 is Phase 2 Serves frontend stories US-30 , US-31"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S11 — Merchant Notifications & Webhooks","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-10",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Event store","direct-debit/docs/product.html#event-store","Every event is written once to an event table: event_id , event_type , merchant_id , mandate_id , occurrence_id (if any), occurred_at , payload , severity . Writing the same event twice (for example two status checks seeing the same change) must not create two rows: use event_type + entity + the new status as the uniqueness key."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Event catalogue","direct-debit/docs/product.html#event-catalogue","Event Raised by Severity contract.created (carries reviewUrl and its expiry), contract.link_resent , contract.notification_sent , contract.instrument_provided , contract.signed , contract.activated , contract.expired S2 to S4 Information contract.signing_failed , contract.rejected , contract.link_expired , contract.error , contract.cancelled S3 , S4 , S6 Action required contract.cancellation_requested , contract.cancellation_link_resent , contract.cancellation_signed , contract.cancellation_rejected S8 Information; cancellation_rejected needs action contract.discarded S7 Information occurrence.submitted , occurrence.paid , occurrence.retry_submitted , occurrence.rolled_over S5 , S6 Information occurrence.rejected , occurrence.failed , occurrence.error , occurrence.skipped , occurrence.rollover_blocked S5 , S6 Action required subscription.paused , subscription.resumed , subscription.schedule_amended , subscription.completed S5 , S7 Information bulk_file.submitted , bulk_file.approved , bulk_file.rejected , bulk_file.completed S9 Information; submitted is Action required for checkers The payload never contains a full or masked instrument number, only IDs, references, status, reason code and label, amounts and dates."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. In-portal notifications","direct-debit/docs/product.html#in-portal-notifications","GET /direct-debit/v1/notifications?unreadOnly=&page= returns events for the stores named in the request, newest first, each with a title, a one-line message, severity and a link target ( mandateId , and collectionId where relevant). POST /direct-debit/v1/notifications/{eventId}/read and .../read-all . Read state is per user. Only users with View Direct Debit for that store receive them. Bulk-file approval requests go to users with the checker permission. Keep 90 days (configurable)."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Webhooks (partners) — Phase 2, not built in the MVP","direct-debit/docs/product.html#webhooks-partners-phase-2-not-built-in-the-mvp","A webhook URL and a signing secret are registered per MID by Geidea admin ( PUT /direct-debit/v1/admin/merchants/{merchantId}/webhook ). The merchant chooses which event types to receive; default all. Delivery: HTTPS POST of { eventId, eventType, occurredAt, merchantId, data } , with an HMAC-SHA256 signature header over the body. A 2xx answer within 10 seconds is success. Otherwise retry after 1 minute, 5 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours and 12 hours, then mark failed and alert Operations. Events can arrive more than once or out of order; eventId lets the receiver ignore repeats. Document this for partners. GET /direct-debit/v1/admin/merchants/{merchantId}/webhook/deliveries lists recent deliveries and their result, for support."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Error handling contract for the Portal (US-31)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#error-handling-contract-for-the-portal-us-31","Every error response carries a machine-readable errorCode , a plain-language message , a list of fieldErrors (field, code, message) and a correlationId the merchant can quote to support. Each story lists its codes. The Portal maps codes to its own wording and never shows DDS or APEX internals."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S11 — Merchant Notifications & Webhooks","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-10","# Point Status a Webhooks are Phase 2, with the partner model: a partner receives the review link and status changes through them. The event store and catalogue (sections 1 and 2 ) are built now so that delivery can be added without change Decided — Phase 2 b Email or SMS alerts to merchant users for action-required events Not in scope; to consider later"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S12 — APEX Federation","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s12-apex-federation","Draft, updated 07-Oct-2026. Business decision of 06-Oct-2026: merchant payout is T+1, and a payment is sent to APEX on the Paid status without waiting for the FT reference. From the meeting with APEX team on 06-Oct-2026, agreed: PTLF file only; static pan ; payout frequency per MID counted from the day the file is loaded. Still needed from APEX team: the field values marked “From APEX” in section 3 and the PTLF file specification. userData stays open until the Direct Debit settlement report format is defined with FinOps and Operations."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S12 — APEX Federation","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-11","Paid collections are sent to APEX once a day so that APEX can price them and pay the merchant. Nothing else goes to APEX: no mandates, no subscriptions, no rejected or failed payments. Reconciling the credits on Geidea’s bank statement by FT reference is an offline Finance/FinOps activity and is not part of this story . Item Value Trigger Daily job at a configurable time, after the 18:30 payment status check on the due date ( S5 section 3 ). See section 4 for why the time matters Format PTLF bulk file only. No Create Transaction API call is made for Direct Debit. The row layout follows the Transaction structure of APEX’s Create Transaction API (APEX Merchant API UAE Specifications v1.27). The PTLF file specification itself is to come from APEX Order Model entities written Payment, APEX File (new) Delivery slice 9 Serves frontend stories US-27 (through S10 section 4 )"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S12 — APEX Federation","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-11",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Which payments are sent","direct-debit/docs/product.html#which-payments-are-sent","Payments with federation_flag = true (status ACCP ) and apex_sent_at empty. The FT reference is not a condition (changed 07-Oct-2026). One APEX record per collection. A retry re-uses the same Payment, so it can never produce a second record. A payment that DDS reports as Paid only at a later status check goes into the next file. The alert for a payment with no FT reference after 7 days stays ( S5 ), but it no longer holds back the APEX record. APEX treats every record in the file as matched and prices it, and the merchant is paid on T+1. So a record must never be sent unless DDS has reported the payment Paid ( ACCP ). If no payment qualifies, no file is sent for that run; the run is still logged ( S14 )."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Values built once per payment","direct-debit/docs/product.html#values-built-once-per-payment","Build these when the payment is first selected, store them on the Payment, and re-use them if the row is ever sent again. Order Model field How it is built Example Payment.apex_intersystem_id DD: + Mandate.oic + : + Payment.payment_ref_no DD:560000001:SAND-1114-000003 Payment.apex_reference_number Last digit of the year + 3-digit day of the year of debit_date + dds_payment_id left-padded with zeros to 8 digits Debit 14-Sep-2026, DDS payment ID 3351 → 625700003351 Payment.apex_order_id Mandate.dda_reference_number SAND-1114 Why not DDS’s own 6-digit payment reference for referenceNumber : it is the collection’s sequence number inside one contract, so every contract has a 000001 ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Field mapping","direct-debit/docs/product.html#field-mapping","Status: Agreed = value settled. From APEX = the value must be supplied by APEX team. Open = waiting on a Geidea decision. APEX field M/O/C Value for Direct Debit Source Status interSystemID M DD:<OIC>:<payment_ref_no> Payment.apex_intersystem_id Agreed originalSystemID O Empty — Agreed messageType M 200 Fixed Agreed processingCode M Payment series 5x0000 Fixed From APEX — exact code pan M Static value, the same for every Direct Debit record (bank account and card alike). APEX uses pan only to classify card products, which does not apply to Direct Debit Fixed From APEX — the static value to use localDate / localTime M YYYYMMDD / HH24MISS of the actual debit Payment.debit_date ; at send time the Day-End report is not yet in, so the date and time the payment turned Paid is used ( S5 section 3.4 rule 4) Agreed; see open point i amount / settlementAmount M Amount collected Payment.claimed_amount Agreed cashbackAmount / additionalFee C 0 Fixed Agreed transactionCurrency / settlementCurrency M 784 Fixed Agreed mcc M Merchant’s MCC Merchant profile Agreed referenceNumber M, 12 Year digit + day of year + DDS payment ID (8) Payment.apex_reference_number Agreed authorizationCode M, 6 HHMMSS of localTime Derived Agreed terminalId M One static value for all Direct Debit records; not registered under merchants Fixed From APEX — the static value merchantId M MID Mandate.merchant_id Agreed acceptorName M Merchant display name Merchant profile Agreed responseCode M 00 Fixed Agreed cardProduct M DD Fixed (new APEX value) Agreed posEntryMode M Fixed value Fixed From APEX — the value to send tranOriginator M Geidea Fixed Agreed tranDestination M DD Fixed (new APEX value) Agreed destinationBin M Fixed value Fixed From APEX — the value to send scheme C DD Fixed (new APEX value) Agreed userData C To be defined Mandate, Occurrence Open — depends on the Direct Debit settlement report format (FinOps, Operations) transactionType O Pay proposed. APEX requires Direct Debit records to bear a specific transaction type Fixed From APEX — the transaction type value terminalName / terminalLocation O DDebit + MID, padded with spaces to 40 characters Derived Agreed channelType M Digital Fixed (new APEX value) Agreed orderId O Merchant reference of the contract Payment.apex_order_id Agreed subscriptionNumber O Same value as orderId Payment.apex_order_id Agreed arn O DDS FT reference Payment.ft_ref_no . Empty when the file is built , because the FT reference arrives the next day. Filled later only once APEX can update an existing record (open point h) Mapping agreed; delivery open isInternationalBin / isPremiumBin O 0 Fixed Agreed tipAmount / tipSettlementAmount M 0 Fixed Agreed isMaskedPan O 0 Fixed To confirm with the static pan paymentStatus , paymentId , paymentDate , paymentMethod , commissionAmount , vat , clearingStatus , iccData , secureInfo , networkIdentifier , cardServiceType C Do not send the field APEX fills these Agreed netAmount , payoutId O Empty APEX fills these Agreed sourceBin , transactionCardCountry , exchangeRate , isVisaInstallment , tenure , vas , vasProvider , providerOrderId , payByLinkReference , cardSegment , cardClass , markupPerc , markupFee , InvoiceNr O/C Empty — Agreed orderId and subscriptionNumber carry the same value on purpose: subscriptionNumber is the natural field, and APEX links and filters Direct Debit payments under one subscription by either field. Change from the earlier draft: the card number is no longer sent to APEX for card-based mandates. A static pan is used for every record, so this story no longer needs the card number or the token vault. The card-number open item in S2 now concerns only what is sent to DDS."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Sending, timing and tracking","direct-debit/docs/product.html#sending-timing-and-tracking","Build one file per run, record it as an APEX File, and mark each included Payment with apex_file_ref and apex_sent_at . Merchant payout is T+1 (Business decision, 06-Oct-2026). The send time must be a configuration value, not fixed in code: APEX holds one payout frequency (T+X) per MID and counts X from the day the file is loaded, not from the debit date. APEX loads PTLF files by about 06:30 and runs pricing and grouping until about 07:15. A file loaded by then is in that day’s normal run. The file is therefore built after the 18:30 payment status check on the due date and delivered before APEX’s 06:30 load the next day, so that the collection is in the normal payout run of the day after the debit. A file that arrives after the 06:30 load is paid that day only if APEX runs a top-up pricing, which creates a second payout for the merchant. This is to be avoided. The exact send time, and how APEX counts the payout day for a file loaded on the evening of the due date compared with early the next morning, is to be confirmed with APEX (open point d). File name, transport and acknowledgement follow the existing PTLF process. The specification is to come from APEX; the Backend team already produces a PTLF for another product (Ali Pay), which can be used as the starting point. If APEX rejects a row, record the reason on the Payment, alert Operations, and include the corrected row in a later file with the same interSystemID and referenceNumber . A payment is never sent twice unless APEX rejected it."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Payout read-back","direct-debit/docs/product.html#payout-read-back","APEX’s existing inquiry API is used: Transaction Inquiry by subscriptionNumber and MID, then Payout Inquiry by payoutId . No new APEX work is needed. Whether the Portal calls it directly or the Backend calls it on a schedule is open; see S10 section 4 ."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. What APEX delivers on its side","direct-debit/docs/product.html#what-apex-delivers-on-its-side","Listed so that the Backend and Portal teams know what to expect. From APEX’s own requirements (FR.01 to FR.05): Direct Debit as a new scheme , supported over the existing onboarding APIs; new values DD for destination and scheme, and Digital for channel. Matched Direct Debit records priced, grouped and paid through the normal process. Inquiry filters for the Merchant Portal: Direct Debit records only; all records excluding Direct Debit (a new “excluded transaction type” or “excluded scheme” filter); by MID; and all payments under one subscription by orderId or subscriptionNumber . Direct Debit shown as a new scheme in internal reports (Report 7) and merchant-facing reports (DTR/CDTR, VAT, MSR). The exclusion filter in point 3 is required: without it Direct Debit records appear in the Portal’s existing Transactions tab among card transactions."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","7. Day-End reconciliation with DDS","direct-debit/docs/product.html#day-end-reconciliation-with-dds","Already in S5 section 3.4 : the daily Day-End report from DDS is compared with what the Backend holds; differences are logged for Operations. This is the Backend’s check against DDS. It is separate from bank statement reconciliation, which is Finance’s."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S12 — APEX Federation","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-7","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Payment.apex_intersystem_id , apex_reference_number , apex_order_id strings Section 2 Payment.apex_file_ref , apex_sent_at , apex_reject_reason FK, timestamp, string Tracking APEX File (new entity) apex_file_ref , business_date , file_name , row_count , total_amount , status , sent_at One row per file sent Backend configuration: apex_file_send_time time; after the 18:30 status check on the due date, before 06:30 the next day; default to be set with APEX Section 4 Mandate.card_token , proposed in the earlier draft, is not needed for APEX."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S12 — APEX Federation","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-11","# Point Status a Values for terminalId , posEntryMode , destinationBin ; exact processingCode ; transaction type; static pan Open — APEX team b PTLF file specification (layout, file name, transport, acknowledgement) Open — APEX team; Ali Pay PTLF as reference c userData content Open — follows the Direct Debit settlement report format, to be defined with FinOps and Operations d Payout day is decided: T+1. Open: the exact file send time (after the 18:30 status check on the due date, before APEX’s 06:30 load the next day) and how APEX counts the payout day for it; for a MID not on daily payout, whether a scheme-level T+X exists in APEX Open — APEX team, DD Backend (Part A section A8 ) e referenceNumber uses DDS’s payment ID, which is DDS’s own record key and is expected to be unique. APEX’s unique key is interSystemID , so no dependency is carried as an open item Note f Exclusion filter for Direct Debit in APEX inquiry, and its use by the Merchant Portal Transactions tab Open — APEX, MP g Bank statement reconciliation by FT reference Outside this story — Finance / FinOps h FT reference on the APEX record. It arrives after the record has been sent, so APEX must be able to update an existing record: a change request with APEX’s vendor (Emcrey), to be raised by APEX once Product documents the requirement. Fallback if delayed: an extract from the DD Backend of paid collections with their FT references, for FinOps Open — Product, APEX team, FinOps i localDate , localTime and referenceNumber use the debit date. The file is now built before the Day-End report arrives, so the date the payment turned Paid is used. If the Day-End report later shows a different date, it is logged as an exception ( S5 section 3.4 ) Note — to confirm with APEX and DD Backend"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S13 — Direct Debit User Permissions","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s13-direct-debit-user-permissions","Draft for review."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S13 — Direct Debit User Permissions","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-12","Who may do what in Direct Debit is decided per user, in Merchant Portal user management, not per merchant in PGW Config. This story defines the permissions, where they are managed, and how the DD Backend enforces them. It replaces the action toggles that were in S1 section 4 . Item Value Users Merchant administrator (assigns permissions); every Merchant Portal user (is checked) Owners MP team: user-management screens and the permission store. DD Backend: enforcement Depends on S1 (Direct Debit enabled for the MID) Delivery slice 1 Serves frontend stories US-01 , US-02 , and a new frontend story for Direct Debit permissions in MP user management"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S13 — Direct Debit User Permissions","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-12",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Permissions","direct-debit/docs/product.html#permissions","Proposed list (07-Oct-2026); the final list is to be agreed with the MP team. The Portal’s role framework works per action (for example Export Transactions is separate from View Transactions), so Direct Debit follows the same pattern instead of the grouped permissions of the earlier draft. Where another story says “Contract actions” or “Subscription actions”, read the matching permission below. Permission (proposed code) Allows Endpoints View Direct Debit ( dd.view ) Menu, KPIs, list, detail, collections, activity history, notifications, download mandate document and bounce memo All read endpoints in S3 (merchant side), S6 , S10 , S11 Export Contracts ( dd.export ) Export of the contract list S10 export Create Contract ( dd.contract.create ) Create a single contract S2 create Manage Unsigned Contract ( dd.contract.manage ) Resend signing link, discard unsigned contract, edit notes S3 resend; S7 discard and notes Pause / Resume Subscription ( dd.subscription.pause ) Pause, resume S7 pause/resume Amend Collection ( dd.collection.amend ) Change date or amount of future collections S7 amend Retry Collection ( dd.collection.retry ) Retry a rejected collection S6 retry Cancel Contract ( dd.contract.cancel ) Start a cancellation of an Active contract; resend the customer’s cancellation link S8 Bulk upload — maker ( dd.bulk.upload ) Template, upload, submit S9 Bulk upload — checker ( dd.bulk.approve ) Approve, reject S9 Every action permission requires dd.view . Selecting an action permission selects View with it, so that no permission granted on its own leads to an empty page. A user may hold maker and checker together but can never approve a file they uploaded ( S9 section 5 ). Contract cancellation gets its own permission when S8 is built. Contract cancellation has its own permission, Cancel Contract ( S8 ). Manual rollover is not in the MVP and has no permission."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Where they are managed (MP)","direct-debit/docs/product.html#where-they-are-managed-mp","The Direct Debit permissions are added to Merchant Portal’s existing role framework as a new permission group: the business administrator (the first user of the business, covering all its stores) creates roles, ticks permissions per module and assigns roles to users. They appear in user management only for merchants with Enable Direct Debit on for at least one store. Permissions apply within the stores (MIDs) a user is assigned to, in line with how the Portal already scopes users to stores. Proposed defaults: the merchant’s administrator role receives all Direct Debit permissions; other roles receive none until granted. Every grant and removal is audited: who, for which user, which permission, which store, when."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. How the Backend enforces them","direct-debit/docs/product.html#how-the-backend-enforces-them","Each request from the Portal carries the user’s identity, the stores (MIDs) the request is for and the user’s Direct Debit permissions for them, in the form the MP and DD Backend teams agree (for example claims in the access token, or a permission lookup service). The Backend checks, in this order: Direct Debit enabled for the MID (403 DD_NOT_ENABLED ); the user may act on every MID named in the request (403 STORE_NOT_ALLOWED ); the user holds the permission for the action (403 ACTION_NOT_ALLOWED ); then the story’s own rules. The permission check runs on every call, so a screen left open after a permission was removed cannot be used to act. The contract detail and collections responses ( S10 section 2 ) list the actions this user may take, combining permission and status rules, so the Portal can hide or disable the rest. Provision for Phase 2: the permission check must not assume a Portal user. In Phase 2, calls made by a partner system with API credentials for a MID are treated as holding all permissions for that MID."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Store scope","direct-debit/docs/product.html#store-scope","Direct Debit follows the Portal’s existing store selection: all of the user’s stores by default, or one or several chosen in the store selector. The list, search, KPIs, export and notifications cover the stores named in the request, and never a store the user is not permitted for. A contract belongs to one MID, so an action on a contract (resend, discard, pause, amend, retry) is checked against that contract’s store. Creating a contract and bulk upload are for one store."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Removing the old toggles","direct-debit/docs/product.html#removing-the-old-toggles","Delete the action toggles from the merchant configuration record and the PGW Config screen ( S1 section 4 ). No data migration is needed: they were never live."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S13 — Direct Debit User Permissions","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-12","# Point Status a How permissions reach the Backend: token claims or a lookup service Open — MP and DD Backend teams b Default permissions per existing Portal role, including whether the business administrator receives all Direct Debit permissions automatically when Direct Debit is enabled Proposed above — MP team to confirm d Final list of permissions and their names Open — MP team, Product c Geidea support staff viewing a merchant’s Direct Debit through MA: read-only role, audit Open — see B17"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Part C — Technical Requirements","Document","S14 — Operations Console & Job Control","direct-debit/docs/product.html#s14-operations-console-job-control","Draft for discussion. Scope, ownership and where the console lives are not yet agreed."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S14 — Operations Console & Job Control","Document","Story summary","direct-debit/docs/product.html#story-summary-13","Direct Debit runs on scheduled jobs against fixed DDS cut-offs. If a job fails, or does not run at all, someone at Geidea must be able to see it, understand what was affected, and act. This story gives every job a run record, detects jobs that did not run, alerts by email, and provides an internal console to inspect a run and re-run it by hand. Without it, alerts would have nothing to act on. Item Value Users Geidea Operations and technical support (to be agreed) Covers Every scheduled job in S1 section 5.4 Order Model entities written Job Run (new), Job Run Record (new), Exception (new) Delivery slice 13. The run log and manual re-run should be built with the first scheduled job Serves frontend stories None in Merchant Portal. Console screens are a separate build"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S14 — Operations Console & Job Control","Document","Requirements","direct-debit/docs/product.html#requirements-13",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","1. Job run record","direct-debit/docs/product.html#job-run-record","Every run of every scheduled job writes one Job Run: Field Meaning job_run_id , job_name Which job business_date , oic The date the run is for (for example the due date of a payment file), and the OIC where the job works per OIC trigger scheduled or manual ; for manual, the user started_at , finished_at Timing status Running, Success, Partial, Failed, Missed selected_count , success_count , failed_count , skipped_count What the run did batch_ref The batch it produced or worked on: Payment File, APEX File or Bulk File error_summary Why it failed, in plain words Each record the run touched is written as a Job Run Record: the entity (mandate, collection or payment), the outcome and the error reason."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","2. Missed-run detection","direct-debit/docs/product.html#missed-run-detection","A job that never starts raises no error of its own. A monitor holds the expected schedule ( S1 section 5.4 ) and, if a job has not started within its allowed delay (default 10 minutes; 5 minutes for the payment file job), writes a Job Run with status Missed and raises an alert."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","3. Alerts","direct-debit/docs/product.html#alerts","A run that ends Failed , Partial or Missed sends an email to a group mailbox (configured per environment), with the job, date, counts, error summary and a link to the run in the console. The existing alerts in other stories go through the same channel: payment file not accepted by 19:45, whole-file rejection, contract-creation call with an unknown outcome, payment with no FT reference after 7 days, reconciliation mismatch, APEX row rejected. One email per run, not one per record."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","4. Console views","direct-debit/docs/product.html#console-views","View Shows Job runs All runs, newest first; filter by job, date, status, OIC. A day view shows every expected job for a date and whether it ran Run detail The run record, its batch, and its records with outcome and error Batch detail A payment file, APEX file or bulk file: its rows, DDS or APEX response, and current state of each row Exceptions Section 6 Configuration Job schedule, bank list, working-day calendar (view and edit, audited)"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","5. Manual actions","direct-debit/docs/product.html#manual-actions","Every job must be safe to run again for the same business date: it only picks up what has not been done, and never sends anything to DDS or APEX twice. Action Rule Re-run a job for a date (and OIC) Allowed when the last run for that date is Failed, Partial or Missed Re-run failed records only Where the job works record by record (status checks, bulk rows, APEX rows) Payment file: re-run Allowed until the DDS cut-off (20:00) , through the API, as long as no file has yet been accepted for that day and OIC (DDS accepts one successful file per day; rejected uploads don’t count) . After that the console says the cut-off has passed and shows the affected collections Payment file: download Download the generated CSV to upload on DDS’s portal by hand Removed: an API client cannot upload files on DDS’s portal — DDS offers API upload only and a read-only monitoring dashboard (DDS, 15-Oct-2026). Use re-run Payment file: mark as uploaded manually Records the DDS payment file ID. Payments are then tied back to DDS records by the next status check and Day-End report ( S5 section 3.4 ) Removed with the manual upload Run a job now For jobs with no cut-off (status sync, FT reference check, Day-End pull, APEX file, retry deadline job, link expiry) Every manual action is audited: who, when, what, and the result."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","6. Exceptions queue","direct-debit/docs/product.html#exceptions-queue","Items that need a decision by a person, not a re-run. Each has a type, the entity, when it was raised, an owner and a resolution note. Exception Raised by Resolution in the console Contract creation with unknown DDS outcome S2 , S3 , S9 Confirm with DDS, then mark as created (enter the DDS mandate ID) or as not created Collection missed its payment-file date S5 Per the rule in open point c Payment with no FT reference after 7 days S5 Chase DDS; enter or re-fetch Day-End reconciliation mismatch S5 Accept DDS’s value or raise with DDS APEX row rejected S12 Correct and re-send with the same identifiers"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › Requirements","Document","7. Access","direct-debit/docs/product.html#access","Geidea-internal only. Two rights: view, and operate (manual actions and configuration edits). Provision for Phase 2: a partner integrated by API, working under its own OIC, may be given access to the console limited to its own OIC or OICs, so it can see the job runs and batches for its contracts. Partner access is not built now, but every view and action must be filterable and restricted by OIC from the start."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S14 — Operations Console & Job Control","Document","Order Model changes","direct-debit/docs/product.html#order-model-changes-8","Entity.field Type / values Purpose Job Run (new entity) Section 1 One row per run Job Run Record (new entity) job_run_id , entity type and ID, outcome , error_reason What each run did per record Exception (new entity) exception_id , type , entity type and ID, raised_at , status (Open / Resolved), owner , resolution , resolved_by , resolved_at Section 6"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit at Geidea — Product, Functional and Technical Reference › S14 — Operations Console & Job Control","Document","Open points","direct-debit/docs/product.html#open-points-13","# Point Status a Where the console lives (Back Office, MA or a standalone tool) and which team builds the screens Open — Product, DD Backend team, Delivery b Minimum scope for go-live. Proposed: run log, missed-run detection, email alerts, re-run, payment file download and manual mark, basic exceptions list. Richer dashboard later Open — Product c A collection whose payment file missed its date: send it in the next day’s file with a new due date (keeping the original on record), or mark it Skipped and roll it over. DDS does not accept a past due date Open — Product, Business d What existing scheduler or monitoring tooling the Backend team already has, and how much of sections 1 to 3 it covers Open — DD Backend team e Who operates the console day to day, and the group mailbox for alerts Open — Operations f Partner access limited to the partner’s own OIC (section 7 ) Phase 2. Keep everything per OIC now"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit","Hub","Direct Debit","direct-debit/index.html","direct debit · solutioning complete Direct Debit Recurring bank-to-bank collections through the UAE Direct Debit Marketplace (DDS). The merchant creates a contract in the Merchant Portal, the customer signs it with UAE PASS, and Geidea collects each payment on its due date and pays the merchant out through APEX."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit","Hub","Documents","direct-debit/index.html#documents","Product Document Part A: Direct Debit at Geidea · Part B: functional requirements · Part C: technical requirements with the Backend stories S1 to S14 v2.7 · 18-Nov-2026"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit","Hub","Following a trail of requirements","direct-debit/index.html#how-to-follow","Every reference is a link Section and story references such as B8 , S5 or S1 section 5.4 jump to that place. Hover to see where it goes. See who points here Under each referenced heading, Referenced from lists every place that refers to it, so you can follow a requirement both ways. Go back along your trail After a jump, the Back button at the bottom returns you to where you were, one step at a time (or press Alt + ←). Blue text changed in recent versions; struck-through text is superseded."],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit","Hub","Jump into the document","direct-debit/index.html#jump",""],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit › Jump into the document","Hub","Part A","direct-debit/index.html#part-a","Direct Debit at Geidea A1. What Direct Debit is A2. Market use case and target segments A3. Payment instruments A4. Key scheme rules and timings A5. Commercial and funds model A6. Who does what A7. End-to-end flows A8. Settlement and merchant payout timing A9. Merchant enablement and configuration A10. User journeys A11. Scope and phasing A12. Operations and support A13. Risks and open items A14. DDS go-live checklist"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit › Jump into the document","Hub","Part B","direct-debit/index.html#part-b","Functional requirements B1. Scope & Actors B2. Merchant Enablement B3. Mandate Creation B4. Mandate Status Lifecycle B5. Contract Signing via UAE Pass B6. Subscription & Collection Schedule B7. Payment Execution - Daily Processing Chain B8. Failure Handling, Retry & Rollover B9. Cancellation B10. Reconciliation & Settlement - To be Finalized with the APEX team B11. Notifications B12. Security & Data Handling B13. API Support B14. Bulk Contract Upload B15. Direct Debit User Permissions B16. Store Selection B17. Geidea Support View B18. Open Items"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit › Jump into the document","Hub","Part C","direct-debit/index.html#part-c","Technical requirements C1. Story map C2. Delivery slices C3. Frontend story alignment C4. Rules for every story C5. Provisions for the Phase 2 partner model C6. Backend stories"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit › Jump into the document","Hub","Backend stories","direct-debit/index.html#backend-stories","S1 to S14 S1 — DD Merchant Enablement & Configuration S2 — Create DDS Contract S3 — Contract Sending & Signing S4 — Mandate Approval & Activation S5 — Daily Collection Cycle S6 — Failed Collection Handling S7 — Contract & Subscription Management S8 — Contract Cancellation S9 — Bulk Contract Upload S10 — Merchant Portal Views & Reporting S11 — Merchant Notifications & Webhooks S12 — APEX Federation S13 — Direct Debit User Permissions S14 — Operations Console & Job Control"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit","Hub","Prototype","direct-debit/index.html#prototype","Merchant Portal prototype Contracts, signing and collections as the merchant and the customer see them; the PGW configuration settings open from the top bar (PGW Config in MA) Illustrative"],["Direct Debit","Direct Debit","Hub","Visual pages","direct-debit/index.html#visual-pages","Being built Visual pages for the contract lifecycle, the daily collection cycle, failed collections and settlement will follow. planned"],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Visual page","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","mcp/calculator.html","mcp · visual page Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent Enter an AED order and a rate. See the amount the customer pays, rounded up. Then refund it in parts and see how PGW converts each refund, why partial refunds round down, and how the final refund closes the gap. Illustrative Reflects solution requirements v3.0 and PGW stories v0.1 (25-Nov-2026) Read the requirement → Stories: S4 section 2 → S6 → HPP simulator End-to-end view Back to MCP Terms. Order currency : what the merchant priced in (AED). Transaction currency : what the customer pays in and what the scheme clears in. Minor unit : the smallest amount of a currency (GBP 0.01). Rate : AED → transaction currency, 7 decimals, from the Visa section of Planet's file, markup included. Rates on this page are dummy. Field names are from the PGW stories. Try AED 100 → GBP AED 99.99, rounded up AED 0.01 INR Refund 60 + 40 Refund in thirds Full refund Refund below AED 0.10 Refund too much Newer rate file Why round down 1"],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Visual page","Order and rate","mcp/calculator.html#order-and-rate","S4 section 2 Order amount (AED) Transaction currency GBP EUR INR USD SAR JPY MVP currencies have 2 decimals. JPY is Phase 2. Rate: 1 AED = … (7 decimals) Filled from the dummy Visa rate when you pick a currency. Change it to test other rates. 4"],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Visual page","Minimum refund (existing rule)","mcp/calculator.html#minimum-refund-existing-rule","S6 check 1.2 MCP adds no minimum of its own. The existing minimum refund amount (AED 0.10) applies, as for any refund. It is enough for MCP: AED 0.10 converts to at least one minor unit in every MVP currency, because each has a rate of 0.1 or more per AED. Existing minimum refund: AED 0.10 AED 0.10 × 0.2137000 = 0.02137 → down = 0.02 GBP at least one minor unit Currency Dummy rate AED 0.10 converts to GBP 0.2137000 0.02 EUR 0.2489000 0.02 INR 23.1200000 2.31 USD 0.2759000 0.02 SAR 1.0345000 0.10 Decided 25-Nov-2026. A refund below AED 0.10 gets the existing refund message. 2"],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Visual page","The quote the customer pays","mcp/calculator.html#the-quote-the-customer-pays","S4 section 2 · S5 section 3 Order (mcpBaseAmount) 100.00 AED What the merchant priced and sees. AED × rate, exact 21.37 GBP 100.00 × 0.2137000 Quote (amount) 21.37 GBP Exact. Nothing to round. HPP shows: GBP 21.37 Order amount AED 100.00 Exchange rate 1 AED = 0.2137 GBP Stored: mcpRate 0.2137000 Rule. Order amount × rate, rounded up to the currency's decimals (decided 24-Nov). The quote is fixed for the HPP session: a newer rate file does not change it. 3"],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Visual page","Refunds in AED, sent in the transaction currency","mcp/calculator.html#refunds-in-aed-sent-in-the-transaction-currency","S6 section 1 · section 2 Refund amount (AED) Refund Refund the rest Clear refunds # Refund AED (mcpBaseAmount) Checks Conversion Sent GBP (amount) Refunded so far P 100.00 purchase 100.00 × 0.2137000 = 21.37 → up 21.37 — No refunds yet. Enter an AED amount, or use a Try preset. AED: what the merchant refunds AED 100.00 left 100.00 GBP: what goes back to the card GBP 21.37 left 21.37 open Left to refund: AED 100.00 · GBP 21.37. The next refund of AED 100.00 is the final one. A newer rate file has loaded since the purchase (GBP 0.2141000 for the same pair) Compare: what if partial refunds were rounded to the nearest minor unit instead of down? Rule. Non-final refund: refund AED × purchase mcpRate , rounded down . Final refund (AED refunded reaches AED paid): purchase amount − all earlier refunds, in the transaction currency (last-cent rule). Never a new rate."],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: AED 100 → GBP","mcp/calculator.html#try=gb",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: AED 99.99, rounded up","mcp/calculator.html#try=round-up",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: AED 0.01","mcp/calculator.html#try=tiny",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: INR","mcp/calculator.html#try=inr",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Refund 60 + 40","mcp/calculator.html#try=refund-60-40",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Refund in thirds","mcp/calculator.html#try=thirds",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Full refund","mcp/calculator.html#try=full",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Refund below AED 0.10","mcp/calculator.html#try=min",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Refund too much","mcp/calculator.html#try=over",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Newer rate file","mcp/calculator.html#try=newer-rate",""],["MCP","Calculator: the quote, refunds and the last cent","Scenario","Try it: Why round down","mcp/calculator.html#try=nearest-fails",""],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","1. Objective","mcp/docs/requirements.html","v3.0 25-Nov-2026 Source: MCP - Solution Requirements v3.0 (25-Nov-2026).docx Version 3.0 · 25-Nov-2026 · Prepared by Ghulam Rabbani (Product) 1. Objective A customer on Geidea’s hosted payment page (HPP) is offered the price in the currency of the country they are in, and can pay in that currency. The merchant keeps pricing, reporting and settlement in its own order currency (AED). Geidea earns an FX markup on every converted payment. Planet Payment provides the rates and holds the markup, as it does for POS DCC today. This document sets out what each Geidea system and team must deliver. It is organized by system. The PGW dev stories (MCP – PGW Dev Stories) give the PGW requirements at build level."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","2. How the merchant experiences this","mcp/docs/requirements.html#how-the-merchant-experiences-this","Nothing changes for the merchant. It creates orders in AED, sees AED in every API response, webhook, report and portal screen, and is settled in its settlement currency as today. The merchant is not told that the customer paid in another currency. This is the design boundary that every requirement below respects."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","3. How the customer experiences this","mcp/docs/requirements.html#how-the-customer-experiences-this","HPP simulator The customer opens the payment page from the merchant’s site, an SDK that shows Geidea HPP, or a Pay-by-Link. PGW finds the customer’s country from the IP address. If the merchant offers that country’s currency, the HPP shows the total in that currency, the order amount in AED and the exchange rate. The customer can keep the proposed currency, choose another currency the merchant offers, or go back to AED. Card, Apple Pay, Google Pay and Samsung Pay are paid in the chosen currency. Tabby and Tamara are available in AED only. The confirmation shows the amount paid and the AED order amount. When PGW does not propose a currency (location not found, the country’s currency is not offered, or the customer is in the UAE), the HPP shows AED only, with no currency selector, as today."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","4. The three currencies","mcp/docs/requirements.html#the-three-currencies","End-to-end view Currency What it is Who sees it Order currency AED. What the merchant priced the order in. Merchant, Merchant Portal, merchant reports. APEX main amount and currency fields. Transaction currency The currency the customer pays in. The scheme authorizes and clears in it. APEX calls it the cardholder currency. Customer, PGW, MPGS/CyberSource, Postilion, scheme, Planet. APEX cardholder fields (internal). Never the merchant. Settlement currency What the merchant is paid out in. Not changed by MCP. Merchant (as today). Worked example used throughout: order AED 100.00. The customer is in the UK and pays GBP 21.37 at Planet’s rate AED → GBP 0.2137000 (markup included). The merchant is settled in SAR."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","5. Scope","mcp/docs/requirements.html#scope",""],["MCP","1. Objective › 5. Scope","Document","5.1 In scope","mcp/docs/requirements.html#in-scope","Any AED order paid on Geidea HPP: orders created by API (Create Session), by an SDK that shows Geidea HPP, or by Pay-by-Link. Includes saved-card payments that the customer starts on HPP. Card payments, including Apple Pay, Google Pay and Samsung Pay. Sale ( paymentOperation = Pay ). Full refund and partial refunds, including several partial refunds on one transaction. UAE merchants on Geidea’s ecommerce gateway, on MPGS and CyberSource. Order currency AED at launch. The solution supports other order currencies through configuration, with no code change."],["MCP","1. Objective › 5. Scope","Document","5.2 Out of scope","mcp/docs/requirements.html#out-of-scope","Orders in another currency (merchants with ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY ). They are processed as today, without MCP. Tabby, Tamara and other alternative payment methods. HPP shows “Available in AED only” under them. Flows with no HPP: Direct API where the merchant collects the card details, recurring and merchant-initiated payments. Authorization and capture ( PreAuthorize ), including partial captures. Merchant rebate on the markup. VPN and proxy users. A wrong location is an accepted risk."],["MCP","1. Objective › 5. Scope","Document","5.3 Phase 2","mcp/docs/requirements.html#phase-2","Zero and three-decimal currencies (JPY, KWD, BHD, OMR). They need PGW changes to amount handling, display, rounding and refunds, and enablement on APEX, Postilion and Planet. Their country mappings (JP, KW, BH, OM) stay inactive in MVP. Every interface format in MVP states the number of decimals, so Phase 2 needs no format change."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","6. Solution approach","mcp/docs/requirements.html#solution-approach",""],["MCP","1. Objective › 6. Solution approach","Document","6.1 How it works","mcp/docs/requirements.html#how-it-works","End-to-end view Planet publishes a daily rate file over SFTP. The rates include the markup. PGW loads, checks and caches the file. The merchant creates an AED order. The customer opens HPP . PGW looks up the customer’s IP in the MaxMind GeoIP2 database that PGW holds, and gets the country. PGW maps the country to a currency. If MCP is on for the merchant and the merchant offers that currency, PGW takes the rate from the cached file and shows the converted amount. The rates are fixed for the payment session. The customer pays. PGW authorizes in the chosen currency through MPGS or CyberSource and stores the rate on the transaction. PGW federates the transaction to APEX with the AED order amount in the main fields and the transaction amount, currency and rate in the new cardholder fields (section 8.3). The scheme file reaches Postilion in the transaction currency. Postilion clears in that currency. PGW’s enrichment file gives Postilion the AED view, so the PTLF to APEX matches the federation (section 8.4). APEX prices and pays out the merchant from the AED amount, as today. APEX includes the MCP transactions in the Daily Transaction File (DTF) to Planet , as for DCC. Planet accounts for the markup (section 8.5). A refund is made in AED by the merchant. PGW converts it at the rate stored on the original transaction."],["MCP","1. Objective › 6. Solution approach","Document","6.2 Systems and their roles","mcp/docs/requirements.html#systems-and-their-roles","System Role in MCP Change PGW Configuration, rate file, geolocation, HPP currency offer, payment, refunds, merchant outputs, federation to APEX, enrichment file Main build APEX Holds AED in the main fields and the transaction currency in new cardholder fields; flags MCP; produces the DTF to Planet New fields (parked, section 8.3); MCP rows in the DTF Postilion Clears in the transaction currency as today; builds the PTLF with the AED view from the enrichment file Enrichment processing (parked, section 8.4) MPGS, CyberSource Authorize and clear in the transaction currency No change. Already set up for the full ISO currency list. Merchant Portal Shows what APEX returns: order and settlement currency No change Planet Payment (external) Daily rate file with markup; MCP merchant registration; DTF processing, reports and markup share Existing DCC rails, MCP product type MaxMind (external) GeoIP2 database: IP address to country New subscription (approved) MW, TMS, Direct Debit Backend, DDS, GSDK, Noon, Lean Not involved No change Business functions: Operations (merchant enablement with Planet), FinOps (reporting), Risk & Compliance (scheme position, section 7)."],["MCP","1. Objective › 6. Solution approach","Document","6.3 Design principles","mcp/docs/requirements.html#design-principles","The merchant world stays in AED. No merchant-facing output carries the transaction currency, the rate or an MCP flag. One conversion, at PGW. PGW converts once and stores the result. No downstream system recalculates an amount. Location decides the offer. MCP is offered only when the customer’s location gives a currency the merchant offers. The customer can then switch between the offered currencies and AED. Rates come from a cached file. No rate is fetched during a payment session. Refunds use the original rate. Never a newer rate."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","7. Scheme position and known risks","mcp/docs/requirements.html#scheme-position-and-known-risks","Outside DCC. Visa’s DCC rules apply when the currency is derived from the card (BIN). In MCP the currency comes from the customer’s IP location, the AED amount is shown next to it, and the customer can switch back to AED. The scheme clears in the currency the customer chose. Planet processes and reports these transactions as MCP (DTF Product Indicator 2 , “Shop in Your Currency”), separate from DCC. Double markup (accepted). A customer whose card is in AED may be shown a foreign currency while abroad. Both amounts are shown and the customer can switch to AED. No further disclosure is shown. No rounding drift. Amounts are stored once by PGW and copied downstream. Rate file availability. When no valid rate file is cached, MCP is suspended and HPP shows AED only (section 8.1). For Risk & Compliance to assess: the IP-based offer as outside DCC obligations; the HPP display (section 8.1, HPP)."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","8. Requirements","mcp/docs/requirements.html#requirements",""],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.1 PGW: PGW engineering team","mcp/docs/requirements.html#pgw-pgw-engineering-team","HPP simulator End-to-end view Build-level detail is in the PGW dev stories S1 to S7. Merchant configuration (S1) Setting Level Purpose MCP toggle in PGW Configuration (name to be set by PGW, for example ENABLE_DYNAMIC_MCP ) Per MID, Live and Test Turns MCP on for the merchant MCP currencies Per MID The transaction currencies the merchant offers, chosen from the MCP currency list MCP currency list System Currencies MCP can offer. A currency is listed only if APEX supports it and Planet’s rate file carries it. 2-decimal currencies only in MVP. Set before go-live: see below. Country-to-currency map System ISO country code → currency (GB → GBP, FR → EUR…). Phase 2 countries inactive. MCP order currencies System Order currencies MCP applies to. AED at launch. The MCP toggle must have a name that is clearly different from ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY and ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY_SETTLEMENT , which mean other things. No markup is set in PGW. Markup is held at Planet and is included in the rates. MCP currency list at go-live. Before production, Product gets the latest list of currencies APEX supports, agrees the list with Business and Commercial, and asks the PGW team to configure it. The list is configuration, so later changes need no code change. The PGW integration team switches MCP on only after Operations confirms the merchant is registered with Planet for MCP (section 8.6). Rate file (S2) - Fetch Planet’s daily rate file over SFTP, decrypt it (if Planet confirms PGP encryption, with Geidea’s private key; Planet holds the public key), parse it and store the rates in a PGW table. Layout: Planet MAS template 194 (Visa and Mastercard sections). Until the SFTP feed is live, load Planet’s sample file. - Basic checks only: header and trailer present, record count matches, file not loaded before. Planet guarantees its rates. A file that fails is not loaded, and Operations is alerted. - Quote from the Visa section . Rate direction: units of transaction currency for 1 unit of order currency (AED → GBP 0.2137000). Converted amount = order amount × rate. - Each rate has a validity window (48 hours in Planet’s sample, so daily files overlap). Use the rate from the latest loaded file that is valid at the time of the quote (S4). - If no valid rate exists for a currency, that currency is not offered. If no rate is valid at all, MCP is suspended for all merchants and Operations is alerted (S4). Geolocation (S3) - Fetch the MaxMind GeoIP2 database (CSV) when MaxMind publishes a new release, parse it and store IP networks with their country in a PGW table. - Look up the customer’s IP address in this table before HPP shows the amount (S4). No external call during the session. - No country found: no MCP for the session. The payment is a regular AED payment. HPP (S4) - MCP is offered only when all of these are true: the order currency is an MCP order currency (AED), paymentOperation = Pay , the MCP toggle is on, a valid rate file is cached, the IP gives a country, the country maps to an active currency, the merchant offers that currency, and the file has a valid rate for it. - Then HPP shows: the total in the proposed currency, the order amount in AED and the rate (“1 AED = 0.2137 GBP”), and a currency selector. The selector lists AED and every currency the merchant offers that has a valid rate. - When MCP is not offered, HPP shows AED only, with no selector. A customer in the UAE gets no selector. - The rates are taken once when HPP first loads and are fixed for the session. Switching currency re-prices from these rates. - The converted amount is rounded up to the currency’s minor unit. Example: AED 99.99 × 0.2137000 = GBP 21.367863 → GBP 21.37. - The selector and the amounts are shown above the wallet buttons. Wallet buttons use the currency and amount on screen. - Tabby and Tamara are available only when the selected currency is AED, with “Available in AED only” under them. - The confirmation shows the amount paid and the AED order amount, for example “GBP "],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.2 MPGS and CyberSource: technical integration team","mcp/docs/requirements.html#mpgs-and-cybersource-technical-integration-team","No change. PGW sends the transaction currency and amount as for any non-AED payment. MPGS and CyberSource are already set up for the full list of ISO currencies."],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.3 APEX: APEX team — parked","mcp/docs/requirements.html#apex-team-parked","Agreed approach (APEX team, 23-Nov-2026). The exact field names and formats are awaited from APEX. The PGW federation story (S8) waits on them. Field Today For an MCP transaction amount Transaction amount Order amount (AED 100.00) transactionCurrency Transaction currency Order currency (AED) settlementAmount , settlementCurrency , exchangeRate Settlement view No change Cardholder Amount (new) — Transaction amount (21.37) Cardholder Currency (new) — Transaction currency (GBP) Transaction Identifier (new) — Y for MCP Cardholder ConvRate (new) — Rate order → transaction currency (0.2137000) Required outcomes - The same fields come from the PGW federation and from the Postilion PTLF, so the two records match. - Refunds carry the same fields: the refund’s converted amount and the original rate. - Chargebacks arrive in the transaction currency. APEX matches them to the original by transaction reference (ARN or RRN), not by amount and currency, and uses the cardholder fields to apply them in AED. Partial chargebacks must never total more than the original AED amount. - A transaction flagged MCP in the PGW federation whose PTLF row does not carry the MCP fields, or the reverse, goes to an exception visible to Operations daily. - Merchant pricing, payout, refunds, merchant APIs and the Merchant Portal continue on the AED fields with no change. - DTF to Planet. APEX produces the DTF, as for DCC. MCP transactions (purchases and refunds) are included with field 22 Product Indicator = 2 and field 20 = the applied rate as a decimal, subject to Planet’s confirmation (section 8.5). Required to be confirmed - Field keys for the API and the PTLF; currency format (alpha GBP or numeric 826 ); amount format and decimals; rate decimals (Planet uses 7); value of the identifier for non-MCP transactions. - Rounding for partial chargebacks (AED = transaction amount ÷ rate). - Exception handling for MCP mismatches."],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.4 Postilion: Postilion team — parked","mcp/docs/requirements.html#postilion-team-parked","The enrichment file format and its data set are being finalized. The PGW enrichment story (S9) waits on this. Required outcomes - Clearing is unchanged: Postilion receives the DCF/TC33 in the transaction currency and clears in it. - For each MCP leg (purchase, refund, partial refund), PGW sends an enrichment record. Approach under discussion: reuse Postilion’s existing enrichment-file processing for PayGlocal transactions, with PGW supplying (a) the matching key (PGW order ID, RRN, authorization code), (b) the order view (AED amount and currency) and the transaction view (amount and currency), (c) the rate and the MCP flag. - Postilion builds the PTLF row for APEX with the order view in amount / transactionCurrency and the transaction view and rate in the new cardholder fields, copied with no recalculation. - Non-MCP ecommerce, POS, and POS DCC with Planet are unchanged. Required to be confirmed - The enrichment layout (columns, positions, decimals), and that MCP records are processed before the matching DCF/TC33. - Matching on PGW order ID + RRN + authorization code for MPGS-routed transactions as well as CyberSource."],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.5 Planet Payment: Jose Albarran Sierra","mcp/docs/requirements.html#planet-payment-jose-albarran-sierra","# Item Status 1 Daily rate file (template 194) over SFTP: connection, folder, whether the file is PGP-encrypted (if so, Geidea shares its public key), file naming, publishing time Asked 13-Nov 2 Rate validity: confirm the 48-hour window, and that the latest file applies where two files are valid Asked 13-Nov 3 Rate file covers every currency pair Geidea offers Asked 13-Nov 4 DTF v1.10 for MCP rows: field 22 Product Indicator = 2 ; field 20 = applied rate as a decimal; card number and markup fields Asked 13-Nov 5 Merchant registration: existing bulk load file with field 47 Product Type = MCP , same acquirer set-up as DCC Confirmed 13-Nov 6 Markup per currency pair held at Planet; Planet guarantees the rate Confirmed APEX produces the DTF and includes the MCP rows (section 8.3)."],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.6 Operations: owner to confirm","mcp/docs/requirements.html#operations-owner-to-confirm","Merchant enablement with Planet. For each merchant approved for MCP, register the merchant with Planet through the existing DCC enablement process (bulk load file, field 47 Product Type = MCP ). The department that runs this process for DCC is to be identified and to agree to run it for MCP. PGW enablement. After Planet confirms, ask the PGW integration team to switch MCP on and set the merchant’s MCP currencies. Act on alerts when a rate file fails or MCP is suspended. Markup changes for a merchant go through Planet and take effect from the rate file."],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.7 Merchant Portal: Merchant Portal team","mcp/docs/requirements.html#merchant-portal-team","No change. The portal shows what APEX returns: order currency and settlement currency. To validate in UAT with an MCP transaction."],["MCP","1. Objective › 8. Requirements","Document","8.8 Reporting: Operations & Finance to provide","mcp/docs/requirements.html#reporting-operations-finance-to-provide","Merchant reports need no change. Internal reporting is defined by Operations & Finance (FinOps), then built by APEX. Areas: MCP activity (both currencies, rate, flag), markup revenue from Planet’s reports, Planet invoice reconciliation, scheme funding in the transaction currency, refunds and chargebacks with both currencies, exceptions (MCP mismatches, rate file failures)."],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","9. End-to-end flow","mcp/docs/requirements.html#end-to-end-flow","End-to-end view: purchase and refunds Daily Planet -> PGW Rate file (template 194, markup included). PGW checks and caches it. Purchase: order AED 100.00, customer in the UK pays GBP 21.37, merchant settled in SAR Merchant -> PGW Create Session, AED 100.00 Customer -> HPP Opens the payment page PGW IP -> MaxMind -> GB -> GBP; GBP on for the merchant; rate 0.2137000 (Visa section) HPP -> Customer GBP 21.37, AED 100.00, 1 AED = 0.2137 GBP, currency selector Customer -> HPP Pays in GBP (card or wallet) PGW -> MPGS/CYBS Authorize GBP 21.37 -> approved PGW -> Merchant Callback: AED 100.00 PGW -> APEX Federation: AED 100.00 in main fields; GBP 21.37, rate, MCP flag in cardholder fields PGW -> Postilion Enrichment record (day end) Scheme -> Postilion DCF/TC33 in GBP 21.37; Postilion clears in GBP Postilion -> APEX PTLF: same convention as the federation APEX Prices on AED 100.00, pays the merchant in SAR APEX -> Planet DTF with the MCP row (Product Indicator 2) Refund: merchant refunds AED 60.00, then AED 40.00 Merchant -> PGW Refund AED 60.00 -> PGW refunds GBP 12.82 (stored rate, rounded down) Merchant -> PGW Refund AED 40.00 -> PGW refunds GBP 8.55 (last-cent rule) PGW -> APEX Each refund federated in AED, with its GBP amount and the original rate"],["MCP","1. Objective","Document","10. Open items","mcp/docs/requirements.html#open-items","# Item Section Team Owner 1 APEX field keys and formats for the four cardholder fields; partial-chargeback rounding; MCP mismatch exception; MCP rows in the DTF 8.3 APEX APEX team 2 Enrichment file layout and data set; processing before the DCF/TC33; MPGS matching 8.4 Postilion, PGW Postilion team, Ghulam Rabbani 3 Rate file SFTP delivery, PGP encryption and key exchange, validity, currency coverage; DTF fields for MCP 8.5 Planet Jose Albarran Sierra 4 Department that runs merchant enablement with Planet for DCC, and its agreement to do it for MCP 8.6 Operations To identify 5 Final name of the MCP toggle 8.1 PGW PGW engineering team 6 Internal MCP reporting requirements 8.8 Operations & Finance FinOps 7 Phase 2: 0 and 3-decimal currencies on PGW, APEX, Postilion and Planet 5.3 All —"],["MCP","Story map","Document","Story map","mcp/docs/stories.html","v0.1 25-Nov-2026 Source: MCP - PGW Dev Stories v0.1 (25-Nov-2026).docx Version 0.1 draft · 25-Nov-2026 · Prepared by Ghulam Rabbani (Product) Team: PGW engineering Reference: MCP – Solution Requirements v3.0 (25-Nov-2026) These stories are the requirement. Each story can be read alone. A story refers to another story only for work that must be built first. Story map # Story What it delivers Depends on Status S1 FE/BE – MCP configuration MCP toggle and currency list per MID in PGW configuration. System lists: MCP currencies, country-to-currency map, MCP order currencies. — Written S2 BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store PGW fetches Planet’s daily rate file, decrypts it, parses it and stores the rates in a table. — Written. Build on Planet’s sample file until SFTP is live (P3). S3 BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store PGW fetches the MaxMind GeoIP2 database, parses it and stores IP networks with their country in a table. P2 Written S4 FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection Country and rate lookups. HPP proposes the customer’s currency, shows both amounts and the rate, lets the customer switch. APMs AED only. MCP suspended when no rate is valid. S1, S2, S3 Written S5 BE – MCP payment and transaction record Payment in the chosen currency. MCP fields on the order and transaction. Wallet check. Order totals. S4 Written S6 BE – MCP refunds Refund in AED converted at the stored rate. Last-cent rule. S5 Written S7 BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED Callback, order inquiry, pay and refund responses, Pay-by-Link inquiry show AED only. S5 Written S8 BE – Federation to APEX MCP fields in createTransaction. S5, P4 Parked: waiting on APEX field names S9 BE – Enrichment file to Postilion MCP records in the enrichment file. S5, P5 Parked: waiting on the file format Build order: S1, S2 and S3 in parallel → S4 → S5 → S6, S7. S8 after P4. S9 after P5."],["MCP","Story map","Document","Prerequisites (not PGW work)","mcp/docs/stories.html#prerequisites-not-pgw-work","# Item Needed for Owner P1 The merchant is registered with Planet for MCP (bulk load file, field 47 Product Type = MCP ). Operations confirms before the PGW team switches MCP on. Before a MID is set up (S1) Operations (department to identify) P2 MaxMind GeoIP2 subscription (monthly first) and evaluation of the IP database. S3 Ghulam Rabbani, PGW engineering P3 Planet rate file delivery: SFTP connection, folder, file name, publishing time, and whether the file is PGP-encrypted (if so, key exchange). S2 (live feed) Planet (Jose Albarran Sierra), PGW P4 APEX field names and formats for the cardholder fields. S8 APEX team P5 Enrichment file format and data set for MCP. S9 Ghulam Rabbani with the Postilion team P6 MCP currency list for production: latest APEX-supported list, agreed with Business and Commercial, given to the PGW team to configure. S1 (go-live) Ghulam Rabbani"],["MCP","Story map","Document","Rules for every story","mcp/docs/stories.html#rules-for-every-story","Terms. Order currency = the currency the merchant created the order in (AED). Transaction currency = the currency the customer pays in; the scheme authorizes and clears in it. Rate = Planet’s rate from the order currency to the transaction currency, markup included. MCP transaction = a transaction where PGW converted the amount. Field names. PGW field names are camelCase . New names in these stories are proposals, except mcpBaseAmount and mcpBaseCurrency (agreed). PGW engineering can rename them. Keep the meaning. The PGW backend applies every rule, also when the screen already blocks the action. Identify the merchant by MID in every API and in all documentation. New fields in merchant APIs are additions. Do not change or remove existing fields. Times are UAE time (UTC+4). Rate validity times in Planet’s file are UTC. Amounts. Calculate with exact decimals, never floating point. Every amount is stored with the number of decimals of its currency (2 in MVP). The merchant never sees the transaction currency, the rate or an MCP flag (S7)."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S1 — FE/BE – MCP configuration","mcp/docs/stories.html#s1-febe-mcp-configuration","Simulator: merchant set-up"],["MCP","Story map › S1 — FE/BE – MCP configuration","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background","The PGW integration team sets merchant settings in PGW Configuration. The settings are stored on the MID’s PAYMENT_GATEWAY product instance, in its data object. The screen saves with the existing product instance update ( PATCH /product/api/v1/productInstance/{productInstanceId} , JSON-Patch replace on data/<key> ). Live and Test instances are separate. The Gateway Settings tab already has ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY ( isMulticurrencyEnabled , currencies ). That setting lets a merchant create orders in other currencies. MCP is a different setting: it lets the customer pay in another currency. The two must stay separate."],["MCP","Story map › S1 — FE/BE – MCP configuration","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal","The PGW integration team can turn MCP on for a MID and choose the currencies the merchant offers. PGW holds three system lists that MCP reads: MCP currencies, the country-to-currency map and MCP order currencies."],["MCP","Story map › S1 — FE/BE – MCP configuration","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary","Item Value Story ID S1 Users PGW integration team (PGW Configuration) Fields written isDynamicMcpEnabled , dynamicMcpCurrencies Save Existing product instance update Depends on — . Before a MID is set up: P1. Followed by S4 Out of scope Markup (held at Planet). Merchant portal login (no access)."],["MCP","Story map › S1 — FE/BE – MCP configuration","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements",""],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Settings in data (Live and Test separately)","mcp/docs/stories.html#settings-in-data-live-and-test-separately","Field Type Default Values isDynamicMcpEnabled Boolean false true = MCP on for this MID dynamicMcpCurrencies Array of String [] ISO 4217 alpha codes, each from the MCP currency list (section 2) Existing MIDs: add both fields with the defaults. The existing product instance search returns the fields. The existing update accepts them."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. System lists (configuration, not per MID)","mcp/docs/stories.html#system-lists-configuration-not-per-mid","Store as configuration. Do not write the values in code. List Content Initial value MCP currency list Currencies MCP can offer: currency code, numeric code, decimals, active 2-decimal currencies that APEX supports and Planet’s file carries. For build and test: GBP, EUR, INR, USD, SAR. Production list from P6. Country-to-currency map ISO 3166 alpha-2 country → currency code, active flag From the Feature Brief: GB → GBP, US → USD, SA → SAR, QA → QAR, IN → INR, CN → CNY, RU → RUB, CH → CHF, AU → AUD, CA → CAD, SG → SGD, Eurozone countries → EUR. JP, KW, BH, OM inactive (Phase 2). MCP order currencies Order currencies MCP applies to AED"],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Screen","mcp/docs/stories.html#screen","Add the section Dynamic multi-currency pricing in PGW Configuration (tab position: PGW engineering, next to Gateway Settings). Toggle with label Dynamic multi-currency pricing and one line of text. Use a label that is clearly different from ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY and ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY_SETTLEMENT . Ship the label translated (not as a raw key). Under the toggle: a table with one row per active currency in the MCP currency list: currency code, decimals, an Offered toggle. When MCP is off, the currency table is disabled. The selected currencies stay stored. A note under the table: “No markup is set here. Markup is held at Planet and is included in the rates.” Discard Changes and Save changes work as on the other tabs."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Checks on Save changes","mcp/docs/stories.html#checks-on-save-changes","# Check Message 4.1 MCP on → at least one currency offered. Select at least one currency. 4.2 Each currency is in the MCP currency list and active. {code} is not available for MCP. Any failure: do not save. Show the message and the banner “Changes not saved. Fix the highlighted fields.” No failure: save the changed fields."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","5. Effect of a saved change","mcp/docs/stories.html#effect-of-a-saved-change","Change Effect MCP turned on Applies to HPP sessions opened after the save. MCP turned off, or a currency removed New HPP sessions do not offer it. Payments already made and their refunds keep their stored rate (S6)."],["MCP","Story map › S1 — FE/BE – MCP configuration","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria","New MID: MCP off, no currencies. Turn MCP on, save with no currency: message 4.1. Turn MCP on, offer GBP and EUR, save, reload: values shown. Turn MCP off, save, reload: MCP off, GBP and EUR still selected and disabled. Change on Test: Live does not change. Remove INR from the MCP currency list in configuration: INR is not shown on the screen and a save with INR is rejected (4.2)."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S2 — BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store","mcp/docs/stories.html#s2-be-planet-rate-file-fetch-parse-and-store","Simulator: yesterday's rates Simulator: no valid file End-to-end: no valid file"],["MCP","Story map › S2 — BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-1","Planet publishes one rate file each day. The rates include the markup. This story only brings the file into PGW: fetch it, decrypt it, parse it and store the rates in a table. S4 reads the table when HPP loads. Layout: Planet MAS template 194, comma-separated, with a Visa section and a Mastercard section. Planet’s sample (USD base; Geidea’s file will have AED as the base currency): PFH,20260214,01 VILFH,HPLANET VISA,20260214,01 VID,1023,$,840,2,€,978,2,0.9123456,2026-02-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-02-15T23:59:59.000Z VID,1024,$,840,2,£,826,2,0.7890123,2026-02-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-02-15T23:59:59.000Z VILFT,TPLANET VISA,1804,1.7013579,2 MCLFH,HPLANET MASTERCARD,20260214,01 MCD,1025,$,840,2,€,978,2,0.9121000,2026-02-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-02-15T23:59:59.000Z MCLFT,TPLANET MASTERCARD,978,0.9121000,1 PFT,TXN RATES,2782,2.6134579,3"],["MCP","Story map › S2 — BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal-1","Each day’s rates are in the PGW rate table, one row per rate."],["MCP","Story map › S2 — BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-1","Item Value Story ID S2 Source Planet, SFTP, once a day Table written mcp_rate (name is a proposal) Depends on P3 for the live feed. Until then, load Planet’s sample file. Followed by S4 Out of scope Rate lookup and MCP suspension (S4). Refunds use the rate stored on the transaction, not this table (S6)."],["MCP","Story map › S2 — BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-1",""],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Fetch","mcp/docs/stories.html#fetch","Collect the file from Planet’s SFTP folder once a day, after Planet’s publishing time (P3). Retry until a set time (configuration). Alert Operations when no file has arrived by then. Encryption. Planet is to confirm whether the file is PGP-encrypted (P3). If it is: Geidea creates a PGP key pair for PGW and gives Planet the public key. Planet encrypts with it. PGW decrypts with the private key. Keep the private key in PGW’s secrets store, never in code or configuration files. Keep the received file as it is, for audit."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Basic checks","mcp/docs/stories.html#basic-checks","# Check 2.1 The first record is PFH and the last record is PFT . 2.2 PFT recordCount = the number of detail records ( VID + MCD ). 2.3 The file date and version have not been loaded before. Any failure: do not load the file. Keep the rows already in the table. Alert Operations with the file name and the failed check. Why only basic checks. Planet guarantees its rates and pays any difference. The checks only make sure the whole file arrived and is not loaded twice."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Parse: what to keep and what to drop","mcp/docs/stories.html#parse-what-to-keep-and-what-to-drop","Records Record Code Action Physical file header PFH Take date and version onto every row. Do not store the record. Section header VILFH , MCLFH Take the card network ( VISA or MASTERCARD ) onto the rows of the section. Do not store the record. Detail record VID , MCD Store one row each (section 4). Section trailer VILFT , MCLFT Drop. Physical file trailer PFT Use for check 2.2. Do not store. Fields of a detail record (in file order) # Field Keep or drop Reason 1 rateId Keep Planet’s ID of the rate. Stored on each MCP transaction. 2 baseCurr Drop Currency symbol (e.g. $ ). Display only. 3 baseCurr2 Keep ISO numeric code of the base (order) currency. 4 baseMinorUnit Keep Decimals of the base currency. 5 targetCurr Drop Currency symbol. Display only. 6 targetCurr2 Keep ISO numeric code of the target (transaction) currency. 7 targetMinorUnit Keep Decimals of the target currency. 8 rate Keep The rate, markup included. 9 validFrom Keep Start of validity (UTC). 10 validTo Keep End of validity (UTC)."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Table mcp_rate","mcp/docs/stories.html#table-mcp_rate","One row per detail record. Example: the Geidea file of 24-Nov-2026, Visa row for GBP. Column Type Value Example rateFileDate Date PFH date 2026-11-24 rateFileVersion String PFH version 01 cardNetwork String VISA for VID rows, MASTERCARD for MCD rows VISA rateId String rateId 2001 baseCurrency String (3) ISO alpha code of baseCurr2 AED (from 784) baseMinorUnit Integer baseMinorUnit 2 targetCurrency String (3) ISO alpha code of targetCurr2 GBP (from 826) targetMinorUnit Integer targetMinorUnit 2 rate Decimal (7 decimals) rate , exactly as in the file. Meaning: 1 unit of baseCurrency = rate units of targetCurrency , markup included. 0.2137000 (1 AED = 0.2137 GBP) validFrom Timestamp (UTC) validFrom 2026-11-24 00:00:00 validTo Timestamp (UTC) validTo 2026-11-25 23:59:59 loadedAt Timestamp When PGW loaded the file 2026-11-24 04:10 UAE Store both sections. S4 quotes from VISA rows only. Keeping the Mastercard rows lets Geidea switch later by configuration. Do not delete the rows of earlier files. Each daily file overlaps the next (48-hour validity in Planet’s sample). Rows older than the retention period (configuration) can be removed."],["MCP","Story map › S2 — BE – Planet rate file: fetch, parse and store","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-1","Load Planet’s sample file: 3 rows. Row rateId 1024: VISA , USD → GBP, 0.7890123, valid 14-Feb 00:00 to 15-Feb 23:59:59 UTC. No currency symbols are stored. Change the PFT record count to 4: the file is not loaded (2.2). Operations is alerted. Earlier rows stay. Load the same file twice: the second load is rejected (2.3). When P3 confirms PGP: an encrypted file is decrypted with the private key and loaded."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S3 — BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store","mcp/docs/stories.html#s3-be-maxmind-database-fetch-parse-and-store","Simulator: no country found Simulator: customer in the UAE"],["MCP","Story map › S3 — BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-2","MCP proposes a currency from the customer’s country. PGW finds the country from the customer’s IP address with the MaxMind GeoIP2 database (approved 23-Nov-2026). MaxMind publishes the database as a zip file of CSV files. This story only brings it into PGW: fetch it, parse it and store it in a table. S4 looks up the IP address when HPP loads."],["MCP","Story map › S3 — BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal-2","The latest MaxMind data is in the PGW geolocation table."],["MCP","Story map › S3 — BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-2","Item Value Story ID S3 Source MaxMind download permalink from the MaxMind account portal, for example https://download.maxmind.com/geoip/databases/GeoIP2-Country-CSV/download?suffix=zip . Basic authentication with the MaxMind account ID and licence key. Table written mcp_geoip_network (name is a proposal) Depends on P2 Followed by S4 Out of scope IP lookup (S4). VPN and proxy detection (accepted risk)."],["MCP","Story map › S3 — BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-2",""],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Fetch","mcp/docs/stories.html#fetch-1","Each day, check the release date with a HEAD request on the permalink (MaxMind’s recommended method). Download only when the release is newer than the one loaded. Keep the account ID and licence key in PGW’s secrets store. Keep the downloaded zip for audit. The zip name carries the release date: GeoIP2-Country-CSV_YYYYMMDD.zip . Download or load failure: keep the current data. Alert Operations."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Files to use","mcp/docs/stories.html#files-to-use","File Use GeoIP2-Country-Blocks-IPv4.csv Load GeoIP2-Country-Blocks-IPv6.csv Load GeoIP2-Country-Locations-en.csv Use to find the country code of each network Other files (other languages, licence, copyright) Do not load If the subscription is the City edition, the file names start with GeoIP2-City . Use the same columns only."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Parse: what to keep and what to drop","mcp/docs/stories.html#parse-what-to-keep-and-what-to-drop-1","Blocks files (IPv4 and IPv6) Column Keep or drop Reason network Keep The IP network in CIDR format, e.g. 81.2.69.0/24 . geoname_id Keep (for the join) Location of the network. Leads to the country code. registered_country_geoname_id Keep (for the join) Country where the ISP registered the network. Used when geoname_id is empty. represented_country_geoname_id Drop Not used for MCP. is_anonymous_proxy , is_satellite_provider , is_anycast Drop Not used for MCP. City edition only: postal_code , latitude , longitude , accuracy_radius Drop Not used for MCP. Locations file Column Keep or drop Reason geoname_id Keep (for the join) Joins to the blocks files. country_iso_code Keep ISO 3166 alpha-2 country code. locale_code , continent_code , continent_name , country_name , is_in_european_union Drop Not used for MCP."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Table mcp_geoip_network","mcp/docs/stories.html#table-mcp_geoip_network","One row per network. The join columns are not stored. Column Type Value Example (illustrative) ipVersion Integer 4 or 6, from the file 4 networkStart IP address First address of network 81.2.69.0 networkEnd IP address Last address of network 81.2.69.255 countryIsoCode String (2) country_iso_code of geoname_id . If geoname_id is empty, of registered_country_geoname_id . Empty if neither has a country. GB databaseDate Date Release date from the zip name 2026-11-24 Load a new release completely before PGW uses it. Until then, S4 uses the previous release. Keep only the release in use and the one before it."],["MCP","Story map › S3 — BE – MaxMind database: fetch, parse and store","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-2","Load a release: one row per network in the two blocks files. No dropped column is stored. A network with an empty geoname_id gets the country of registered_country_geoname_id . The HEAD check finds no newer release: nothing is downloaded. A failed download keeps the current data and alerts Operations. While a new release loads, lookups still use the previous release."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","mcp/docs/stories.html#s4-febe-hpp-currency-offer-and-selection","Simulator: GBP offered Simulator: rounding up Simulator: Tabby End-to-end view"],["MCP","Story map › S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-3","The customer pays on Geidea HPP. HPP opens from Create Session ( POST /payment-intent/api/v2/direct/session ), from an SDK that shows Geidea HPP, or from Pay-by-Link. MCP is decided when HPP loads. Flows that never show HPP (Direct API with card details from the merchant, recurring and merchant-initiated payments) are never MCP. MCP is offered strictly on location. When PGW does not propose a currency, HPP is the same as today: AED only, no selector."],["MCP","Story map › S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal-3","When the customer’s location gives a currency the merchant offers, HPP shows the amount in that currency, the AED amount and the rate. The customer can switch between the offered currencies and AED. The quote stays fixed for the session."],["MCP","Story map › S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-3","Item Value Story ID S4 Reads isDynamicMcpEnabled , dynamicMcpCurrencies (S1), system lists (S1), mcp_rate (S2), mcp_geoip_network (S3) Session fields written mcpCountry , mcpProposedCurrency , mcpQuotes[] , isMcpOffered Depends on S1, S2, S3 Followed by S5"],["MCP","Story map › S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","Document","Flow","mcp/docs/stories.html#flow","The classic MCP path: a UK customer pays an AED 100.00 order in GBP. MCP payment on HPP"],["MCP","Story map › S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-3",""],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Decide the offer (when HPP first loads)","mcp/docs/stories.html#decide-the-offer-when-hpp-first-loads","PGW runs these checks in order. The first “no” stops the check: MCP is not offered and HPP shows AED only, with no selector. # Check Source 1.1 The order currency is in the MCP order currencies list ( AED ). S1 1.2 paymentOperation = Pay . Session 1.3 isDynamicMcpEnabled = true for the MID and instance. S1 1.4 MCP is not suspended (section 2). S2 1.5 The country lookup (section 2) returns a country. S3 1.6 The country maps to an active currency, other than the order currency. S1 1.7 The currency is in dynamicMcpCurrencies of the MID. S1 1.8 The rate lookup (section 2) returns a rate for order currency → that currency. S2 All “yes”: the mapped currency is the proposed currency . Set isMcpOffered = true . Why 1.6 excludes the order currency. A customer in the UAE maps to AED. Nothing is converted, so MCP is not offered and HPP shows no selector."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Lookups","mcp/docs/stories.html#lookups","Country lookup (table from S3) 1. Take the customer’s public IP address as received by HPP, after any Geidea load balancer. 2. Find the row of mcp_geoip_network with the same ipVersion where networkStart ≤ IP ≤ networkEnd . Result: countryIsoCode . 3. No row, an empty countryIsoCode or a private address (e.g. 10.0.0.1): no country. 4. Store the IP address and the country on the session, for support. Rate lookup (table from S2) 1. Take the rows of mcp_rate with cardNetwork = VISA , baseCurrency = order currency, targetCurrency = the currency, and validFrom ≤ now ≤ validTo . 2. Use the row of the latest file (highest rateFileDate , then rateFileVersion ). 3. No row: no rate. The currency is not offered. Example: file 20261123 has GBP valid to 24-Nov 23:59:59 UTC. File 20261124 has GBP valid to 25-Nov 23:59:59 UTC. At 24-Nov 10:00 UTC both are valid. PGW uses 20261124. MCP suspended 1. When mcp_rate has no VISA row valid now, MCP is suspended for all merchants. Every HPP shows AED only. 2. Alert Operations when MCP becomes suspended and when it resumes."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Quotes (fixed for the session)","mcp/docs/stories.html#quotes-fixed-for-the-session","Calculator: rounding up When MCP is offered, build one quote for each currency in dynamicMcpCurrencies that has a rate (section 2), at the time HPP first loads: Quote field Value currency Transaction currency amount Order amount × rate , rounded up to the currency’s decimals rate From the rate lookup (7 decimals) rateId , rateFileDate , rateFileVersion From the rate lookup Store the quotes on the session ( mcpQuotes[] ). A reload of the same session uses the stored quotes. A newer rate file does not change them. Never recalculate an amount from a newer rate during the session. Rounding up, examples (rate AED → GBP 0.2137000): Order amount (AED) × rate Quote (GBP) 100.00 21.3700000 21.37 99.99 21.3678630 21.37 0.01 0.0021370 0.01"],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Display","mcp/docs/stories.html#display","# Element Content 4.1 Total The quote amount and currency of the selected currency (first: the proposed currency), e.g. “GBP 21.37”. 4.2 Order amount “Order amount AED 100.00”. Shown only when the selected currency is not AED. 4.3 Rate “Exchange rate 1 AED = 0.2137 GBP”. The rate as in the file, trailing zeros removed. Shown only when the selected currency is not AED. 4.4 Currency selector Paying in : AED and every currency with a quote. Shown only when MCP is offered. 4.5 Wallet buttons Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay built with the selected currency and amount. Shown below 4.1 to 4.4. 4.6 Tabby, Tamara Selectable only when the selected currency is AED. Show “Available in AED only” under them when MCP is offered. 4.7 Pay button “Pay GBP 21.37” (selected currency and amount). Changing the selector updates 4.1 to 4.7 from the stored quotes. AED shows the order amount. When the customer selects a non-AED currency while Tabby or Tamara is selected, select card. No further disclosure text about the conversion."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","5. Backend checks when HPP submits the payment","mcp/docs/stories.html#backend-checks-when-hpp-submits-the-payment","# Check On failure 5.1 The submitted currency is AED or a currency in mcpQuotes[] . Reject. Message: “Currency not available. Reload the page.” 5.2 The submitted amount equals the stored quote (or the order amount for AED). Reject. Same message. 5.3 Tabby or Tamara only with AED. Reject. Message: “This payment method is available in AED only.” PGW engineering sets the error codes."],["MCP","Story map › S4 — FE/BE – HPP currency offer and selection","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-3","MID with MCP on, GBP offered. Customer IP in the UK, order AED 100.00: HPP shows GBP 21.37, AED 100.00, “1 AED = 0.2137 GBP” and the selector. isMcpOffered = true . Same customer selects AED: total AED 100.00, no order amount line, no rate line. Tabby is selectable. Customer in the UAE: AED only, no selector. isMcpOffered = false . Customer in India, INR not offered by the MID: AED only, no selector. Customer in Japan: AED only, no selector (Phase 2 country). No country found: AED only, no selector. MCP suspended (no valid rate file): AED only, no selector. Order in USD, or paymentOperation = PreAuthorize : no MCP. Order AED 99.99, customer in the UK: GBP 21.37. A new rate file loads while the page is open, then the customer reloads: the amount does not change. Two valid rate files: the quote uses the later file. No VISA rate valid now: MCP suspended, AED only, Operations alerted. A customer IP with no matching network: no country, AED only. A tampered submit with GBP 20.00: rejected (5.2). Pay-by-Link opened from the UK: same behaviour as 1."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S5 — BE – MCP payment and transaction record","mcp/docs/stories.html#s5-be-mcp-payment-and-transaction-record","Order model: after the purchase Simulator: Apple Pay Simulator: wallet mismatch End-to-end view"],["MCP","Story map › S5 — BE – MCP payment and transaction record","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-4","S4 gives a fixed quote. When the customer pays in a non-AED currency, PGW authorizes in that currency through MPGS or CyberSource, as for any non-AED payment. PGW must keep two views of the payment: the order view in AED, which the merchant and APEX use, and the transaction view in the paid currency, which the scheme uses."],["MCP","Story map › S5 — BE – MCP payment and transaction record","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal-4","PGW authorizes in the currency the customer chose, at the quoted amount. PGW stores both views and the rate on the transaction. Order totals stay in the order currency."],["MCP","Story map › S5 — BE – MCP payment and transaction record","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-4","Item Value Story ID S5 Geidea endpoints HPP pay flow; POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay , POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token Fields written Order: isMcpOffered . Transaction: isMcp , mcpBaseAmount , mcpBaseCurrency , mcpRate , mcpRateId , mcpRateFileDate , mcpRateFileVersion Depends on S4 Followed by S6, S7, S8, S9"],["MCP","Story map › S5 — BE – MCP payment and transaction record","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-4",""],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Authorization","mcp/docs/stories.html#authorization","Send the selected currency and the quote amount to MPGS or CyberSource, as for any non-AED payment. No new processor fields. MPGS and CyberSource are set up for the full ISO currency list. AED selected: process as today. A declined payment can be retried in the same session with the same quote."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Wallet check","mcp/docs/stories.html#wallet-check","For Apple Pay, Google Pay and Samsung Pay, compare the amount and currency in the wallet payment data with the quote of the selected currency. Different: reject the payment. Message: “Payment amount does not match. Try again.” Do not send it to the processor."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Fields","mcp/docs/stories.html#fields","Order | Field | Type | Value | |—|—|—| | amount , currency | Existing | Order amount and currency (AED 100.00). Not changed by MCP. | | isMcpOffered | Boolean | From S4. Internal. Not sent to the merchant. | Transaction (every transaction of the order: purchase and refund) | Field | Type | Value (purchase in the example) | |—|—|—| | amount , currency | Existing | Transaction view: 21.37 GBP | | isMcp | Boolean | true only when a conversion was applied to this transaction | | mcpBaseAmount | Decimal | Order view of this transaction: 100.00. For non-MCP transactions = amount . | | mcpBaseCurrency | String | AED . For non-MCP transactions = currency . | | mcpRate | Decimal (7) | 0.2137000. Empty when isMcp = false . | | mcpRateId , mcpRateFileDate , mcpRateFileVersion | String | From the quote. Empty when isMcp = false . | Why mcpBaseAmount is filled for every transaction. Order totals, the merchant outputs (S7) and the federation (S8) read one field for all transactions. Why the name is not originalAmount . PGW already uses originalAmount for the amount before fees."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Order totals","mcp/docs/stories.html#order-totals","Compute every order total (paid, refunded, remaining to refund) from mcpBaseAmount . Never add amount values of transactions in different currencies. Example: purchase mcpBaseAmount 100.00, refund mcpBaseAmount 60.00 → refunded AED 60.00, remaining AED 40.00."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","5. Confirmation","mcp/docs/stories.html#confirmation","The HPP confirmation shows the paid amount and the AED order amount: “GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00)”. Pay-by-Link: PGW sends the customer an email receipt when the customer has an email address. For an MCP payment it shows the same: “GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00)”. AED payments: as today."],["MCP","Story map › S5 — BE – MCP payment and transaction record","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-4","Pay GBP 21.37 on an AED 100.00 order by card: processor receives GBP 21.37. Transaction: amount 21.37, currency GBP, isMcp true, mcpBaseAmount 100.00, mcpBaseCurrency AED, mcpRate 0.2137000. Pay in AED on an MCP-offered session: isMcp false, mcpBaseAmount 100.00 AED. isMcpOffered true on the order. Apple Pay with GBP 21.37 in the token: approved. Token with GBP 21.36: rejected before the processor. Decline, then retry in the same session: same quote and rate. Order inquiry totals are in AED. Confirmation shows “GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00)”. Pay-by-Link paid in GBP by a customer with an email address: the email receipt shows “GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00)”."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S6 — BE – MCP refunds","mcp/docs/stories.html#s6-be-mcp-refunds","Order model: refunds 60 + 40 Calculator: refunds AED 60 + 40 End-to-end: refunds AED 60 + 40"],["MCP","Story map › S6 — BE – MCP refunds","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-5","The merchant refunds in AED with the existing refund API (refund v2, signed with timestamp + merchantPublicKey + refundAmount + orderId ). For an MCP transaction the customer paid in another currency, so the refund must go to the processor in that currency. PGW converts it at the rate stored on the original transaction. Converting each partial refund on its own can leave a cent unrefunded. The last-cent rule closes the gap at PGW."],["MCP","Story map › S6 — BE – MCP refunds","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal-5","A refund of an MCP transaction is sent in the transaction currency at the stored rate. The refunds of a transaction never total more or less than the amount paid, in both currencies."],["MCP","Story map › S6 — BE – MCP refunds","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-5","Item Value Story ID S6 Geidea endpoint Existing refund v2. refundAmount in the order currency. No new request fields. Fields written Refund transaction: amount , currency , isMcp , mcpBaseAmount , mcpBaseCurrency , mcpRate , mcpRateId , mcpRateFileDate , mcpRateFileVersion Depends on S5 Out of scope Manual refunds by FinOps through Postilion. Void (as today)."],["MCP","Story map › S6 — BE – MCP refunds","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-5",""],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Checks","mcp/docs/stories.html#checks","Calculator: refund too much # Check Message 1.1 Existing refund checks, on AED: refundAmount ≤ AED paid − AED already refunded (from mcpBaseAmount ). Existing message 1.2 Existing minimum refund amount (0.10). Existing message Why no MCP-specific minimum. The existing minimum of AED 0.10 always converts to at least one minor unit in every MVP currency, because each of them has a rate of 0.1 or more per AED. Example: AED 0.10 × 0.2137000 = GBP 0.021370 → GBP 0.02."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Conversion (original purchase isMcp = true)","mcp/docs/stories.html#conversion-original-purchase-ismcp-true","Calculator: why round down Final refund: AED already refunded + refundAmount = AED paid. Refund amount in transaction currency = purchase amount − all earlier refunds’ amount . Other refunds: refundAmount × purchase mcpRate , rounded down to the currency’s decimals. Use the purchase mcpRate . Never look up a new rate. Send the refund to the processor in the transaction currency. Store on the refund transaction: amount and currency = converted amount and transaction currency; mcpBaseAmount = refundAmount ; mcpBaseCurrency = AED; isMcp = true ; rate fields copied from the purchase. Original purchase isMcp = false : refund as today. Why round down. Each earlier refund then stays at or below its exact value, so the final refund is never negative."],["MCP","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Examples (purchase AED 100.00 = GBP 21.37, rate 0.2137000)","mcp/docs/stories.html#examples-purchase-aed-100.00-gbp-21.37-rate-0.2137000","Calculator: 60 + 40 Calculator: thirds Refund (AED) Calculation Refund (GBP) 60.00 60.00 × 0.2137000 = 12.822 → round down 12.82 40.00 (final) 21.37 − 12.82 8.55 Total 100.00 21.37 Refund (AED) Calculation Refund (GBP) 33.33 7.122621 → round down 7.12 33.33 7.122621 → round down 7.12 33.34 (final) 21.37 − 7.12 − 7.12 7.13 Total 100.00 21.37"],["MCP","Story map › S6 — BE – MCP refunds","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-5","Refund AED 60.00 then AED 40.00 on the example: processor receives GBP 12.82 then GBP 8.55. Order refunded total AED 100.00. Refund AED 33.33, 33.33, 33.34: GBP 7.12, 7.12, 7.13. Full refund AED 100.00 in one call: GBP 21.37. A new rate file has loaded since the purchase: refund still uses 0.2137000. Refund AED 100.01: rejected (1.1)."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S7 — BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED","mcp/docs/stories.html#s7-be-merchant-facing-outputs-in-aed","Order model: merchant sees AED Simulator: after Pay End-to-end view"],["MCP","Story map › S7 — BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-6","The merchant must never see the conversion. Existing merchant integrations read transaction.amount and transaction.currency . These fields must stay in the payloads and carry the AED values for MCP transactions, so no merchant integration breaks."],["MCP","Story map › S7 — BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED","Document","Goal","mcp/docs/stories.html#goal-6","Every merchant-facing output shows an MCP transaction in AED, with no rate and no MCP flag."],["MCP","Story map › S7 — BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-6","Item Value Story ID S7 Outputs Callback to callbackUrl ; order inquiry GET /pgw/api/v1/direct/order/{orderId} and GET /pgw/api/v1/direct/order?MerchantReferenceId= ; pay responses ( /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay , /pay/token ); refund response; Pay-by-Link ( …/v1/direct/eInvoice ) inquiry Depends on S5 Out of scope Merchant Portal (shows APEX data, no change). Setting to send the conversion fields (none)."],["MCP","Story map › S7 — BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED","Document","Requirements","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-6","For a transaction with isMcp = true , fill amount with mcpBaseAmount and currency with mcpBaseCurrency in every output listed above. This applies to purchases and refunds. Order totals in these outputs come from mcpBaseAmount (S5 section 4). Do not return isMcp , isMcpOffered , mcpRate , mcpRateId , mcpRateFileDate , mcpRateFileVersion , mcpBaseAmount or mcpBaseCurrency . Transactions with isMcp = false : no change. Example (purchase of the example, then refund AED 60.00): \"transactions\": [ { \"type\": \"Pay\", \"amount\": 100.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"status\": \"Success\" }, { \"type\": \"Refund\", \"amount\": 60.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"status\": \"Success\" } ] Field names other than amount and currency are as in today’s payload."],["MCP","Story map › S7 — BE – Merchant-facing outputs in AED","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-6","MCP purchase GBP 21.37 / AED 100.00: callback and order inquiry show 100.00 AED. Refund AED 60.00 (GBP 12.82 to the processor): refund response and inquiry show 60.00 AED. No output contains GBP, the rate or an MCP field. A non-MCP USD order ( ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY ): unchanged."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S8 — BE – Federation to APEX (parked: waiting on P4)","mcp/docs/stories.html#s8-be-federation-to-apex-parked-waiting-on-p4","End-to-end: federation (parked)"],["MCP","Story map › S8 — BE – Federation to APEX (parked: waiting on P4)","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-7","PGW federates each transaction to APEX in real time (createTransaction). APEX agreed (23-Nov-2026) to hold the order view in its main fields and the transaction view in four new cardholder fields. The exact field names and formats are awaited."],["MCP","Story map › S8 — BE – Federation to APEX (parked: waiting on P4)","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-7","Item Value Story ID S8 Depends on S5, P4"],["MCP","Story map › S8 — BE – Federation to APEX (parked: waiting on P4)","Document","Requirements (to complete when P4 is received)","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-to-complete-when-p4-is-received","APEX field PGW source (MCP transaction) amount mcpBaseAmount transactionCurrency mcpBaseCurrency settlementAmount , settlementCurrency , exchangeRate No change Cardholder Amount (name from APEX) amount Cardholder Currency (name from APEX) currency Transaction Identifier (name from APEX) Y when isMcp = true Cardholder ConvRate (name from APEX) mcpRate Purchases and refunds carry the same fields. A refund sends its own converted amount and the purchase rate. Non-MCP transactions: as today. APEX produces the DTF to Planet from these records, as for DCC. No PGW file to Planet."],["MCP","Story map › S8 — BE – Federation to APEX (parked: waiting on P4)","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-7","To be completed when P4 is received."],["MCP","Story map","Document","S9 — BE – Enrichment file to Postilion (parked: waiting on P5)","mcp/docs/stories.html#s9-be-enrichment-file-to-postilion-parked-waiting-on-p5",""],["MCP","Story map › S9 — BE – Enrichment file to Postilion (parked: waiting on P5)","Document","Background","mcp/docs/stories.html#background-8","Postilion builds the PTLF to APEX from the scheme file, which is in the transaction currency. PGW’s enrichment file gives Postilion the AED view of each MCP leg, so the PTLF matches the PGW federation. The format and data set are being agreed with Postilion."],["MCP","Story map › S9 — BE – Enrichment file to Postilion (parked: waiting on P5)","Document","Story summary","mcp/docs/stories.html#story-summary-8","Item Value Story ID S9 Depends on S5, P5"],["MCP","Story map › S9 — BE – Enrichment file to Postilion (parked: waiting on P5)","Document","Requirements (to complete when P5 is received)","mcp/docs/stories.html#requirements-to-complete-when-p5-is-received","One record for each MCP leg (purchase, refund, partial refund) in the existing daily enrichment file. Data to supply: matching key (PGW order ID, RRN, authorization code), order view ( mcpBaseAmount , mcpBaseCurrency ), transaction view ( amount , currency ), mcpRate , MCP flag. Decimals stated for each amount."],["MCP","Story map › S9 — BE – Enrichment file to Postilion (parked: waiting on P5)","Document","Acceptance criteria","mcp/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-8","To be completed when P5 is received."],["MCP","Story map","Document","Open points","mcp/docs/stories.html#open-points","# Point Story Owner / status 1 Final name and position of the MCP toggle and section. S1 PGW engineering 2 Daily deadline for the rate file before Operations is alerted, and row retention period. S2 Rabbani with Operations 3 Planet: SFTP delivery details, rate validity and currency coverage (P3). S2 Planet – asked 13-Nov 4 MaxMind database refresh frequency under the subscription. S3 PGW engineering"],["MCP","End-to-end view","Visual page","End-to-end view","mcp/end-to-end.html","visual · mcp End-to-end view One AED order from the daily rate file to the merchant's payout and refunds, across the customer, the merchant, PGW, Planet, the processor and scheme, Postilion and APEX. Change the settings and the flow redraws. Illustrative Reflects solution requirements v3.0 and PGW stories v0.1 (25-Nov-2026) Read the requirement → Stories → HPP simulator → Terms: order currency = AED, what the merchant priced in; transaction currency = what the customer pays in (APEX: cardholder currency). Rates are dummy values. Field names are the stories' proposals. Steps marked parked wait on APEX field names (S8) and the Postilion enrichment format (S9). Customer location UK (GBP offered) UAE / not found / not offered Rate file Valid No valid file Customer pays in GBP (proposed) Switches to AED Method Card Apple Pay Tabby Refund None AED 60 + AED 40 Reset"],["MCP","End-to-end view","Visual page","From the rate file to payout","mcp/end-to-end.html#from-the-rate-file-to-payout","Order AED 100.00 · rate AED → GBP 0.2137000 (dummy) · merchant settled in SAR The customer in the UK pays GBP 21.37 for an AED 100.00 order. The merchant sees AED throughout and is paid out in SAR. Action by the customer or merchant Processing Money moves or is converted Parked or open Failed MCP not applied Daily rates HPP offer Payment Federation and clearing Settlement Customer Merchant PGW / HPP Planet MPGS / CYBS · scheme Postilion APEX 01 Publishes the rate file Template 194, markup included 02 Checks and caches it S2 03 Creates the order AED 100.00 04 Opens the payment page From the site, SDK or Pay-by-Link 05 IP → GB → GBP MaxMind database in PGW · S3 06 Offers GBP 21.37 Rounded up · quote fixed · S4 07 Sees GBP 21.37 and AED 100.00 1 AED = 0.2137 GBP · selector 08 Pays GBP 21.37 by card 09 Checks amount = quote S4, S5 10 Authorizes GBP 21.37 Approved 11 Stores both views Order view, paid view, rate · S5 12 Confirmation GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00) 13 Callback: AED 100.00 No rate, no flag · S7 14 Federation: AED + cardholder fields Parked: field names from APEX · S8 15 Enrichment record Parked: file format · S9 16 Clears GBP 21.37 Scheme file in the paid currency 17 PTLF to APEX Same convention as the federation (parked) 18 DTF with the MCP row From APEX · Product Indicator 2 19 Pays the merchant From AED 100.00, in SAR Scroll sideways to see the whole flow → 01 Planet Publishes the rate file Template 194, markup included 02 PGW / HPP Checks and caches it S2 03 Merchant Creates the order AED 100.00 04 Customer Opens the payment page From the site, SDK or Pay-by-Link 05 PGW / HPP IP → GB → GBP MaxMind database in PGW · S3 06 PGW / HPP Offers GBP 21.37 Rounded up · quote fixed · S4 07 Customer Sees GBP 21.37 and AED 100.00 1 AED = 0.2137 GBP · selector 08 Customer Pays GBP 21.37 by card 09 PGW / HPP Checks amount = quote S4, S5 10 MPGS / CYBS · scheme Authorizes GBP 21.37 Approved 11 PGW / HPP Stores both views Order view, paid view, rate · S5 12 Customer Confirmation GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00) 13 Merchant Callback: AED 100.00 No rate, no flag · S7 14 APEX Federation: AED + cardholder fields Parked: field names from APEX · S8 15 Postilion Enrichment record Parked: file format · S9 16 Postilion Clears GBP 21.37 Scheme file in the paid currency 17 APEX PTLF to APEX Same convention as the federation (parked) 18 Planet DTF with the MCP row From APEX · Product Indicator 2 19 APEX Pays the merchant From AED 100.00, in SAR ← Previous Next step → Step 1 of 19"],["MCP","End-to-end view › From the rate file to payout","Visual page","1. Publishes the rate file","mcp/end-to-end.html#aDetail","Planet → PGW · SFTP, PGP Planet publishes one rate file each day over SFTP. Each rate is from AED to one currency, with the markup already in it, and is valid for 48 hours in Planet's sample, so two daily files overlap. Visa section (VID) and Mastercard section (MCD) Delivery details (folder, keys, file name, time) are still with Planet Simulator: the rate file Dummy row in Planet's layout { \"file\" : \"PFH,20261124,01\", \"visaRow\" : \"VID,2001,AED,784,2,GBP,826,2,0.2137000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z\" }"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","mcp/hpp-simulator.html","mcp · visual page HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees Set up the merchant, place the customer somewhere in the world and choose the rate-file state. The HPP on the right reacts as PGW would. Pay to see what PGW stores and what the merchant sees. Illustrative Reflects solution requirements v3.0 and PGW stories v0.1 (25-Nov-2026) Read the requirement → Stories: S4 → S5 → End-to-end view Back to MCP Terms. Order currency : what the merchant priced in (AED). Transaction currency : what the customer pays in, and what the scheme clears in (APEX calls it cardholder currency ). Settlement currency : what the merchant is paid out in; MCP does not change it. Rate : Planet's rate from AED to the transaction currency, markup included. Decided after PRD v2 (09-Nov) and now in solution requirements v3.0 Geolocation is MaxMind GeoIP2 (a database held by PGW), not Planet. Planet offers no geolocation (13-Nov). Rates come from Planet's daily rate file (template 194). PGW quotes from the Visa section , the higher of the two rates (24-Nov). The converted amount is rounded up to the currency's minor unit (24-Nov). Refunds keep the PRD rule: round down, with the last-cent rule on the final refund. MCP is offered strictly on location. No proposed currency (UAE, no country, currency not offered, Phase 2) means AED only and no currency selector (25-Nov). The confirmation and receipt show the amount paid and the AED order amount (25-Nov). The MCP toggle is in PGW Configuration, built by the PGW team. It is switched on after Operations registers the merchant with Planet for MCP (24-Nov). The rate file gets basic checks only (header, trailer, record count, not loaded twice). The existing minimum refund amount covers MCP (25-Nov). Try UK customer pays in GBP Switches back to AED Germany: EUR US: USD Customer in the UAE No country found INR off for merchant Japan (Phase 2) MCP off Order in USD Direct API Pay-by-Link Yesterday's rates No valid rate file Wants Tabby Rounding up Apple Pay in GBP Wallet amount mismatch 1"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","Merchant set-up","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#merchant-set-up","PGW Configuration General Payment Methods Gateway Settings Multi-currency pricing Webhooks Dynamic multi-currency pricing Offer the customer a price in their own currency on HPP, based on their location. The merchant keeps pricing, reporting and settlement in the order currency. Enabled Currencies offered From the MCP currency list (APEX-supported and present in Planet's rate file). Currency Decimals Offered GBP 2 EUR 2 INR 2 USD 2 SAR 2 JPY Phase 2 0 No markup is set here. Markup is held at Planet and is already in the rates. Geidea screen shown in the Geidea Design System. Toggle name is a placeholder until PGW names it (must differ from ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY and ENABLE_MULTI_CURRENCY_SETTLEMENT ). 2"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","The order","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#the-order","merchant → PGW How the order is paid Create Session (API), customer pays on HPP SDK that shows Geidea HPP Pay-by-Link Direct API (card details collected by the merchant) Recurring or merchant-initiated payment Order currency AED USD Order amount MVP commitment is AED orders only. The solution is provisioned for any order currency (17-Nov). 3"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","Where the customer is","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#where-the-customer-is","customer IP → MaxMind → country United Kingdom GBP France EUR Germany EUR India INR United States USD Saudi Arabia SAR United Arab Emirates AED Japan JPY No country found Customer IP 81.2.69.142 dummy MaxMind result GB Mapped currency GBP PGW looks up the IP in the MaxMind GeoIP2 database it holds (no call out during the session), then maps the country to a currency using its own country-to-currency table. 4"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","Planet rate file","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#planet-rate-file","Planet → PGW, daily over SFTP Today's file loaded Today's file missing, yesterday's still valid No valid file File 20261124 v01 fetched, checked (header, trailer, record count) and stored in the PGW rate table. Every HPP quote today comes from this table. Test Merchant"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees › Planet rate file","Visual page","Choose how to pay","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#hpp","Total GBP 21.37 Order amount AED 100.00 Exchange rate 1 AED = 0.2137 GBP Paying in AED GBP EUR INR USD SAR Apple Pay | GBP 21.37 Google Pay | GBP 21.37 or pay with Credit or debit card Visa, Mastercard Card number · MM/YY · CVV Tabby Available in AED only Tamara Available in AED only Pay GBP 21.37 The amount shown stays fixed for this payment session. HPP as the customer sees it · Geidea Design System · Illustrative Test: the wallet token comes back with a different amount 5"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","Why the HPP looks like this","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#why-the-hpp-looks-like-this","PGW checks, in order 1 Order is paid on Geidea HPP Create Session, then HPP Yes 2 Order currency is AED Yes 3 MCP is on for this merchant Yes 4 A valid rate file is cached File 20261124 v01, valid to 25-Nov 23:59 UTC Yes 5 MaxMind returns a country for the customer IP 81.2.69.142 → GB Yes 6 Country maps to an active MCP currency GB → GBP Yes 7 Currency is on for this merchant Yes 8 Rate AED → currency is in the file (Visa section) 1 AED = 0.2137000 GBP Yes HPP proposes GBP . MCP_offered = Y. The customer can switch to any offered currency or back to AED. 6"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","The rate PGW used","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#the-rate-pgw-used","cached file · Visa section PFH,20261124,01 VILFH,HPLANET VISA,20261124,01 VID,2001,AED,784,2,GBP,826,2,0.2137000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z VID,2002,AED,784,2,EUR,978,2,0.2489000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z VID,2003,AED,784,2,INR,356,2,23.1200000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z VID,2004,AED,784,2,USD,840,2,0.2759000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z VID,2005,AED,784,2,SAR,682,2,1.0345000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z VID,2006,AED,784,2,JPY,392,0,41.8500000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z VILFT,TPLANET VISA,4074,66.7430000,6 MCLFH,HPLANET MASTERCARD,20261124,01 MCD,3001,AED,784,2,GBP,826,2,0.2135000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z MCD,3002,AED,784,2,EUR,978,2,0.2486000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z MCD,3003,AED,784,2,INR,356,2,23.0950000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z MCD,3004,AED,784,2,USD,840,2,0.2756000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z MCD,3005,AED,784,2,SAR,682,2,1.0334000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z MCD,3006,AED,784,2,JPY,392,0,41.8000000,2026-11-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-11-25T23:59:59.000Z MCLFT,TPLANET MASTERCARD,4074,66.6661000,6 PFT,TXN RATES,8148,133.4091000,12 Layout: Planet template 194 (Visa section VILFH/VID/VILFT, Mastercard section MCLFH/MCD/MCLFT). Rates are dummy, AED-based, markup included. Planet's sample writes the currency symbol in baseCurr/targetCurr; codes are used here for readability. Mastercard rows are greyed: PGW quotes from the Visa section. AED 100.00 × 0.2137000 = 21.3700000 GBP round up to 2 decimals → GBP 21.37 Try an order amount like 99.99 to see the rounding at work. 7"],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Visual page","After Pay","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#after-pay","what PGW stores · what the merchant sees Press Pay on the HPP to see the result. Sources: MCP solution requirements v3.0 section 8.1 and PGW stories S1 to S5, S7 (25-Nov-2026, drafts); Planet rate file template 194 and sample (Project_Feed/MCP/Planet Files); MCP project note, decisions 13-Nov to 24-Nov-2026. All rates, IP addresses and file values on this page are dummy values."],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: UK customer pays in GBP","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=gb-card",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Switches back to AED","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=gb-aed",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Germany: EUR","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=eu",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: US: USD","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=us",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Customer in the UAE","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=uae",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: No country found","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=geo-miss",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: INR off for merchant","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=cur-off",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Japan (Phase 2)","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=jp",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: MCP off","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=mcp-off",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Order in USD","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=order-usd",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Direct API","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=direct-api",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Pay-by-Link","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=pbl",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Yesterday's rates","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=rate-yday",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: No valid rate file","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=rate-none",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Wants Tabby","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=apm",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Rounding up","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=rounding",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Apple Pay in GBP","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=wallet",""],["MCP","HPP simulator: location, rates and what the customer sees","Scenario","Try it: Wallet amount mismatch","mcp/hpp-simulator.html#try=wallet-mismatch",""],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing","Hub","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing","mcp/index.html","feature MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing A customer on Geidea HPP is offered the price in their own currency, based on where they are, and can pay in it. The merchant prices, sees and is settled in AED as today, and never sees the conversion. Rates come from Planet's daily file, markup included. Launch target Jan-2027."],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing","Hub","One order, three currencies: the main change","mcp/index.html#one-order-three-currencies-the-main-change","The change to discuss first. Inside PGW, the existing transaction amount and currency now hold what the customer paid (GBP 21.37). Everything the merchant sees, and APEX's main fields, stay in AED. Two new fields, mcpBaseAmount and mcpBaseCurrency , carry the AED value on every purchase and refund, so no system downstream converts anything. order currency"],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing › One order, three currencies: the main change","Hub","What the merchant priced","mcp/index.html#what-the-merchant-priced","AED 100.00 Where Parameter Create Session amount currency existing PGW order amount currency existing PGW transaction mcpBaseAmount mcpBaseCurrency new Callback, order inquiry transaction.amount transaction.currency existing · AED for MCP APEX amount transactionCurrency existing · AED for MCP Seen by the merchant, the Merchant Portal and APEX main fields. MVP: AED orders only. transaction currency"],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing › One order, three currencies: the main change","Hub","What the customer pays","mcp/index.html#what-the-customer-pays","GBP 21.37 Where Parameter PGW transaction amount currency existing · paid currency PGW transaction isMcp mcpRate mcpRateId new PGW order isMcpOffered new · internal MPGS / CYBS amount, currency existing APEX Cardholder Amount, Cardholder Currency, Cardholder ConvRate, Transaction Identifier new · names pending Chosen on HPP. Authorized and cleared with the scheme in this currency. Never shown to the merchant. settlement currency"],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing › One order, three currencies: the main change","Hub","What the merchant is paid","mcp/index.html#what-the-merchant-is-paid","SAR Where Parameter APEX settlementAmount settlementCurrency existing APEX exchangeRate existing · order → settlement Unchanged by MCP. The AED order amount is converted to it for payout, as today. exchangeRate is not the MCP rate. Worked example: rate AED → GBP 0.2137, markup included. New PGW field names are the stories' proposals, except mcpBaseAmount and mcpBaseCurrency (agreed). Story S5 · Requirement"],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing","Hub","Documents","mcp/index.html#documents","Solution requirements What each Geidea system and team delivers for MCP v3.0 · 25-Nov-2026 PGW dev stories S1 to S7 written; S8 (APEX federation) and S9 (enrichment file) parked v0.1 · 25-Nov-2026 Interface notes APEX fields, enrichment record, Planet rate file and DTF in preparation"],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing","Hub","Visual pages","mcp/index.html#visual-pages","End-to-end view One AED order from Planet's rate file to payout and refunds, in one swimlane; switch location, rate file, currency, method and refunds and the flow redraws open HPP simulator Merchant set-up, customer location and rate-file state drive what the HPP offers; pay and see what PGW stores and what the merchant sees open Calculator Quote, rounding up, partial refunds with the last-cent rule; compare with rounding to nearest open Order model One AED 100.00 order step by step: HPP offer, purchase in GBP, EUR or INR (or AED), refunds that close it; order and transaction fields, AED totals, merchant and processor views open Rate file lifecycle Daily load, checks, validity overlap, MCP suspended planned What can go wrong Geolocation miss, missing rate file, wallet mismatch, record mismatches planned Federation and enrichment The same purchase and refunds as sent to APEX in real time and to Postilion in the enrichment file parked: APEX field names, enrichment format"],["MCP","MCP: dynamic multi-currency pricing","Hub","Stories and where to see them","mcp/index.html#stories","S1 · Configuration Simulator 1 S2 · Planet rate file: fetch and store Simulator 4 · End-to-end S3 · MaxMind database: fetch and store Simulator 3 S4 · HPP offer and selection Simulator · Calculator · End-to-end S5 · Payment and record Order model · Simulator 7 · End-to-end S6 · Refunds Order model · Calculator · End-to-end S7 · Merchant outputs in AED Order model · Simulator 7 · End-to-end S8 · Federation to APEX (parked) End-to-end S9 · Enrichment file (parked) End-to-end"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","mcp/order-model.html","mcp · visual page Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund Follow one AED 100.00 order through PGW. Step through the HPP offer, the purchase in the customer's currency and the refunds that close the order. See which fields PGW writes at each step, how order totals stay in AED, and what the merchant and the processor see. Illustrative Reflects PGW stories v0.1 (25-Nov-2026) Stories: S5 → S6 → S7 → Calculator HPP simulator End-to-end view Back to MCP Terms. Order currency : what the merchant priced in (AED). Transaction currency : what the customer pays in and what the scheme clears in. Order view of a transaction: mcpBaseAmount + mcpBaseCurrency (AED). Transaction view : amount + currency (paid currency). Rates are dummy (Visa section of a file dated 20261124, version 01). New field names are the stories' proposals, except mcpBaseAmount and mcpBaseCurrency (agreed). PGW engineering can rename them. Try HPP offer After the purchase GBP, refunds 60 + 40 GBP, refunds in thirds GBP, one full refund EUR, 60 + 40 INR, 60 + 40 Customer picks AED 1"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","The order","mcp/order-model.html#the-order","merchant offers GBP, EUR, INR Customer pays in GBP EUR INR AED (UK customer picks AED) Customer location gives GBP, which the merchant offers. HPP proposes it and the customer pays in it. Merchant refunds (AED) 60 + 40 33.33 × 2 + 33.34 100 in one none Order: AED 100.00 from Create Session. MID with MCP on ( S1 ). 2"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","Steps","mcp/order-model.html#steps-2","Order created Create Session · AED 100.00 HPP opens Customer in United Kingdom · GBP proposed Purchase GBP 21.37 (AED 100.00) Refund 1 AED 60.00 → GBP 12.82 Refund 2 (final) AED 40.00 → GBP 8.55 ← Back Next step → 3"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","What happened","mcp/order-model.html#what-happened","S6"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund › What happened","Visual page","The merchant refunds AED 40.00 (final refund)","mcp/order-model.html#nowOut","This refund brings the AED refunded to AED 100.00, so it is the final refund. PGW does not multiply by the rate. It sends what is left of the amount paid, so the refunds add up exactly to GBP 21.37. Final refund (AED already refunded 60.00 + 40.00 = AED 100.00): GBP 21.37 − 12.82 = GBP 8.55 View the order model as JSON Table JSON for developers, table for everyone else. Same data. 4"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","PGW order model","mcp/order-model.html#pgw-order-model","S4 · S5 · S6 // HPP session (S4): fixed when HPP first loads { \"mcpCountry\" : \"GB\" , \"mcpProposedCurrency\" : \"GBP\" , \"isMcpOffered\" : true , \"mcpQuotes\" : [ { \"currency\" : \"GBP\" , \"amount\" : 21.37 , \"rate\" : 0.2137000 , \"rateId\" : \"2001\" , \"rateFileDate\" : \"2026-11-24\" , \"rateFileVersion\" : \"01\" }, { \"currency\" : \"EUR\" , \"amount\" : 24.89 , \"rate\" : 0.2489000 , \"rateId\" : \"2002\" , \"rateFileDate\" : \"2026-11-24\" , \"rateFileVersion\" : \"01\" }, { \"currency\" : \"INR\" , \"amount\" : 2312.00 , \"rate\" : 23.1200000 , \"rateId\" : \"2003\" , \"rateFileDate\" : \"2026-11-24\" , \"rateFileVersion\" : \"01\" } ] } // PGW order { \"orderId\" : \"…\" , \"amount\" : 100.00 , \"currency\" : \"AED\" , \"isMcpOffered\" : true , // other order fields as today \"transactions\" : [ { // purchase \"type\" : \"Pay\" , \"status\" : \"Success\" , \"amount\" : 21.37 , \"currency\" : \"GBP\" , \"isMcp\" : true , \"mcpBaseAmount\" : 100.00 , \"mcpBaseCurrency\" : \"AED\" , \"mcpRate\" : 0.2137000 , \"mcpRateId\" : \"2001\" , \"mcpRateFileDate\" : \"2026-11-24\" , \"mcpRateFileVersion\" : \"01\" // other transaction fields as today }, { // refund 1 \"type\" : \"Refund\" , \"status\" : \"Success\" , \"amount\" : 12.82 , \"currency\" : \"GBP\" , \"isMcp\" : true , \"mcpBaseAmount\" : 60.00 , \"mcpBaseCurrency\" : \"AED\" , \"mcpRate\" : 0.2137000 , \"mcpRateId\" : \"2001\" , \"mcpRateFileDate\" : \"2026-11-24\" , \"mcpRateFileVersion\" : \"01\" // other transaction fields as today }, { // refund 2, final \"type\" : \"Refund\" , \"status\" : \"Success\" , \"amount\" : 8.55 , \"currency\" : \"GBP\" , \"isMcp\" : true , \"mcpBaseAmount\" : 40.00 , \"mcpBaseCurrency\" : \"AED\" , \"mcpRate\" : 0.2137000 , \"mcpRateId\" : \"2001\" , \"mcpRateFileDate\" : \"2026-11-24\" , \"mcpRateFileVersion\" : \"01\" // other transaction fields as today } ] } Order totals, computed from mcpBaseAmount : paid AED 100.00 · refunded AED 100.00 · remaining AED 0.00 Why not amount ? The refunds' amount values are GBP: 12.82 + 8.55 = 21.37. Read as AED, the order would show AED 21.37 refunded and allow AED 78.63 more. The purchase amount (21.37) is GBP too. \"isMcp\" new MCP field \"amount\" existing field, holds the paid currency for an MCP transaction + written at this step Structure and MCP fields only. Other fields are as today and not shown. New names are the stories' proposals, except mcpBaseAmount and mcpBaseCurrency (agreed). 4"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","PGW order and HPP session","mcp/order-model.html#pgw-order-and-hpp-session","S4 · S5 section 3 Field Value Note Order amount existing 100.00 Order amount. Not changed by MCP. currency existing AED Order currency. isMcpOffered new · internal true Set when HPP loads. Never sent to the merchant. HPP session (S4) mcpCountry new GB From the IP lookup. Kept for support. mcpProposedCurrency new GBP First currency shown on HPP. mcpQuotes[] new GBP 21.37 @ 0.2137000 (rateId 2001) EUR 24.89 @ 0.2489000 (rateId 2002) INR 2,312.00 @ 23.1200000 (rateId 2003) One per offered currency. Fixed for the session. Each also holds rateFileDate 2026-11-24, rateFileVersion 01."],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund › PGW order and HPP session","Visual page","Order totals existing · computed from mcpBaseAmount","mcp/order-model.html#order-totals-existing-computed-from-mcpbaseamount","Paid AED 100.00 purchase mcpBaseAmount Refunded AED 100.00 60.00 + 40.00 Remaining to refund AED 0.00 order fully refunded S5 section 4: compute every order total from mcpBaseAmount . Never add amount values of transactions in different currencies. Why not amount ? The refunds' amount values are GBP: 12.82 + 8.55 = 21.37. Read as AED, the order would show AED 21.37 refunded and allow AED 78.63 more. The purchase amount (21.37) is GBP too. 5"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","Transactions of the order","mcp/order-model.html#transactions-of-the-order","S5 · S6 Field Purchase type Pay Refund 1 type Refund Refund 2 · final type Refund Transaction view (what the processor and scheme use) amount existing · paid currency 21.37 12.82 8.55 currency existing · paid currency GBP GBP GBP Order view (what totals, merchant outputs and APEX read) mcpBaseAmount new · agreed name 100.00 60.00 40.00 mcpBaseCurrency new · agreed name AED AED AED Conversion isMcp new true true true mcpRate new 0.2137000 0.2137000 0.2137000 mcpRateId new 2001 2001 2001 mcpRateFileDate new 2026-11-24 2026-11-24 2026-11-24 mcpRateFileVersion new 01 01 01 written at this step existing as today existing · paid currency same field, holds the transaction view for MCP new MCP field 5"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","Merchant sees","mcp/order-model.html#merchant-sees","S7 Callback and order inquiry, transactions : \"transactions\": [ { \"type\" : \"Pay\" , \"amount\" : 100.00 , \"currency\" : \"AED\" , \"status\" : \"Success\" }, { \"type\" : \"Refund\" , \"amount\" : 60.00 , \"currency\" : \"AED\" , \"status\" : \"Success\" }, { \"type\" : \"Refund\" , \"amount\" : 40.00 , \"currency\" : \"AED\" , \"status\" : \"Success\" } ] For isMcp = true, PGW fills amount with mcpBaseAmount and currency with mcpBaseCurrency . No rate, no MCP field, no transaction currency. Other field names as in today's payload. Merchant Portal shows APEX data (no PGW change). 6"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","Processor receives","mcp/order-model.html#processor-receives","MPGS or CyberSource Call amount currency Payment 21.37 GBP Refund 1 12.82 GBP Refund 2 8.55 GBP Existing processor fields only. No new processor fields. MPGS and CyberSource are set up for the full ISO currency list. 7"],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Visual page","Checks on the order","mcp/order-model.html#checks-on-the-order","S5 section 4 · S6 goal 2 pass Order totals in AED, from mcpBaseAmount : paid 100.00, refunded 100.00, remaining 0.00. pass Every transaction of the order carries the purchase rate 0.2137000 (rateId 2001). No new rate is looked up for a refund. pass Refunds add up to the amount paid in both currencies: AED 100.00 = AED 100.00, GBP 21.37 = GBP 21.37. pass Merchant outputs show AED only (3 transactions). Not on this page. The same purchase and refunds as sent to APEX ( S8 ) and in the enrichment file to Postilion ( S9 ): parked until APEX gives the field names and the enrichment format is agreed. Void and chargebacks: not covered here."],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: HPP offer","mcp/order-model.html#try=offered",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: After the purchase","mcp/order-model.html#try=after-pay",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: GBP, refunds 60 + 40","mcp/order-model.html#try=gbp-60-40",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: GBP, refunds in thirds","mcp/order-model.html#try=gbp-thirds",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: GBP, one full refund","mcp/order-model.html#try=gbp-full",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: EUR, 60 + 40","mcp/order-model.html#try=eur-60-40",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: INR, 60 + 40","mcp/order-model.html#try=inr-60-40",""],["MCP","Order model: one MCP order, from purchase to the last refund","Scenario","Try it: Customer picks AED","mcp/order-model.html#try=aed-paid",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html","v1.2 25-Nov-2026 Source: NT PGW Dev Stories v1.2 (Lab copy).docx Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) Version 1.2 · 25-Nov-2026 · Prepared by Ghulam Rabbani (Product) Team: PGW engineering team Changes in v1.2 (25-Nov-2026): Open point 1 and the closed-points note now refer to the master toggle Generate and Use Network Token . Team names in place of people's names. NT-C1 acceptance criteria renumbered (5a, 5b → 6, 7; old 6 → 8). No change to any requirement. Changes in v1.1 (19-Nov-2026): Rule 1 now uses one master toggle, Generate and Use Network Token , per MID, default OFF. NT-C1: one CYBS call only (no enrollment call), no securityCode , adapter returns failures, no secrets in logs. NT-0 acceptance criterion 5 added. These stories are the requirement. Each story can be read alone. A story refers to another story only for work that must be built first."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","Story map","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-map","Payment explorer Token layers # Story What it delivers Depends on Status NT-0 Network token data model and merchant APIs New transaction object networkTokenDetails . New fields on the Geidea token record. Object returned to the merchant. — Written NT-M1 Update MPGS token generation PGW reads the network token provider when it creates the MPGS token. PGW updates the Geidea token record. NT-0 Written NT-M2 MPGS Pay (card number) PGW records network token data on Pay and Authorize with a card number. NT-0 Written NT-M3 MPGS token CIT PGW records network token data on CIT with a Geidea token. NT-0, NT-M1 Written NT-M4 MPGS token MIT PGW records network token data on MIT with a Geidea token. NT-0, NT-M1 Written NT-M5 MPGS capture, refund, void PGW records network token data on follow-on transactions. NT-M2, NT-M3, NT-M4 Written NT-C1 Create CYBS instrument identifier PGW creates a CYBS instrument identifier for a saved card. NT-0 Written NT-C2 CYBS Pay (card number) PGW records network token data on Pay and Authorize with a card number. NT-0 Written NT-C3 CYBS token CIT PGW pays CIT with the instrument identifier. PGW records network token data. NT-0, NT-C1 Written NT-C4 CYBS token MIT PGW pays MIT with the instrument identifier. PGW records network token data. NT-0, NT-C1 Written NT-C5 CYBS capture, refund, void PGW records network token data on follow-on transactions. NT-C2, NT-C3, NT-C4 Written NT-A1 APEX network token keys Network token data sent to APEX in userData . NT-M2–M4, NT-C2–C4 Waiting on APEX team NT-X Token status and retry control PGW stops using a Geidea token after a \"do not retry\" decline. NT-M3, NT-M4, NT-C3, NT-C4 Waiting on Risk & Compliance Build order: NT-0 → NT-M1 → NT-M2, NT-M3, NT-M4 → NT-M5. CYBS in parallel: NT-C1 → NT-C2, NT-C3, NT-C4 → NT-C5."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","Prerequisites (not PGW work)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#prerequisites-not-pgw-work","# Item Needed for Owner P1 MPGS: MDES onboarding, Visa VTS via TRID 40000000000 , Enable Network Tokens per merchant NT-M1 to NT-M5 (to see network tokens in test and live) Merchant integration team, MPGS P2 CYBS: network token provisioning on the vault. Visa and Mastercard token permission per MID. In place on MID TEST-CYBS-01 . NT-C1 to NT-C5 Merchant integration team P3 CYBS Decision Manager rule for MITs (a Mastercard MIT was rejected without DECISION_SKIP in the 11-Nov POC) NT-C4 Risk & Compliance"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","Rules for every story","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#rules-for-every-story","Switches and rollout Master toggle. The existing PGW configuration parameter Generate and Use Network Token is the one master toggle for all work in these stories. It is set per MID. Default: OFF for all MIDs. Toggle OFF: PGW behaves exactly as today. PGW does not create CYBS instrument identifiers. PGW pays CIT and MIT on CYBS with the card number. PGW sets networkTokenDetails = null . PGW does not change the Geidea token record. Toggle ON: the stories apply. The existing toggles still apply as today: Enable tokenization , CUSTOMER_INITIATED_TRANSACTIONS , MERCHANT_INITIATED_TRANSACTIONS . Do not use the other network token parameters ( Use Network Token , Send NT to merchant ). Rollout: switch ON for the production test MID, test in production, then switch ON for all merchants. A later release can remove this toggle. MPGS: MPGS configuration per MID (P1) decides if MPGS pays with a network token. The PGW toggle controls only what PGW records. CYBS: the PGW toggle controls instrument identifier creation and payment with it. CYBS token permission per MID (P2) decides if CYBS uses a network token. A failure to read or record network token data never changes the payment result. Log the failure. Continue. Never store or log a full card number or a full network token number. Gateway field names are as in MPGS REST API version 80 and CyberSource REST API. PGW field names are camelCase . New fields in merchant APIs are additions. Do not change or remove existing fields. Copy gateway values as received, unless a rule in the story says otherwise."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-0 — Network token data model and merchant APIs","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-0-network-token-data-model-and-merchant-apis","What PGW records"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-0 — Network token data model and merchant APIs","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background","Visa charges a fee on card-not-present transactions that do not use a network token. PGW does not record if a transaction used a network token. PGW does not record if a saved card has a network token."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-0 — Network token data model and merchant APIs","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal","PGW has one object to record network token data on each transaction, for MPGS and CYBS. PGW returns this object to the merchant. The Geidea token record has fields for network token data and token status."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-0 — Network token data model and merchant APIs","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary","This story adds the object and the fields. It does not fill them. The gateway stories (NT-M1 to NT-M5, NT-C1 to NT-C5) fill them. Item Value Story ID NT-0 Consumers Merchants (order inquiry, callback, pay responses). PGW stories NT-M1 to NT-C5. Gateway API used None Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails ; Geidea token record Depends on — Followed by NT-M1 to NT-M5, NT-C1 to NT-C5 Out of scope Filling the fields (gateway stories). Token status logic (NT-X)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-0 — Network token data model and merchant APIs","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. New transaction object networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#new-transaction-object-networktokendetails","Field Type Values Meaning isNetworkToken Boolean true / false true = the gateway paid with a network token. tokenProvider String VTS / MDES / null Network token provider. VTS = Visa. MDES = Mastercard. null when isNetworkToken = false . maskedTokenNumber String e.g. 489537xxxxxx0001 (MPGS), 5222220000000000 (CYBS) / null Masked network token number. CYBS returns only the token BIN, so the rest is zero-filled. tokenExpiryMonth String e.g. 11 / null Network token expiry month. tokenExpiryYear String e.g. 28 / null Network token expiry year. paymentAccountReference String e.g. V0010000000000000000000000001 / null PAR. One value per card."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Transactions that carry the object","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#transactions-that-carry-the-object","Add the object to transactions of type Pay , Authorization , Capture , Refund , Void . Do not add the object to Authentication transactions. Before a gateway story fills the object, set it to null . Transactions created before this release: networkTokenDetails = null ."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Return the object to the merchant","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#return-the-object-to-the-merchant","Merchant API Where Order inquiry GET /pgw/api/v1/direct/order/{orderId} order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails Callback to callbackUrl order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay response order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token response order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. New fields on the Geidea token record (internal)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#new-fields-on-the-geidea-token-record-internal","Field Type Values Default (new and existing tokens) Meaning schemeTokenProvider String VTS / MDES / null null A network token exists for this saved card. null = none seen yet. tokenStatus String ACTIVE / INACTIVE ACTIVE For NT-X. Not read or changed in this release. tokenStatusReason String free text null For NT-X. Not changed in this release. tokenStatusDate DateTime UAE time null For NT-X. Not changed in this release. cybsInstrumentIdentifierId String 19 digits / null null CYBS instrument identifier. Set by NT-C1. networkTokenState String e.g. ACTIVE / null null Network token state from CYBS. Set by NT-C1. Do not return the token record fields in any merchant API in this release."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","5. Update rules","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#update-rules","Field Rule networkTokenDetails Write once per transaction. Do not update after that. schemeTokenProvider When it is not null , do not change it. tokenStatus , tokenStatusReason , tokenStatusDate Do not change in this release."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-0 — Network token data model and merchant APIs","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria","Order inquiry for an existing order returns networkTokenDetails = null on each Pay , Authorization , Capture , Refund , Void transaction. Authentication transactions do not have the object. Existing Geidea tokens have tokenStatus = ACTIVE and all other new fields = null . Existing merchant API fields do not change. Generate and Use Network Token OFF on a MID: networkTokenDetails = null on all new transactions. The Geidea token record does not change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-M1 — Update MPGS token generation","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-m1-update-mpgs-token-generation","Explorer: MPGS save card"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M1 — Update MPGS token generation","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-1","On MPGS, PGW creates an MPGS token after a card is saved. When network tokens are enabled on MPGS (P1), MPGS asks Visa or Mastercard for a network token for the card. The MPGS token response shows if a network token exists. PGW does not read this today."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M1 — Update MPGS token generation","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-1","PGW records on the Geidea token record if a network token exists for the saved card."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M1 — Update MPGS token generation","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-1","Item Value Story ID NT-M1 Gateway MPGS, API version 80 MPGS API used Create or update token: PUT /api/rest/version/80/merchant/{merchantId}/token/{tokenId} Entities written Geidea token record: schemeTokenProvider Depends on NT-0 Followed by NT-M3, NT-M4 Out of scope Payment requests and responses (NT-M2 to NT-M5). Token status (NT-X)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M1 — Update MPGS token generation","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-1",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Trigger","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#trigger","No change. PGW calls the MPGS token endpoint at the same point as today ( SendToken , after a save-card payment)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request","No change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Response fields to read","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#response-fields-to-read","MPGS field Type Example Meaning schemeToken.provider String VTS A network token exists. Absent if none exists yet. Note: in version 80 the token response has no masked network token number, no token expiry and no network token status."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing","# Condition Action 4.1 schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token is not null Do not change it. Stop. 4.2 schemeToken.provider = VTS or MDES Set schemeTokenProvider = the value received. 4.3 schemeToken.provider is absent Leave schemeTokenProvider = null . 4.4 schemeToken.provider has any other value Leave schemeTokenProvider = null . Log the value. 4.5 The MPGS token call fails No change from today. Why 4.3: MPGS can create the network token after the token call. NT-M3 and NT-M4 set schemeTokenProvider at the first payment with a network token."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M1 — Update MPGS token generation","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-1","Save a Visa card on an MPGS MID with network tokens enabled. Token response has schemeToken.provider = VTS . Geidea token has schemeTokenProvider = VTS . Same for Mastercard: MDES . MID without network tokens enabled: schemeTokenProvider = null . Save the same card again (same tokenId ): schemeTokenProvider does not change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-M2 — MPGS Pay (card number)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-m2-mpgs-pay-card-number","Mapper: MPGS card"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M2 — MPGS Pay (card number)","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-2","A Pay or Authorize with a card number does not use a network token. PGW records the result so that every transaction carries the same object."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M2 — MPGS Pay (card number)","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-2","PGW fills networkTokenDetails on Pay and Authorize with a card number on MPGS."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M2 — MPGS Pay (card number)","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-2","Item Value Story ID NT-M2 Gateway MPGS, API version 80 Geidea endpoints POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay ; HPP payment with card number MPGS API used PUT /api/rest/version/80/merchant/{merchantId}/order/{orderId}/transaction/{transactionId} , apiOperation = PAY or AUTHORIZE Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails Depends on NT-0 Followed by NT-M5 Out of scope Token creation (NT-M1). Payments with a Geidea token (NT-M3, NT-M4)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M2 — MPGS Pay (card number)","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-2",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-1","No change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Response fields to read","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#response-fields-to-read-1","MPGS field Type Example sourceOfFunds.type String CARD sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand String VISA sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificNumber String 489537xxxxxx0001 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month String 11 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year String 28 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.paymentAccountReference String V0010000000000000000000000001"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails","PGW field MPGS source Rule isNetworkToken sourceOfFunds.type true if SCHEME_TOKEN . false for any other value. tokenProvider sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand If isNetworkToken = true : VISA → VTS , MASTERCARD → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber …card.deviceSpecificNumber Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryMonth …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryYear …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year Copy. null if absent. paymentAccountReference …card.paymentAccountReference Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-1","Fill the object from the MPGS response of the same transaction. If the response has no sourceOfFunds , set the object to null . Apply to approved and declined transactions. Do not change the Geidea token record."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M2 — MPGS Pay (card number)","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-2","Pay with a Visa card number: isNetworkToken = false , tokenProvider = null . PAR in the MPGS response is copied to paymentAccountReference . Same result for AUTHORIZE and for HPP. The object is returned in order inquiry, callback and the /pay response."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-M3 — MPGS token CIT","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-m3-mpgs-token-cit","Explorer: MPGS CIT Mapper"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M3 — MPGS token CIT","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-3","PGW pays a customer-initiated transaction (CIT) with the MPGS token of a saved card. When network tokens are enabled on MPGS (P1), MPGS sends the network token to the scheme in place of the card. Tests on 16–17 Nov 2026 confirmed this for Visa and Mastercard. PGW does not record it."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M3 — MPGS token CIT","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-3","PGW fills networkTokenDetails on token CIT. PGW sets schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token when a network token is first seen. The payment request does not change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M3 — MPGS token CIT","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-3","Item Value Story ID NT-M3 Gateway MPGS, API version 80 Geidea endpoints POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token ( initiatedBy = Payer ); HPP with tokenId MPGS API used PUT /api/rest/version/80/merchant/{merchantId}/order/{orderId}/transaction/{transactionId} , apiOperation = PAY or AUTHORIZE , sourceOfFunds.token Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails ; Geidea token record: schemeTokenProvider Depends on NT-0, NT-M1 Followed by NT-M5, NT-X Out of scope Token status and retry control (NT-X)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M3 — MPGS token CIT","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-3",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-2","No change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Response fields to read","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#response-fields-to-read-2","MPGS field Type Example sourceOfFunds.type String SCHEME_TOKEN sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand String MASTERCARD sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificNumber String masked token number sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month String 11 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year String 28 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.paymentAccountReference String PAR"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails-1","PGW field MPGS source Rule isNetworkToken sourceOfFunds.type true if SCHEME_TOKEN . false for any other value. tokenProvider sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand If isNetworkToken = true : VISA → VTS , MASTERCARD → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber …card.deviceSpecificNumber Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryMonth …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryYear …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year Copy. null if absent. paymentAccountReference …card.paymentAccountReference Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-2","Fill the object from the MPGS response of the same transaction. If the response has no sourceOfFunds , set the object to null . Apply to approved and declined transactions. If isNetworkToken = true and schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token is null : set schemeTokenProvider = tokenProvider . If schemeTokenProvider is not null : do not change it. Keep saving authorizationResponse.merchantAdviceCode and response.acquirerCode as today. NT-X uses them."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M3 — MPGS token CIT","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-3","Token CIT, Visa, network tokens enabled: isNetworkToken = true , tokenProvider = VTS , masked number, expiry and PAR filled. Same for Mastercard: MDES . Geidea token with schemeTokenProvider = null gets VTS or MDES after the first network token CIT. Token CIT on a MID without network tokens: isNetworkToken = false . schemeTokenProvider does not change. Same results for AUTHORIZE and for HPP with tokenId ."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-M4 — MPGS token MIT","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-m4-mpgs-token-mit","Explorer: MPGS MIT Mapper"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M4 — MPGS token MIT","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-4","PGW pays a merchant-initiated transaction (MIT) with the MPGS token of a saved card. When network tokens are enabled on MPGS (P1), MPGS sends the network token in place of the card. Tests on 16–17 Nov 2026 confirmed this for Visa and Mastercard. PGW does not record it."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M4 — MPGS token MIT","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-4","PGW fills networkTokenDetails on token MIT. PGW sets schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token when a network token is first seen. The payment request does not change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M4 — MPGS token MIT","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-4","Item Value Story ID NT-M4 Gateway MPGS, API version 80 Geidea endpoint POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token ( initiatedBy = Merchant ) MPGS API used PUT /api/rest/version/80/merchant/{merchantId}/order/{orderId}/transaction/{transactionId} , apiOperation = PAY , sourceOfFunds.token Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails ; Geidea token record: schemeTokenProvider Depends on NT-0, NT-M1 Followed by NT-M5, NT-X Out of scope Token status and retry control (NT-X)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M4 — MPGS token MIT","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-4",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-3","No change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Response fields to read","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#response-fields-to-read-3","MPGS field Type Example sourceOfFunds.type String SCHEME_TOKEN sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand String VISA sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificNumber String 489537xxxxxx0001 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month String 11 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year String 28 sourceOfFunds.provided.card.paymentAccountReference String V0010000000000000000000000001"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails-2","PGW field MPGS source Rule isNetworkToken sourceOfFunds.type true if SCHEME_TOKEN . false for any other value. tokenProvider sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand If isNetworkToken = true : VISA → VTS , MASTERCARD → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber …card.deviceSpecificNumber Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryMonth …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryYear …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year Copy. null if absent. paymentAccountReference …card.paymentAccountReference Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-3","Fill the object from the MPGS response of the same transaction. If the response has no sourceOfFunds , set the object to null . Apply to approved and declined transactions. If isNetworkToken = true and schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token is null : set schemeTokenProvider = tokenProvider . If schemeTokenProvider is not null : do not change it. Keep saving authorizationResponse.merchantAdviceCode and response.acquirerCode as today. NT-X uses them."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","5. Example: Visa MIT MIT-0000A001-0000000001","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#example-visa-mit-mit-0000a001-0000000001","MPGS response (excerpt): \"sourceOfFunds\": { \"type\": \"SCHEME_TOKEN\", \"token\": \"0A1B2C3D4E5F60718293A4B5C6D7E8F9\", \"provided\": { \"card\": { \"brand\": \"VISA\", \"deviceSpecificNumber\": \"489537xxxxxx0001\", \"deviceSpecificExpiry\": { \"month\": \"11\", \"year\": \"28\" }, \"paymentAccountReference\": \"V0010000000000000000000000001\", \"storedOnFile\": \"STORED\" } } } PGW transaction: \"networkTokenDetails\": { \"isNetworkToken\": true, \"tokenProvider\": \"VTS\", \"maskedTokenNumber\": \"489537xxxxxx0001\", \"tokenExpiryMonth\": \"11\", \"tokenExpiryYear\": \"28\", \"paymentAccountReference\": \"V0010000000000000000000000001\" }"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M4 — MPGS token MIT","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-4","The example above gives the PGW object above. Same for Mastercard: MDES . Geidea token with schemeTokenProvider = null gets VTS or MDES after the first network token MIT. MIT on a MID without network tokens: isNetworkToken = false . schemeTokenProvider does not change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-M5 — MPGS capture, refund, void","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-m5-mpgs-capture-refund-void","Explorer: MPGS refund"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M5 — MPGS capture, refund, void","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-5","Capture, refund and void follow a Pay, Authorize, CIT or MIT. MPGS returns the source of funds on these transactions. Tests on 16–17 Nov 2026 confirmed capture and refund on network tokens."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M5 — MPGS capture, refund, void","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-5","PGW fills networkTokenDetails on capture, refund and void on MPGS."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M5 — MPGS capture, refund, void","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-5","Item Value Story ID NT-M5 Gateway MPGS, API version 80 Geidea endpoints POST /pgw/api/v1/direct/capture ; POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/refund ; POST /pgw/api/v3/direct/void MPGS API used PUT /api/rest/version/80/merchant/{merchantId}/order/{orderId}/transaction/{transactionId} , apiOperation = CAPTURE , REFUND or VOID Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails Depends on NT-M2, NT-M3, NT-M4 Out of scope Geidea token record (no change in this story)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M5 — MPGS capture, refund, void","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-5",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-4","No change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails-3","PGW field MPGS source Rule isNetworkToken sourceOfFunds.type true if SCHEME_TOKEN . false for any other value. tokenProvider sourceOfFunds.provided.card.brand If isNetworkToken = true : VISA → VTS , MASTERCARD → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber …card.deviceSpecificNumber Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryMonth …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month Copy. null if absent. tokenExpiryYear …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year Copy. null if absent. paymentAccountReference …card.paymentAccountReference Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-4","Fill the object from the MPGS response of the same transaction. If the response has no sourceOfFunds : copy networkTokenDetails from the original Pay or Authorization transaction of the order. If the original transaction has no object: set the object to null . Do not change the Geidea token record."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-M5 — MPGS capture, refund, void","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-5","Capture of a network token Authorize: isNetworkToken = true , same provider and PAR as the Authorize. Refund and void: same. Capture of a card-number Authorize: isNetworkToken = false ."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-C1 — Create CYBS instrument identifier","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-c1-create-cybs-instrument-identifier","Explorer: save card (T1) On demand (T2) Failure"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C1 — Create CYBS instrument identifier","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-6","On CYBS, PGW does not create a gateway token when a card is saved. PGW sends the card number from the Geidea vault on each CIT and MIT. CYBS cannot use a network token this way. A CYBS instrument identifier links the card to a network token. A POC on 11 Nov 2026 (MID TEST-CYBS-01 ) confirmed: CYBS creates the network token with the instrument identifier, and payments with it use the network token."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C1 — Create CYBS instrument identifier","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-6","PGW creates a CYBS instrument identifier for each saved card on CYBS and stores it on the Geidea token."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C1 — Create CYBS instrument identifier","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-6","Item Value Story ID NT-C1 Gateway CyberSource REST API (Token Management Service) CYBS API used POST /tms/v1/instrumentidentifiers Entities written Geidea token record: cybsInstrumentIdentifierId , networkTokenState Depends on NT-0 Followed by NT-C3, NT-C4 Out of scope Payment requests (NT-C2 to NT-C5). Creating the identifier inside the payment ( actionList: [\"TOKEN_CREATE\"] ): not used. CYBS customer and payment instrument tokens. Network token lifecycle updates from CYBS."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C1 — Create CYBS instrument identifier","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-6",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Triggers","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#triggers","# Trigger When T1 After save card A Pay or Authorize with save card is approved on a CYBS MID, and PGW saves the card. Same point as SendToken on MPGS. T2 On demand NT-C3 or NT-C4 calls it before a CIT or MIT when cybsInstrumentIdentifierId = null . This covers cards saved before go-live and failed T1 calls."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Pre-checks","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#pre-checks","# Check On failure 2.1 The MID is routed to CYBS. Do not call CYBS. No error to the merchant. 2.2 Enable tokenization is ON for the MID. Do not call CYBS. No error to the merchant. 2.2a Generate and Use Network Token is ON for the MID. Do not call CYBS. No error to the merchant. 2.3 The card number and expiry are available in the Geidea vault for this token. Do not call CYBS. Log. No error to the merchant."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-5","CYBS field M/O/C Value type M enrollable card card.number M Card number from the Geidea vault card.expirationMonth M 2 digits, e.g. 02 card.expirationYear M 4 digits, e.g. 2029 card.securityCode — Do not send. { \"type\": \"enrollable card\", \"card\": { \"number\": \"<PAN>\", \"expirationMonth\": \"02\", \"expirationYear\": \"2029\" } } Headers: the same as PGW payment calls on the MID: v-c-merchant-id , v-c-date , Digest , Signature (HTTP Signature, HmacSHA256), Content-Type: application/json . No profile-id header. (Proven in the 11-Nov POC, Postman collection \"CYBS NT POC\", request 2.) HTTP status: Status Meaning Action 201 New instrument identifier created Success (section 5) 200 Instrument identifier already exists for this card Success (section 5) Any other Failure Section 5.3 This is the only CYBS call in NT-C1. Do not call POST /tms/v1/instrumentidentifiers/{id}/enrollment or any other TMS endpoint. (Test log 18-Nov-2026: the Cybersource adapter sent this extra call. CYBS rejected it with HTTP 400 invalidFields , card.number .)"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Response fields to read","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#response-fields-to-read-4","CYBS field Type Example Meaning id String (19 digits) 7000000000000000001 Instrument identifier. CYBS generates it. PGW cannot set it. tokenizedCard.state String ACTIVE Network token state at creation."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","5. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-5","# Outcome Action 5.1 Success, id received Save id in cybsInstrumentIdentifierId . Save tokenizedCard.state in networkTokenState ( null if absent). 5.2 Success, id differs from the stored value Replace the stored value. Log the old and new value. 5.3 Failure (timeout, HTTP 4xx, HTTP 5xx) Leave cybsInstrumentIdentifierId = null . Log the error. Do not change the result of the payment (T1). Return \"not created\" to the caller (T2). The Cybersource adapter returns the failure to PGW. It does not return a success code ( 000 ) when a CYBS call fails. Note: the same card gives the same id . The CYBS test bench showed instrumentidentifierNew = true for a new card and false for a known card."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","6. Sensitive data","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#sensitive-data","Read the card number from the vault only for this call. Do not log the request body. Do not log the merchant SharedSecretKey , MerchantKeyId or the CVV."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C1 — Create CYBS instrument identifier","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-6","Save a Visa card on MID TEST-CYBS-01 : Geidea token gets a 19-digit cybsInstrumentIdentifierId and networkTokenState = ACTIVE . Same for Mastercard. Save the same card again: same cybsInstrumentIdentifierId . CYBS returns an error: the save-card payment result does not change. cybsInstrumentIdentifierId = null . MID routed to MPGS: no call to CYBS. CYBS MID with Enable tokenization OFF: no call to CYBS. CYBS MID with Generate and Use Network Token OFF: no call to CYBS. CIT and MIT go out with the card number, as today. Logs do not contain the card number."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-C2 — CYBS Pay (card number)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-c2-cybs-pay-card-number","Mapper: CYBS card"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C2 — CYBS Pay (card number)","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-7","A Pay or Authorize with a card number does not use a network token. PGW records the result so that every transaction carries the same object."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C2 — CYBS Pay (card number)","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-7","PGW fills networkTokenDetails on Pay and Authorize with a card number on CYBS."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C2 — CYBS Pay (card number)","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-7","Item Value Story ID NT-C2 Gateway CyberSource REST API Geidea endpoints POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay ; HPP payment with card number CYBS API used POST /pts/v2/payments Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails Depends on NT-0 Followed by NT-C5 Out of scope Instrument identifier creation after save card (NT-C1). Payments with a Geidea token (NT-C3, NT-C4)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C2 — CYBS Pay (card number)","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-7",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Request","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-6","No change."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails-4","PGW field CYBS response source Rule isNetworkToken processingInformation.paymentSolution true if 015 or 014 . false for any other value or if absent. tokenProvider processingInformation.paymentSolution 015 → VTS . 014 → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber paymentInformation.card.bin (token BIN, 6 digits) If isNetworkToken = true : the 6-digit BIN followed by 10 zeros (16 digits). Example: 522222 → 5222220000000000 . Otherwise null . tokenExpiryMonth Not in the CYBS response null tokenExpiryYear Not in the CYBS response null paymentAccountReference processorInformation.paymentAccountReferenceNumber Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-6","Fill the object from the CYBS response of the same transaction. Apply to approved and declined transactions. Do not change the Geidea token record."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C2 — CYBS Pay (card number)","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-7","Pay with a Visa card number: isNetworkToken = false , tokenProvider = null . PAR in the CYBS response is copied to paymentAccountReference . Same result for Authorize and for HPP."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-C3 — CYBS token CIT","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-c3-cybs-token-cit","Explorer: CYBS CIT Mapper"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C3 — CYBS token CIT","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-8","On CYBS, PGW pays a CIT with the card number from the Geidea vault. CYBS cannot use a network token this way. A POC on 11 Nov 2026 (MID TEST-CYBS-01 ) confirmed: a CIT with the instrument identifier uses the network token. Test CY-0000C002-0000000002 (Mastercard): 3DS ran, paymentSolution = 014 , PAR returned."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C3 — CYBS token CIT","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-8","PGW pays token CIT with the CYBS instrument identifier. PGW fills networkTokenDetails . PGW sets schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token when a network token is first seen."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C3 — CYBS token CIT","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-8","Item Value Story ID NT-C3 Gateway CyberSource REST API Geidea endpoints POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token ( initiatedBy = Payer ); HPP with tokenId CYBS API used POST /pts/v2/payments Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails ; Geidea token record: schemeTokenProvider Depends on NT-0, NT-C1 Followed by NT-C5, NT-X Out of scope Token status and retry control (NT-X). 3DS flow (no change)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C3 — CYBS token CIT","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-8",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Choose the funding source","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#choose-the-funding-source","# Condition Action 1.1 cybsInstrumentIdentifierId is not null Send the request in section 2. 1.2 cybsInstrumentIdentifierId = null Call NT-C1 (trigger T2). If it returns an id , send the request in section 2. 1.3 NT-C1 returns \"not created\" Send the request with the card number, as today."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Request change","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-change","Change only paymentInformation . Add tokenInformation . Do not change any other field. CYBS field M/O/C Value paymentInformation.instrumentIdentifier.id M cybsInstrumentIdentifierId paymentInformation.card.number — Do not send. paymentInformation.card.expirationMonth M From the Geidea vault, as today paymentInformation.card.expirationYear M From the Geidea vault, as today paymentInformation.card.securityCode C Send when the cardholder enters the CVV, as today tokenInformation.networkTokenOption M prefer // today \"paymentInformation\": { \"card\": { \"number\": \"<PAN>\", \"expirationMonth\": \"02\", \"expirationYear\": \"2029\", \"securityCode\": \"<CVV>\" } } // new \"paymentInformation\": { \"instrumentIdentifier\": { \"id\": \"7000000000000000001\" }, \"card\": { \"expirationMonth\": \"02\", \"expirationYear\": \"2029\", \"securityCode\": \"<CVV>\" } }, \"tokenInformation\": { \"networkTokenOption\": \"prefer\" } No change to: processingInformation.authorizationOptions.initiator , stored-credential flags, consumerAuthenticationInformation , order and amount fields."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails-5","PGW field CYBS response source Rule isNetworkToken processingInformation.paymentSolution true if 015 or 014 . false for any other value or if absent. tokenProvider processingInformation.paymentSolution 015 → VTS . 014 → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber paymentInformation.card.bin (token BIN, 6 digits) If isNetworkToken = true : the 6-digit BIN followed by 10 zeros (16 digits). Example: 522222 → 5222220000000000 . Otherwise null . tokenExpiryMonth Not in the CYBS response null tokenExpiryYear Not in the CYBS response null paymentAccountReference processorInformation.paymentAccountReferenceNumber Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-7","Fill the object from the CYBS response of the same transaction. Apply to approved and declined transactions. If isNetworkToken = true and schemeTokenProvider is null : set schemeTokenProvider = tokenProvider . If schemeTokenProvider is not null : do not change it. A decline on the instrument identifier is final. Do not retry with the card number. Save on the transaction, if PGW does not save them today: processorInformation.responseCode , processorInformation.merchantAdvice.code , processorInformation.merchantAdvice.codeRaw . NT-X uses them."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C3 — CYBS token CIT","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-8","Token CIT, Mastercard, MID TEST-CYBS-01 : request has instrumentIdentifier.id and no card number. Response paymentSolution = 014 . isNetworkToken = true , tokenProvider = MDES , PAR filled. Same for Visa: 015 , VTS . Token without an instrument identifier: NT-C1 is called, then the CIT uses the new id . NT-C1 fails: the CIT is sent with the card number. isNetworkToken = false . 3DS runs as today. CVV is sent when entered. Geidea token with schemeTokenProvider = null gets VTS or MDES after the first network token CIT."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-C4 — CYBS token MIT","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-c4-cybs-token-mit","Explorer: CYBS MIT Mapper"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C4 — CYBS token MIT","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-9","On CYBS, PGW pays an MIT with the card number from the Geidea vault and the network transaction ID of the first payment. CYBS cannot use a network token this way. A POC on 11 Nov 2026 (MID TEST-CYBS-01 ) confirmed: an MIT with the instrument identifier uses the network token for Visa ( 015 ) and Mastercard ( 014 )."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C4 — CYBS token MIT","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-9","PGW pays token MIT with the CYBS instrument identifier. PGW fills networkTokenDetails . PGW sets schemeTokenProvider on the Geidea token when a network token is first seen."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C4 — CYBS token MIT","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-9","Item Value Story ID NT-C4 Gateway CyberSource REST API Geidea endpoint POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token ( initiatedBy = Merchant ) CYBS API used POST /pts/v2/payments Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails ; Geidea token record: schemeTokenProvider Depends on NT-0, NT-C1. Prerequisite P3 (Decision Manager rule). Followed by NT-C5, NT-X Out of scope Token status and retry control (NT-X)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C4 — CYBS token MIT","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-9",""],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","1. Choose the funding source","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#choose-the-funding-source-1","# Condition Action 1.1 cybsInstrumentIdentifierId is not null Send the request in section 2. 1.2 cybsInstrumentIdentifierId = null Call NT-C1 (trigger T2). If it returns an id , send the request in section 2. 1.3 NT-C1 returns \"not created\" Send the request with the card number, as today."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","2. Request change","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#request-change-1","Change only paymentInformation . Add tokenInformation . Do not change any other field. CYBS field M/O/C Value paymentInformation.instrumentIdentifier.id M cybsInstrumentIdentifierId paymentInformation.card.number — Do not send. paymentInformation.card.expirationMonth M From the Geidea vault, as today paymentInformation.card.expirationYear M From the Geidea vault, as today paymentInformation.card.securityCode — Do not send (MIT). tokenInformation.networkTokenOption M prefer // today \"paymentInformation\": { \"card\": { \"number\": \"<PAN>\", \"expirationMonth\": \"02\", \"expirationYear\": \"2029\" } } // new \"paymentInformation\": { \"instrumentIdentifier\": { \"id\": \"7000000000000000001\" }, \"card\": { \"expirationMonth\": \"02\", \"expirationYear\": \"2029\" } }, \"tokenInformation\": { \"networkTokenOption\": \"prefer\" } No change to: processingInformation.authorizationOptions.initiator , stored-credential flags, processingInformation.authorizationOptions.initiator.merchantInitiatedTransaction.previousTransactionId (the stored transactions[].cybersourceDetails.networkTransactionId ), order and amount fields."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","3. Mapping to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#mapping-to-networktokendetails-6","PGW field CYBS response source Rule isNetworkToken processingInformation.paymentSolution true if 015 or 014 . false for any other value or if absent. tokenProvider processingInformation.paymentSolution 015 → VTS . 014 → MDES . Otherwise null . maskedTokenNumber paymentInformation.card.bin (token BIN, 6 digits) If isNetworkToken = true : the 6-digit BIN followed by 10 zeros (16 digits). Example: 522222 → 5222220000000000 . Otherwise null . tokenExpiryMonth Not in the CYBS response null tokenExpiryYear Not in the CYBS response null paymentAccountReference processorInformation.paymentAccountReferenceNumber Copy. null if absent."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › Requirements","Document","4. Processing","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#processing-8","Fill the object from the CYBS response of the same transaction. Apply to approved and declined transactions. If isNetworkToken = true and schemeTokenProvider is null : set schemeTokenProvider = tokenProvider . If schemeTokenProvider is not null : do not change it. A decline on the instrument identifier is final. Do not retry with the card number. Save on the transaction, if PGW does not save them today: processorInformation.responseCode , processorInformation.merchantAdvice.code , processorInformation.merchantAdvice.codeRaw . NT-X uses them."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C4 — CYBS token MIT","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-9","Token MIT, Visa, MID TEST-CYBS-01 : request has instrumentIdentifier.id , no card number, no CVV, the stored network transaction ID. Response paymentSolution = 015 . isNetworkToken = true , tokenProvider = VTS . Same for Mastercard: 014 , MDES (needs P3). Token without an instrument identifier: NT-C1 is called, then the MIT uses the new id . NT-C1 fails: the MIT is sent with the card number. isNetworkToken = false ."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-C5 — CYBS capture, refund, void","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-c5-cybs-capture-refund-void","Explorer: CYBS refund"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C5 — CYBS capture, refund, void","Document","Background","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#background-10","Capture, refund and void follow a Pay, Authorize, CIT or MIT. They refer to the original CYBS payment. They do not carry a funding source. The CYBS response does not show network token data."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C5 — CYBS capture, refund, void","Document","Goal","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#goal-10","PGW fills networkTokenDetails on capture, refund and void on CYBS."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C5 — CYBS capture, refund, void","Document","Story summary","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#story-summary-10","Item Value Story ID NT-C5 Gateway CyberSource REST API Geidea endpoints POST /pgw/api/v1/direct/capture ; POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/refund ; POST /pgw/api/v3/direct/void CYBS API used Capture POST /pts/v2/payments/{id}/captures ; refund POST /pts/v2/payments/{id}/refunds ; void POST /pts/v2/payments/{id}/voids . No change to the requests. Entities written transactions[].networkTokenDetails Depends on NT-C2, NT-C3, NT-C4 Out of scope Geidea token record (no change in this story)."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C5 — CYBS capture, refund, void","Document","Requirements","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#requirements-10","Request: no change. Copy networkTokenDetails from the original Pay or Authorization transaction of the order. If the original transaction has no object: set the object to null . Do not change the Geidea token record."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource) › NT-C5 — CYBS capture, refund, void","Document","Acceptance criteria","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-10","Capture of a network token Authorize: same networkTokenDetails as the Authorize. Refund and void: same. Capture of a card-number Authorize: isNetworkToken = false ."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-A1 — APEX network token keys (Waiting on APEX team)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-a1-apex-network-token-keys-waiting-on-apex-team","Not yet defined Proposal to discuss: send network token data to APEX in userData , in the existing key format ( TOKEN , CHANNEL_TYPE , AUTH_PERFORMED , AUTH_TXN_ID exist today). Key Values Example NETWORK_TOKEN Y / N 213NETWORK_TOKEN11Y NT_PROVIDER VTS / MDES 211NT_PROVIDER13VTS Questions: Is PAR needed? Display in Merchant Portal or reports? Does next-day clearing already carry a token indicator?"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","NT-X — Token status and retry control (Waiting on Risk & Compliance)","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#nt-x-token-status-and-retry-control-waiting-on-risk-compliance","Not yet defined Uses the NT-0 fields tokenStatus , tokenStatusReason , tokenStatusDate . Proposal for MPGS (to be confirmed): set INACTIVE after merchant advice code 03 , 21 or DE 39 04 , 14 , 15 , 41 , 43 , 46 , 62 , 78 on token CIT or MIT. Exclude 54 , 63 , 88 . Reject payments on an INACTIVE token with a new PGW code (\"Token inactive. Use another card.\"). CYBS values: waiting on Risk & Compliance."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization — PGW Dev Stories (MPGS and CyberSource)","Document","Open points","network-tokenization/docs/stories.html#open-points","Open point 1 # Point Story Status 1 When Generate and Use Network Token is switched ON for a CYBS MID (with Enable tokenization ON), PGW starts to create instrument identifiers and pay CIT/MIT with them. Confirm that a payment with an instrument identifier on a MID without CYBS token permission (P2) goes out as the card, so P2 is the PVT control. NT-C1, NT-C3, NT-C4 Merchant integration team / PGW engineering team to verify on a test MID Closed 17-Nov: CYBS maskedTokenNumber = token BIN + zero fill. CYBS capture, refund and void endpoints (refund and void proven on the test bench 14-Nov). Decision Manager MIT rule = operational, with Risk (P3). TMS headers and HTTP status (headers proven 11-Nov; 201 new / 200 existing per CYBS docs). Closed 19-Nov: master toggle = Generate and Use Network Token per MID, default OFF (rule 1); this replaces the 17-Nov decision \"no new toggle\". NT-C1 = one CYBS call only."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","Hub","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","network-tokenization/index.html","feature Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token Visa charges a PAN Integrity Fee on card-not-present Visa payments that do not use a Visa network token. In the UAE the network token requirement for cards on file started on 18-Apr-2026 and the fee on 17-Oct-2026. Phase 1: every saved-card payment (CIT and MIT) through PGW goes out with the network token, and PGW records it on the transaction."],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","Hub","Two gateways, two different changes","network-tokenization/index.html#two-gateways-two-different-changes","The point to understand first. On MPGS the network token already works with today's PGW requests; PGW only has to record it. On CyberSource PGW sends the card number today, so PGW must pay differently : create an instrument identifier and pay with it. MPGS · record only"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token › Two gateways, two different changes","Hub","The payment request does not change","network-tokenization/index.html#the-payment-request-does-not-change","PGW already pays CIT and MIT with the MPGS token. With network tokens set up for the MID at MPGS, MPGS sends the network token by itself. Proven through PGW on 16–17 Nov for Visa and Mastercard. New: PGW reads sourceOfFunds.type = SCHEME_TOKEN and records it (NT-M1 to NT-M5). CyberSource · pay differently"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token › Two gateways, two different changes","Hub","PGW pays with an instrument identifier","network-tokenization/index.html#pgw-pays-with-an-instrument-identifier","Today PGW sends the card number on every CIT and MIT, so CYBS cannot use a network token. New: create the instrument identifier at save card (NT-C1) and pay with it and networkTokenOption = prefer (NT-C3, NT-C4). Proven in the 11-Nov POC and the 14-Nov test bench: Visa 015 , Mastercard 014 . Both: new object transactions[].networkTokenDetails returned to the merchant (NT-0). One master toggle per MID, default OFF (Rule 1). Story map"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","Hub","Documents","network-tokenization/index.html#documents","PGW dev stories NT-0, MPGS NT-M1 to NT-M5, CYBS NT-C1 to NT-C5 written; NT-A1 (APEX) and NT-X (token status) parked v1.2 · 25-Nov-2026"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","Hub","Visual pages","network-tokenization/index.html#visual-pages","Token layers Card number, Geidea token, gateway token, network token: who holds what, how each is created, PAR open Payment explorer Sequence diagram per gateway: save card, CIT, MIT, refund; switch the toggles and the card's state; see what changes, how the payment goes to the scheme and what PGW records open What PGW records Gateway response to networkTokenDetails , field by field; where the merchant sees it; the Geidea token record open Switches and rollout PGW master toggle versus gateway set-up on MPGS and CYBS; rollout steps; partners on Geidea's MSO or their own open Not yet defined NT-A1 APEX keys, NT-X token status, open point 1, guest checkout POC parked and in POC"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","Hub","Stories and where to see them","network-tokenization/index.html#stories","NT-0 · Data model and merchant APIs What PGW records · Token layers NT-M1 · MPGS token generation Explorer: save card NT-M2 · MPGS Pay (card number) Mapper · Explorer NT-M3 · MPGS token CIT Explorer · Not provisioned yet · Mapper NT-M4 · MPGS token MIT Explorer · Mapper NT-M5 · MPGS capture, refund, void Explorer · No sourceOfFunds NT-C1 · Create CYBS instrument identifier Explorer: save card (T1) · On demand (T2) · Failure NT-C2 · CYBS Pay (card number) Mapper NT-C3 · CYBS token CIT Explorer · Mapper NT-C4 · CYBS token MIT Explorer · Mapper NT-C5 · CYBS capture, refund, void Explorer NT-A1 · APEX keys (parked) Not yet defined NT-X · Token status and retry (parked) Not yet defined"],["Network Tokenization","Network Tokenization: saved-card payments with the scheme token","Hub","Proven in tests","network-tokenization/index.html#proven-in-tests","11-Nov · CYBS POC Instrument identifier created with the network token ACTIVE ; CIT and MIT approved on the network token (Visa 015 , Mastercard 014 ). Mastercard MIT needed the Decision Manager rule (P3). 14-Nov · CYBS test bench Save card, then CIT with the instrument identifier : 3DS ran, paymentSolution 014 , token BIN and PAR returned; refund and void. 16–17 Nov · MPGS through PGW Token CIT and MIT went out as SCHEME_TOKEN for Visa and Mastercard, with no change to PGW requests; 3DS, capture and refunds on the network token. 25-Nov · MPGS POC Token then pay, back to back : card number with asynchronous provisioning, network token with attempt-synchronous. Guest checkout POC"],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","Visual page","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html","visual page Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout What is outside the agreed stories today. Proposals are shown as proposals; nothing on this page is a requirement until the stories say so. Illustrative Reflects PGW dev stories v1.2 · 25-Nov-2026, and tests to 25-Nov-2026 Open points"],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","Visual page","NT-A1: APEX network token keys","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#nt-a1-apex-network-token-keys","PARKED · waiting on APEX team NT-A1 Proposal to discuss: send network token data to APEX in userData , in the format of the existing keys ( TOKEN , CHANNEL_TYPE , AUTH_PERFORMED , AUTH_TXN_ID ). Key Values Example NETWORK_TOKEN Y / N 213NETWORK_TOKEN11Y NT_PROVIDER VTS / MDES 211NT_PROVIDER13VTS"],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout › NT-A1: APEX network token keys","Visual page","How the example reads (our reading; to confirm with the APEX team)","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#how-the-example-reads-our-reading-to-confirm-with-the-apex-t","2 digits in key length 13 key length NETWORK_TOKEN key 1 digits in value length 1 value length Y value"],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout › NT-A1: APEX network token keys","Visual page","Questions","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#questions","Is PAR needed? Should it show in the Merchant Portal or reports? Does next-day clearing already carry a token indicator?"],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","Visual page","NT-X: token status and retry control","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#nt-x-token-status-and-retry-control","PARKED · waiting on Risk & Compliance NT-X PGW stops using a Geidea token after a \"do not retry\" decline. NT-0 already adds tokenStatus (default ACTIVE ), tokenStatusReason and tokenStatusDate ; nothing reads or changes them in this release. The CYBS stories save processorInformation.responseCode and merchantAdvice.code / codeRaw so NT-X can use them."],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout › NT-X: token status and retry control","Visual page","Proposal for MPGS (to be confirmed)","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#proposal-for-mpgs-to-be-confirmed","On token CIT or MIT Codes Proposed action Merchant advice code 03 , 21 Set the token INACTIVE DE 39 response code 04 , 14 , 15 , 41 , 43 , 46 , 62 , 78 Set the token INACTIVE DE 39 response code 54 , 63 , 88 Excluded: token stays ACTIVE Payment on an INACTIVE token new PGW code Reject: \"Token inactive. Use another card.\" Not defined: CYBS fields and values, CIT versus MIT differences, soft-decline retry limits."],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","Visual page","Open point 1: no token permission on CYBS","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#open-point-1-no-token-permission-on-cybs","TO VERIFY · PGW engineering team, during build Open point 1 With the PGW toggle ON, PGW creates an instrument identifier and pays with it and networkTokenOption = prefer . On a MID without CYBS token permission (P2), the payment must go out as the card, so P2 stays the control. CYBS documentation says prefer falls back to the card. This is not tested on a Geidea MID yet. See the case in the Payment explorer · Switches"],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","Visual page","Guest checkout: tokenize before pay","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#guest-checkout-poc","IN POC · not a work item Phase 1 covers saved cards (CIT and MIT). Visa's PAN Integrity Fee also applies to guest-checkout Visa payments that are not network-tokenized (from 17-Oct-2026 in the UAE). Visa's answer for guest checkout is Click to Pay. A POC on MPGS tests another route: create the gateway token for every new card before the payment, so the first payment can already go out with a network token. The question: can the network token exist in time when the payment follows straight after the token is created? run 1 · repository provisioning: asynchronous token pay 0.6 s: CARD NT ~30 s Payment approved but sent with the card number. The network token (VTS) appeared about 30 seconds later. Asynchronous provisioning loses the race. Card number run 2 · repository provisioning: attempt synchronous token + NT ~1 s pay: SCHEME_TOKEN Token creation took about 1 second and returned with the network token. The payment straight after went out as SCHEME_TOKEN . Network token Context. Geidea e-commerce payments run 3DS, so in a real flow the customer's 3DS time sits between token creation and payment. Visa test card, MPGS test MID TEST-MPGS-01 , 25-Nov-2026. Not tested on CyberSource. Whether this meets the guest-checkout requirement is not confirmed."],["Network Tokenization","Not yet defined: parked stories, open point, guest checkout","Visual page","Not in this release","network-tokenization/not-yet-defined.html#not-in-this-release","OUT OF SCOPE Token inquiry or token-state check before payment · CYBS network token webhooks and lifecycle updates · returning gateway tokens to merchants, or accepting them · MPGS API upgrade (version 80 is enough) · creating the instrument identifier inside the payment ( actionList: [\"TOKEN_CREATE\"] ) · CYBS customer and payment instrument tokens."],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Visual page","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html","visual page Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side Pick a gateway, a card, the switches and the saved card's state. The sequence shows each call between the merchant, PGW, the gateway and the scheme, what changes for PGW, and how the payment goes to the scheme: with the network token or with the card number. Illustrative Reflects PGW dev stories v1.2 · 25-Nov-2026 Story map Rules for every story Terms. Geidea token = the tokenId the merchant holds. Gateway token = MPGS token (MPGS) or instrument identifier, II (CyberSource). Network token = the token Visa (VTS) or Mastercard (MDES) issues for the card; the gateway sends it with a cryptogram. PGW never sees the full network token or the cryptogram. CIT = customer-initiated, MIT = merchant-initiated payment with a saved card. All identifiers are dummy values. Token layers explains the layers."],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Visual page","Try a case","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try-a-case","try MPGS: token CIT, Visa MPGS: token MIT, Mastercard MPGS: first payment, save card MPGS: network token not provisioned yet MPGS: PGW toggle OFF MPGS: MID without NT set-up MPGS: refund of a token CIT CYBS: first payment, save card CYBS: token CIT, Mastercard CYBS: token MIT, Visa CYBS: card saved before go-live CYBS: instrument identifier not created CYBS: PGW toggle OFF CYBS: MID without token permission CYBS: refund of a token CIT"],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Visual page","Settings","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#h-set","Gateway MPGS CyberSource Card Visa Mastercard Payment First payment, save card Token CIT Token MIT Refund of a token CIT PGW: Generate and Use Network Token ON OFF MPGS: network tokens set up for the MID (P1) Yes No Saved card on MPGS Network token provisioned Not provisioned yet MPGS: the gateway set-up decides if the network token is used. The PGW toggle decides only what PGW records. Rule 1"],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Visual page","Result","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#h-out","MPGS · Visa went to the scheme with Network token (VTS) fee exposure · PGW record No Visa PAN Integrity Fee PGW records it"],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Visual page","Sequence","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#h-seq","click a step for the payload Merchant / HPP Geidea PGW MPGS TEST-MPGS-01 Scheme VTS / MDES Token CIT: the customer pays with the saved card 1 POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token , initiatedBy = Payer (or HPP with tokenId ) NO CHANGE The merchant sends only the Geidea token: { \"tokenId\": \"0a1b2c3d-4e5f-6071-8293-a4b5c6d7e8f9\", \"initiatedBy\": \"Payer\" } 3DS runs as today. No change. 2 Pay with the MPGS token: sourceOfFunds.token = 0A1B2C3D4E5F60718293A4B5C6D7E8F9 NO CHANGE NT-M3 No change to the request. The MPGS token is the Geidea tokenId without dashes, in upper case (merchant-supplied token). { \"apiOperation\": \"PAY\", \"sourceOfFunds\": { \"token\": \"0A1B2C3D4E5F60718293A4B5C6D7E8F9\" } } 3 Authorisation with the VTS network token and a cryptogram GATEWAY The gateway swaps the gateway token for the network token and gets a cryptogram from VTS. PGW does not see either. Proven through PGW on 16–17 Nov for Visa and Mastercard CIT, MIT and 3DS on the network token. 4 Approved NO CHANGE 5 Payment response: sourceOfFunds.type = SCHEME_TOKEN NO CHANGE Response excerpt. Highlighted: the fields PGW reads. { \"result\": \"SUCCESS\", \"response\": { \"gatewayCode\": \"APPROVED\", \"acquirerCode\": \"00\" }, \"sourceOfFunds\": { \"type\": \"SCHEME_TOKEN\" , \"token\": \"0A1B2C3D4E5F60718293A4B5C6D7E8F9\", \"provided\": { \"card\": { \"brand\": \"VISA\" , \"deviceSpecificNumber\": \"489537xxxxxx0001\" , \"deviceSpecificExpiry\": { \"month\": \"11\", \"year\": \"28\" } , \"paymentAccountReference\": \"V0010000000000000000000000001\" , \"storedOnFile\": \"STORED\" } } } } 6 Fill transactions[].networkTokenDetails (approved and declined) NEW NT-M3 7 First network token seen: set schemeTokenProvider = VTS on the Geidea token (only when it is null ) NEW NT-M3 8 Response, callback and order inquiry carry networkTokenDetails NEW NT-0 NO CHANGE today's behaviour CHANGED an existing call changes NEW build needed GATEWAY done by the gateway ASYNC in the background SKIPPED not done in this case"],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Visual page","What PGW records","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#h-rec","Field-by-field mapping transactions[].networkTokenDetails (returned to the merchant) { \"isNetworkToken\": true, \"tokenProvider\": \"VTS\", \"maskedTokenNumber\": \"489537xxxxxx0001\", \"tokenExpiryMonth\": \"11\", \"tokenExpiryYear\": \"28\", \"paymentAccountReference\": \"V0010000000000000000000000001\" } Geidea token record (internal, not returned) Field Before After schemeTokenProvider null VTS tokenStatus ACTIVE ACTIVE tokenStatus is added now; nothing reads or changes it in this release (NT-X). Green rows changed in this case."],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: token CIT, Visa","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-cit",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: token MIT, Mastercard","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-mit",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: first payment, save card","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-save",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: network token not provisioned yet","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-pending",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: PGW toggle OFF","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-toggle-off",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: MID without NT set-up","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-no-nt",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: MPGS: refund of a token CIT","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=mpgs-refund",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: first payment, save card","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-save",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: token CIT, Mastercard","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-cit",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: token MIT, Visa","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-mit",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: card saved before go-live","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-old-card",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: instrument identifier not created","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-ii-fail",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: PGW toggle OFF","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-toggle-off",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: MID without token permission","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-no-permission",""],["Network Tokenization","Payment explorer: MPGS and CyberSource side by side","Scenario","Try it: CYBS: refund of a token CIT","network-tokenization/payment-explorer.html#try=cybs-refund",""],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used","Visual page","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html","visual page Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used Two switches decide the outcome: one in PGW, one at the gateway. They do different things on MPGS and on CyberSource. The PGW switch is a safety switch for the rollout, per MID, default OFF. Illustrative Reflects PGW dev stories v1.2 · 25-Nov-2026 Rule 1: master toggle Prerequisites P1–P3"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used","Visual page","The switches","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#the-switches","PGW configuration · per MID · default OFF"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › The switches","Visual page","Generate and Use Network Token","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#generate-and-use-network-token","The one master toggle for all NT work (Rule 1). Exists today; v1.1 gives it this meaning. A later release can remove it. PGW configuration · existing, unchanged"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › The switches","Visual page","Enable tokenization, CIT, MIT","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#enable-tokenization-cit-mit","Enable tokenization , CUSTOMER_INITIATED_TRANSACTIONS , MERCHANT_INITIATED_TRANSACTIONS still apply as today. MPGS · per MID and token repository (P1)"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › The switches","Visual page","Network tokens on MPGS","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#network-tokens-on-mpgs","MDES onboarding, Visa VTS via an existing TRID, Enable Network Tokens per merchant. Set by the merchant integration team. CYBS · per MID (P2)"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › The switches","Visual page","Token permission on CYBS","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#token-permission-on-cybs","Network token provisioning on the vault; Visa and Mastercard token permission per MID. PGW configuration · not used"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › The switches","Visual page","Use Network Token, Send NT to merchant","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#use-network-token-send-nt-to-merchant","Exist from an earlier project. Not used in this release."],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used","Visual page","Who decides: click a row","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#who-decides",""],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › Who decides: click a row","Visual page","MPGS","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#mpgs-matrix","token CIT or MIT PGW toggle MPGS set-up (P1) Payment goes with PGW records ON Yes Network token Yes OFF Yes Network token null ON No Card number Yes OFF No Card number null The target state. MPGS already pays with the network token; PGW records it (NT-M3, NT-M4). Try it"],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › Who decides: click a row","Visual page","CyberSource","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#cybs-matrix","token CIT or MIT PGW toggle Token permission (P2) Payment goes with PGW records ON Yes Network token Yes OFF Yes Card number null ON No Expected card (not verified) Yes OFF No Card number null The target state. PGW creates the instrument identifier and pays with it; CYBS uses the network token (NT-C1, NT-C3, NT-C4). Try it Both tables assume the existing tokenization, CIT and MIT toggles are ON. See a case in the Payment explorer ."],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used","Visual page","Rollout","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#rollout","Release with the toggle OFF on every MID. PGW behaves exactly as today: no instrument identifiers, CYBS CIT and MIT with the card number, networkTokenDetails = null . Switch ON for the production test MID and test in production. Without the toggle, a fault would hit every card-on-file merchant at release and the only fix would be a code rollback. Switch ON per card-on-file MID. The MID list is reviewed with Business and Risk & Compliance first. Gateway set-up (P1, P2) must be in place for the MID. New card-on-file merchants: network tokens are mandatory. Agreed in the 19-Nov mandate alignment meeting. A later release removes the toggle. Network tokens then apply wherever save card, CIT or MIT is enabled."],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used","Visual page","Partners that connect to the gateway directly","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#partners",""],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › Partners that connect to the gateway directly","Visual page","On Geidea's MSO","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#on-geidea-s-mso","Geidea switches network tokens on for their MIDs at the gateway, sends an advisory, and spot-checks their CIT and MIT. The PGW toggle does not apply: these partners do not pay through PGW."],["Network Tokenization","Switches and rollout: who decides if a network token is used › Partners that connect to the gateway directly","Visual page","On their own MSO","network-tokenization/switches-and-rollout.html#on-their-own-mso","Risk & Compliance sends an advisory. The partner generates and uses network tokens on its own set-up."],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what","Visual page","Token layers: who holds what","network-tokenization/token-layers.html","visual page Token layers: who holds what One saved card has up to four identities. The merchant only ever holds the Geidea token. The scheme fee depends on the last layer: did the gateway send a network token to Visa, or the card number? Illustrative Reflects PGW dev stories v1.2 · 25-Nov-2026 NT-0 NT-C1"],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what","Visual page","The four layers of one saved card","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#the-four-layers","Gateway MPGS CyberSource Card Visa Mastercard See it as All layers Merchant Geidea PGW Gateway Scheme and issuer Card number PAN 411111xxxxxx1111 Held by the Geidea vault (PGW) and the gateway. Sent to the gateway on card-number payments. PGW maps it to Geidea token tokenId 0a1b2c3d-4e5f-6071-8293-a4b5c6d7e8f9 Held by PGW and the merchant. The only token the merchant sends. One per card per merchant and gateway. PGW pays MPGS with MPGS token gateway token · exists today 0A1B2C3D4E5F60718293A4B5C6D7E8F9 Created by PGW at save card ( SendToken ): the Geidea tokenId without dashes, upper case. Stored by MPGS. the gateway swaps it for Network token VTS · plus a cryptogram per payment 489537xxxxxx0001 Issued by Visa (VTS), held by the gateway. PGW sees only the masked number and expiry in the payment response. Green edge: the layer that avoids the Visa fee. Red edge: the layer the mandate wants out of the payment. Values are dummy; the network token number is shown as PGW records it (masked)."],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what","Visual page","How the layers are created","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#how-the-layers-are-created","1 · save card"],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what › How the layers are created","Visual page","Card saved","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#timeline","The customer pays with the card number and ticks save card. PGW creates the Geidea token. No change. 2 · save card"],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what › How the layers are created","Visual page","MPGS token","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#timeline","PGW sends SendToken with its own token value. No change. New: PGW reads schemeToken.provider (NT-M1). 3 · async, 20–30 s in tests"],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what › How the layers are created","Visual page","Network token","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#timeline","With network tokens set up for the MID (P1), MPGS asks VTS for a network token. Existing MPGS tokens get one automatically. 4 · each CIT / MIT"],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what › How the layers are created","Visual page","Payment","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#timeline","PGW pays with the MPGS token, as today. MPGS sends the network token and cryptogram. New: PGW records it (NT-M3, NT-M4)."],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what","Visual page","PAR: one value per card","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#par-one-value-per-card","The Payment Account Reference (PAR) is the same for the card number and every network token of that card. It lets Geidea link payments made with the card number and with a network token to one card, without storing the card number. PGW copies it to paymentAccountReference on every transaction where the gateway returns it, on card-number payments too (NT-M2, NT-C2). Dummy PARs on these pages: Visa V0010000000000000000000000001 , Mastercard 5001TESTPAR000000000000000002 ."],["Network Tokenization","Token layers: who holds what","Visual page","An analogy","network-tokenization/token-layers.html#an-analogy","Card number The house key. Whoever copies it can open the door anywhere. Geidea token The ticket the merchant gets at the key desk. Useless outside Geidea. Gateway token The gateway's locker number for the key. MPGS uses Geidea's ticket number as the locker number; CyberSource gives its own number (the instrument identifier). Network token A copy of the key, cut by Visa or Mastercard, that only opens this door for this merchant. Each use needs a one-time code (the cryptogram). Visa charges a fee when the house key is used instead."],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Visual page","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html","visual page What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails Both gateways fill the same six-field object, from different response fields. Pick a response and follow each field through its rule. The object is written once per transaction and returned to the merchant. Illustrative Reflects PGW dev stories v1.2 · 25-Nov-2026 NT-0 NT-M3 mapping NT-C3 mapping"],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Visual page","Mapper","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#mapper","try MPGS token CIT, Visa MPGS token MIT, Mastercard MPGS Pay with card number MPGS response without sourceOfFunds CYBS token CIT, Mastercard (014) CYBS token MIT, Visa (015) CYBS Pay with card number"],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails › Mapper","Visual page","Gateway response","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#h-resp","MPGS v80 · excerpt { \"result\": \"SUCCESS\", \"response\": { \"gatewayCode\": \"APPROVED\", \"acquirerCode\": \"00\" }, \"sourceOfFunds\": { \"type\": \"SCHEME_TOKEN\" , \"token\": \"0A1B2C3D4E5F60718293A4B5C6D7E8F9\", \"provided\": { \"card\": { \"brand\": \"VISA\" , \"deviceSpecificNumber\": \"489537xxxxxx0001\" , \"deviceSpecificExpiry\": { \"month\": \"11\" , \"year\": \"28\" } , \"paymentAccountReference\": \"V0010000000000000000000000001\" , \"storedOnFile\": \"STORED\" } } } }"],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails › Mapper","Visual page","Rules","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#rules-2","NT-M3 Source value Rule PGW field \"SCHEME_TOKEN\" true if SCHEME_TOKEN, else false isNetworkToken true \"VISA\" if network token: VISA → VTS, MASTERCARD → MDES; else null tokenProvider \"VTS\" \"489537xxxxxx0001\" copy; null if absent maskedTokenNumber \"489537xxxxxx0001\" \"11\" copy; null if absent tokenExpiryMonth \"11\" \"28\" copy; null if absent tokenExpiryYear \"28\" \"V0010000000000000000000000001\" copy; null if absent paymentAccountReference \"V0010000000000000000000000001\""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails › Mapper","Visual page","PGW transaction","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#pgw-transaction","\"networkTokenDetails\": { \"isNetworkToken\": true, \"tokenProvider\": \"VTS\", \"maskedTokenNumber\": \"489537xxxxxx0001\", \"tokenExpiryMonth\": \"11\", \"tokenExpiryYear\": \"28\", \"paymentAccountReference\": \"V0010000000000000000000000001\" } If the Geidea token has schemeTokenProvider = null , PGW sets it to VTS on a token CIT or MIT."],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Visual page","The object","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#the-object","Field Type Values MPGS source CYBS source isNetworkToken Boolean true / false sourceOfFunds.type = SCHEME_TOKEN processingInformation.paymentSolution = 015 or 014 tokenProvider String VTS / MDES / null …card.brand : VISA → VTS , MASTERCARD → MDES 015 → VTS , 014 → MDES maskedTokenNumber String masked / null …card.deviceSpecificNumber , copied paymentInformation.card.bin + 10 zeros tokenExpiryMonth String e.g. 11 / null …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.month not returned: null tokenExpiryYear String e.g. 28 / null …card.deviceSpecificExpiry.year not returned: null paymentAccountReference String PAR / null …card.paymentAccountReference processorInformation.paymentAccountReferenceNumber …card = sourceOfFunds.provided.card . MPGS REST API version 80. NT-0 requirement 1"],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Visual page","Which transactions carry it","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#which-transactions-carry-it","Transaction Object MPGS CYBS Pay, Authorization (card number) yes from the response (NT-M2) from the response (NT-C2) Pay, Authorization (token CIT, MIT) yes from the response (NT-M3, NT-M4) from the response (NT-C3, NT-C4) Capture, Refund, Void yes from the response; no sourceOfFunds : copy from the original always copied from the original Authentication no never carries the object Before this release, or toggle OFF null NT-0 2.4 and criterion 5 Approved and declined transactions both get the object. Written once; never updated."],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Visual page","Where the merchant sees it","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#where-the-merchant-sees-it","Merchant API Path Order inquiry GET /pgw/api/v1/direct/order/{orderId} order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails Callback to callbackUrl order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay response order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails POST /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token response order.transactions[].networkTokenDetails An addition only: existing fields do not change. The token record fields below are not returned."],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Visual page","The Geidea token record (internal)","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#the-geidea-token-record","Field Values Default Written by Update rule schemeTokenProvider VTS / MDES / null null NT-M1 at token creation; NT-M3, NT-M4, NT-C3, NT-C4 at the first network token payment Once set, never changed cybsInstrumentIdentifierId 19 digits / null null NT-C1 Replaced if CYBS returns a different id (logged) networkTokenState e.g. ACTIVE / null null NT-C1 ( tokenizedCard.state ) With the identifier tokenStatus ACTIVE / INACTIVE ACTIVE nothing in this release For NT-X ( not yet defined ) tokenStatusReason , tokenStatusDate text, UAE time null nothing in this release For NT-X NT-0 requirements 4 and 5"],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: MPGS token CIT, Visa","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=mpgs-cit-visa",""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: MPGS token MIT, Mastercard","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=mpgs-mit-mc",""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: MPGS Pay with card number","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=mpgs-card",""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: MPGS response without sourceOfFunds","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=mpgs-no-sof",""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: CYBS token CIT, Mastercard (014)","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=cybs-cit-mc",""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: CYBS token MIT, Visa (015)","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=cybs-mit-visa",""],["Network Tokenization","What PGW records: gateway response to networkTokenDetails","Scenario","Try it: CYBS Pay with card number","network-tokenization/what-pgw-records.html#try=cybs-card",""],["Split Payout","Advice lifecycle","Visual page","Advice lifecycle","split-payout/advice-lifecycle.html","visual · split payout Advice lifecycle A split advice tells APEX how to pay one flagged transaction. This page follows an advice from the moment it is sent to the moment the money moves: its statuses at APEX, what PGW records, and what happens to the money each day. Illustrative Reflects product doc v1.6 (6.3–6.5, 7.3, 7.5) and PGW stories v0.6 (SP-7 to SP-9) Read the requirement → End-to-end view → Stories: SP-7 → SP-8 → SP-9 → Terms: partner = the merchant (MID) with split payout on; vendor = a payee of the split. Action A creates an advice at APEX, U replaces it. PGW sends A in real time for a split sent with the order or a Payout Advice API NEW. An UPDATE is not sent in real time: the day-end file carries it and APEX overwrites the earlier advice (agreed with APEX on 23-Nov; stories v0.6 and doc v1.6 still describe a real-time U). APEX advice fields and statuses are drafts until the Emcrey spec (P3)."],["Split Payout","Advice lifecycle","Visual page","The statuses at APEX","split-payout/advice-lifecycle.html#statuses","NOT FINAL · TRANSACTION HELD · UPDATE VIA DAY-END FILE FINAL · NO FURTHER UPDATES A, checks pass A before the transaction A, a structural check fails transaction arrives unmatched at day end amount check fails U in day-end file valid, paid on P P + N days, no valid advice no advice transaction held PENDING_MATCH before the transaction CREATED awaiting validation REJECTED reason code recorded PROCESSED paid out RELEASED after N days"],["Split Payout","Advice lifecycle › The statuses at APEX","Visual page","CREATED","split-payout/advice-lifecycle.html#sd","Received and passed structural checks; awaiting amount validation and payout. Money Held until the payout. Partner can UPDATE with the latest advice ID, on the transaction date only; it reaches APEX in the day-end file and overwrites this advice. PGW shows ACCEPTED (from the real-time response). Source: product doc 6.5 Source: product doc 6.5 Advice statuses · 6.4 · 6.3"],["Split Payout","Advice lifecycle","Visual page","Day by day","split-payout/advice-lifecycle.html#days","Split with the order Deferred split Rejected, then corrected Advice before the transaction Released after N days APEX not reachable The partner sends the legs in Create Session. PGW sends the advice the moment the payment succeeds. When Partner PGW APEX APEX status PGW record Money Day 0 Creates a session with the legs; the customer pays. Checks the split (SP-6). Federates the transaction with splitPayout = Y. Sends advice A in real time (SP-7). Holds the transaction. Structural checks pass. CREATED ACCEPTED held Day 0, after midnight — Day-end file carries advice A (SP-9). File matches the real-time advice: no action. CREATED included in file held Before P — — Amount validation: Rule 1, Rule 2 and single payout pass. CREATED — held P Sees the result in the split payout report. — Pays each vendor, settles the residual. PROCESSED — paid out ← Previous Next → Show all Step 1 of 4 Days are illustrative. Day 0 = payment day. P = the transaction's scheduled payout date under the MID's payout schedule. N = days set on the partner MID (can be 0)."],["Split Payout","Advice lifecycle","Visual page","What the partner can still do","split-payout/advice-lifecycle.html#what-the-partner-can-still-do","APEX status Payout Advice API NEW Payout Advice API UPDATE Update in the day-end file Money no advice Yes , if the order was created with deferredSplit — — held PENDING_MATCH No : a split exists Yes , with the latest advice ID, on the transaction date only Overwrites the advice held CREATED No Yes , with the latest advice ID, on the transaction date only Overwrites the advice held until payout REJECTED No Yes , on the transaction date only: this is how a rejection is corrected Overwrites the advice held PROCESSED No PGW stores it; APEX refuses it Refused : final paid out RELEASED No PGW stores it; APEX refuses it Refused : final released to the partner Source: product doc 7.3 · 6.4 · SP-8 section 2 . Rejections at each step: What can go wrong . The day-end file route for updates was agreed with APEX on 23-Nov; stories v0.6 and doc v1.6 still describe a real-time U. An UPDATE is accepted on the transaction date only (rule of 24-Nov); one sent after that day’s file is rejected."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","1. Introduction","split-payout/docs/product.html","v1.6 10-Nov-2026 Source: Vendor Payout Service - MVP v1.6 (10-Nov-2026).docx Product and implementation requirements · Ecomm (PGW) · Version 1.6 · 10-Nov-2026 1. Introduction The Vendor Payout Service lets a partner (a Geidea merchant) instruct Geidea to pay part of each sale to its own vendors (suppliers). The customer pays once, on the partner’s MID, and the transaction is authorized and cleared with the scheme exactly as today. After settlement, Geidea transfers each vendor’s share to the vendor’s own UAE bank account under Geidea’s domestic fund transfer service and settles the remainder to the partner. This is the working requirements document for implementation. It carries the approved product design and sets out the work items for PGW and APEX, and is kept current as solutioning progresses with the APEX team and its platform vendor."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","Scope","split-payout/docs/product.html#scope","Channel: Ecomm through PGW only. Split modes: Dynamic, Fixed and Profile based. Currency: AED only. The partner and its vendors are paid in AED. Vendors: UAE businesses holding a business registration (trade license). Payouts are domestic only (UAE IBANs, no cross-border). Partners: select large partners, approved case by case. Unlicensed aggregators are excluded. Expected volume: 10 to 20 partners a year."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","Terms used in this document","split-payout/docs/product.html#terms-used-in-this-document","Term Meaning Partner The Geidea merchant that sells to the customer and instructs Geidea how to split the proceeds. Holds a normal Geidea MID. Vendor / beneficiary A business the partner pays out to. Defined in APEX as a vendor definition, not a MID. Beneficiary ID Unique ID APEX generates for each vendor definition. The only vendor identifier the partner and PGW use. Split advice The instruction for one transaction: which beneficiaries receive how much. Split flag Marker PGW sets on a transaction in its federation to APEX when a split advice is expected. APEX holds only flagged transactions. Deferred split Order created without split detail, where the partner declares (deferredSplit) that it will send the advice later through the Payout Advice API. Residual What remains of the partner’s settlement after vendor payouts and remittance fees; settled to the partner. Remittance fee Fee charged per payout (each vendor transfer and the partner’s own payout). N Days, set per partner MID, after which a flagged transaction without a valid advice is released to the partner. EOD split advice file Day-end file from PGW to APEX carrying the day’s advices; backup and update channel to the real-time API. PENDING_MATCH Status of an advice APEX received before the transaction itself reached APEX."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","2. Use Cases","split-payout/docs/product.html#use-cases","View it visually Use case Example Typical mode Marketplace A customer buys from several sellers in one checkout; each seller receives its share. Dynamic Delivery platform An order payment is shared between the restaurant or store and the platform. Dynamic Aggregator with standard terms An insurance aggregator applies a different, pre-agreed split per insurance provider. Profile Standing revenue share A business always passes the same share of every sale to a supplier. Fixed"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","3. Solution Overview","split-payout/docs/product.html#solution-overview","The solution has two parts. The operational setup is done once per partner and per vendor, by people, through agreed operational routes. The transaction and payout flow runs on every transaction, entirely in Geidea systems. Figure 1 shows both end to end. Section 5 describes the operational setup, and sections 6 and 7 what APEX and PGW need to deliver."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 3. Solution Overview","Document","3.1 Solution at a glance","split-payout/docs/product.html#solution-at-a-glance","End-to-end view Figure 1. From partner setup to vendor payout"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 3. Solution Overview","Document","3.2 Split modes","split-payout/docs/product.html#split-modes","View it visually Each partner MID uses one split mode. Mode How the split is defined What the partner sends Dynamic The partner sends the split with each transaction. Vendor legs in the order creation request, or later through the Payout Advice API (deferred split). Fixed One split template, configured by Geidea in PGW, applies to every transaction. Nothing. PGW applies the template to every order. Profile Geidea configures a catalog of split templates in PGW, each with a profile ID. A profile ID in the order creation request, or later through the Payout Advice API (deferred split)."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 3.2 Split modes","Document","Rules common to all modes","split-payout/docs/product.html#rules-common-to-all-modes","A leg names a beneficiary ID and a value. The value is either an amount or a percentage of the gross transaction amount , never both. All legs of one advice use the same split type (all amounts or all percentages). Percentages are converted to amounts by APEX, not by PGW. Each amount is rounded to 2 decimals; the partner’s residual absorbs the rounding difference. Templates and profiles are configured by Geidea only. The partner has no access to split configuration. A split advice carries beneficiary IDs and values only. Bank details always come from Geidea’s records, never from the partner’s request."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 3. Solution Overview","Document","3.3 Design principles","split-payout/docs/product.html#design-principles","Geidea controls who is paid. Vendors are verified and defined by Geidea. A partner can only pay vendors defined against its own MID. A vendor is not a merchant. Vendors are vendor definitions in APEX, not MIDs. They have no products, channels, users or portal access. Payouts never exceed the partner’s net. The partner’s residual can never go negative and no vendor leg can be zeroed by commission or fees. All or nothing. An advice is processed in full or rejected in full. A partial split is never paid. Fail safe, not fail open. PGW flags every split transaction in its federation to APEX. APEX holds flagged transactions until a valid advice is processed, so a missing or failed advice delays the partner’s settlement rather than paying vendor money to the partner. Real-time advice, settlement-time payout. Split advices reach APEX in real time through an API, with a day-end file as backup and update channel. Payouts still run with settlement. Fully automated. Vendor payouts and the partner’s residual run without any manual finance step. The scheme transaction is unchanged. Authorization and clearing work as today; federation to APEX only gains the split flag. Risk approves every partner. The service is never enabled by default."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","4. Money Flow","split-payout/docs/product.html#money-flow","View it visually"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 4. Money Flow","Document","4.1 How funds move","split-payout/docs/product.html#how-funds-move","APEX calculates the partner’s settlement for the transaction and deducts Geidea’s MDR and VAT on the MDR. The remainder is the partner’s net. APEX makes the vendor payouts from the partner’s net. Whether a separate staging account is used, or APEX posts the entries directly from the merchant payout account, is to be defined by Finance. For a transaction with a valid split advice, APEX calculates each vendor’s payout: split amount (converted from a percentage of the gross transaction amount where needed), minus the commission configured on the vendor definition, if any, minus the remittance fee when the partner MID is set to deduct it from vendor payouts, rounded to 2 decimals. A remittance fee is charged for each payout: every vendor transfer and the partner’s own payout of its residual. It is configured on the partner MID, with any applicable VAT. For vendor transfers, an option on the partner MID sets who bears the fee: the partner (deducted from its residual) or each vendor (deducted from that vendor’s payout). The fee on the partner’s own payout is always deducted from its residual. Commission deducted from a vendor’s leg stays with the partner and reaches it in its residual. APEX transfers each vendor’s net payout to its IBAN and settles the residual to the partner. Geidea’s revenue on the transaction is its MDR. Geidea invoices the partner VAT on its MDR for the full transaction amount, however the transaction is split; any invoicing between the partner and its vendors is the partner’s own matter. The movement of funds between accounts, its accounting, and VAT handling on vendor commission and remittance fees are to be defined by Finance."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 4. Money Flow","Document","4.2 Worked example","split-payout/docs/product.html#worked-example","View it visually Transaction AED 1,000. Geidea MDR 1% (VAT on MDR left out for simplicity). An amount split across three vendors; two have a commission configured. A remittance fee of AED 1.00 per payout (illustrative) is set on the partner MID. The transaction ends in four payouts: three to the vendors and one to the partner."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 4.2 Worked example","Document","Option 1 – partner bears the transfer cost of all payouts","split-payout/docs/product.html#option-1-partner-bears-the-transfer-cost-of-all-payouts","Vendor Split amount Commission Commission amount Net payout Vendor A 500.00 2.00% 10.00 490.00 Vendor B 300.00 1.50% 4.50 295.50 Vendor C 100.00 None 0.00 100.00 Total 900.00 14.50 885.50 Partner settlement AED Transaction amount less Geidea MDR (1,000.00 − 10.00) 990.00 Less: net payouts to vendors (payouts 1 to 3) 885.50 Less: remittance fees (4 payouts × 1.00) 4.00 Residual paid to the partner (payout 4: 90.00 unallocated after MDR + 14.50 commission − 4.00 remittance fees) 100.50"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 4.2 Worked example","Document","Option 2 – each vendor bears its own transfer cost","split-payout/docs/product.html#option-2-each-vendor-bears-its-own-transfer-cost","The same transaction, with the partner MID set to deduct the remittance fee from each vendor’s payout. The fee on the partner’s own payout is still charged to the partner. Vendor Split amount Commission amount Remittance fee Net payout Vendor A 500.00 10.00 1.00 489.00 Vendor B 300.00 4.50 1.00 294.50 Vendor C 100.00 0.00 1.00 99.00 Total 900.00 14.50 3.00 882.50 Partner settlement AED Transaction amount less Geidea MDR (1,000.00 − 10.00) 990.00 Less: net payouts to vendors (payouts 1 to 3) 882.50 Less: remittance fees deducted from vendor payouts, to Geidea (3 × 1.00) 3.00 Less: remittance fee on the partner’s payout (1 × 1.00) 1.00 Residual paid to the partner (payout 4: 90.00 unallocated after MDR + 14.50 commission − 1.00 remittance fee) 103.50"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 4. Money Flow","Document","4.3 Outcomes","split-payout/docs/product.html#outcomes","Money flow: outcomes What can go wrong: after payout Situation What happens Transaction not flagged for split Settles to the partner through the normal process, after Geidea’s MDR. Flagged, valid advice processed Vendors receive their net payouts; the partner receives the residual. Flagged, advice rejected Nothing is paid to vendors; the transaction stays held. The rejection and its reason appear in the split payout report. The partner may send a corrected, complete advice through the Payout Advice API. Flagged, no advice received The transaction stays held (for example, a deferred split the partner never sent, or an advice lost in transit and not in the day-end file). Flagged, no valid advice within N days Released to the partner after Geidea’s MDR, and listed in an exception report for Operations and Finance. N is set per partner MID, can be zero, and counts from the transaction’s scheduled payout date. Advice received before the transaction Parked as PENDING_MATCH and linked when the transaction arrives; rejected with a reason if still unmatched at day end. Advice in API but missing in the day-end file Marked as mismatched; settlement held until resolved. Transfer to a vendor fails Existing exception process: retry up to the configured limit, then an exception record, with the case shown in the split payout report. Geidea contacts the partner only, never the vendor."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","5. Partner and Vendor Enablement (Operational)","split-payout/docs/product.html#partner-and-vendor-enablement-operational","View it visually Partner and vendor setup is operational. It is handled by the teams below through agreed operational routes (for example, an email from Business carrying the vendor details), and is detailed in the Operations SOP. No system build is needed for these steps in the MVP."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 5. Partner and Vendor Enablement (Operational)","Document","5.1 Partner eligibility and agreement","split-payout/docs/product.html#partner-eligibility-and-agreement","The service is offered only to select large partners, case by case. Unlicensed aggregators are excluded. Business presents each partner request and its business case to Risk. Risk reviews and approves the partner before any onboarding; the service is never enabled by default. The partner agreement is revisited by Business with Legal so that it covers the payout service and its liabilities: dispute and chargeback liability, refunds and chargebacks deducted from the partner, recovery of any shortfall, and terms related to domestic fund transfer processing."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 5. Partner and Vendor Enablement (Operational)","Document","5.2 Setup steps","split-payout/docs/product.html#setup-steps","View it visually # Step Done by Result 1 Request the service and share the vendors’ details and documents (business registration, relationship or contract with the partner, IBAN), the split configuration and any vendor commission. Partner Request with Business 2 Present the partner request and its business case to Risk. Business Request with Risk 3 Review and approve the partner and its business case. Risk Partner approved 4 Onboard each vendor once: collect and check the due diligence documents (business registration, IBAN in the business’s name, agreement or contract with the partner) and keep them on record; screen the beneficiary. Screening hits go to Risk under the existing process. Operations Vendors verified 5 Create the vendor definitions, linked to the partner MID with IBAN and any commission. Enable split payout on the partner MID with remittance fee, fee-bearer option and N. Share the beneficiary IDs with the PGW integration team. APEX team Beneficiary IDs 6 Enable split payout on the partner MID and configure the split mode, template or profiles, and beneficiary IDs (section 7.1). PGW integration team Partner configured 7 Share the beneficiary IDs and the API specs with the partner and support its integration. PGW integration team Partner integrated 8 Go live. Partner Live Due diligence and screening are done once, when a vendor is onboarded, not on every payout. The documents are kept on record to support each payout (for example, under the Central Bank travel rule). New vendors, changes to a vendor’s details or commission, and deactivation follow the same operational route. Vendors have no portal access; Geidea communicates with the partner only."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","6. APEX","split-payout/docs/product.html#apex","This section sets out what APEX needs to deliver. How each item is built, including the API and file formats, is for the APEX team to define with its platform vendor; structures shown here are the current working proposal."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.1 Vendor definition","split-payout/docs/product.html#vendor-definition","APEX holds a vendor definition for each vendor, identified by a unique beneficiary ID generated by APEX. A vendor definition is not a MID. It holds at least the vendor’s name and IBAN, and is linked to the partner MID, so a partner can only pay its own vendors. A commission (fixed or percentage) can be set at vendor level when the partner requests it. A vendor can be activated and deactivated; a deactivated vendor can no longer be paid. Vendor definitions are created and maintained by the APEX team from the operational setup (section 5)."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.2 Split payout enablement on the partner MID","split-payout/docs/product.html#split-payout-enablement-on-the-partner-mid","Setting Purpose Split payout enabled Identifies a partner MID whose settlement follows the money flow in section 4. Remittance fee Fee per payout (each vendor transfer and the partner’s own payout), with any applicable VAT. Fee bearer for vendor transfers Partner (deducted from residual) or vendor (deducted from each vendor’s payout). The fee on the partner’s own payout is always the partner’s. N (days) Days after which a flagged transaction without a valid advice is released to the partner. Can be zero. The payout cap (%) does not apply to split payout partners. The payout schedule (scheme schedule, matched status or advance payout, as configured on the MID) continues to apply."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.3 Hold of flagged transactions","split-payout/docs/product.html#hold-of-flagged-transactions","Advice lifecycle: released after N days APEX receives the split flag with the transaction in PGW’s federation (real-time Create Transaction API and the day-end transaction file). Unflagged transactions of a split-enabled MID settle to the partner as normal. A flagged transaction is held from payout until a valid advice is PROCESSED for it. If no valid advice is processed within N days of the transaction’s scheduled payout date — whether the advice was never received or was rejected and not corrected — APEX releases the transaction to the partner after Geidea’s MDR, sets the advice status to RELEASED where one exists, and lists the transaction in an exception report for Operations and Finance. The timer runs on the flagged transaction, not on an advice record."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.4 Split advice intake","split-payout/docs/product.html#split-advice-intake","Advice lifecycle APEX receives split advices from PGW through two channels, following the same model as transaction federation today."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6.4 Split advice intake","Document","Real-time Split Advice API","split-payout/docs/product.html#real-time-split-advice-api","PGW sends each advice as soon as it has one: at payment success for a split sent with the order, and on every Payout Advice API call. One request can carry several advices. Working structure (APEX to finalise): <SplitAdvices> <SplitAdvice> <Action>A | U</Action> <!-- A = create, U = update --> <Merchant>partner MID</Merchant> <Transaction_Reference>interSystemID</Transaction_Reference> <Split_Rules> <Split_Rule> <BeneficiaryID>BEN000301</BeneficiaryID> <Percentage></Percentage> <!-- either Percentage ... --> <Amount>600.00</Amount> <!-- ... or Amount, not both --> </Split_Rule> </Split_Rules> </SplitAdvice> </SplitAdvices> PGW / product term APEX structure Partner (split-enabled merchant) <Merchant> MID Beneficiary / beneficiaryId <BeneficiaryID> (APEX may name it PartnerID; “partner” in this document means the merchant) Payout Advice API NEW / UPDATE <Action> A / U interSystemID <Transaction_Reference> Partner residual Settlement to the merchant The API runs the structural checks synchronously and returns them in the response: split enabled on the MID; every beneficiary found, active and linked to the MID; MID matches the transaction; transaction carries the split flag; one of percentage or amount per rule; A only when no advice exists for the transaction; U only when the existing advice is CREATED, REJECTED or PENDING_MATCH. Amount validation runs later (section 6.5)."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6.4 Split advice intake","Document","Transaction not yet on APEX","split-payout/docs/product.html#transaction-not-yet-on-apex","Advice lifecycle: advice before the transaction An advice that arrives before its transaction is not rejected. APEX parks it as PENDING_MATCH and links it when the transaction arrives. If still unmatched at day end, it is rejected with a reason code so the partner sees it in the split payout report."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6.4 Split advice intake","Document","Day-end split advice file","split-payout/docs/product.html#day-end-split-advice-file","Advice lifecycle: APEX not reachable What can go wrong: day-end file PGW sends a day-end file after the Ecomm cut-off (midnight) with every advice of the day, including updates. It is the backup to the API and the channel for updates. APEX reconciles it against the API: Case APEX action Advice in the API and in the file, same split No action. Advice in the file with a different split Overwrite with the file’s split and process. Advice in the file, not received over the API Process as new (covers an API failure). Advice received over the API, missing in the file Mark as mismatched and hold settlement until resolved. No file received Process with what was received over the API. The file carries a trailer with the record count. PGW sends an empty file with a zero count on days with no advices, so APEX can tell “no advices today” from “file not received”. File format to be defined by APEX."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.5 Amount validation","split-payout/docs/product.html#amount-validation","Money flow: amount validation What can go wrong: amount validation Run by APEX as a scheduled process once pricing is available (in-call validation if the platform vendor can support it). Any failure rejects the whole advice with a reason code. Rule Check Rule 1 – partner residual not negative net − Σ advised beneficiary amounts + Σ beneficiary commissions − remittance fees borne by the partner ≥ 0 (net = transaction amount − MDR − VAT on MDR) Rule 2 – every leg above zero advised amount − commission − remittance fee (only when the vendor bears it) > 0, for every beneficiary Single payout A transaction is never paid out twice. “Split ≤ net” is not a sufficient check: it passes advices that leave the partner negative once fees are taken, and rejects valid advices where vendor commission covers the gap. Examples (transaction 1,000, MDR 10, VAT 0.50, net 989.50, fee 1.00 per payout, three beneficiaries at 2% commission): Fee bearer Advised Commission Partner residual Result Partner 400 / 300 / 250 = 950 19.00 989.50 − 950 + 19 − 4 = 54.50 Valid Partner 400 / 300 / 300 = 1,000 20.00 989.50 − 1,000 + 20 − 4 = 5.50 Valid (above net, covered by commission) Partner 400 / 300 / 310 = 1,010 20.20 989.50 − 1,010 + 20.20 − 4 = −4.30 Reject (Rule 1) Vendor 400 / 300 / 250 = 950 19.00 989.50 − 950 + 19 − 1 = 57.50 Valid; vendors receive 391 / 293 / 244 Vendor one leg of 0.80 0.02 — Reject (Rule 2): 0.80 − 0.02 − 1.00 = −0.22 Either one leg of 4.00, fixed commission 5.00 5.00 — Reject (Rule 2)"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6.5 Amount validation","Document","Advice statuses","split-payout/docs/product.html#advice-statuses","Advice lifecycle: statuses Status Meaning CREATED Received and passed structural checks; awaiting amount validation and payout. PENDING_MATCH Received before the transaction reached APEX. REJECTED Failed a check; reason code recorded. Can be corrected with U within N days. PROCESSED Paid out to vendors and residual settled. No further updates. RELEASED N days passed without a valid advice; settled to the partner after MDR. No further updates. Reason codes are shared with PGW and appear in the split payout report so the partner knows what to fix."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.6 Calculation and payout","split-payout/docs/product.html#calculation-and-payout","View it visually Convert percentage splits to amounts against the gross transaction amount; round each to 2 decimals. Calculate each vendor’s commission and net payout, and the partner’s residual (section 4). Rounding differences go to the residual. Make the vendor payouts from the partner’s net as domestic fund transfers to each vendor’s IBAN, deduct remittance fees as set on the partner MID, and settle the residual to the partner, with no manual finance step. Account mechanics as defined by Finance. Handle failed transfers through the existing exception process."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.7 Reporting","split-payout/docs/product.html#reporting","Split payout report to the partner , per transaction: transaction amount, Geidea’s MDR, amount paid to each vendor, commission recovered from each vendor, remittance fees, residual, advice status and any rejection or failure reason. This is the partner’s channel for the outcome of its advices. Payout report to each vendor : amount received against each transaction reference and any remittance fee deducted. FinOps screen in APEX : split advices with filters (partner, status, date) and export. Exception report : transactions released after N days, mismatched advices, failed transfers. Finance and reconciliation reports as required by Finance. Partner and vendor report content comes from Business. Format and delivery to be defined with APEX."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 6. APEX","Document","6.8 APEX work items","split-payout/docs/product.html#apex-work-items","# Work item Section APX-01 Vendor definition with APEX-generated beneficiary ID, name, IBAN, partner MID link, optional commission, activation and deactivation. 6.1 APX-02 Split payout settings on the partner MID: enabled flag, remittance fee, fee-bearer option, N. 6.2 APX-03 Accept the split flag in transaction federation (Create Transaction API and day-end transaction file). 6.3 APX-04 Hold flagged transactions from payout; release after N days from the scheduled payout date; exception report. 6.3 APX-05 Real-time Split Advice API (A/U) with synchronous structural checks and response codes; store advices with status. 6.4 APX-06 PENDING_MATCH parking, matching on transaction arrival, day-end rejection with reason. 6.4 APX-07 Day-end split advice file intake: reconciliation against the API, trailer check, empty-file handling, mismatch hold. 6.4 APX-08 Amount validation: Rule 1, Rule 2, single payout; percentage conversion on the gross amount; rounding. 6.5 APX-09 Advice statuses (CREATED, PENDING_MATCH, REJECTED, PROCESSED, RELEASED) and reason codes. 6.5 APX-10 Payout calculation and execution: commission, remittance fees per fee-bearer option, domestic fund transfers, residual; payout cap not applied; failed-transfer exception handling. 6.6 APX-11 Accounting entries and account mechanics as defined by Finance. 4.1, 6.6 APX-12 Split payout report (partner), vendor payout report, exception report, Finance reports. 6.7 APX-13 FinOps screen with filters and export. 6.7"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","7. PGW (Ecomm)","split-payout/docs/product.html#pgw-ecomm",""],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.1 Configuration","split-payout/docs/product.html#configuration","Set by the PGW integration team per partner MID, from the operational setup in section 5.2. The partner has no access. Setting Purpose Split payout enabled Turns the service on for the MID. Off by default. Split mode Dynamic, Fixed or Profile. Fixed template Fixed mode only: list of legs (beneficiary ID + amount or percentage). Profile catalog Profile mode only: profile IDs, each with its list of legs. Beneficiary IDs The partner’s beneficiary IDs, entered as received from the APEX team. Used to validate the partner’s requests. PGW holds no vendor bank details and does not check vendor status. A beneficiary ID that is wrong or no longer active is rejected by APEX."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.2 Order creation request","split-payout/docs/product.html#order-creation-request","View it visually The split travels in the existing order creation request. The partner never sends bank details, fees or commission. Field Type Mode Description splitPayoutType string Dynamic AMOUNT or PERCENTAGE. Applies to every leg of the order. splitPayoutTransaction array Dynamic One object per vendor leg. splitPayoutTransaction[].beneficiaryId string Dynamic Must be one of the partner’s configured beneficiary IDs. splitPayoutTransaction[].value number Dynamic Amount in order currency (2 decimals) for AMOUNT; percentage of the order amount (up to 2 decimals) for PERCENTAGE. splitPayoutTransaction[].reference string Dynamic Optional. Partner’s own reference for the leg; returned in the split payout report. splitPayoutTransaction[].description string Dynamic Optional. Free text. splitPayoutProfileId string Profile ID of a profile in the partner’s configured profile catalog. deferredSplit boolean Dynamic, Profile Optional. true when the partner will send the split later through the Payout Advice API. Must not be sent together with split detail. PGW also keeps a derived order attribute, splitPayout (boolean), set when the order carries split detail or a profile ID, or is a Fixed-mode order. It is returned on the order and is not a request field. Split detail / profile ID deferredSplit PGW action Split flag to APEX Present (or Fixed mode) false / absent Accept; splitPayout = true Yes Absent true Accept; split expected through the Payout Advice API Yes Absent false / absent Normal order No Present true Reject (ambiguous; use UPDATE to change an inline split) — Sending split fields or deferredSplit for a MID where split payout is not enabled, or split fields that do not match the MID’s mode, rejects the order. An empty splitPayoutTransaction array counts as absent."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7.2 Order creation request","Document","Dynamic mode, amount split","split-payout/docs/product.html#dynamic-mode-amount-split","{ \"merchantId\": \"20100000101\", \"merchantReferenceId\": \"ORD-DEMO-1000\", \"amount\": 1000.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"paymentOperation\": \"Pay\", \"splitPayoutType\": \"AMOUNT\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\": [ { \"beneficiaryId\": \"BEN000301\", \"value\": 600.00, \"reference\": \"SELLER-A\", \"description\": \"Products - Seller A\" }, { \"beneficiaryId\": \"BEN000302\", \"value\": 300.00, \"reference\": \"SELLER-B\", \"description\": \"Products - Seller B\" }, { \"beneficiaryId\": \"BEN000303\", \"value\": 100.00, \"reference\": \"DELIVERY-CO\", \"description\": \"Delivery fee\" } ] }"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7.2 Order creation request","Document","Dynamic mode, percentage split (of the gross order amount)","split-payout/docs/product.html#dynamic-mode-percentage-split-of-the-gross-order-amount","{ \"merchantId\": \"20100000101\", \"merchantReferenceId\": \"ORD-DEMO-1001\", \"amount\": 1000.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"paymentOperation\": \"Pay\", \"splitPayoutType\": \"PERCENTAGE\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\": [ { \"beneficiaryId\": \"BEN000301\", \"value\": 60.00, \"reference\": \"SELLER-A\" }, { \"beneficiaryId\": \"BEN000302\", \"value\": 30.00, \"reference\": \"SELLER-B\" }, { \"beneficiaryId\": \"BEN000303\", \"value\": 8.00, \"reference\": \"DELIVERY-CO\" } ] } Percentage splits should leave room for Geidea’s MDR and remittance fees: a split totalling 100% fails Rule 1 (section 6.5) unless vendor commission covers them."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7.2 Order creation request","Document","Profile mode","split-payout/docs/product.html#profile-mode","{ \"merchantId\": \"20100000101\", \"merchantReferenceId\": \"POL-DEMO-2001\", \"amount\": 2500.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"paymentOperation\": \"Pay\", \"splitPayoutProfileId\": \"PRF-INSURER-A\" }"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7.2 Order creation request","Document","Deferred split (Dynamic or Profile)","split-payout/docs/product.html#deferred-split-dynamic-or-profile","{ \"merchantId\": \"20100000101\", \"merchantReferenceId\": \"ORD-DEMO-4001\", \"amount\": 750.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"paymentOperation\": \"Pay\", \"deferredSplit\": true } Fixed mode: the partner sends a normal order with no split fields. PGW applies the MID’s fixed template and stores the resulting split against the order."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.3 Payout Advice API","split-payout/docs/product.html#payout-advice-api","End-to-end view: flow B Simulator: Payout Advice API What can go wrong: Payout Advice API A PGW API through which a partner sends a split advice for an existing order, or updates one. Available to Dynamic and Profile MIDs; a Fixed MID gets its split from the template. Endpoint and field naming to be finalised by PGW engineering. Content M/O/C Description Action M NEW or UPDATE. Order reference M The PGW order the advice applies to. Advice ID C Mandatory for UPDATE: the advice ID returned by the latest NEW or UPDATE. Split M Dynamic: splitPayoutType and splitPayoutTransaction as in section 7.2. Profile: splitPayoutProfileId. Always the complete split, never a single leg."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7.3 Payout Advice API","Document","Rules","split-payout/docs/product.html#rules","NEW is accepted only for an order created with deferredSplit = true that has no advice yet. Repeating a NEW with the same content returns the original advice ID, so a retry after a timeout is safe. UPDATE is accepted for any flagged order (split sent at order creation or through NEW) and must carry the latest advice ID, so an older retried request cannot undo a newer correction. APEX accepts it only while the advice is CREATED, REJECTED or PENDING_MATCH; it is refused once PROCESSED or RELEASED. An UPDATE is how a partner corrects a rejected advice. An advice for an order that is not flagged is rejected: the transaction may already have settled to the partner. The order must be a successful sale under the calling partner’s MID. PGW forwards every accepted NEW or UPDATE to the APEX Split Advice API in real time (action A or U). The response returns the advice ID with status accepted, or rejected with a reason code from PGW’s checks or from APEX’s synchronous checks. The final outcome reaches the partner through the split payout report. If APEX cannot be reached, PGW retries; the day-end file carries the advice in any case, and the transaction stays held because it is flagged."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.4 Split flag in federation","split-payout/docs/product.html#split-flag-in-federation","PGW sets the split flag on the transaction it federates to APEX when the order has splitPayout = true or deferredSplit = true. The flag is carried in the real-time Create Transaction API and in the day-end transaction record, on the record APEX settles from, keyed by interSystemID. Field name and position to be agreed with APEX."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.5 Forwarding advices to APEX","split-payout/docs/product.html#forwarding-advices-to-apex","Real time. For a split sent with the order (including Fixed and Profile, resolved into legs), PGW sends action A to the APEX Split Advice API once the payment succeeds. For the Payout Advice API, PGW sends A or U on each accepted call. Day-end file. After the Ecomm cut-off (midnight), PGW sends APEX the split advice file with every advice of the day, including updates, in the latest state held by PGW. The file has a trailer with the record count and is sent every day, with a zero count when there are no advices. Format defined by APEX. PGW keeps each advice’s status: pending, sent to APEX, APEX response (accepted or rejected with reason), included in day-end file."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.6 Validations","split-payout/docs/product.html#validations","What can go wrong PGW validates at order creation, before any payment is taken, and on every Payout Advice API call. Any failure rejects the request with a specific error code (codes to be defined by PGW). Check Order creation Payout Advice API Split payout is enabled for the MID. Yes Yes The request matches the configured mode: legs for Dynamic, a valid profile ID for Profile, no split fields for Fixed. Yes Yes (Fixed MIDs rejected) Split detail and deferredSplit are not sent together. Yes — Every beneficiary ID is one of the partner’s configured beneficiary IDs. Yes Yes All legs use one split type. Yes Yes Amount splits add up to no more than the order amount; percentage splits to no more than 100%. Yes Yes The order exists, is a successful sale and belongs to the calling MID. — Yes The order is flagged for split. — Yes NEW: order created with deferredSplit and no advice exists. UPDATE: advice ID is the latest for the order. — Yes"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.7 Transaction flow","split-payout/docs/product.html#transaction-flow","End-to-end view Simulator The partner creates the order with split detail (Dynamic), a profile ID (Profile), deferredSplit (split to follow), or no split (Fixed, where PGW applies the template). PGW validates the request (section 7.6). If invalid, the order is rejected before any payment is taken. The customer pays. PGW authorizes through the existing route (MPGS or CyberSource), confirms the payment to the partner and federates the transaction to APEX in real time with the split flag where applicable. PGW stores the resolved split against the order and sends it to the APEX Split Advice API in real time. For a deferred split, or to correct an advice, the partner calls the Payout Advice API; PGW validates and forwards it to APEX in real time. After the Ecomm cut-off (midnight), PGW sends the day-end split advice file. APEX holds the flagged transaction, validates the advice, and pays out or releases after N days (section 6)."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction › 7. PGW (Ecomm)","Document","7.8 PGW work items","split-payout/docs/product.html#pgw-work-items","# Work item Section PGW-01 Split payout configuration per partner MID: enabled, mode, Fixed template, Profile catalog, beneficiary IDs. 7.1 PGW-02 Order creation: accept split fields and deferredSplit; derived splitPayout attribute; mode and combination checks. 7.2 PGW-03 Resolve and store the split against the order (Fixed and Profile into legs) with advice ID and status. 7.2, 7.5 PGW-04 Split flag in transaction federation to APEX (real-time API and day-end transaction record). 7.4 PGW-05 Payout Advice API (NEW / UPDATE) with the rules in section 7.3. 7.3 PGW-06 Real-time forwarding of advices to the APEX Split Advice API (A / U), retries, mapping of APEX responses and reason codes. 7.3, 7.5 PGW-07 Day-end split advice file after the midnight cut-off, with trailer, sent every day including empty days. 7.5 PGW-08 Validations in section 7.6 with error codes. 7.6 PGW-09 Merchant API documentation: split fields, deferredSplit, Payout Advice API, error and reason codes. 7.2, 7.3"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","8. Refunds and Chargebacks","split-payout/docs/product.html#refunds-and-chargebacks","What can go wrong: refunds and chargebacks Refunds and chargebacks are debited from the partner’s MID through the normal process. Vendors and their payouts are not involved, and Geidea does not recover amounts from vendors. A chargeback on a transaction whose split has already been paid out is handled the same way as a chargeback on any settled transaction today. Because a partner’s residual can be small, a refund or chargeback may exceed the partner’s settlement on the day. How the shortfall is recovered (for example, from the partner’s later settlements) is to be covered in the partner agreement and confirmed by Finance. New splits should not be held back to recover a partner’s negative balance, as that would stop vendor payments for an amount the partner owes."],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","9. Out of Scope and Later Phases","split-payout/docs/product.html#out-of-scope-and-later-phases","Item Status POS (TMS and MW): split advice for POS transactions. Expected design: MW exposes a split API to the partner, forwards advices to the APEX Split Advice API in real time and generates its own day-end file, as PGW does. Open: how a POS transaction is flagged for hold, as there is no order-creation step. Later phase Merchant Portal: SPLIT tag and filter on Transactions, Payout Split table on transaction detail, split payout view. A split advice inquiry API from APEX is taken up with this view. Later phase MA: APIs on top of APEX for vendor creation and split payout enablement. Later phase Individuals as vendors Out of scope Cross-border payouts Out of scope Vendor portal access or self-service Out of scope Real-time payouts; partial processing of an advice Out of scope"],["Split Payout","1. Introduction","Document","10. Open Items","split-payout/docs/product.html#open-items","# Item Owner 1 Split Advice API specification, day-end advice file format and trailer, field name and position of the split flag in federation. APEX team with Emcrey; PGW 2 Whether amount validation can run in the API call or only as a scheduled process. APEX with Emcrey 3 Reason code list for APEX rejections, shared with PGW for the API response and report. APEX, PGW 4 APEX delivery date for the split scope. APEX team 5 Account mechanics (staging account or direct posting) and accounting entries. Finance 6 VAT on vendor commission and remittance fees; remittance fee amount; whether funds move to a separate account for the remittance fee. Finance 7 Default value of N. Finance, Business 8 Recovery of a refund or chargeback shortfall when the partner’s balance goes negative. Finance, Legal, Business 9 Partner and vendor report content; Finance and reconciliation reports. Business, Finance 10 PGW error codes and Payout Advice API contract review. PGW engineering team"],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","Story map","split-payout/docs/stories.html","v0.6 26-Nov-2026 Source: Vendor Payout Service - PGW Stories v0.6.docx Version 0.6 draft · 26-Nov-2026 · Prepared by Ghulam Rabbani (Product) Team: PGW engineering team Changes in v0.6 (26-Nov-2026): Partner IDs are generated by APEX when Operations creates each partner in APEX. Operations sends the same list to the PGW integration team and the merchant (P2, terms). Format agreed with the APEX team: 3–20 letters, digits or hyphens (SP-3 4.1; open point 1 closed). The Used in column is removed from the Partner ID table; the delete check stays (SP-1, SP-3, SP-4, SP-5). Team names in place of people's names. These stories are the requirement. Each story can be read alone. A story refers to another story only for work that must be built first. Story map End-to-end view Onboarding Split modes Simulator Money flow Advice lifecycle What can go wrong # Story What it delivers Depends on Status SP-1 FE – Split payout configuration screen The new tab in PGW Configuration as a screen the user can navigate. No save. — Written SP-2 BE – Split payout configuration setup New settings on the product instance. Leg and profile limits. Partner ID search across MIDs. — Written SP-3 FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode Turn split on and off, manage Partner IDs, select Dynamic. Save and checks. SP-1, SP-2 Written SP-4 FE/BE – Fixed mode Fixed template. Save and checks. SP-3 Written SP-5 FE/BE – Profile mode Profile catalog. Save and checks. SP-3 Written SP-6 BE – Create session with split payout Split fields on Create Session. PGW checks and stores the split. Split flag to APEX. SP-2 Written (PTLF part waits on P4) SP-7 BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration PGW sends each split to APEX. SP-6, P3 Waiting on Emcrey SP-8 BE – PGW Split Payout API Merchant API to send a split later (NEW) or correct it (UPDATE). SP-6, SP-7 Written SP-9 BE – Day-end split advice file All advices of the day to APEX after the midnight cut-off. SP-7, P3 Waiting on Emcrey Build order: SP-1 and SP-2 in parallel → SP-3 → SP-4, SP-5 → SP-6 → SP-8. SP-7 and SP-9 after P3."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","Prerequisites (not PGW work)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#prerequisites-not-pgw-work","Onboarding: P1 and P2 # Item Needed for Owner P1 Risk approves the merchant for split payout. Operations onboards and screens each partner (Operations SOP). Before a MID is set up (SP-3) Risk & Compliance, Operations P2 APEX enables split on the merchant MID. Operations creates each partner definition in APEX, where APEX generates the Partner ID, and sends the same list of Partner IDs and partner names to the PGW integration team and to the merchant. Before a MID is set up (SP-3) APEX team, Operations P3 Emcrey shares the APEX Payout Advice API spec and the day-end advice file spec. SP-7, SP-9 APEX / Emcrey P4 The APEX team approves the PTLF spec with the split flag as field 49. SP-6 (PTLF part) APEX team"],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","Rules for every story","split-payout/docs/stories.html#rules-for-every-story","Terms. Merchant = the Geidea merchant whose MID has split payout enabled. Partner = a business that the merchant shares its payout with (for example a seller on a marketplace). The merchant tells PGW how much each partner gets. APEX pays the partner from the merchant's settlement. A partner is defined in APEX. It is not a MID. Partner ID = the ID that APEX generates when Operations creates the partner in APEX. Leg = one line of a split: one Partner ID and its amount or percentage. Field names. PGW field names are camelCase. Names in these stories are proposals. PGW engineering can rename them. Keep the meaning. The PGW backend applies every rule, also when the screen already blocks the action. Identify the merchant by MID in every API and in all documentation. New fields in merchant APIs are additions. Do not change or remove existing fields. Times are UAE time (UTC+4)."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-1 — FE – Split payout configuration screen","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-1-fe-split-payout-configuration-screen","Simulator: configuration"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-1 — FE – Split payout configuration screen","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background","The PGW integration team sets merchant settings in the Backoffice: Merchants → Impersonate merchant → Payment Gateway → Configuration. Split payout needs a new tab there. This story builds the screen only. SP-3, SP-4 and SP-5 connect it to the backend."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-1 — FE – Split payout configuration screen","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal","The tab Split Payout Settings exists and the user can navigate all of it. Nothing is saved."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-1 — FE – Split payout configuration screen","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary","Item Value Story ID SP-1 Users PGW integration team (Backoffice) Depends on — Followed by SP-3, SP-4, SP-5 Out of scope Save. Checks. Data from the backend."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-1 — FE – Split payout configuration screen","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Tab","split-payout/docs/stories.html#tab","Add the tab Split Payout Settings after Visa installment Settings and before NOTIFICATION Settings . The gateway_Instance selector (Live / Test) applies, as on the other tabs. Only Backoffice users who can open Payment Gateway Configuration see the tab. The merchant's own portal login does not show it."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Content of the tab","split-payout/docs/stories.html#content-of-the-tab","# Section Shows Component (existing example) 2.1 Split payout Title, one-line description, ENABLED / Disabled toggle. When off, the tab shows nothing else. Section with toggle (NOTIFICATION Settings) 2.2 Split mode Shown when the toggle is on. Three tiles: Dynamic , Fixed , Profile , each with one line of text. One can be selected. Bordered tiles (Gateway Settings: Payment methods) 2.3 Partner IDs Shown when the toggle is on. Table: Partner ID, Partner name, edit and delete icons. Link + Add Partner ID . Table with Add, edit, delete (Provider Settings: Provider Accounts) 2.4 Partner ID pop-up Opens from + Add Partner ID and from edit. Fields: Partner ID , Partner name (optional) . Buttons: Cancel , Save changes . On edit, Partner ID is read-only. Pop-up (Provider Settings: Edit Account) 2.5 Fixed template Shown when Fixed is selected. Split type radio: Amount (AED) / Percentage of gross order amount . One row per leg: Partner ID dropdown, value field, delete icon. Link + Add leg . Total below the rows. Grey fields, dropdown (all tabs) 2.6 Profile catalog Shown when Profile is selected. Table: Profile ID, Description, Split type, Legs, edit and delete icons. Link + Add profile . Table with Add, edit, delete 2.7 Profile pop-up Opens from + Add profile and from edit. Fields: Profile ID , Description (optional) , split type radio, leg rows as in 2.5. Buttons: Cancel , Save changes . On edit, Profile ID is read-only. Pop-up 2.8 Buttons Discard Changes and Save changes at the bottom of the tab. All tabs"],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Behaviour in this story","split-payout/docs/stories.html#behaviour-in-this-story","The toggle, tiles and radios change what the tab shows, as in section 2. In the pop-ups, Save changes adds or updates the row on the screen only. Cancel closes the pop-up. + Add leg adds an empty leg row. The delete icon removes a row from the screen. The Partner ID dropdown in a leg lists the Partner IDs in the table (2.3). + Add leg is hidden when a template or profile has 10 legs. + Add profile is hidden when there are 10 profiles. (SP-2 makes these limits configurable. SP-4 and SP-5 read them.) Save changes (2.8) does nothing in this story. Discard Changes clears all changes on the screen. The tab does not read data from the backend. When opened, the toggle is off and the tables are empty. Profile mode: the full tab Profile pop-up"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-1 — FE – Split payout configuration screen","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria","The tab shows after Visa installment Settings in the Backoffice. A merchant portal login does not show it. Toggle off: only the Split payout section shows. Toggle on: Split mode and Partner IDs show. Fixed selected: Fixed template shows. Profile selected: Profile catalog shows. Dynamic selected: neither shows. Add, edit and delete a Partner ID, a leg and a profile on the screen. With 10 legs, + Add leg is hidden. With 10 profiles, + Add profile is hidden. Save changes sends no request."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-2 — BE – Split payout configuration setup","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-2-be-split-payout-configuration-setup","Simulator: configuration Onboarding: Partner IDs from APEX"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-2 — BE – Split payout configuration setup","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-1","Merchant PGW settings are stored on the MID's PAYMENT_GATEWAY product instance, in its data object. The Configuration screen reads it with the existing product instance search and saves with the existing product instance update (PATCH /product/api/v1/productInstance/{productInstanceId}, JSON-Patch replace on data/<key>). The key isSplitPayoutEnabled already exists in data (value false). No screen uses it."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-2 — BE – Split payout configuration setup","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-1","The product instance data holds all split payout settings. The leg limit and the profile limit are system settings. PGW can find if a Partner ID is already used on another MID."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-2 — BE – Split payout configuration setup","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-1","Item Value Story ID SP-2 Depends on — Followed by SP-3, SP-4, SP-5, SP-6 Out of scope Screen (SP-1). Save checks (SP-3 to SP-5)."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-2 — BE – Split payout configuration setup","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-1",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Settings in data (PAYMENT_GATEWAY product instance, Live and Test separately)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#settings-in-data-payment_gateway-product-instance-live-and-test-separately","Field Type Default Values isSplitPayoutEnabled Boolean false Existing key. true = split payout on. splitPayoutMode String null DYNAMIC / FIXED / PROFILE splitPayoutPartners Array [] Item: partnerId (String), partnerName (String, max 100, optional) splitPayoutFixedTemplate Object null splitType (String: AMOUNT / PERCENTAGE), legs (Array) splitPayoutProfiles Array [] Item: profileId (String), description (String, optional), splitType (String), legs (Array) Leg item: partnerId (String), value (Number, 2 decimals). Existing MIDs: add the new fields with the defaults above. Do not change isSplitPayoutEnabled. The existing product instance search returns the new fields. The existing product instance update accepts them. partnerName is for the integration team only. Do not send it to the merchant or to APEX."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Limits (system settings, not per MID)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#limits-system-settings-not-per-mid","Setting Default Meaning Maximum legs per split 10 Applies to the Fixed template, each profile, and each split received in SP-6 and SP-8. Maximum profiles per MID 10 Applies to splitPayoutProfiles. Store both as configuration. Do not write the values in code. The Configuration screen can read both values."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Partner ID search","split-payout/docs/stories.html#partner-id-search","Input: Partner ID, instance (Live or Test). Output: the MID that has this Partner ID in splitPayoutPartners, or none. Compare without case."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-2 — BE – Split payout configuration setup","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-1","Product instance search for an existing MID returns the new fields with their defaults. Update splitPayoutMode with the existing product instance update. The search returns the new value. Change the leg limit to 5 in configuration. The screen reads 5. Partner ID search returns the MID that has the ID, and none for an unused ID. Live and Test results are separate."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-3 — FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-3-febe-enable-split-partner-ids-and-dynamic-mode","Simulator: Dynamic configuration Split modes What can go wrong: configuration"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-3 — FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-2","SP-1 built the tab. SP-2 added the settings. This story connects them for the base settings that every mode needs: the split toggle, the split mode and the Partner IDs. With this story, a MID can be fully set up for Dynamic mode. In Dynamic mode the merchant sends the split with each order (SP-6) or later through the Split Payout API (SP-8). No template is needed."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-3 — FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-2","The PGW integration team can turn split payout on for a MID, select a mode, add the merchant's Partner IDs and save. For Dynamic mode, the set-up is then complete."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-3 — FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-2","Item Value Story ID SP-3 Users PGW integration team (Backoffice) Fields written isSplitPayoutEnabled, splitPayoutMode, splitPayoutPartners Save Existing product instance update, as the other tabs Depends on SP-1, SP-2. Before a MID is set up: P1, P2. Followed by SP-4, SP-5"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-3 — FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-2",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Load","split-payout/docs/stories.html#load","When the tab opens, show the saved values of the selected instance."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Split toggle","split-payout/docs/stories.html#split-toggle","On → store isSplitPayoutEnabled = true. Off → false. Turning the toggle off keeps the other settings stored. They are not applied while split is off."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Split mode","split-payout/docs/stories.html#split-mode","Store the selected tile in splitPayoutMode: DYNAMIC, FIXED or PROFILE. When the mode changes, the template or profiles of the old mode stay stored. They are not applied. Show a note under the mode: \"The Fixed template / profiles are stored but not applied in this mode.\""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Partner IDs","split-payout/docs/stories.html#partner-ids","What can go wrong: Partner ID entry Add and edit in the pop-up. The pop-up checks the entry when the user clicks Save changes in the pop-up: # Check Message (under the field) 4.1 Partner ID has 3–20 letters, digits or hyphens. Remove spaces at start and end. Use 3–20 letters, digits or hyphens. 4.2 Partner ID is not already in the table. Compare without case. This Partner ID is already in the list. 4.3 Partner ID is not on another MID of the same instance (SP-2 search). This Partner ID is configured on another MID (MID nnnnnnnnnnn). A partner belongs to one merchant MID only. 4.4 Partner name: maximum 100 characters. Use up to 100 characters. Delete from the table: # Check Message 4.5 The Partner ID is not used in the Fixed template or in a profile. P000301 is used in Fixed template. Remove it there first. Why 4.3. APEX links each partner to one merchant MID. A Partner ID on a second MID makes APEX reject every split from that MID after the customer has paid. Note: the format in 4.1 is the Partner ID format agreed with the APEX team. Enter Partner IDs and partner names exactly as received from Operations."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","5. Save","split-payout/docs/stories.html#save","What can go wrong: save checks On Save changes (bottom of the tab): # Check Message 5.1 Split on → a mode is selected. Select a split mode. 5.2 Split on → at least one Partner ID. Add at least one Partner ID. Any failure: do not save. Show the message under the section and the banner \"Changes not saved. Fix the highlighted fields.\" No failure: save the changed fields with the existing product instance update. After a successful save, use the saved values as the new starting point for Save changes and Discard Changes . Example: turn split off, save, turn it on, save again → the second save stores true. Save changes is off when nothing changed. Discard Changes returns to the last saved values."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","6. Effect of a saved change","split-payout/docs/stories.html#effect-of-a-saved-change","Change Effect Split turned off New sessions with split fields are rejected (SP-6). Split Payout API calls are rejected (SP-8). Orders already flagged stay flagged; APEX releases them after N days. Operations also turns split off in APEX. Partner ID deleted New sessions and API calls with this ID are rejected. Splits already sent to APEX do not change. Any other change Applies to sessions created after the save. Split payout off Dynamic mode with Partner IDs Add Partner ID blocked by 4.3"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-3 — FE/BE – Enable split, Partner IDs and Dynamic mode","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-2","Open the tab on a new MID: split off, no mode, no Partner IDs. Turn split on, save without a mode: message 5.1. Nothing is saved. Select Dynamic, save without a Partner ID: message 5.2. Add P000301, then add p000301: message 4.2. Add an ID that is on another MID: message 4.3 with that MID. Select Dynamic, add two Partner IDs, save, reload: values are shown. Turn split off, save, turn split on, save, reload: split is on. Change on the Test instance: the Live instance does not change."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-4 — FE/BE – Fixed mode","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-4-febe-fixed-mode","Simulator: Fixed configuration Split modes What can go wrong: Fixed template"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-4 — FE/BE – Fixed mode","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-3","In Fixed mode the merchant sends a normal order with no split fields. PGW applies the same split to every order of the MID. The integration team sets this split once, as the Fixed template. SP-3 is in place: split is on and Partner IDs exist."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-4 — FE/BE – Fixed mode","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-3","The PGW integration team can set and save the Fixed template. With this story, a MID can be fully set up for Fixed mode."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-4 — FE/BE – Fixed mode","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-3","Item Value Story ID SP-4 Users PGW integration team (Backoffice) Fields written splitPayoutFixedTemplate (splitType, legs[] with partnerId, value) Save Existing product instance update Depends on SP-3 Followed by SP-6 (applies the template to each order)"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-4 — FE/BE – Fixed mode","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-3",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Template","split-payout/docs/stories.html#template","Split type: Amount (AED) → AMOUNT. Percentage of gross order amount → PERCENTAGE. One type for all legs. Each leg: Partner ID from the MID's Partner IDs, and a value. Show the total of all legs below the rows."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Checks on Save changes","split-payout/docs/stories.html#checks-on-save-changes","These checks apply when splitPayoutMode = FIXED. # Check Message (under the leg or the total) 2.1 At least one leg. Add at least one leg. 2.2 Each leg has a Partner ID. Select a Partner ID. 2.3 Each Partner ID occurs once in the template. Each partner can appear once. 2.4 Each value is above 0, with up to 2 decimals. Enter a value above 0, up to 2 decimals. 2.5 Number of legs ≤ maximum legs (SP-2, default 10). At the maximum, hide + Add leg . Maximum 10 legs. 2.6 Split type PERCENTAGE: total ≤ 100. Percentages cannot add up to more than 100%. Any failure: do not save. Show the banner \"Changes not saved. Fix the highlighted fields.\" No failure: save splitPayoutFixedTemplate with the existing product instance update. Save and discard work as in SP-3 section 5. For AMOUNT, do not check the total at setup. The order amount is not known. SP-6 rejects an order when the legs add up to more than the order amount."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Effect","split-payout/docs/stories.html#effect","A saved template applies to sessions created after the save. Partner IDs used in the template cannot be deleted (SP-3 check 4.5)."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Example","split-payout/docs/stories.html#example","Template: PERCENTAGE, P000301 = 60, P000302 = 30. Order AED 200. PGW sends to APEX: P000301 60%, P000302 30% (SP-6, SP-7). APEX pays them from the merchant's net. The merchant keeps the rest. \"splitPayoutFixedTemplate\": { \"splitType\": \"PERCENTAGE\", \"legs\": [ { \"partnerId\": \"P000301\", \"value\": 60.00 }, { \"partnerId\": \"P000302\", \"value\": 30.00 } ] } Fixed template Save blocked by 2.3, 2.2, 2.4 and 2.6"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-4 — FE/BE – Fixed mode","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-3","Fixed, PERCENTAGE, 60 + 30: saved. Reload shows the template. 60 + 30 + 15: message 2.6. Same Partner ID in two legs: message 2.3. Leg with value 0: message 2.4. 10 legs: + Add leg is hidden. Delete a Partner ID used in the template: message \"P000301 is used in Fixed template. Remove it there first.\""],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-5 — FE/BE – Profile mode","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-5-febe-profile-mode","Simulator: Profile configuration Split modes What can go wrong: profiles"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-5 — FE/BE – Profile mode","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-4","In Profile mode the merchant sends a profile ID with each order. Each profile holds a split that the integration team sets up front. Example: an insurance aggregator with one profile per insurer. SP-3 is in place: split is on and Partner IDs exist."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-5 — FE/BE – Profile mode","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-4","The PGW integration team can add, edit, delete and save profiles. With this story, a MID can be fully set up for Profile mode."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-5 — FE/BE – Profile mode","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-4","Item Value Story ID SP-5 Users PGW integration team (Backoffice) Fields written splitPayoutProfiles[] (profileId, description, splitType, legs[]) Save Existing product instance update Depends on SP-3 Followed by SP-6, SP-8 (apply the profile the merchant sends)"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-5 — FE/BE – Profile mode","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-4",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Profile pop-up","split-payout/docs/stories.html#profile-pop-up","Fields: Profile ID , Description (optional) , split type ( Amount (AED) / Percentage of gross order amount ), legs (Partner ID and value). The pop-up checks the profile when the user clicks Save changes in the pop-up: # Check Message 1.1 Profile ID has 1–50 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores. Use 1–50 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores. 1.2 Profile ID is not used by another profile of this MID. Compare without case. This profile ID already exists for this MID. 1.3 At least one leg. Add at least one leg. 1.4 Each leg has a Partner ID. Select a Partner ID. 1.5 Each Partner ID occurs once in the profile. Each partner can appear once. 1.6 Each value is above 0, with up to 2 decimals. Enter a value above 0, up to 2 decimals. 1.7 Number of legs ≤ maximum legs (SP-2, default 10). At the maximum, hide + Add leg . Maximum 10 legs. 1.8 Split type PERCENTAGE: total ≤ 100. Percentages cannot add up to more than 100%. On edit, Profile ID is read-only. To change an ID, delete the profile and add a new one. The pop-up Save changes updates the table on the screen only."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Checks on Save changes (bottom of the tab)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#checks-on-save-changes-bottom-of-the-tab","# Check Message 2.1 Mode PROFILE: at least one profile. Add at least one profile. 2.2 Number of profiles ≤ maximum profiles (SP-2, default 10). At the maximum, hide + Add profile . Maximum 10 profiles. Any failure: do not save. Show the banner \"Changes not saved. Fix the highlighted fields.\" No failure: save splitPayoutProfiles with the existing product instance update. Save and discard work as in SP-3 section 5. For AMOUNT profiles, do not check the total at setup. SP-6 rejects an order when the legs add up to more than the order amount."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Effect","split-payout/docs/stories.html#effect-1","Saved profiles apply to sessions and API calls after the save. A deleted profile: new sessions with this profile ID are rejected (SP-6). Partner IDs used in a profile cannot be deleted (SP-3 check 4.5)."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Example","split-payout/docs/stories.html#example-1","\"splitPayoutProfiles\": [ { \"profileId\": \"PRF-INSURER-A\", \"description\": \"Motor policy - Insurer A\", \"splitType\": \"PERCENTAGE\", \"legs\": [ { \"partnerId\": \"P000401\", \"value\": 90.00 }, { \"partnerId\": \"P000403\", \"value\": 5.00 } ] }, { \"profileId\": \"PRF-INSURER-B\", \"description\": \"Motor policy - Insurer B\", \"splitType\": \"AMOUNT\", \"legs\": [ { \"partnerId\": \"P000402\", \"value\": 500.00 }, { \"partnerId\": \"P000403\", \"value\": 25.00 } ] } ] Profile catalog Profile pop-up"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-5 — FE/BE – Profile mode","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-4","Add the two profiles in the example, save, reload: both are shown. Add a profile with ID prf-insurer-a: message 1.2. Profile with percentages 90 + 15: message 1.8. Mode Profile with no profile, save: message 2.1. 10 profiles: + Add profile is hidden."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-6 — BE – Create session with split payout","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-6-be-create-session-with-split-payout","End-to-end view Simulator: Create Session Split modes Money flow What can go wrong: Create Session"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-6 — BE – Create session with split payout","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-5","A merchant starts every payment with Create Session (POST /payment-intent/api/v2/direct/session). This covers the hosted payment page, the Direct API, and token CIT and MIT. The session becomes the order. For split payout, PGW must know the split of each order and tell APEX that the transaction is a split transaction. APEX then holds the settlement until it receives the split (SP-7). The PGW order already has the field splitPayoutTransaction (empty today)."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-6 — BE – Create session with split payout","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-5","A merchant with split payout on can send the split, a profile ID, or deferredSplit with Create Session. PGW checks the split before payment and rejects a wrong request. PGW stores the split on the order and returns it in order inquiry and callback. PGW sends the split flag to APEX with each successful split transaction."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-6 — BE – Create session with split payout","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-5","Item Value Story ID SP-6 Geidea endpoint POST /payment-intent/api/v2/direct/session (HPP, Direct API, CIT, MIT) Settings read isSplitPayoutEnabled, splitPayoutMode, splitPayoutPartners, splitPayoutFixedTemplate, splitPayoutProfiles, maximum legs (SP-2) Order fields written splitPayout, deferredSplit, splitPayoutType, splitPayoutProfileId, splitPayoutTransaction[] APEX createTransaction federation: new field splitPayout. PTLF: field 49 (waits on P4). Depends on SP-2. A MID set up with SP-3 to SP-5. Followed by SP-7, SP-8 Out of scope Sending the split to APEX (SP-7). Split after payment (SP-8). Pay by Link. Drop-in session v1. Refunds (no change: debited from the merchant as today)."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-6 — BE – Create session with split payout","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-5",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. New request fields (Create Session)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#new-request-fields-create-session","Field Type M/O/C Mode Rule splitPayoutType String C – M when splitPayoutTransaction is sent Dynamic AMOUNT or PERCENTAGE. One type for all legs. splitPayoutTransaction Array O Dynamic One item per leg. splitPayoutTransaction[].partnerId String M Dynamic A Partner ID of the MID splitPayoutTransaction[].value Number M Dynamic Above 0, up to 2 decimals. AED for AMOUNT. Percent of the order amount for PERCENTAGE. splitPayoutTransaction[].reference String O Dynamic Merchant's reference for the leg. Max 50. splitPayoutTransaction[].description String O Dynamic Free text. Max 100. splitPayoutProfileId String O Profile A profile ID of the MID deferredSplit Boolean O Dynamic, Profile true = the merchant sends the split later with the Split Payout API (SP-8). Fixed mode: the merchant sends no split fields. PGW applies the Fixed template. An empty splitPayoutTransaction array counts as not sent."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. What PGW does with each request","split-payout/docs/stories.html#what-pgw-does-with-each-request","Split modes: one order, three modes # MID setting Request PGW action splitPayout on the order 2.1 Split off No split fields Normal session false 2.2 Split off Any split field or deferredSplit Reject — 2.3 Dynamic splitPayoutTransaction Check (section 3). Store the legs. true 2.4 Dynamic deferredSplit = true, no legs Store deferredSplit = true. No legs. true 2.5 Dynamic No split fields, no deferredSplit Normal session. No split. false 2.6 Fixed No split fields Copy the Fixed template legs to the order. Check the amount (3.6). true 2.7 Fixed Any split field or deferredSplit Reject — 2.8 Profile splitPayoutProfileId Copy the profile legs to the order. Check (section 3). true 2.9 Profile deferredSplit = true, no profile ID Store deferredSplit = true. true 2.10 Profile splitPayoutTransaction Reject — 2.11 Any Split detail or profile ID and deferredSplit = true Reject —"],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Checks before payment","split-payout/docs/stories.html#checks-before-payment","Simulator: Create Session What can go wrong: checks 3.1–3.9 Any failure rejects the session (HTTP 400). PGW engineering sets the error codes. # Check Message 3.1 Split fields are allowed for this MID and mode (section 2). Split payout is not enabled for this merchant. / Split fields do not match the merchant's split mode. 3.2 paymentOperation = Pay. Split payout is supported for Pay only. 3.3 Order currency = AED. Split payout is supported in AED only. 3.4 Each partnerId is a Partner ID of the MID. Each occurs once. Unknown partner ID: {id}. / Duplicate partner ID: {id}. 3.5 Number of legs ≤ maximum legs (SP-2). Maximum {n} legs. 3.6 AMOUNT: total of legs ≤ order amount. PERCENTAGE: total ≤ 100. Split total is more than the order amount. / Split total is more than 100%. 3.7 Each value is above 0, up to 2 decimals. Invalid split value. 3.8 splitPayoutProfileId is a profile of the MID. Unknown split profile: {id}. 3.9 Not split detail and deferredSplit together. Send split detail or deferredSplit, not both. Why 3.2. In this release APEX handles split only on a sale. Authorize and capture are not included."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Store on the order","split-payout/docs/stories.html#store-on-the-order","Order field Type Value splitPayout Boolean true when the order is a split order (section 2). Not a request field. deferredSplit Boolean From the request. Default false. splitPayoutType String AMOUNT / PERCENTAGE. From the request, template or profile. null when deferred. splitPayoutProfileId String From the request. null otherwise. splitPayoutTransaction[] Array Legs: partnerId, value, reference, description. Existing field. Store the split as it is at session creation. A later change to the MID settings does not change the order. Do not convert percentages to amounts. APEX converts them on the gross amount."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","5. Return to the merchant","split-payout/docs/stories.html#return-to-the-merchant","Return the fields of section 4 in: Order inquiry GET /pgw/api/v1/direct/order/{orderId} and GET /pgw/api/v1/direct/order?MerchantReferenceId=. The callback to callbackUrl. The pay responses (/pgw/api/v2/direct/pay, /pgw/api/v2/direct/pay/token)."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","6. Split flag to APEX","split-payout/docs/stories.html#split-flag-to-apex","Simulator: APEX federation Advice lifecycle When PGW federates a successful Pay transaction of an order with splitPayout = true, send splitPayout = Y in createTransaction. Add the field at the end of the payload. For all other transactions send splitPayout = N. PTLF: send the same value as field 49 (waits on P4). Why. APEX holds a transaction with splitPayout = Y until it receives a valid split. If PGW does not send the split, APEX releases the money to the merchant after N days. Without the flag, APEX pays the merchant the next day and the partners' money stays with the merchant."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","7. Example (Dynamic, amount split)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#example-dynamic-amount-split","{ \"amount\": 1000.00, \"currency\": \"AED\", \"merchantReferenceId\": \"ORD-DEMO-1000\", \"paymentOperation\": \"Pay\", \"timestamp\": \"...\", \"signature\": \"...\", \"callbackUrl\": \"...\", \"splitPayoutType\": \"AMOUNT\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\": [ { \"partnerId\": \"P000301\", \"value\": 600.00, \"reference\": \"SELLER-A\" }, { \"partnerId\": \"P000302\", \"value\": 300.00, \"reference\": \"SELLER-B\" }, { \"partnerId\": \"P000303\", \"value\": 100.00, \"reference\": \"DELIVERY\" } ] } Profile: send \"splitPayoutProfileId\": \"PRF-INSURER-A\". Deferred: send \"deferredSplit\": true. Fixed: send no split fields."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-6 — BE – Create session with split payout","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-5","Dynamic MID, the example above: session created. Order inquiry returns splitPayout = true and the three legs. Same request on a MID with split off: rejected (3.1). Legs total AED 1,001 on an AED 1,000 order: rejected (3.6). Unknown Partner ID: rejected (3.4). paymentOperation = PreAuthorize with split: rejected (3.2). Fixed MID, order with no split fields: order has the template legs. Profile MID, splitPayoutProfileId = PRF-INSURER-A: order has the profile legs. Unknown profile: rejected (3.8). deferredSplit = true: order has splitPayout = true and no legs. Split detail and deferredSplit together: rejected (3.9). Successful split payment: createTransaction has splitPayout = Y. Normal payment: N. Change the Fixed template after a session: the order keeps the old legs."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-7 — BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-7-be-apex-payout-advice-api-integration-waiting-on-emcrey-p3","End-to-end view: APEX not reachable Simulator: advice to APEX Advice lifecycle What can go wrong: sending to APEX"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-7 — BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-6","APEX holds a split transaction until it receives the split (the \"advice\"). APEX provides a Payout Advice API. PGW must send each split to this API. The API spec is not final yet. This story gives the requirement from the agreed APEX requirement (APEX team, 11-Nov-2026). Field names follow the APEX draft and can change with the final spec."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-7 — BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-6","PGW sends the split of each successful split order to APEX. PGW sends new and updated splits from the Split Payout API (SP-8). PGW keeps the status of each advice."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-7 — BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-6","Item Value Story ID SP-7 APEX API used Payout Advice API (spec from Emcrey, P3) Depends on SP-6, P3 Followed by SP-8, SP-9"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-7 — BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-6",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. When PGW sends an advice","split-payout/docs/stories.html#when-pgw-sends-an-advice","# Trigger Action 1.1 Pay succeeds on an order with legs in splitPayoutTransaction (Dynamic, Fixed, Profile) Send action A (create) 1.2 SP-8 accepts a NEW Send action A 1.3 SP-8 accepts an UPDATE Send action U (replace the whole advice) Orders with deferredSplit = true and no legs: send nothing until SP-8."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Content (APEX draft)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#content-apex-draft","APEX field PGW source Action A or U Merchant MID TransactionReference interSystemID of the Pay transaction SplitRules[].PartnerID splitPayoutTransaction[].partnerId SplitRules[].Percentage value when splitPayoutType = PERCENTAGE. Else empty. SplitRules[].Amount value when splitPayoutType = AMOUNT. Else empty. Send percentage or amount, never both."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Advice record on the order","split-payout/docs/stories.html#advice-record-on-the-order","Field Values splitAdviceId Generated by PGW at the first advice of the order. A new ID for each UPDATE. splitAdviceStatus PENDING (not sent yet), SENT, ACCEPTED, REJECTED splitAdviceReason APEX reason code and text when REJECTED"],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Errors and retry","split-payout/docs/stories.html#errors-and-retry","Advice lifecycle: APEX not reachable Advice lifecycle: rejected, then corrected Try it: APEX not reachable APEX not reachable or timeout: keep PENDING and retry. Retry count and interval: PGW engineering. The day-end file (SP-9) carries the advice in any case. APEX rejects: store REJECTED and the reason. The merchant can correct it with an UPDATE (SP-8). A failure here never changes the payment result."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-7 — BE – APEX Payout Advice API integration (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-6","To be completed when P3 is received."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-8 — BE – PGW Split Payout API","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-8-be-pgw-split-payout-api","End-to-end view: deferred split Simulator: Payout Advice API Advice lifecycle: deferred split What can go wrong: Payout Advice API"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-8 — BE – PGW Split Payout API","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-7","Some merchants do not know the split when the customer pays. They create the session with deferredSplit = true (SP-6) and send the split later. A merchant can also correct a split that APEX rejected. This story adds a merchant API for both. PGW checks the request and sends it to APEX (SP-7)."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-8 — BE – PGW Split Payout API","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-7","The merchant can send the split of a paid order (NEW). The merchant can replace the split of an order (UPDATE). PGW returns the result at once and sends the advice to APEX."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-8 — BE – PGW Split Payout API","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-7","Item Value Story ID SP-8 Geidea endpoint New. Name and path: PGW engineering (e.g. POST /pgw/api/v1/direct/splitPayout). Same authentication as the other Direct API calls. Modes Dynamic and Profile. Fixed MIDs are rejected (the template applies). Depends on SP-6, SP-7 Out of scope Split inquiry API (later, with the Merchant Portal view)."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-8 — BE – PGW Split Payout API","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-7",""],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","1. Request","split-payout/docs/stories.html#request","Field Type M/O/C Rule action String M NEW or UPDATE orderId String M PGW order ID splitAdviceId String C – M for UPDATE The latest advice ID of the order splitPayoutType String C – M for Dynamic AMOUNT / PERCENTAGE splitPayoutTransaction[] Array C – M for Dynamic Same as SP-6: partnerId, value, reference, description splitPayoutProfileId String C – M for Profile A profile of the MID The request always carries the full split, never one leg."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","2. Checks","split-payout/docs/stories.html#checks","What can go wrong: checks 2.1–2.6 Any failure: reject (HTTP 400) with the message. PGW engineering sets the error codes. # Check Message 2.1 Split is on for the MID. Mode is Dynamic or Profile. Split payout is not enabled for this merchant. / Not allowed for Fixed split. 2.2 The order exists, belongs to the calling MID and has a successful Pay. Order not found. / Order is not paid. 2.3 The order has splitPayout = true. Order is not a split order. 2.4 NEW: the order has deferredSplit = true and no advice yet. A split already exists for this order. Use UPDATE. 2.5 UPDATE: splitAdviceId is the latest advice ID of the order. Advice ID is not the latest. 2.6 The split passes the SP-6 checks 3.4 to 3.8 (Partner IDs, legs limit, totals, values, profile). As in SP-6. Why 2.3. An order without the flag was not held by APEX. Its money may already be paid to the merchant. Why 2.5. A late retry of an old request must not overwrite a newer correction."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","3. Processing","split-payout/docs/stories.html#processing","Repeat of the same NEW with the same content: return the first splitAdviceId. Do not create a second advice. Store the split on the order (fields as in SP-6 section 4). For UPDATE, replace the legs. Send to APEX with SP-7 (NEW → A, UPDATE → U). APEX accepts U only while the advice is not yet processed or released. APEX rejects it after that. Return the APEX reason."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","4. Response","split-payout/docs/stories.html#response","Field Value splitAdviceId New advice ID splitAdviceStatus ACCEPTED or REJECTED (from APEX) or PENDING (APEX not reached) reasonCode, reasonMessage When rejected The final payout result reaches the merchant in the APEX split payout report."],["Split Payout","Story map › Requirements","Document","5. Example","split-payout/docs/stories.html#example-2","{ \"action\": \"NEW\", \"orderId\": \"a1b2c3d4-...\", \"splitPayoutType\": \"PERCENTAGE\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\": [ { \"partnerId\": \"P000301\", \"value\": 60.00 }, { \"partnerId\": \"P000302\", \"value\": 30.00 } ] }"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-8 — BE – PGW Split Payout API","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-7","Order created with deferredSplit = true and paid: NEW accepted. Advice ID returned. NEW on an order that already has a split: rejected (2.4). UPDATE with an older advice ID: rejected (2.5). NEW on an order without the split flag: rejected (2.3). Call on a Fixed MID: rejected (2.1). Same NEW sent twice: same advice ID."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","SP-9 — BE – Day-end split advice file (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","split-payout/docs/stories.html#sp-9-be-day-end-split-advice-file-waiting-on-emcrey-p3","End-to-end view Simulator: day-end file Advice lifecycle What can go wrong: day-end file"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-9 — BE – Day-end split advice file (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Background","split-payout/docs/stories.html#background-8","APEX uses a day-end file as a backup to the real-time API, the same way as transaction federation today. If an advice is lost on the API, the file carries it. If the file and the API differ, the file wins."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-9 — BE – Day-end split advice file (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Goal","split-payout/docs/stories.html#goal-8","PGW sends APEX one file each day with all advices of the day."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-9 — BE – Day-end split advice file (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Story summary","split-payout/docs/stories.html#story-summary-8","Item Value Story ID SP-9 Depends on SP-7, P3 (file format)"],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-9 — BE – Day-end split advice file (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Requirements","split-payout/docs/stories.html#requirements-8","Send the file after the Ecomm cut-off (midnight, UAE time), for the day that ended. Include every advice created or updated that day, in its latest state (action A or U). Add a trailer with the number of records. On a day with no advices, send an empty file with a record count of 0. File name, format, delivery path: from P3."],["Split Payout","Story map › SP-9 — BE – Day-end split advice file (waiting on Emcrey, P3)","Document","Acceptance criteria","split-payout/docs/stories.html#acceptance-criteria-8","To be completed when P3 is received."],["Split Payout","Story map","Document","Open points","split-payout/docs/stories.html#open-points","What can go wrong: cases not defined yet # Point Story Owner / status 1 Partner ID convention: who issues it (PGW, Operations or APEX) and its format. MA must be able to use the same convention later. SP-3 Closed 26-Nov-2026: APEX generates the Partner ID when Operations creates the partner. Format: SP-3 4.1. 2 Where isSplitPayoutEnabled and the order field splitPayoutTransaction come from, and what reads them today. SP-2, SP-6 PGW engineering 3 Partner ID search across MIDs: confirm it is possible on product instance data. SP-2 PGW engineering 4 The tab is in Merchant Portal Backoffice (owner: MA team). Confirm the PGW team builds it, as for Pay by Bank settings. SP-1 Rabbani with MA team 5 Are all three modes in the first build, or Dynamic first? SP-3 to SP-5 Rabbani 6 After a mode change: how an UPDATE is checked for an order created in the old mode. SP-8 Rabbani 7 Pay by Link with split payout. SP-6 Later"],["Split Payout","End-to-end view","Visual page","End-to-end view","split-payout/end-to-end.html","visual · split payout End-to-end view One order from start to payout, across the customer, the partner, PGW, APEX and the vendors. Change the settings below and both flows redraw: the split mode, when the split is sent, who bears the transfer fees, and what goes wrong on the way. Illustrative Reflects product doc v1.6 and PGW stories v0.6 Read the requirement → Stories: SP-6 → SP-8 → Terms: partner = the merchant (MID) with split payout on; vendor = a payee of the split, with an ID from APEX. Field names in the payloads are the stories' proposals (the stories call the vendor ID partnerId ). APEX advice fields are drafts until the Emcrey spec (P3). Order amount AED 1,000 in every example. Split mode Dynamic Fixed Profile Split type Amount Percentage Split sent With the order Later (deferredSplit) Transfer fees borne by Partner Vendors APEX real-time call Reached Not reachable APEX outcome Accepted Rejected in the call, corrected same day Amount check fails Reset to the default"],["Split Payout","End-to-end view","Visual page","A. From Create Session to payout","split-payout/end-to-end.html#a-from-create-session-to-payout","Dynamic MID · amount split The split (P000301 500.00, P000302 300.00) travels with the order. APEX accepts it and pays on the payout date. Action by the customer or partner PGW or APEX processing Held at APEX Money moves Rejected Waiting or retry Released to the partner Order and payment Hold and advice Validation Payout Customer Partner PGW APEX Vendors 01 Create Session AMOUNT legs 02 Check and store the split row 2.3 · checks 3.1–3.9 03 Pays card, wallet 04 Federate the sale splitPayout = Y 05 Hold the transaction until a valid advice 06 Send advice A real time · ACCEPTED 07 Structural checks advice CREATED 08 Day-end file after midnight · backup 09 Amount validation Rule 1, Rule 2: pass 10 Pay out on P vendors, then the residual 11 Vendors receive their payouts leg − commission 12 Partner receives the residual after MDR, all fees Scroll sideways to see the whole flow → 01 Partner Create Session AMOUNT legs 02 PGW Check and store the split row 2.3 · checks 3.1–3.9 03 Customer Pays card, wallet 04 PGW Federate the sale splitPayout = Y 05 APEX Hold the transaction until a valid advice 06 PGW Send advice A real time · ACCEPTED 07 APEX Structural checks advice CREATED 08 PGW Day-end file after midnight · backup 09 APEX Amount validation Rule 1, Rule 2: pass 10 APEX Pay out on P vendors, then the residual 11 Vendors Vendors receive their payouts leg − commission 12 Partner Partner receives the residual after MDR, all fees ← Previous Next step → Step 1 of 12"],["Split Payout","End-to-end view › A. From Create Session to payout","Visual page","1. Create Session","split-payout/end-to-end.html#aDetail","Partner → PGW (HPP, Direct API, token CIT/MIT) The partner sends the split with the order: the split type and one leg per vendor. Try it in the simulator Story SP-6 section 1 Product doc 7.2 POST /payment-intent/api/v2/direct/session { \"amount\" : 1000, \"currency\" : \"AED\", \"paymentOperation\" : \"Pay\", \"merchantReferenceId\" : \"ORD-1001\", \"splitPayoutType\" : \"AMOUNT\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\" : [ { \"partnerId\" : \"P000301\", \"value\" : 500 }, { \"partnerId\" : \"P000302\", \"value\" : 300 } ], ... : ... other Create Session fields }"],["Split Payout","End-to-end view","Visual page","B. Deferred split: from the Payout Advice API to payout","split-payout/end-to-end.html#b-deferred-split-from-the-payout-advice-api-to-payout","Starts with: a paid order created with deferredSplit, held at APEX, no advice yet The partner sends the split after payment. APEX accepts it and pays on the payout date. Scroll sideways to see the whole flow → ← Previous Next step → Shown when Split sent is set to Later (deferredSplit) ."],["Split Payout","Split Payout","Hub","Split Payout","split-payout/index.html","feature Split Payout A partner's ecommerce order is paid out to the partner and its vendors from one transaction."],["Split Payout","Split Payout","Hub","Documents","split-payout/index.html#documents","Product document Vendor Payout Service, MVP v1.6 · 10-Nov-2026 PGW dev stories Vendor Payout Service, PGW stories v0.6"],["Split Payout","Split Payout","Hub","Visual pages","split-payout/index.html#visual-pages","End-to-end view One order from Create Session, or a Payout Advice API call, to the vendor and partner payouts; switch mode, split type, deferred split, fees and failures and the flow redraws open Money flow Who is paid, and from where, for one order; edit the numbers, see fees and APEX amount validation open Onboarding Operational and system steps in one swimlane, from partner request to go-live open Split modes Dynamic, Fixed and Profile explained side by side open Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator One section per PGW work item: configuration, Create Session, APEX federation, Payout Advice API, day-end file open Advice lifecycle Advice statuses at APEX, and day by day: deferred split, update, rejection, release open What can go wrong Every rejection and exception, from configuration to after payout, each with a \"Try it\" in the simulator open"],["Split Payout","Split Payout","Hub","Stories and where to see them","split-payout/index.html#stories","Prerequisites P1, P2 · Operations, Risk and APEX set-up Onboarding SP-1 · Configuration screen Simulator 1 SP-2 · Configuration settings and limits Simulator 1 · Onboarding SP-3 · Enable split, Partner IDs, Dynamic Simulator 1 · Split modes · What can go wrong SP-4 · Fixed mode Simulator 1 · Split modes · What can go wrong SP-5 · Profile mode Simulator 1 · Split modes · What can go wrong SP-6 · Create Session with split End-to-end view · Simulator 2–3 · Split modes · Money flow · What can go wrong SP-7 · Advice to APEX End-to-end view · Simulator 3 · Advice lifecycle · What can go wrong SP-8 · Payout Advice API End-to-end view · Simulator 4 · Advice lifecycle · What can go wrong SP-9 · Day-end advice file End-to-end view · Simulator 5 · Advice lifecycle · What can go wrong"],["Split Payout","Money flow","Visual page","Money flow","split-payout/money-flow.html","visual · split payout Money flow Who is paid, and from where, for one order. Starts on the product doc's worked example; change any number to see the effect. Band widths are to scale. Illustrative Reflects product doc v1.6 · 10-Nov-2026 Read the requirement → Stories: SP-6 check 3.6 →"],["Split Payout","Money flow","Visual page","The numbers","split-payout/money-flow.html#the-numbers","Doc worked example (4.2) MDR : what Geidea deducts from the partner (MID). Commission : what the partner takes from each vendor's leg. Numbers are fictional; what matters is how each component works. Reset to the doc example Order amount (AED) Geidea MDR (%) VAT on MDR (%) Fee per payout (AED) Split type Amount Percentage Vendor Split (AED) Partner commission (%) × × × + Add vendor"],["Split Payout","Money flow","Visual page","One order, end to end","split-payout/money-flow.html#one-order-end-to-end","No split Split · partner bears transfer fees Split · vendors bear transfer fees The partner bears the transfer cost of every payout (each vendor’s and its own). APEX accepts the advice. Rule 1 and Rule 2 pass: vendors are paid and the partner receives the residual (advice PROCESSED). Money flow for one order CUSTOMER PAYS ACQUIRING (GEIDEA / APEX) PAID TO Geidea MDR 10.00 + VAT 0.00 Partner net: AED 990.00 Vendor A: AED 490.00 Vendor B: AED 295.50 Vendor C: AED 100.00 Remittance fees: AED 4.00 Partner residual: AED 100.50 Customer AED 1,000.00 Geidea MDR AED 10.00 (1%) Partner net AED 990.00 · held for split Vendor A AED 490.00 · to vendor IBAN Vendor B AED 295.50 · to vendor IBAN Vendor C AED 100.00 · to vendor IBAN Remittance fees AED 4.00 · 4 × 1.00, from partner Partner residual AED 100.50 · incl. 14.50 partner commission Money paid out Kept by Geidea (MDR, VAT, remittance fees) Held at APEX Bands under 0.5% of the order are drawn at a minimum width so they stay visible."],["Split Payout","Money flow › One order, end to end","Visual page","How each payout is calculated","split-payout/money-flow.html#how-each-payout-is-calculated","Calculated by APEX, not PGW. A percentage split is first converted against the gross order amount. Each amount is rounded to 2 decimals; the partner's residual absorbs the rounding. Vendor A split 500.00 − partner commission 2% 10.00 − remittance fee 0.00 = 490.00 Vendor B split 300.00 − partner commission 1.5% 4.50 − remittance fee 0.00 = 295.50 Vendor C split 100.00 − partner commission none 0.00 − remittance fee 0.00 = 100.00"],["Split Payout","Money flow › One order, end to end","Visual page","What the partner receives","split-payout/money-flow.html#what-the-partner-receives","The residual: everything left of the partner's net after vendor payouts and fees. Transaction amount less Geidea MDR 1,000.00 − 10.00 990.00 Less: net payouts to vendors payouts 1 to 3 −885.50 Less: remittance fees 4 payouts × 1.00, all charged to the partner −4.00 Paid to the partner payout 4: 90.00 unallocated after MDR + 14.50 commission − 4.00 remittance fees 100.50"],["Split Payout","Money flow","Visual page","APEX amount validation","split-payout/money-flow.html#amount-validation","A scheduled APEX process, once pricing is available. Any failure rejects the whole advice with a reason code. \"Split ≤ net\" is not enough: it passes advices that leave the partner negative once fees are taken, and rejects valid ones where commission covers the gap."],["Split Payout","Money flow › APEX amount validation","Visual page","pass Rule 1 – partner residual not negative","split-payout/money-flow.html#rules","net − Σ advised amounts + Σ partner commissions − remittance fees borne by the partner ≥ 0 net (1,000.00 − MDR 10.00 − VAT 0.00) 990.00 − Σ advised amounts −900.00 + Σ partner commissions +14.50 − remittance fees borne by the partner (4 payouts) −4.00 result 100.50"],["Split Payout","Money flow › APEX amount validation","Visual page","pass Rule 2 – every leg above zero","split-payout/money-flow.html#rules","advised amount − commission − remittance fee (only when the vendor bears it) > 0, for every vendor Vendor A: 500.00 − 10.00 490.00 Vendor B: 300.00 − 4.50 295.50 Vendor C: 100.00 − 0.00 100.00"],["Split Payout","Money flow › APEX amount validation","Visual page","rule Single payout","split-payout/money-flow.html#rules","A transaction is never paid out twice. Source: product doc v1.6, 6.5 Amount validation . Source: product doc v1.6, 4.1 How funds move and 4.2 Worked example. The doc example uses MDR 1%, remittance fee AED 1.00 per payout (illustrative), VAT on MDR left out."],["Split Payout","Money flow","Visual page","The sequence in APEX","split-payout/money-flow.html#the-sequence-in-apex","APEX calculates the partner's settlement and deducts Geidea's MDR and VAT on the MDR. The remainder is the partner's net. For a transaction with a valid split advice, APEX calculates each vendor's payout: split amount, minus commission on the vendor definition (if any), minus the remittance fee when the partner MID is set to deduct it from vendors. Rounded to 2 decimals. A remittance fee is charged for every payout: each vendor transfer and the partner's own payout. The fee on the partner's payout always comes from its residual. Commission taken from a vendor leg stays with the partner and reaches it in the residual. APEX transfers each vendor's net payout to its IBAN and settles the residual to the partner. Not yet defined (Finance) Whether vendor payouts go through a separate staging account, or APEX posts them directly from the merchant payout account. Accounting of fund movements, and VAT on vendor commission and remittance fees. Source: product doc v1.6, 4.1 How funds move ."],["Split Payout","Money flow","Visual page","What happens in each situation","split-payout/money-flow.html#outcomes","paid · normal Transaction not flagged for split Settles to the partner through the normal process, after Geidea's MDR. paid · split Flagged, valid advice processed Vendors receive their net payouts; the partner receives the residual. held Flagged, advice rejected Nothing is paid to vendors; the transaction stays held. The rejection and reason show in the split payout report. The partner may send a corrected, complete advice through the Payout Advice API. held Flagged, no advice received The transaction stays held. Example: a deferred split never sent, or an advice lost in transit and not in the day-end file. released Flagged, no valid advice within N days Released to the partner after Geidea's MDR and listed in an exception report for Operations and Finance. N is set per partner MID, can be zero, and counts from the scheduled payout date. parked Advice received before the transaction Parked as PENDING_MATCH and linked when the transaction arrives; rejected with a reason if still unmatched at day end. held Advice in API but missing in the day-end file Marked as mismatched; settlement held until resolved. exception Transfer to a vendor fails Existing exception process: retry up to the configured limit, then an exception record, shown in the split payout report. Geidea contacts the partner only, never the vendor. Source: product doc v1.6, 4.3 Outcomes ."],["Split Payout","Onboarding","Visual page","Onboarding","split-payout/onboarding.html","visual · split payout Onboarding How a partner gets split payout, from request to go-live. Most steps are operational (emails and documents between teams). Three steps change system set-up: Operations creates the vendors in APEX (APEX generates the beneficiary IDs), the APEX team enables the partner MID, and PGW configures it. Illustrative Reflects product doc v1.6, sections 5 and 7.1, and Operations SOP v1.1 (26-Nov-2026) Read the requirement → Stories: prerequisites P1, P2 → After set-up: End-to-end view → Changed 26-Nov-2026 (agreed with the APEX team): Operations creates the vendors in APEX and APEX generates each beneficiary ID. Operations sends the same list to the PGW integration team and the partner. Product doc v1.6 section 5.2 still says the APEX team creates the vendors. Terms: partner = the Geidea merchant (MID) that asks for split payout; vendor = a payee of the split. Operations creates each vendor in APEX, which generates its beneficiary ID; Operations sends the same list to PGW and the partner. PGW uses the ID to check the partner's requests."],["Split Payout","Onboarding","Visual page","Who does what","split-payout/onboarding.html#who-does-what","Operational step (no system build in the MVP) System set-up Side path Timing not set in the doc Request and approval Vendors PGW set-up Live Partner Business Risk Legal Operations APEX team PGW integration 01 · ops Request the service 02 · ops Present to Risk 03 · ops Approve the partner 04 · ops Onboard each vendor 05 · system Create vendors in APEX 06 · system Enable split on the partner MID 07 · system Configure the partner MID in PGW 08 · ops Support the partner’s integration 09 · ops Go live Screening hit → Risk, existing process Business + Legal revisit the partner agreement (doc 5.1) Request and approval 01 · ops Partner Request the service 02 · ops Business Present to Risk 03 · ops Risk Approve the partner Business + Legal revisit the partner agreement (doc 5.1). The doc does not set when. Vendors 04 · ops Operations Onboard each vendor Side path: screening hit → Risk, existing process 05 · system Operations Create vendors in APEX 06 · system APEX team Enable split on the partner MID PGW set-up 07 · system PGW integration Configure the partner MID in PGW 08 · ops PGW integration Support the partner’s integration Live 09 · ops Partner Go live ← Previous Next step → Show all Step 1 of 9"],["Split Payout","Onboarding › Who does what","Visual page","1. Request the serviceOperational","split-payout/onboarding.html#detail","Done by: Partner The partner asks for the service and shares the vendors’ details and documents, the split configuration and any vendor commission. Business registration Relationship or contract with the partner IBAN Source: product doc 5.2 step 1"],["Split Payout","Onboarding › 1. Request the serviceOperational","Visual page","COMES IN","split-payout/onboarding.html#detail","Partner’s own request"],["Split Payout","Onboarding › 1. Request the serviceOperational","Visual page","GOES OUT TO BUSINESS","split-payout/onboarding.html#detail","Vendor details + documents Split configuration Vendor commission"],["Split Payout","Onboarding › 1. Request the serviceOperational","Visual page","RESULT","split-payout/onboarding.html#detail","Request with Business"],["Split Payout","Onboarding","Visual page","Where each set-up item lives","split-payout/onboarding.html#where-each-set-up-item-lives","Item Set by Kept in Used for Vendor documents (business registration, IBAN in the business's name, agreement with the partner) Operations Operations record Support for each payout, e.g. the Central Bank travel rule Vendor definition with IBAN and any commission Operations, in the APEX interface APEX, linked to the partner MID Paying the vendor legs Split payout on the partner MID: remittance fee, fee-bearer option, N APEX team APEX Fees and the N-day hold. See Money flow Beneficiary IDs APEX generates; Operations sends the list; PGW integration team enters APEX and PGW configuration PGW checks that each leg in a request uses a known ID Split payout enabled, split mode, fixed template or profile catalog PGW integration team PGW configuration (the partner has no access) Building the split for each order. See Split modes and the configuration modal Source: product doc 5.2 , 7.1"],["Split Payout","Onboarding","Visual page","Rules that apply","split-payout/onboarding.html#rules-that-apply","Offered only to select large partners, case by case. Unlicensed aggregators are excluded. Risk approves the partner before any onboarding. The service is never on by default. Business revisits the partner agreement with Legal: dispute and chargeback liability, refunds and chargebacks deducted from the partner, recovery of any shortfall, and domestic fund transfer terms. Due diligence and screening happen once per vendor, when it is onboarded, not on every payout. New vendors, changes to a vendor's details or commission, and deactivation follow the same operational route. Vendors have no portal access. Geidea talks to the partner only. PGW holds no vendor bank details and does not check vendor status. APEX rejects a beneficiary ID that is wrong, no longer active or not linked to the partner MID. Source: product doc 5.1 , 5.2 , 7.1"],["Split Payout","Onboarding","Visual page","Still to add","split-payout/onboarding.html#still-to-add","Detail from the Vendor Payout Service Operations SOP v1.1 (26-Nov-2026): the route and form of each handover, checklists, and how the change and deactivation paths run. Steps 5 to 9 follow SOP v1.1; the other steps follow the product doc."],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","split-payout/simulator.html","simulator · split payout Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator One section per PGW work item: configure split payout on a MID, create a session, follow the transaction to APEX, send or correct a split with the Payout Advice API, and generate the day-end file. Illustrative Reflects PGW stories v0.6 and product doc v1.6 Read the stories → Read the product doc → Terms follow the PGW stories. Merchant = the MID with split payout on (the \"partner\" in the product doc). Partner = a payee of the split, with a Partner ID from APEX (a \"vendor\" in the product doc). Field names are the stories' proposals. APEX advice fields and the day-end file layout are drafts until the Emcrey spec (P3). Updates reach APEX only in the day-end file, as agreed with APEX on 23-Nov, and are accepted on the transaction date only (rule of 24-Nov). Stories v0.6 still say real time. Nothing here calls a real system. 1"],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Split Payout PGW Configuration","split-payout/simulator.html#split-payout-pgw-configuration","PGW configuration · Split Payout tab · SP-1 to SP-5 load example — Split off Dynamic Fixed Profile Open PGW configuration Saved settings · MID 9000123 isSplitPayoutEnabled true splitPayoutMode DYNAMIC maximum legs (SP-2) 10 last saved example loaded splitPayoutPartners P000301 Seller A P000302 Seller B P000303 Delivery Co Source: SP-1 to SP-5 · SP-2 settings An order keeps the split as it was at session creation. Change and save the configuration, then select an earlier order in section 3: it does not change (SP-6 section 4). 2"],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Update Create Session API","split-payout/simulator.html#update-create-session-api","Merchant → PGW · POST /payment-intent/api/v2/direct/session · SP-6 Order amount currency AED USD SAR paymentOperation Pay PreAuthorize merchantReferenceId Split Split with the order deferredSplit = true No split (normal payment) AMOUNT PERCENTAGE partnerId value reference × × × + Add leg Any Partner ID can be typed, including one that is not configured, to see the checks. For other combinations (for example a profile ID on a Dynamic MID), use Edit JSON. Request body Edit JSON Back to form { \"amount\" : 1000, \"currency\" : \"AED\", \"merchantReferenceId\" : \"ORD-DEMO-0001\", \"paymentOperation\" : \"Pay\", \"splitPayoutType\" : \"AMOUNT\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\" : [ { \"partnerId\" : \"P000301\", \"value\" : 500, \"reference\" : \"LEG-1\" }, { \"partnerId\" : \"P000302\", \"value\" : 300, \"reference\" : \"LEG-2\" }, { \"partnerId\" : \"P000303\", \"value\" : 100, \"reference\" : \"LEG-3\" } ], \"timestamp\" : ... , \"signature\" : ... , \"callbackUrl\" : ... } Create session 2"],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Create Session result: checks and order","split-payout/simulator.html#create-session-result-checks-and-order","PGW · SP-6 sections 1 to 5 order — Send a Create Session to see the checks. 3"],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Update APEX Create Transaction Federation","split-payout/simulator.html#update-apex-create-transaction-federation","Customer → PGW → APEX · SP-6 section 6, SP-7 APEX response Accepts (checks pass) Rejects: Partner ID inactive at APEX Not reachable (timeout) Create a session first. 4"],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Payout Advice Create/Update API","split-payout/simulator.html#payout-advice-create-update-api","Merchant → PGW → APEX · independent API · SP-8, SP-7 APEX response Accepts (checks pass) Rejects: Partner ID inactive at APEX Not reachable (timeout) Create a session first. 5"],["Split Payout","Split Payout Config & Payment Simulator","Visual page","Day-End Split Advice File Generation","split-payout/simulator.html#day-end-split-advice-file-generation","PGW → APEX · SP-9 · after the Ecomm cut-off (midnight, UAE) Day 1 Run day-end Day 1 so far No orders today yet. The file carries every advice created or updated today, in its latest state (action A or U). A PENDING advice is included: the file is the backup when the real-time call failed. It is also the only route for an UPDATE: the APEX API only creates advices (agreed with APEX on 23-Nov). Orders with no advice are not in the file. Source: SP-9 · Product doc 6.4 Files sent No file sent yet. Run day-end to send the Day 1 file."],["Split Payout","Split modes","Visual page","Split modes","split-payout/split-modes.html","visual · split payout Split modes Each partner MID uses one split mode. The mode decides who defines the split, and when: the partner with each order, or Geidea once in the PGW configuration. Illustrative Reflects product doc v1.6 and PGW stories v0.6 Read the requirement → Terms: partner = the Geidea merchant (MID) with split payout on; vendor = a payee of the split, identified by an ID from APEX (the stories call it a Partner ID). Field names are from the PGW stories and can be renamed by PGW engineering."],["Split Payout","Split modes","Visual page","The three modes side by side","split-payout/split-modes.html#the-three-modes-side-by-side",""],["Split Payout","Split modes › The three modes side by side","Visual page","Dynamic","split-payout/split-modes.html#dynamic","The partner sends the split with each order, or later. Who defines the split Partner per order Set up by Geidea in PGW Vendor IDs only. No template. Sent with the order splitPayoutType + splitPayoutTransaction[] , or deferredSplit = true , or nothing (normal payment) Split after payment Yes: Payout Advice API, NEW or UPDATE with legs Typical use Marketplace: several sellers in one checkout. Delivery platform: restaurant or store and the platform. Try it in the simulator →"],["Split Payout","Split modes › The three modes side by side","Visual page","Fixed","split-payout/split-modes.html#fixed","One template, set by Geidea, applies to every order. Who defines the split Geidea once, for all orders Set up by Geidea in PGW Vendor IDs + one template ( splitPayoutFixedTemplate ) Sent with the order Nothing. Any split field is rejected. Split after payment No. The Payout Advice API rejects a Fixed MID. Typical use Standing revenue share: the same share of every sale goes to a supplier. Try it in the simulator →"],["Split Payout","Split modes › The three modes side by side","Visual page","Profile","split-payout/split-modes.html#profile","Geidea sets a catalog of templates; the partner picks one per order. Who defines the split Geidea defines the catalog, partner picks per order Set up by Geidea in PGW Vendor IDs + profile catalog ( splitPayoutProfiles ), up to 10 profiles Sent with the order splitPayoutProfileId , or deferredSplit = true . Legs are rejected. Split after payment Yes: Payout Advice API, NEW or UPDATE with a profile ID Typical use Aggregator with standard terms: an insurance aggregator with a pre-agreed split per insurer. Try it in the simulator → Source: product doc v1.6, 2 Use cases and 3.2 Split modes ; stories SP-3 , SP-4 , SP-5 , SP-6 section 2 , SP-8 check 2.1 ."],["Split Payout","Split modes","Visual page","One order, three modes","split-payout/split-modes.html#one-order-three-modes","Dynamic Fixed Profile order amount (AED)"],["Split Payout","Split modes › One order, three modes","Visual page","Geidea Set up once in PGW","split-payout/split-modes.html#st1","Vendor IDs only. No split is stored. { \"splitPayoutMode\" : \"DYNAMIC\", \"splitPayoutPartners\" : [ \"P000301\", \"P000302\", \"P000303\" ] }"],["Split Payout","Split modes › One order, three modes","Visual page","Partner Sent with each order","split-payout/split-modes.html#st2","The partner sends the legs it wants for this order. { \"amount\" : 1000, \"currency\" : \"AED\", \"paymentOperation\" : \"Pay\", \"splitPayoutType\" : \"AMOUNT\", \"splitPayoutTransaction\" : [ { \"partnerId\" : \"P000301\", \"value\" : 500 }, { \"partnerId\" : \"P000302\", \"value\" : 300 }, { \"partnerId\" : \"P000303\", \"value\" : 100 } ], \"...\" : ... other Create Session fields }"],["Split Payout","Split modes › One order, three modes","Visual page","Legs on the order, sent to APEX","split-payout/split-modes.html#st3","PGW stores and sends the amounts: Vendor ID Amount P000301 500.00 P000302 300.00 P000303 100.00 Partner keeps the rest 100.00 Before MDR, VAT, partner commission and remittance fees. See Money flow . The split can differ on every order. The partner can also send deferredSplit = true and send the split after payment with the Payout Advice API. Examples: stories SP-4 section 4 (Fixed template) and SP-5 section 4 (profile catalog); Dynamic legs as in the product doc worked example. Illustrative values."],["Split Payout","Split modes","Visual page","Rules for every mode","split-payout/split-modes.html#rules-for-every-mode","A leg names a vendor ID and a value. The value is an amount or a percentage of the gross transaction amount, never both. All legs of one split use the same type: all amounts or all percentages. Percentages are converted to amounts by APEX, not by PGW. Each amount is rounded to 2 decimals; the partner's residual absorbs the rounding difference. Templates and profiles are configured by Geidea only. The partner has no access to split configuration. A split carries vendor IDs and values only. Bank details always come from Geidea's records, never from the partner's request. Maximum 10 legs per split (template, profile, or a split sent with an order or through the API). Configured as a system setting. Amount legs are checked against the order amount at Create Session, not at setup: the order amount is not known when a template or profile is saved. Source: product doc v1.6, 3.2 Rules common to all modes ; stories SP-2 section 2, SP-4 section 2, SP-6 check 3.6."],["Split Payout","Split modes","Visual page","Changing the mode or the set-up","split-payout/split-modes.html#changing-the-mode-or-the-set-up","Change saved in PGW Effect Mode changed Applies to orders created after the save. The old mode's template or profiles stay stored but are not applied. Orders already created keep their split. Template or profile edited Applies to orders created after the save. Orders already created keep the legs they were given. Profile deleted New orders with that profile ID are rejected. Vendor ID deleted New orders and API calls with that ID are rejected. Splits already sent to APEX do not change. A vendor ID used in the template or a profile cannot be deleted. Split turned off New orders with split fields and Payout Advice API calls are rejected. Orders already flagged stay flagged; APEX releases them after N days. Operations also turns split off in APEX. Source: stories SP-3 section 3 , SP-3 section 6 , SP-4 section 3 , SP-5 section 3 , SP-6 section 4 ."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","What can go wrong","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html","visual · split payout What can go wrong Every rejection and exception in a split payout, in the order an order meets them: from the PGW configuration to after the payout. Each case shows the message, what happens to the money and how it is fixed. \"Try it\" opens the simulator in that exact state. Illustrative Reflects PGW stories v0.6 and product doc v1.6 Read the requirement → Stories: SP-6 checks → SP-8 checks → Terms: partner = the merchant (MID) with split payout on; vendor = a payee of the split. Updates reach APEX only in the day-end file, as agreed with APEX on 23-Nov, and are accepted on the transaction date only (rule of 24-Nov). Stories v0.6 and doc v1.6 still describe a real-time update. The PGW stories call these merchant and partner, so the messages quoted from them say \"partner ID\" for a vendor's ID. Messages are verbatim from the stories; PGW engineering sets the error codes. 1 Configuration 5 cases 2 Create Session 17 cases 3 Payout Advice API 8 cases 4 Sending to APEX 1 cases 5 APEX checks 3 cases 6 Amount validation 3 cases 7 Day-end file 4 cases 8 Payout and after 4 cases 45 of 45 cases Rejected Held at APEX Released to the partner Handled, no loss Not defined yet"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","1. Configuration","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-cfg","PGW back office, before any order. Nothing is saved until the checks pass."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 1. Configuration","Visual page","SP-3 4.1–4.4 Partner ID entry is not valid Not saved","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cfg-partner-id","When Adding or editing a Partner ID in the pop-up. Message Use 3–20 letters, digits or hyphens. This Partner ID is already in the list. This Partner ID is configured on another MID (MID nnnnnnnnnnn). A partner belongs to one merchant MID only. Use up to 100 characters. Money None. Nothing is saved. Fix Correct the entry. For 4.3, ask Operations which MID the vendor belongs to; Operations created it in APEX. Note Why 4.3: APEX links each vendor to one partner MID. A Partner ID on a second MID makes APEX reject every split from that MID after the customer has paid. Open the configuration Story: SP-3 section 4 Product doc 7.1"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 1. Configuration","Visual page","SP-3 4.5 Deleting a Partner ID that is still in use Not deleted","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cfg-delete-used","When The Partner ID is used in the Fixed template or in a profile. Message P000301 is used in Fixed template. Remove it there first. Money None. Fix Remove the leg from the template or profile first. Open a Fixed configuration Story: SP-3 section 4"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 1. Configuration","Visual page","SP-3 5.1–5.2 Split on with no mode or no Partner ID Not saved","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cfg-save","When Save changes with split payout on. Message Select a split mode. Add at least one Partner ID. Money None. Fix Select a mode and add at least one Partner ID. Open with split off Story: SP-3 section 5 Product doc 7.1"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 1. Configuration","Visual page","SP-4 2.1–2.6 Fixed template is not valid Not saved","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cfg-fixed","When Save changes in Fixed mode. Message Add at least one leg. Select a Partner ID. Each partner can appear once. Enter a value above 0, up to 2 decimals. Maximum 10 legs. Percentages cannot add up to more than 100%. Money None. Fix Correct the template. Note An AMOUNT template is not checked against a total at set-up: the order amount is not known yet. Create Session checks it (see 3.6 below). Open a Fixed configuration Story: SP-4 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 1. Configuration","Visual page","SP-5 2.1–2.2 Profile mode with no profile, or too many Not saved","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cfg-profile","When Save changes in Profile mode. Message Add at least one profile. Maximum 10 profiles. Money None. Fix Add a profile, or remove one. Open a Profile configuration Story: SP-5 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","2. Create Session","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-cs","PGW checks the split before payment (SP-6). A failure rejects the session (HTTP 400): no payment is taken."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 2.2 · 3.1 Split fields sent to a MID with split payout off Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-off","When Any split field or deferredSplit on a MID where split payout is off. Message Split payout is not enabled for this merchant. Money No payment is taken. Fix Remove the split fields, or ask Geidea to enable split payout (see Onboarding). Try it Story: SP-6 section 3 Product doc 7.6"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 2.7 · 3.1 Split fields sent to a Fixed MID Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-fixed-fields","When A Fixed MID sends any split field or deferredSplit. PGW applies the template itself. Message Split fields do not match the merchant's split mode. Money No payment is taken. Fix Send no split fields on a Fixed MID. Try it Story: SP-6 section 2 Product doc 7.6"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 2.10 · 3.1 Legs sent to a Profile MID Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-profile-legs","When A Profile MID sends splitPayoutTransaction instead of a profile ID. Message Split fields do not match the merchant's split mode. Money No payment is taken. Fix Send splitPayoutProfileId. Try it Story: SP-6 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.1 Profile ID sent to a Dynamic MID Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-profile-on-dynamic","When A Dynamic MID sends splitPayoutProfileId. Message Split fields do not match the merchant's split mode. Money No payment is taken. Fix Send the legs in splitPayoutTransaction. Note SP-6 section 2 has no row for this combination; check 3.1 covers it. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 2.11 · 3.9 Split detail and deferredSplit together Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-both","When Legs or a profile ID, and deferredSplit = true, in one request. Message Send split detail or deferredSplit, not both. Money No payment is taken. Fix Send one or the other. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3 Product doc 7.6"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 1 Legs without splitPayoutType Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-no-type","When splitPayoutTransaction is sent with no splitPayoutType, or a leg has no partnerId or value. Message Field rule: splitPayoutType is mandatory when splitPayoutTransaction is sent (AMOUNT or PERCENTAGE). Money No payment is taken. Fix Send splitPayoutType with the legs. Try it Story: SP-6 section 1"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.2 Split on an operation other than Pay Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-not-pay","When paymentOperation is not Pay. Message Split payout is supported for Pay only. Money No payment is taken. Fix Use Pay. Note Why: in this release APEX handles split only on a sale. Authorize and capture are not included. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.3 Split on a non-AED order Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-not-aed","When Order currency is not AED. Message Split payout is supported in AED only. Money No payment is taken. Fix Use AED. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.4 Vendor ID not configured on the MID Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-unknown-id","When A partnerId is not one of the MID’s configured IDs. Message Unknown partner ID: {id}. Money No payment is taken. Fix Use a configured ID, or ask Geidea to add the vendor (see Onboarding). Try it Story: SP-6 section 3 Product doc 7.6"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.4 Same vendor ID twice Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-duplicate","When A partnerId occurs more than once. Message Duplicate partner ID: {id}. Money No payment is taken. Fix Send one leg per vendor. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.5 More legs than the maximum Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-too-many","When More legs than the system maximum (default 10, SP-2). Message Maximum {n} legs. Money No payment is taken. Fix Send 10 legs or fewer. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.6 Amount legs above the order amount Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-over-total","When AMOUNT: the legs add up to more than the order amount. Message Split total is more than the order amount. Money No payment is taken. Fix Reduce the legs. Note Passing 3.6 does not guarantee APEX accepts the amounts: APEX Rule 1 also takes MDR, VAT, commission and fees into account (see Amount validation). Try it Story: SP-6 section 3 Product doc 7.6 Money flow"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.6 Percentage legs above 100% Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-pct-over","When PERCENTAGE: the legs add up to more than 100. Message Split total is more than 100%. Money No payment is taken. Fix Reduce the percentages. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 2.6 · 3.6 Fixed AMOUNT template above the order amount Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-fixed-over","When A Fixed MID with an AMOUNT template larger than this order. Message Split total is more than the order amount. Money No payment is taken. The partner cannot fix this per order. Fix Geidea changes the template, or the partner sends larger orders only. Try it Story: SP-4 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.7 Leg value of 0, negative or more than 2 decimals Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-bad-value","When A value is not above 0 with up to 2 decimals. Message Invalid split value. Money No payment is taken. Fix Correct the value. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 3.8 Profile ID not configured Session rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-unknown-profile","When splitPayoutProfileId is not a profile of the MID. Message Unknown split profile: {id}. Money No payment is taken. Fix Use a configured profile ID. Try it Story: SP-6 section 3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 2. Create Session","Visual page","SP-6 section 2 Profile MID sends no profile ID and no deferredSplit Not defined","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#cs-profile-none","When A Profile MID sends a plain session with no split fields. Money Not defined. The simulator treats it as a normal session (no split, paid to the partner as today). Fix — Open point: SP-6 section 2 has no row for this case. Story: SP-6 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","3. Payout Advice API","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-api","The partner sends or corrects a split after payment (SP-8). A failure rejects the call (HTTP 400). The transaction stays held."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","SP-8 2.1 Called for a Fixed MID, or with split off Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-fixed","When The MID has split off, or is in Fixed mode. Message Split payout is not enabled for this merchant. Not allowed for Fixed split. Money Nothing changes on the order. Fix A Fixed split comes from the template; ask Geidea to change it. Try it Story: SP-8 section 2 Product doc 7.3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","SP-8 2.2 Order not found, not this MID’s, or not paid Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-not-paid","When The order does not exist, belongs to another MID, or has no successful Pay. Message Order not found. Order is not paid. Money Nothing changes. Fix Send the split after the payment succeeds. Try it Story: SP-8 section 2 Product doc 7.3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","SP-8 2.3 Order was created without split Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-not-split","When The order has splitPayout = false. Message Order is not a split order. Money The transaction was not held. It settles to the partner as normal. Fix None for this order. Create future orders with a split or deferredSplit. Note Why: an order without the flag was not held by APEX. Its money may already be paid to the partner. Try it Story: SP-8 section 2 Product doc 7.3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","SP-8 2.4 NEW for an order that already has a split Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-new-exists","When NEW on an order not created with deferredSplit, or that already has an advice. Message A split already exists for this order. Use UPDATE. Money Nothing changes. The existing advice stands. Fix Use UPDATE with the latest advice ID. Note Repeating a NEW with the same content returns the first advice ID, so a retry after a timeout is safe (product doc 7.3). Try it Story: SP-8 section 2 Product doc 7.3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","SP-8 2.5 UPDATE with an older advice ID Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-stale-id","When splitAdviceId is not the latest advice ID of the order. Message Advice ID is not the latest. Money Nothing changes. The newer correction stands. Fix Use the advice ID from the latest response. Note Why: a late retry of an old request must not overwrite a newer correction. Try it Story: SP-8 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","SP-8 2.6 The split fails the Create Session checks Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-over-total","When Unknown or duplicate vendor ID, too many legs, total too high, bad value or unknown profile (SP-6 3.4 to 3.8). Message As in SP-6, for example: Split total is more than the order amount. Money Nothing changes. The transaction stays held. Fix Correct the split and send again. Try it Story: SP-8 section 2"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","Rule 24-Nov UPDATE after the transaction date Call rejected","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-late-update","When An UPDATE sent after the transaction date, once PGW has generated that day’s day-end file. Message Message to be set in stories v0.6. Money Nothing changes. The advice keeps its last status; a held transaction stays held until a valid advice or the N-day release. Fix Send updates on the transaction date, before midnight (UAE time). Note Rule set by Rabbani on 24-Nov: updates are accepted on the transaction date only. Updates reach APEX only in that day’s day-end file (agreed with APEX on 23-Nov). Try it Story: SP-8 section 2 Product doc 7.3"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 3. Payout Advice API","Visual page","Open point 6 UPDATE after the MID’s mode was changed Not defined","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#api-after-change","When An order created in the old mode is updated after Geidea changed the mode. Money Not defined. Fix — Open point 6 in the stories: how an UPDATE is checked for an order created in the old mode. Story: Open points"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","4. Sending to APEX","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-send","PGW forwards the advice in real time (SP-7). A failure here never changes the payment result."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 4. Sending to APEX","Visual page","SP-7 4 APEX not reachable or times out Advice PENDING, retried","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#apex-down","When The real-time Split Advice API call fails. Money The transaction stays held (it is flagged). The payment result does not change. Fix None by the partner. PGW retries (count and interval: PGW engineering). The day-end file carries the advice in any case. Try it Story: SP-7 section 4 Product doc 7.5 Advice lifecycle"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","5. APEX checks","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-apex","APEX structural checks, in the call (product doc 6.4)."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 5. APEX checks","Visual page","APEX 6.4 Vendor not found, inactive or not linked to the MID Advice REJECTED · held","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#apex-reject","When APEX does not find a beneficiary, it is deactivated, or it is linked to another MID. Message Reason code from APEX (APEX to define). Money Nothing is paid to vendors. The transaction stays held. Fix The partner sends a corrected, complete split with UPDATE on the same day (the transaction date). PGW stores it and the day-end file carries it to APEX. If the vendor was deactivated, contact Geidea. Note PGW holds no vendor bank details and does not check vendor status: only APEX catches an inactive vendor. Try it Story: SP-7 section 4 Product doc 6.4 Advice lifecycle"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 5. APEX checks","Visual page","APEX 6.4 Other structural checks fail Advice REJECTED · held","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#apex-other","When MID does not match the transaction; transaction not flagged; both percentage and amount in a rule; A when an advice already exists; an update (in the day-end file) for an advice already PROCESSED or RELEASED. Message Reason code from APEX. Money The transaction stays held. Fix Mostly set-up or sequence errors: Geidea investigates. After PROCESSED or RELEASED the advice is final: no further updates. Note APEX no longer checks that split payout is enabled on the MID: PGW checks it, as it holds the split set-up (agreed with APEX on 23-Nov). Product doc 6.4 Advice lifecycle"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 5. APEX checks","Visual page","APEX 6.4 Advice arrives before its transaction, never matched PENDING_MATCH → REJECTED","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#apex-unmatched","When The advice reaches APEX before the transaction. APEX parks it as PENDING_MATCH. If still unmatched at day end, APEX rejects it with a reason code. Message Reason code from APEX. Money Held until a valid advice is processed, or released after N days. Fix Usually none: the transaction arrives and APEX links the advice. If rejected, the partner sends an UPDATE. Product doc 6.4 Advice lifecycle"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","6. Amount validation","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-amt","APEX checks the amounts once pricing is known (product doc 6.5). Any failure rejects the whole advice."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 6. Amount validation","Visual page","APEX Rule 1 Partner residual would be negative Advice REJECTED · held","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#amt-rule1","When net − Σ advised amounts + Σ vendor commissions − transfer fees borne by the partner < 0 (net = amount − MDR − VAT on MDR). Message Reason code from APEX, shown in the split payout report. Money Nothing is paid to vendors. The transaction stays held. Fix An UPDATE is accepted on the transaction date only. If this rejection comes after that date, it cannot be corrected and the transaction is released to the partner after N days. Note A split that passes PGW check 3.6 (total ≤ order amount) can still fail here, because MDR, VAT and fees come off first. Open points: whether PGW learns of this rejection (the real-time call already returned ACCEPTED); and when APEX runs amount validation, same day or later. If later, the rejection cannot be corrected. Product doc 6.5 Try it in Money flow"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 6. Amount validation","Visual page","APEX Rule 2 A vendor leg would be zero or negative Advice REJECTED · held","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#amt-rule2","When For any vendor: advised amount − commission − transfer fee (if the vendor bears it) ≤ 0. Example: a leg of 0.80, commission 0.02, fee 1.00 gives −0.22. Message Reason code from APEX. Money The whole advice is rejected, not only that leg. Fix Correctable only with an UPDATE on the transaction date; otherwise the transaction is released after N days. Product doc 6.5 Try it in Money flow"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 6. Amount validation","Visual page","APEX single payout A transaction would be paid out twice Blocked by APEX","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#amt-twice","When Any path that would pay the same transaction again. Money Blocked: a transaction is never paid out twice. Fix — Product doc 6.5"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","7. Day-end file","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-file","APEX reconciles the day-end file against the real-time advices (product doc 6.4)."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 7. Day-end file","Visual page","APEX 6.4 File split differs from the real-time advice File wins","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#file-diff","When The same advice is in the API and the file, with a different split. Money APEX overwrites with the file’s split and processes it. Fix None. This is also how every UPDATE reaches APEX: the APEX API only creates advices (agreed with APEX on 23-Nov). Story: SP-9 Product doc 6.4"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 7. Day-end file","Visual page","APEX 6.4 Advice only in the file Processed as new","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#file-only","When The real-time call never reached APEX. Money APEX processes it as new. This is how an API failure is covered. Fix None. Try it Story: SP-9 Product doc 6.4"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 7. Day-end file","Visual page","APEX 6.4 Advice received over the API, missing in the file Mismatched · held","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#file-missing","When APEX has the advice from the real-time call, but the day-end file does not carry it. Money APEX marks it mismatched and holds settlement until resolved. Fix Geidea investigates (PGW and APEX). Story: SP-9 Product doc 6.4"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 7. Day-end file","Visual page","APEX 6.4 No day-end file received API advices used","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#file-none","When The file does not arrive. Money APEX processes what it received over the API. Fix Geidea investigates the file delivery. Note PGW sends an empty file with a zero record count on days with no advices, so APEX can tell \"no advices today\" from \"file not received\". Story: SP-9 Product doc 6.4"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong","Visual page","8. Payout and after","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#stage-after","Hold timer, transfers, refunds and chargebacks (product doc 4.3, 6.3, 8)."],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 8. Payout and after","Visual page","APEX 6.3 No valid advice within N days RELEASED","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#after-released","When N days after the scheduled payout date, the transaction still has no PROCESSED advice: never sent, or rejected and not corrected. Money Released to the partner after Geidea’s MDR. Vendors are not paid through Geidea. Listed in the exception report for Operations and Finance. Fix Final. No further updates to the advice. Note N is set per partner MID and can be zero. The timer runs on the transaction, not on an advice. Product doc 6.3 Advice lifecycle"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 8. Payout and after","Visual page","Doc 4.3 Deferred split never sent Held, then released","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#after-never","When The order was created with deferredSplit = true and the partner never calls the Payout Advice API. Money Held; released to the partner after N days as above. Fix The partner sends NEW before N days pass. Product doc 4.3 Advice lifecycle"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 8. Payout and after","Visual page","Doc 4.3 · 6.6 Transfer to a vendor fails Exception process","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#after-transfer","When The domestic transfer to the vendor’s IBAN fails. Money Existing exception process: retry up to the configured limit, then an exception record. The case shows in the split payout report. Fix Geidea contacts the partner only, never the vendor. Product doc 6.6"],["Split Payout","What can go wrong › 8. Payout and after","Visual page","Doc 8 Refund or chargeback after the payout Debited from the partner","split-payout/what-can-go-wrong.html#after-refund","When A refund or chargeback on a split transaction. Money Debited from the partner’s MID as normal. Vendors and their payouts are not involved; Geidea does not recover from vendors. A small residual can leave the partner short on the day. Fix Shortfall recovery is set in the partner agreement and confirmed by Finance. New splits are not held back to recover it. Product doc 8"]]};
